window.FY_POSTS=[{"id":"2025-12-06_the-stories-we-live-05","date":"2025-12-06","title":"The Stories We Live 05","titleZh":"我们生活的故事05","subtitle":"Or: Those Who Refuse to Sink","subtitleZh":"又：那些拒绝沉没的人","excerpt":"Prologue If the 2020s began with people being jolted awake by a fault line, then spending the next few years trying to adjust to this new normal, this year should count as…","excerptZh":"（序） 如果说2020年代的开始是人们被断层惊醒，又在随后的几年中努力适应这种新常态的话，今年应该算得上是“装都不装了”年代的开始。这其中最大的变化应该…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2025-12-06_我们生活的故事05.jpg","tags":["our stories"],"tagsZh":["我们生活的故事"],"readingMin":33,"htmlEn":"<h2>Prologue</h2>\n<p><br>If the 2020s began with people being jolted awake by a fault line, then spending the next few years trying to adjust to this new normal, this year should count as the beginning of the era when nobody even bothers pretending anymore. The greatest change has been psychological. A few years ago, we thought the world was about to fall apart. We worried about the future, so we were always trying to stop something and, while we were at it, looking wistfully back at the old days. We were powerless as events unfolded, but we still imagined we could wait them out.<br><br>Perhaps everyone had finally had enough of sleeping with their heads buried under the covers. We all lost patience with that half-dead doze. Some grew restless to do something; others simply rolled up their sleeves and began pushing the boulder. Yet the days that had seemed to hang by mere threads now felt impossibly heavy. Was the knife too blunt, or the net too tight? Everyone believed they were the one being ignored, restrained, insulted, or harmed. Faced with the shackles clamped around us, we all craned our necks toward the shell that seemed ready to break open.<br><br>What comes after the shell breaks? Who the fuck cares.<br><br></p>\n<h2>I</h2>\n<p><br>I have a theory: the artichoke became food only because the people trimming it and the people eating it were two different groups.<br><br>Before you reach the tender heart, you have to strip away the tough outer leaves, the thorny tips and pointed crown, the fuzzy choke, the fibrous skin of the stem, and the hard base. By the end, an entire green lotus bud has been whittled down to one tiny lotus seat.<br><br>When Elon Musk assembled the Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), the federal government must have looked much the same to him: waiting obediently to die, with very little of it actually useful.<br><br>Musk&#x27;s romantic fantasy is understandable. Once you treat government as nothing more than an inorganic, functional machine—and what a bloated machine it is—every process inside it can be abstracted into a shortest-path problem and radically optimized for efficiency. Within eighty hours of its creation, DOGE had efficiently canceled forty million dollars in contracts; within a little over half a year, it had cut roughly two hundred thousand federal jobs.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a>, <a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>DOGE announces results from its first 80 hours of work — and it&#x27;s way behind schedule, Business Insider, Jan 24, 2025</span></span><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>Trump Has Forced Out Nearly 10% Of The Federal Workforce, HuffPost, Aug 26, 2025</span></span><br><br>The people Musk put to work at DOGE were not seasoned insiders but brilliant young engineers with almost no experience.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>The Young, Inexperienced Engineers Aiding Elon Musk’s Government Takeover, WIRED, Feb 2, 2025</span></span> They knew systems and quantification, and they believed that in any complex, high-dimensional, abstract problem, gradient descent could solve everything.<br><br>The method rests on an approximation: a person is a resource; a person is a component; a person is a geometric symbol in an input-output ratio.<br><br>But people are still people.<br><br>And where there are people, politics follows.<br><br>Musk has believed in and exploited first-principles thinking his entire life, to astonishing commercial success. But this may have been the first time he himself became the first principle. Someone had figured him out: put Musk on a pedestal beneath a grand title that looked vaguely like “minister,” and he would dance with excitement, hack through the mess, and offend crowds of people he never cared about anyway.<br><br>It may be conspiratorial to say that Musk was deliberately used as a weapon. A more realistic possibility is that certain “enablers” wanted his radical methods to tear open a breach at lightning speed, then planned to let events run their course if all went well. That did not happen. Once public fury began to boil, the enablers took the referee&#x27;s chair with an air of “let me be fair here,” diverted attention, and shifted the cost-cutting plan into a less visible mode.<br><br>But hey, you and I still thought it was all the fault of that goddamn Musk!<br><br>In <em>Life Is Elsewhere</em>, Milan Kundera wrote: “Youth, poetry, and revolution are one and the same.” All three arise from idealistic passion; all three distort the substance of life through lyricism. Many revolutions failed not because of enemies, blockades, or repression, but because they were too romantic and too pure—because they took ideas too seriously and people not seriously enough.<br><br>So youth, poetry, and the Department of Government Efficiency are also one and the same. Exactly the same.<br><br></p>\n<h2>II</h2>\n<p><br>OpenAI&#x27;s most powerful man, Sam Altman, once said he liked to “build in public.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>Sam Altman says OpenAI&#x27;s new releases make him feel like a &#x27;YC founder&#x27; building things in public all over again, Business Insider,…</span></span> He dismissed old-fashioned annual launches and the practice of saving up for one giant announcement, so OpenAI created a new corporate culture: ship without warning and ruin a rival&#x27;s launch event.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>On February 15, 2024, OpenAI released the video model Sora; later that day, Google released Gemini 1.5 Pro. On May 13, 2024, OpenAI…</span></span><br><br>But those tricks belonged to a year ago, when OpenAI was lonely at the top and everyone else combined still could not beat it. It had the leisure to invent new ways of provoking people. Altman solemnly said, “We named it ChatGPT and not a person&#x27;s name very intentionally”—which, in his telling, was meant to keep people from getting addicted to this phenomenal product with a cold codename.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman has &#x27;deep misgivings&#x27; about people becoming friends with AI, Business Insider, Oct 18, 2023</span></span><br><br>OpenAI is no longer so relaxed. More precisely, it is panicking its ass off. Not long ago, with growth slowing, ChatGPT loosened its restrictions on adult content. Altman&#x27;s response was that the company was “not the elected moral police of the world.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>Sam Altman says OpenAI isn&#x27;t &#x27;moral police of the world&#x27; after erotica ChatGPT post blows up, CNBC, Oct 15, 2025</span></span> Then Google released Gemini 3, and the whole company sounded a code red over its survival.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>Sam Altman declares ‘Code Red’ as Google’s Gemini surges—three years after ChatGPT caused Google CEO Sundar Pichai to do the same,…</span></span> At the product level, ChatGPT now has to defend growth and retention—which is to say it would love nothing more than for you to spend every day getting horny over a cold codename.<br><br>I have run through all this news to make one point: Altman really is one hell of a flexible loudmouth. But think one step further and you realize that all three men leading America&#x27;s big three in AI—Sam, Dario, and Musk—are eager to put themselves forward, strut around, and say something shocking.<br><br>Sometimes the coincidence makes me laugh out loud. At this turning point in human history, the figures taking the stage exposed their cores so early, and those cores conform so perfectly to the <strong>stereotypes</strong> of similar characters in fictional worlds.<br><br>(Let me repeat something I wrote in 2022: “To anthropomorphize with the greatest possible charity: America is like Gendo Ikari, with a righteous plan known to all and a private scheme behind it; China is like Asuka, who believes it is the elite&#x27;s duty to save the ignorant masses. Neither is easy to deal with.”)<br><br>Sam and Dario are the Gendo Ikari and Asuka of AI.<br></p>\n<h3>1.0</h3>\n<p><br>Sam&#x27;s hypocrisy is obvious to everyone. Just as conspicuous are his political cunning and personal charm. The famous venture capitalist Paul Graham long ago described Altman&#x27;s magic: “You could parachute him into an island full of cannibals and come back in 5 years and he&#x27;d be the king.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>A fundraising survival guide, Paul Graham, Aug 2008</span></span> Indeed, during OpenAI&#x27;s internal purge in 2023, a man publicly denounced by the board as dishonest somehow returned to the center of power as though nothing had happened. Anyone who can manipulate politics with such ease obviously has the skill to match his ambition. But what is Altman&#x27;s ambition?<br><br>In the middle of the year, Sam published “The Gentle Singularity” on his personal blog.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-10\" aria-label=\"Reference 10\">10</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">10</span><span>The Gentle Singularity, Sam Altman, Jun 10, 2025</span></span> “We are past the event horizon; the takeoff has started,” he claimed. Given our benign trajectory, humanity could move safely and smoothly into the future through good governance and a consensus on values. It makes perfect sense, and not one word of it should be believed. Gendo Ikari&#x27;s story tells us that although you can never quite tell what this man is thinking, one thing is certain: what he wants is absolutely not what he says. I do not believe in the gentle singularity. More than that: in any future where he remains center stage, I should not permit myself even that hope.<br></p>\n<h3>2.0</h3>\n<p><br>To describe Dario&#x27;s personality more precisely, we need to bring in a second fictional universe. The more accurate formulation is this: Dario is Asuka after a stint at <strong>Red Coast Base</strong>.<br><br>Dario himself says almost nothing about his time at Baidu Research. But there is no way private hostility—or at least private emotion—played no part when, at a moment that demanded no such public alignment, he announced that Chinese companies and their overseas subsidiaries would be barred from using Claude,<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-11\" aria-label=\"Reference 11\">11</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">11</span><span>Updating restrictions of sales to unsupported regions, Anthropic, Sep 4, 2025</span></span> or when he solemnly proclaimed that Anthropic had foiled a Chinese government plot to conduct cyberattacks with AI.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-12\" aria-label=\"Reference 12\">12</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">12</span><span>Disrupting the first reported AI-orchestrated cyber espionage campaign, Anthropic, Nov 13, 2025</span></span><br><br>It is hard to associate Dario himself with political maneuvering. He was not even invited to Trump&#x27;s White House dinner on AI and technological sovereignty.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-13\" aria-label=\"Reference 13\">13</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">13</span><span>Sam Altman, Tim Cook, and other tech leaders lauded Trump at a White House AI dinner, Business Insider, Sep 4, 2025</span></span> Meanwhile, he blurted out in an interview that one-fifth of entry-level white-collar jobs would disappear within one to five years.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-14\" aria-label=\"Reference 14\">14</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">14</span><span>AI CEO says technology ‘moving very quickly,’ could soon replace more jobs, CNN, Sep 17, 2025</span></span> (The wily old Altman was inside the White House flattering Trump, having also written the blog full of lies mentioned above.) Dario plainly has little experience in commercial warfare, and I would bet he is proud of that inexperience.<br><br>That is Dario&#x27;s arrogance: almost everything he says, he says sincerely. He sincerely believes that no AI but his is safe enough. He sincerely believes the dull-witted public needs to be awakened by a thunderclap. He believes the evolution of humanity is his personal responsibility. Until he learns to be smooth, you can believe anything he says. But you should also know that he is the kind of person who would pull the lever in the trolley problem without hesitation and never suffer a pang of conscience. He would believe that the extraordinary people chosen to grow the new world naturally deserve rights beyond the norms of existing society.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-15\" aria-label=\"Reference 15\">15</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">15</span><span>Confessions, Kanae Minato, 2007.</span></span><br><br>(As for what happened at Red Coast Base? Before the Advent, he may keep it all to himself.)<br></p>\n<h3>3.0</h3>\n<p><br>Once we have Red Coast and Ye Wenjie, how could we leave out the triumphalist Zhang Beihai? Electric cars, interplanetary rockets, xAI built to fight OpenAI, lavish funding for Trump&#x27;s campaign: every one of Musk&#x27;s actions is for the future, every stroke of madness and genius born from despair at humanity.<br></p>\n<h3>4.0</h3>\n<p><br>Let us vote democratically! Between hypocrisy, arrogance, and despair, choose the habitat best suited to humankind.<br><br>Or would you rather turn your gaze eastward?<br><br></p>\n<h2>III</h2>\n<p><br>From DeepSeek&#x27;s thunderclap at the beginning of the year, through Alibaba&#x27;s Qwen models dominating the rankings, to Kimi K2 Thinking&#x27;s long-horizon reasoning and tool use, it is now an industry consensus that China has some of the world&#x27;s best open-source large language models. These results were achieved under severe constraints on compute. I find that encouraging, but not surprising: when it comes to tightening our belts and fighting our way back, we do possess unrivaled historical experience.<br><br>I know another way of living with a tightened belt. Not long ago, I watched quite a few guides to low-cost urban survival, the practice known as “wall-hanging”: living on odd jobs and day wages, holding consumption to the absolute minimum, and spending your entire life in power-saving mode. Among young people in somewhat better circumstances, another trend has emerged: “free ingredient mystery boxes,” or collecting vegetables that fall from wholesale trucks or look too ugly to sell as market stalls open or close.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-16\" aria-label=\"Reference 16\">16</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">16</span><span>Foraging at wholesale markets around the country has become Gen Z&#x27;s new nightlife, Tencent, Sep 7, 2025</span></span> Videos of both can now be found online, which means their existence is an officially permitted expression within the language of “consumption downgrading.”<br><br>There are more explicit ways to talk about tightening belts, such as: “Manual laborers are sexually repressed Android users.” Every noun in that sentence opens onto its own black-comic theoretical framework, so nearly every hot social issue in China this year falls within its range.<br><br>There are many ways to refuse to sink on a tightened belt. They may take the form of conversations, follow-along interviews, first-person records, or clipped livestreams. Whether they lean toward self-narration, experience, or entertainment, at least someone is speaking—even if that speech is deconstructive or mocking. And whether they set out to laugh at themselves, ask a question, or expose something, the fact that these videos become popular tells us where attention is gathering.<br><br>I cannot say more about this. I only hope that some of our economic and spiritual deprivation can be eliminated, and that these antagonisms can be reconciled with greater tenderness.<br><br></p>\n<h2>IV</h2>\n<p><br>I do not know a single one of the young people who film themselves scavenging vegetables at markets. That makes what I just said sound phony, and I entirely accept the criticism: everything I described above came to me secondhand. What follows, however, is based on fieldwork. If you want to become an adult, do not go to Silicon Valley.<br><br>I first visited California in 2018. I was a naive kid fresh from reading <em>The Enigma of Silicon Valley</em> and <em>The Crest of the Wave</em>,<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-17\" aria-label=\"Reference 17\">17</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">17</span><span>The Crest of the Wave (《浪潮之巅》), Wu Jun, 2011; The Enigma of Silicon Valley (《硅谷之谜》), Wu Jun, 2015.</span></span> and my fellow graduate students and I all dreamed of going to Silicon Valley. The trip left me disillusioned, because I decided that the technology center of the world and I were fundamentally incompatible. I wrote at the time: “These tech giants carve the territory into concession-like enclaves, each with an air of autonomy. The indifference to their surroundings born of this sheltered isolation once again displays the refined elitism I despise most.”<br><br>This year, Silicon Valley exported a concept called “taste” to the world. The idea is that, in the age of AI, how one uses tools will matter less and less—AI can stitch anything together for you—while the decisive difference between things will be human taste. Here are a few moments of exquisite Silicon Valley taste: taking home enough food from the free company lunch to feed yourself and your entire extended family at dinner; bringing company plates and bowls home so you never have to buy tableware; buying a Tesla but charging it exclusively at work; maintaining a detailed taxonomy of points and cash-back benefits across more than a dozen credit cards, and taking pride in it.<br><br>For elite employees paid far more than the general public, these habits seem rather petty. I am not the only person who finds them ridiculous. But most critiques then follow the path of “vision” and “aesthetics,” until everything is blamed on “small-town test-taking strivers with no humanities education.” I want to offer another view.<br><br>Once mature products and advertising systems have passed their high-growth phase, Silicon Valley&#x27;s data-driven decision framework begins to “overoptimize.” To meet a metric, engineering teams rack their brains to push click-through rate, time spent, daily active users, and so on toward a limit visible only in the statistics. Apple&#x27;s products are “no longer cool.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-18\" aria-label=\"Reference 18\">18</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">18</span><span>This came from a friend who had just left Apple for a startup. I think I have already lost this kind of purity when I look at many…</span></span> Nobody would dream of using something as antiquated as Facebook. Yet quarter after quarter, these companies&#x27; core products still contribute steady, marginal growth to earnings. Silicon Valley engineers have brought this system into their daily lives: benefits, cash back, subsidies, and taxes are optimized to the limit, until life becomes one enormous exercise in reward hacking.<br><br>Quantifiability sits at the core of the Silicon Valley elite mind, and it also shapes the Valley&#x27;s appetite for comparison. Just as you look like a hick walking down a Shanghai street without a bottle of pour-over coffee, in Silicon Valley your identity comes under suspicion if your T-shirt and backpack bear no company logo. You probably do not work at a big tech company. If you do, how could you resist displaying your badge at all times? After one quick glance, the stranger passing by can be modeled immediately as a function of salary, title, and address. Anything beyond that is irrelevant, and truly cannot be seen. Sometimes it is seen, but there is no reason to admit it.<br><br>Silicon Valley loves persuading people outside software to “learn to code,” which tells you that software engineering occupies the gleaming top rung of its ladder. Engineers from Chinese backgrounds, however, complain of a career ceiling: they can perfect their technical ability but never break through some invisible promotion line. At the same time, they watch more and more people of Indian background rise along the senior management track. To be fair, this is not about so-called technical competence. It really is about politics and manipulation—flattery, nepotism, games of talk. But these things do not take place in public. They do not appear among the official evaluation criteria of the performance system, so they are treated as dirty.<br><br>Silicon Valley has a simple moral code. Its most naked power games unfold there, yet rank-and-file engineers are well protected—or, you could say, well deceived. They cannot see power circulating, so they believe in a “clean” meritocracy: only achievements captured by rankings and scoring systems count as legitimate excellence.<br><br>This is not entirely their fault. Silicon Valley&#x27;s “elite” branding and its student mentality are two sides of the same coin. In values and lived experience, the Valley is terrifyingly homogeneous.<br><br>I have heard that, even after the pandemic, many Chinese universities continue to impose a closed-management system resembling high school: cards at the gates, approval required to leave, students&#x27; daily lives strictly confined within the campus walls, with little chance for friction with society as it actually exists. Silicon Valley offices resemble campuses; three free meals a day resemble the cafeteria; periodic reviews resemble semester report cards. Big-tech employees live inside a preset schedule: commuter shuttle, buffet lunch, gym after work, then the Bay Area&#x27;s three clichés on weekends.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-19\" aria-label=\"Reference 19\">19</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">19</span><span>Fruit picking, hiking, and playing Werewolf.</span></span> You can earn far more than your peers in such a system while rarely having to face a genuinely public situation.<br><br>Historically, wealth and power generally match. The more money you have, the louder your voice. As people move upward, they naturally assume more social roles and acquire greater influence and authority. The strangeness of Silicon Valley is that a substantial number of people possess immense economic power while apparently not existing in society at all. A poverty of diversity has turned the place into an echo chamber inside an ivory tower. Silicon Valley looks like a continuing-education institute for practitioners of polished self-interest.<br><br>When Chinese internet giants lay people off, they say the employees have “graduated.” It is cruel, but also really fucking precise.<br><br></p>\n<h2>V</h2>\n<p><br>Some people are no longer students but refuse to graduate; another group of students is desperate to drop out. Venture capital firm a16z says AI has created a structural opportunity, making 2025 the best opportunity for young founders,<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-20\" aria-label=\"Reference 20\">20</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">20</span><span>From Dorm Rooms to Boardrooms: Why Now Is a Special Time for Young Founders, a16z, Mar 21, 2025</span></span> while the share of current college students or recent graduates in Y Combinator has shot from 10 percent to 30 percent.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-21\" aria-label=\"Reference 21\">21</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">21</span><span>Sam Altman Says He Envies Today&#x27;s College Dropouts, Business Insider, Oct 7, 2025</span></span><br><br>They have brought some genuine student behavior to Silicon Valley, such as not drinking at parties—some founders are under the legal drinking age. But more than that, they have brought the fearlessness and appetite for a gamble peculiar to the young.<br><br>You might not expect that, alongside open-source large language models, Chinese technology companies also exported the 996 work culture this year: 9 a.m. to 9 p.m., six days a week.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-22\" aria-label=\"Reference 22\">22</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">22</span><span>Silicon Valley AI Startups Are Embracing China’s Controversial ‘996’ Work Schedule, WIRED, Jul 23, 2025</span></span> Silicon Valley&#x27;s AI startups have abandoned big tech&#x27;s concern for work-life balance and benefits. Nearly everyone is in the office at least five days a week. Founders tell employees plainly: if your passion cannot sustain an eighty-hour workweek, you should not join us.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-23\" aria-label=\"Reference 23\">23</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">23</span><span>Three weeks after acquiring Windsurf, Cognition offers staff the exit door, TechCrunch, Aug 5, 2025</span></span><br><br>There is, of course, a logic to this madness. AI products have not yet built their moats. The land grab is raging, and the giants are circling. Everyone in the game believes the winner will take all, so beneath the accelerationism lies a genuine fear for survival: if you are not greedy enough, you become food.<br><br>In China, manual laborers who go all in seem to feel the whole social and cultural climate pushing from behind. Behind these young founders lies an equally cold reality: AI replacing white-collar work; STEM unemployment in the United States exceeding humanities unemployment for the first time; large companies reducing hiring across the board; entry-level jobs disappearing. Yet the forces creating this climate are by no means limited to anxiety.<br><br>Even among founders, Cluely&#x27;s CEO stands out. He first became known for recording himself using AI software he had written to “cheat” his way through an Amazon interview. Columbia University expelled him, and he rode the attention into founding Cluely. From the beginning, the company has prized internet instinct: one hiring requirement was at least one hundred thousand social-media followers. It bought a giant billboard in Times Square, but filled it with just three lines of black lowercase text on white:<br></p>\n<blockquote class=\"pull\"><p>hi i&#x27;m roy im 21<br>this was very expensive<br>pls buy my thing</p></blockquote>\n<p><br>This year, I listened to a podcast featuring Roy.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-24\" aria-label=\"Reference 24\">24</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">24</span><span>Building Cluely: The Viral AI Startup that raised $15M in 10 Weeks w/ Roy Lee, a16z YouTube, Jun 25, 2025</span></span> On his blog, he says his genius lies in understanding the mechanics of internet virality, while “those middle-aged geezers only post dry shit on social media.” As he said it, he had the look of a scammer watching older people fumble with their feature phones:<br><br>These people are rich and know nothing. Why not fleece them?<br><br>The young blood and audacity of Silicon Valley founders deserve praise. Or put it this way: if you have barely entered college when rich people tell you that degrees are useless,<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-25\" aria-label=\"Reference 25\">25</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">25</span><span>Peter Thiel pays people $200,000 to drop out of college; Palantir has begun recruiting directly from high school through its Merito…</span></span> then watch an endless wave of layoffs and graduates tumbling straight into unemployment, how are you supposed to save yourself? If you still possess confidence, you will probably embrace a form of agency built around “refusing mediocrity,” one in which your identity has to be confirmed by severing yourself from the mediocre masses.<br><br>I have read in more than one place that many Silicon Valley founders are fans of Ayn Rand.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-26\" aria-label=\"Reference 26\">26</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">26</span><span>I Thought I Knew Silicon Valley. I Was Wrong, WIRED, Sep 22, 2025</span></span> I read <em>The Fountainhead</em> when I was young, but my own ambition was never large enough, so I was moved only by its solitude, independence, and resolve. When I returned to my notes this year, I realized that Rand reduces the “ideal man” to an embodiment of willpower and originality. Evil, by contrast, is given a concrete subject: you can point to a person, a group, or a system and say that it obstructs humanity&#x27;s march toward greatness. In the introduction to <em>The Fountainhead</em>, she wrote: “In every generation, only a few men continue to understand and fully to achieve the proper stature of man—and the rest betray it.” This framework, with its crisp division between good and evil, is perfectly suited to a Silicon Valley with a special fetish for disruption.<br><br>Here lies the problem. In the stories these AI founders tell, there is the close-up of “My heart is in the work” and the long shot of “AI will reshape everything.” There is the justice of devoting oneself completely to technological progress, and the evil of trying to obstruct the advance of AI applications. Most of the gray territory in the middle may as well not exist. High-profile startup Mercor proclaims a mission of “unlocking human potential,”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-27\" aria-label=\"Reference 27\">27</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">27</span><span>Unlocking Human Potential in the AI Economy, Mercor, Oct 27, 2025</span></span> then turns around and cuts the pay of its data annotators,<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-28\" aria-label=\"Reference 28\">28</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">28</span><span>An AI startup powering Meta and OpenAI cut thousands of workers — then offered them a similar project for less money, Business Insi…</span></span> as though their potential had become a little too great.<br><br>We could summarize the whole thing as one more episode of “capitalist hypocrisy and cruelty.” But I see it more as Kua Fu chasing the sun—the giant who pursued it until he died of thirst. These young people genuinely believe in the distant vision of AI radically improving humanity&#x27;s fate. But the vision has no connection to society as it exists; more than that, everything now happening in society seems to threaten their utopia.<br><br>This has happened more than once before: we will benefit humanity, but first we have to destroy you.<br><br></p>\n<h2>VI</h2>\n<p><br>The lack of connection is at the root of everything.<br><br>Silicon Valley&#x27;s elite move directly from school into big tech, or directly into being a founder. They see people as abstractions: labor costs, head count, membership conversion, customer acquisition. But in their own lives, they rarely have to face more flesh-and-blood people. They fundamentally lack the sensation of feet planted on earth—what one might call “grassroots experience.” And this is absolutely not just a problem for Silicon Valley or the Department of Government Efficiency.<br><br>The individualization brought by modernity, combined with social media&#x27;s projection of distant vistas straight onto the individual, has produced a kind of weightlessness: a rupture in the sense of control and a confusion of identity. Where is the boundary between person and society? What lies between a person and the world? Some describe this condition as “the missing middle distance.”<br><br>I gave this year&#x27;s retrospective the subtitle “Those Who Refuse to Sink.” When I say refusing to sink, what I really mean is rebuilding the middle distance: beyond grand narratives and personal experience, seeing once again the concrete faces of other people.<br><br>But I am not nostalgic. I know the old middle distance was no good either.<br><br>This year, I read several sociological books about rural China. That middle distance was deeply stifling, like an airtight wall forcing “people to direct all their attention toward the village right in front of them... The meaning of life becomes winning the envy and applause of the village, with little motivation to improve one&#x27;s life. In this culture, interpersonal relations weigh too heavily; they squeeze the individual out.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-29\" aria-label=\"Reference 29\">29</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">29</span><span>“A Maverick Pig” (《一只特立独行的猪》), Wang Xiaobo.</span></span> That middle distance was pathological. As Chinese urbanization broke apart traditional relations, it quickly swept them into history&#x27;s dustbin—though not quite cleanly.<br><br>In the middle distance, the previous generation&#x27;s problems concerned conscience and courage: do you dare to leave, and once you have, can you hold yourself back? Ours concern hope and honesty. I think we are increasingly able to tolerate ourselves and increasingly unable to tolerate other people. We are too good at reducing everything to “none of my fucking business” and “none of your fucking business.” At the slightest friction in values, we turn away from everything we share. We accept loneliness because we assume no form of common life is worth the time it takes to cultivate and repair.<br><br>Technology has given us the freedom to block, the freedom to leave the group, the freedom to escape. Rebuilding demands more patience. I do not want to pretend things have nothing to do with me. I do not want to pass off detachment as elegance. I do not want to say the first to care is the one who loses. My thought is very simple: I need other people, and I need other people to need me.<br><br></p>\n<h2>VII</h2>\n<p><br>No one can deny that we are living through a historical cycle of profound change. The unprecedented events alone are almost too many to follow. Yet it is just as easy to imagine that, in <em>A Short History of XX</em> two hundred years from now, every life lived by our generation will be compressed into a handful of sentences.<br><br>Historical writing requires a distilled thesis and a commanding view. Everyone understands that dimensionality reduction always brings information loss. I feel nothing about that in the abstract. But when I imagine facing such compression myself, I find it hard to breathe. Statistics is uncanny. A common denominator is really nothing at all. The parts averaged away, the parts represented out of existence, are the true substance of life. Only the flattened fluctuations of the everyday deserve to be called life itself.<br><br>No one can deny that history&#x27;s moments of “phase transition” always arrive at points of structural imbalance, and circumstance plays a substantial role. California was the first state in American history to grant women property rights. It was not because the legislators had suffered an attack of conscience, but because without such rights, the migrant men arriving en masse would have become an increasingly destabilizing force, and an already extreme gender imbalance would have worsened.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-30\" aria-label=\"Reference 30\">30</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">30</span><span>Women in the California gold rush, Wikipedia</span></span> The Emancipation Proclamation came from Lincoln&#x27;s moral convictions, but even more from his wartime strategy—the emancipation policy was a means of winning the war. As he said: “My paramount object in this struggle is to save the Union, and is not either to save or to destroy slavery.”<br><br>But if we stop asking questions there—if we decide that the wheel of history rolls forward on its own, casually correcting a few injustices along the way and randomly selecting a few lucky spectators to hit the jackpot and become heroes—then we fall into the aerial-view trap again. We reduce everything to mechanical structures, as though the course of history bore no relation to human agency.<br><br>For a long stretch last year, I was terrified. I thought: how miserable, how deflating, to live through history&#x27;s garbage time, spending one&#x27;s days inside a span destined to be forgotten. Now I understand. There is too much history books do not bother to mention, so most of history is garbage time to begin with. But that has a positive meaning.<br><br>These unremembered stretches carry uncertain trends. They hold contradictory forms of new life sprouting through the everyday, multiple forces already in motion. What they represent is not a fated ending but formation itself.<br><br>This year, I visited three new places: Hawaii, Sacramento, and New Orleans. On the surface, they are merely scattered American place names. In some <em>Short History of the United States</em>, they might be condensed into three unrelated phrases: vacation destination, birthplace of the Gold Rush, home of jazz.<br><br>But I found folds hidden inside all three names, folds that could be unfolded in a polynomial expansion. Hawaii was the epicenter of the attack on Pearl Harbor, but it also stages the most authentically American fireworks display. Sacramento uses a freeway to sever the city from its riverbank, as though afraid to admit that it once lived by water. The lower Mississippi resembles a fish skeleton: seven hundred rib-like roads branching from its spine still lead toward the seven hundred plantations that multiplied here two centuries ago. After these wounds receded, the fertile ground continued to struggle. It remains one of America&#x27;s poorest and most polluted regions.<br><br>Across these journeys, I strung together an understanding of many things: how national memory is distorted and consumed; how capital and development plans break or conceal the historical grain of cities; how thunderous proclamations of progress are systematically obstructed; how economics, class, and culture continually become sources of dependence or shackles across different times and places.<br><br>Yes, from three thousand feet above the ground, we can make any number of deterministic claims—for example, that the Americas were colonized because their continental axis ran north to south.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-31\" aria-label=\"Reference 31\">31</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">31</span><span>Guns, Germs, and Steel, Jared Diamond, 1997.</span></span> But only by entering the streets, seeing walls peel, smelling the air found nowhere else, and hearing the meaningless static between monument and monument can we be moved, and can we understand the true scale of these folds.<br><br>History is gradual by nature. Iron the folds flat and they open into a long slope with no end in sight, not a step waiting to trip us—and at every present moment, we do indeed stand at the beginning of that slope.<br><br></p>\n<h2>VIII</h2>\n<p><br>To understand history, I think, is mainly to understand its inertia. Epochal events do not appear from nowhere, and they do not transform everyone&#x27;s life overnight. They are more like buoys in a harbor: sailors use them to find their position, but the currents beneath the surface never stop moving.<br><br>In 1928, the Representation of the People (Equal Franchise) Act passed, and British women gained the vote on equal terms. But for many days before that, amid advancing industrialization and urbanization, women had already been working in factories, hospitals, and offices. Their growing participation in the relations of social production gradually widened the scope in which rights had to apply. The Act merely gave this current its belated name.<br><br>But a promise on paper does not mean a happy ending. Women&#x27;s rights did not fully improve with the vote. Unequal pay for equal work, invisible career ceilings, stereotypes of the “strong woman” and the “good woman”: these problems remain unresolved even today. The definition of “feminism” itself has been diminished in public discourse, from a vanguard identity into a story of conflict between the sexes. The year 1928 is a vivid marker in the movement for women&#x27;s rights. A long uphill road led to it, and an equally long tail trails behind.<br><br>In 1965, civil-rights legislation dismantled legal segregation. Many of the Martin Luther Kings who began boycotts and sit-ins, then were beaten, arrested, and executed, did not live to see that day. But without decades of people throwing themselves into organizing and mobilization, race would never have reached the public agenda in the first place.<br><br>Afterward, overt discrimination was no longer accepted by the mainstream, but suspicious neighborhood boundaries, biased lending policies, and disparities in sentencing persisted. These aftereffects would require more Martins, and those Martins might no longer have names of their own.<br><br>So when I say “garbage time has a positive meaning,” I do not mean that I am waiting for some “formation” and hoping it suits my tastes. I hope to adopt an orientation toward action. I want to write the stories of years that have not yet settled into history, because what people actually do determines which way the moment of “phase transition” will finally tip.<br><br>(I suspect Foucault wanted a productive account of power, rather than a purely negative and repressive one, from a similar concern with agency.)<br><br>I keep writing in order to make quick sketches of the details against a purely aerial narrative. To act only as a recorder carries its own passivity—as though I could do nothing but lie on the riverbed, watching the glimmering light drift across my body.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-32\" aria-label=\"Reference 32\">32</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">32</span><span>“Years Like Flowing Water” (《似水流年》), Wang Xiaobo, 1992.</span></span> In the end, because I live inside history, I cannot become detached and panoramic.<br><br>I want always to write from the perspective of an ordinary humanist. Inevitably, among the things I witness or hear, some deserve sympathy, praise, grief, or contempt, and I will take a clear position toward them. But I do not want to confine myself to the framework of victimhood. I do not want clean binary oppositions. I do not want to preach heroism or construct figures resembling gods and demons. At the end of the story, I do not want to seal everything inside retribution, the reversal of extremes, the return of fortune after adversity, or the justice of heaven.<br><br>History always remembers direction and structure, I think. But people live on the ground, not along the curvature of the sky. From the ground, what we see is unstable and unresolved: an everyday life in which good and evil are mixed. At this scale, an orientation toward action asks us to remain sensitive, to welcome being moved, and to refuse to grow accustomed.<br><br>I would like to say I write in order to refuse forgetting, but that too is a heroic formulation. So all I can say is: I think, therefore I am—except that by “am,” I mean not that I exist, but where I ought to stand.<br><br></p>\n<h2>IX. For Maimai</h2>\n<p><br>Maimai<br>laugh out loud when you laugh<br>Maimai<br>life is never elsewhere<br><br>Maimai<br>your first language is Sichuanese<br>come, repeat after me<br>a dog chasing a motorbike<br>knows nothing of science<br><br>Maimai<br>when you are sad, plant yourself in the earth<br>the earth and stars will offer no words of comfort<br>but the earth is so immense<br>and the sky so vast<br><br>Maimai<br>the soul accumulates fat too<br>when that happens, people burp and fart<br>and are even more likely to find new things indigestible<br><br>Maimai<br>after sunlight plows across me<br>little suns glow red and hot on my skin<br>I am allergic, but that is not all—<br>Maimai<br>beauty can make anything pregnant<br>do not remain unmoved by her<br><br>Maimai<br>when the sun goes down, it gets cold<br>when it gets cold, put on more clothes<br>I will say the first line to you countless times<br>your mother will say the second line countless times<br>but thirty years from now<br>it will be your turn to say them<br><br>Maimai, you may go live on Mars<br>but do not forget this Earth and all its life<br>because here we have<br>not only crimes, but courage, songs, and miracles</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/elon-musk-doge-early-spending-dei-contracts-2025-1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DOGE announces results from its first 80 hours of work — and it&#x27;s way behind schedule</a>, Business Insider, Jan 24, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.huffpost.com/entry/trump-fires-10-percent-federal-workforce_n_68aca73de4b01646d84f8363\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Trump Has Forced Out Nearly 10% Of The Federal Workforce</a>, HuffPost, Aug 26, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/elon-musk-government-young-engineers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Young, Inexperienced Engineers Aiding Elon Musk’s Government Takeover</a>, WIRED, Feb 2, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/sam-altman-openai-releases-make-him-feel-yc-founder-again-2025-4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman says OpenAI&#x27;s new releases make him feel like a &#x27;YC founder&#x27; building things in public all over again</a>, Business Insider, Apr 2, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span>On February 15, 2024, OpenAI released the video model Sora; later that day, Google released Gemini 1.5 Pro. On May 13, 2024, OpenAI released its flagship model GPT-4o; Google I/O 2024 opened the following day…</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/sam-altman-misgivings-friendships-ai-personality-chatbots-openai-2023-10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman has &#x27;deep misgivings&#x27; about people becoming friends with AI</a>, Business Insider, Oct 18, 2023</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span><a href=\"https://www.cnbc.com/2025/10/15/altman-open-ai-moral-police-erotica-chatgpt.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman says OpenAI isn&#x27;t &#x27;moral police of the world&#x27; after erotica ChatGPT post blows up</a>, CNBC, Oct 15, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://fortune.com/2025/12/02/sam-altman-declares-code-red-google-gemini-ceo-sundar-pichai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman declares ‘Code Red’ as Google’s Gemini surges—three years after ChatGPT caused Google CEO Sundar Pichai to do the same</a>, Fortune, Dec 2, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span><a href=\"https://www.paulgraham.com/fundraising.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">A fundraising survival guide</a>, Paul Graham, Aug 2008</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-10\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">10</span><span><a href=\"https://blog.samaltman.com/the-gentle-singularity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Gentle Singularity</a>, Sam Altman, Jun 10, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-11\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">11</span><span><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/updating-restrictions-of-sales-to-unsupported-regions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Updating restrictions of sales to unsupported regions</a>, Anthropic, Sep 4, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-12\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">12</span><span><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/disrupting-AI-espionage\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Disrupting the first reported AI-orchestrated cyber espionage campaign</a>, Anthropic, Nov 13, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-13\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">13</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/whos-who-tech-leaders-attended-dinner-white-house-altman-pichai-2025-9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman, Tim Cook, and other tech leaders lauded Trump at a White House AI dinner</a>, Business Insider, Sep 4, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-14\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">14</span><span><a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/09/17/business/anthropic-warns-ai-could-soon-replace-jobs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI CEO says technology ‘moving very quickly,’ could soon replace more jobs</a>, CNN, Sep 17, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-15\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">15</span><span><em>Confessions</em>, Kanae Minato, 2007.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-16\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">16</span><span><a href=\"https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20250907A06WMR00\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Foraging at wholesale markets around the country has become Gen Z&#x27;s new nightlife</a>, Tencent, Sep 7, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-17\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">17</span><span><em>The Crest of the Wave</em> (《浪潮之巅》), Wu Jun, 2011; <em>The Enigma of Silicon Valley</em> (《硅谷之谜》), Wu Jun, 2015.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-18\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">18</span><span>This came from a friend who had just left Apple for a startup. I think I have already lost this kind of purity when I look at many things.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-19\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">19</span><span>Fruit picking, hiking, and playing Werewolf.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-20\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">20</span><span><a href=\"https://a16z.com/from-dorm-rooms-to-boardrooms-why-now-is-a-special-time-for-young-founders/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">From Dorm Rooms to Boardrooms: Why Now Is a Special Time for Young Founders</a>, a16z, Mar 21, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-21\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">21</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/sam-altman-openai-envies-college-dropouts-stanford-devday-2025-10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman Says He Envies Today&#x27;s College Dropouts</a>, Business Insider, Oct 7, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-22\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">22</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/silicon-valley-china-996-work-schedule/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Silicon Valley AI Startups Are Embracing China’s Controversial ‘996’ Work Schedule</a>, WIRED, Jul 23, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-23\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">23</span><span><a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/05/three-weeks-after-acquiring-windsurf-cognition-offers-staff-the-exit-door/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Three weeks after acquiring Windsurf, Cognition offers staff the exit door</a>, TechCrunch, Aug 5, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-24\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">24</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BR1-JrGbwxY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Building Cluely: The Viral AI Startup that raised $15M in 10 Weeks w/ Roy Lee</a>, a16z YouTube, Jun 25, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-25\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">25</span><span>Peter Thiel pays people $200,000 to drop out of college; Palantir has begun recruiting directly from high school through its Meritocracy Fellowship.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-26\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">26</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/silicon-valley-politics-shift/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">I Thought I Knew Silicon Valley. I Was Wrong</a>, WIRED, Sep 22, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-27\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">27</span><span><a href=\"https://www.mercor.com/blog/series-c/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Unlocking Human Potential in the AI Economy</a>, Mercor, Oct 27, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-28\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">28</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/mercor-cuts-contractors-meta-project-less-money-musen-nova-ai-2025-11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An AI startup powering Meta and OpenAI cut thousands of workers — then offered them a similar project for less money</a>, Business Insider, Nov 12, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-29\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">29</span><span>“A Maverick Pig” (《一只特立独行的猪》), Wang Xiaobo.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-30\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">30</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women_in_the_California_gold_rush\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Women in the California gold rush</a>, Wikipedia</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-31\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">31</span><span><em>Guns, Germs, and Steel</em>, Jared Diamond, 1997.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-32\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">32</span><span>“Years Like Flowing Water” (《似水流年》), Wang Xiaobo, 1992.</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>（序）<br><br>如果说2020年代的开始是人们被断层惊醒，又在随后的几年中努力适应这种新常态的话，今年应该算得上是“装都不装了”年代的开始。这其中最大的变化应该是心态上的：前几年我们觉得世界摇摇欲坠，对未来忧心忡忡，于是总想要制止点什么，顺便缅怀回想一下老日子；看到事件发生时无能为力，但又觉得捱捱也就会过去。<br><br>不知道是不是受够了这种把头盖在被子里睡闷觉的日子，所有人都终于对半死不活的假寐失去了耐性，或汲汲躁动于想做点什么，有的甚至干脆撸起袖子推起石头——但那些仿佛只是被丝线悬住的日子此刻却又显无比滞重（是刀太钝了还是网太紧了？）。每个人都觉得自己才是被漠视、掣肘、侮辱或损害的那个，而面对施与自己的桎梏——我们都翘首以盼那呼之欲出的破壳。<br><br>破壳之后是什么？管他妈的。<br><br><br>（一）<br><br>我有一个理论：洋蓟之所以成为食物，是因为剥洋蓟的和吃洋蓟的其实是两拨人。<br><br>因为在最终得到可食用的嫩叶心之前，你得先削掉外层老叶、叶尖带刺和顶部尖锥、花托中的绒毛、茎的外皮以及底部的硬蒂。一通操作下来，洋蓟从一整朵待开的绿色莲花变成了迷你的坐莲似的一小块。<br><br>马斯克组建效率部（DOGE）的时候，他眼中的联邦政府部门大概就跟这簇洋蓟没什么两样：乖乖等死，而且有用的部分确实不多。<br><br>我们确实可以理解马斯克的浪漫幻想：一旦把政府只当做是台无机质功能性的机器（它是那么臃肿！），其中各个流程都可以被抽象成“最短路线”问题大幅优化提升效率。所以效率部在成立80小时内就高效地砍掉了四千万美元的合同，并在半年多内削减约二十万联邦岗位。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a>, <a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>DOGE announces results from its first 80 hours of work — and it&#x27;s way behind schedule, Business Insider, Jan 24 2025</span></span><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>Trump Has Forced Out Nearly 10% Of The Federal Workforce, Huffpost, Aug 26, 2025</span></span><br><br>马斯克组建的效率部里干活的不是什么资深内行专家，而是几乎没有经验的天才少年工程师 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>The Young, Inexperienced Engineers Aiding Elon Musk’s Government Takeover, Wired, Feb 02, 2025</span></span>，他们精通系统、量化，相信在高维和抽象的复杂问题之中，梯度下降可以解决一切。<br><br>这套方法论依赖一个近似：人是一种资源，人是一种零件，人是投入产出比中的一个几何符号。<br><br>但人毕竟只是人。<br><br>而一旦有了人，也就有了江湖。<br><br>马斯克一辈子都笃信和利用第一性原理（这让他取得了惊人的商业成就），但这可能是他头一次成为”第一性“本身：有人摸透了他的脾气——如果把马斯克供到一个貌似”部长“的高帽上，他就会手舞足蹈、快刀斩乱麻，然后到处得罪一大群他本就不在乎的人。<br><br>说马斯克被别有用心地被当枪使可能是阴谋论，更现实的情况应该是，一些“赋能者”希望借助这种激进的做派闪电般撕开一道口子，如果情况良好就任其发展——这并没有发生；而一旦要面对沸腾的民怨，则以“我说句公道话”的姿态走上仲裁席，调虎离山，然后将成本控制计划转入更加隐蔽的执行模式。<br><br>但是嘿，你我还都以为全该怪那该死的马斯克呢！<br><br>昆德拉在《生活在别处》中写道：“青春，诗歌，革命是一回事”。因为它们都产生于理想主义的激情，都以抒情的方式在扭曲本质的生活。很多革命失败了，不是因为敌对、封锁、或镇压，而是因为它们太浪漫太纯粹，太把观念当回事，又太不把人当回事了。<br><br>所以其实青春，诗歌和国家效率部也都是一回事，也是完全相同的一回事。<br><br><br>（二）<br><br>OpenAI的最大权力者山姆·奥特曼曾说，他喜欢Build in public <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>Sam Altman says OpenAI&#x27;s new releases make him feel like a &#x27;YC founder&#x27; building things in public all over again, Business Insider,…</span></span>，并对老派的年度发布会和憋大新闻的做法不以为然，所以OpenAI创立了新的企业文化：毫无预兆地更新，给友商的发布会添堵 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>2024年2月15日，发布视频模型Sora；当日晚些时候，谷歌发布Gemini 1.5 Pro。2024年5月13日，发布旗舰模型GPT-4o；次日，谷歌召开I/O 2024发布会……</span></span>。<br><br>但这些技俩又是一年前的事了，那个时候的OpenAI寂寞的很，所有人加起来都打不过它，所以它就有些闲心去别出心裁地招惹别人。奥特曼当时崇高地说道：把这款现象级产品命名为ChatGPT这个冷冰冰的代号，就是为了提醒大家千万别上瘾 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman has &#x27;deep misgivings&#x27; about people becoming friends with AI, Business Insider, Oct 18, 2023</span></span>。<br><br>可是现在的OpenAI没那么从容了，或者说它其实慌得一批。不久前，面对增长放缓，ChatGPT放宽了成人内容的限制，奥特曼对此的回应是“公司又不是道德警察” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>Sam Altman says OpenAI isn&#x27;t &#x27;moral police of the world&#x27; after erotica ChatGPT post blows up, CNBC, Oct 15, 2025</span></span>。而在谷歌的Gemini 3发布后，整个公司干脆拉响关乎存亡红色警报 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>Sam Altman declares ‘Code Red’ as Google’s Gemini surges—three years after ChatGPT caused Google CEO Sundar Pichai to do the same,…</span></span>，具体到产品层面，ChatGPT要重点保增长保粘性，也就是说，它现在巴不得你天天对着一个冷冰冰的代号发情。<br><br>我引用了这么多新闻，只是想说明一件事：奥特曼可真是个身段灵活的大嘴巴啊。但你接着再想就会发现，其实美国AI御三家的所有掌门人——山姆、达里奥、马斯克，都是不惮于抛头露面、招摇过市和语惊四座的。<br><br>我有时会对这种巧合笑出声：在这个人类历史的转折点上，这些登台亮相的人物是如此早早地暴露了自己的核心，而这些核心又是如此符合虚构世界中相似角色的<strong>刻板印象</strong>。<br><br>（我要再重复一遍2022年写过的东西：“以最佳的善意来拟人：美国像是碇源堂，他有一个天下皆知的正义计划和背后的小算盘；中国像是明日香，她相信拯救愚民是精英的任务。——和这两种人打交道都不轻松。”）<br><br>而山姆和达里奥就是AI界的碇源堂和明日香。<br><br>1.0<br><br>山姆的虚伪是有目共睹的，和他袒露的虚伪相提并论的则是他的权术伎俩和人格魅力。著名风投家保罗·格雷厄姆早就说过说奥特曼的魔法就在于“哪怕你把他扔到语言不通的食人岛上，他也能成为这群生番的族长。” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>A fundraising survival guide, Paul Graham, Aug, 2008</span></span> 的确，2023年的那场OpenAI的内部肃清中，一个被委员会公开斥为“不诚实”的人，居然又无事发生一样回到了权力中心——能如此举重若轻地操弄政治，当然有着于野心相匹配的手腕。但奥特曼的野心是什么？<br><br>年中，山姆在个人博客上写下“温和奇点” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-10\" aria-label=\"Reference 10\">10</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">10</span><span>The Gentle Singularity, Sam Altman, Jun 10, 2025</span></span>，他在文中声称：我们已经跨过技术奇点，并且从目前的良性轨迹来看，人类可以通过良好的治理和价值观的共识，安全平滑地迈向未来。很有道理，但一句也不能信，因为碇源堂的故事告诉我们，你虽然摸不清这个男人在想什么，但有一件事可以确定：他想要的绝对不是他说出口的。温和奇点不可信，非但不信，在他仍留在舞台中央的任何未来里，我甚至都不应该报以这样的希望。<br><br>2.0<br><br>为了更准确地表达达里奥的人格特质，为此还需要引入第二个虚构现实。更准确的说法是：达里奥是曾在<strong>红岸基地</strong>待过的明日香。<br><br>达里奥本人对在百度研究院的经历讳莫如深，但在明显不需要鲜明站队的日子宣布禁止一切中国企业以及中国企业在海外子公司使用Claude <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-11\" aria-label=\"Reference 11\">11</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">11</span><span>Updating restrictions of sales to unsupported regions, Anthropic, Sep 4, 2025</span></span>，又或者煞有介事地宣布成功挫败一起中国政府使用AI进行网络攻击的阴谋 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-12\" aria-label=\"Reference 12\">12</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">12</span><span>Disrupting the first reported AI-orchestrated cyber espionage campaign, Anthropic, Nov 13, 2025</span></span>，这其中不可能没有掺杂敌意或至少是一些私人情绪。<br><br>很难把达里奥本人和权术这个词联系起来——特朗普在白宫招待的关于AI和科技主权的晚宴上他根本没被邀请 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-13\" aria-label=\"Reference 13\">13</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">13</span><span>Sam Altman, Tim Cook, and other tech leaders lauded Trump at a White House AI dinner, Business Insider, Sep 4, 2025</span></span>；而他本人则愣头愣脑地在采访中冲动预言1/5的基础白领工作将在一至五年内消失 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-14\" aria-label=\"Reference 14\">14</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">14</span><span>AI CEO says technology ‘moving very quickly,’ could soon replace more jobs, CNN, Sep 17, 2025</span></span>。（老奸巨猾的奥特曼这个时候正在白宫里面拍特朗普的马屁，又写了上面说到的那篇全是谎话的博客）显然，他在商战上是没什么经验的，而我敢说他甚至为这种无经验感到骄傲。<br><br>达里奥就是这样的傲慢，他几乎从来都是在认真说真心话；他是真心觉得除了自家的AI其他的都不够安全，他真心觉得驽钝的公众需要被一声惊雷唤醒，他觉得让人类进化他责无旁贷……在他变圆滑之前，你可以相信他的任何话，但你也得知道，他是那种在电车难题中会果断拉下杠杆并从不为此受到良心折磨的人，他会认为被选中的非凡人物为了新世界的成长，当然该有超越现行社会规范的权利 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-15\" aria-label=\"Reference 15\">15</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">15</span><span>告白，湊佳苗，2007</span></span>。<br><br>（至于红岸基地里发生了什么？降临之前他可能会把一切都藏在心里。）<br><br>3.0<br><br>有了红岸和叶文洁，怎么能没有胜利主义的章北海呢？电车、星际火箭、为了和OpenAI对抗而建立的xAI、大力资助特朗普的竞选……马斯克的所有行动都是为了未来，所有疯狂和天才的手笔都是出于对人类的绝望。<br><br>4.0<br><br>让我们民主地投票吧！在虚伪、傲慢和绝望之中，选出人类最适合的居所。<br><br>还是说，你想把眼光投向东方呢？<br><br><br>（三）<br><br>从年初deepseek的惊雷，到后面阿里巴巴Qwen模型的霸榜，再到Kimi K2 Thinking的长程推理与工具调用，中国有世界上最顶尖的开源大模型这件事已经是行业共识，更何况这些成绩是在算力被严重制约的条件下达成的。我为此感到鼓舞，但也谈不上意外，因为在勒紧裤腰带打翻身仗这件事情上，我们确实有着无与伦比的历史经验。<br><br>关于勒紧裤腰带，我还知道另一种人生态度。前些日子我看过不少低成本的城市生存指南，即所谓“挂壁”。过挂壁人生，就是仅靠零工和日结挣钱，以最低限度的消费维持一种全天候节能模式的生活。另外在一群景况更好的年轻人中间还流行一种所谓“免费食材盲盒”，就是指在菜场出摊和收摊的时候去捡些批发货车上掉下来的或者卖相不佳的蔬菜 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-16\" aria-label=\"Reference 16\">16</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">16</span><span>去各地批发市场“打野”，成了00后的新型夜生活，腾讯网，Sep 07, 2025</span></span>。这两类的视频如今在网络上都找得到，也就是说它们的存在是官方允许的在“消费降级”语境下的适当表达。<br><br>关于勒紧裤腰带，还有一些更为露骨的表述，比如“力工就是性压抑的安卓用户”。这句话里的每一个名词都能引出一套独立的黑色幽默的理论框架，所以它的射程里几乎荟萃了今年中国所有热门的社会议题。<br><br>关于勒紧裤腰带，有多种多样的拒绝沉没。它们可能是对谈、跟踪采访、第一人称记录，或者直播切片。无论是否偏向自叙、体验或娱乐性质，至少有人在发声，哪怕是解构性的、戏谑的发声；而无论他们的出发点是否想要自嘲、发问或者想要曝光，这类视频能火，本身就说明焦点在向哪个方向聚拢。<br><br>对此我无法说得更多，但我希望一些经济和精神的匮乏可以被消除，这些对立可以被更温柔地弥合。<br><br><br>（四）<br><br>那些拍自己在市场捡菜的年轻人我一个都不认识，这就让我刚刚的话显得假惺惺的，对于这种批评我完全接受，因为对于上面描述的东西我完全只是道听途说。但是下面的观点则是基于田野调查：想当个成年人，就别去硅谷。<br><br>2018年，我第一次去加州。那时我是一个刚刚读完《硅谷之迷》和《浪潮之巅》<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-17\" aria-label=\"Reference 17\">17</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">17</span><span>浪潮之巅，吴军，2011；硅谷之谜，吴军，2015</span></span> 的愣头青，我和我周围的研究生们做梦都想去硅谷。但旅行后我感到失落，因为我认定自己和世界的科技中心三观不合，那时我写道：“（这些大厂）各自形成一个个租界样的划分，颇有种自治的意味。而那种从被庇护的隔绝中生出的对周遭的淡漠感，则再次展现为一种我最为厌恶的精英的精致性。”<br><br>硅谷今年给世界输送了个叫做“品味”（taste）的概念，就是说AI时代如何使用工具会变得越来越不重要（AI可以帮你缝合一切），而拉开事物差距的主要在于人的品味。而以下是一些硅谷的有品时刻：从公司的免费午餐里帮自己和三亲六戚打包一份晚饭，把公司的碗盘带回家从而免于买餐具，购买特斯拉但只用公司的充电桩，详细区分自己的十余张信用卡的每一项积分和返现并以此为荣。<br><br>对于一群薪资远超大众的精英打工者，这些做法看上去有些小家子气。也不仅仅是我一个人觉得这种现象令人哑然失笑，但在多数情况下，之后的批判会沿着”“格局”啊“审美”啊这样的线索展开下去，到最后总是被归结为一种“小镇做题家人文熏陶的匮乏”。而我想提供另一种视角。<br><br>在成熟产品和广告系统上，硅谷数据驱动的决策框架在度过高增长期后就会开始“过度优化”：为了完成一个指标，工程团队要费尽心思把点击率、停留时长、日活等等，推到一个仅有统计意义的极限。苹果的产品已经”不酷了” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-18\" aria-label=\"Reference 18\">18</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">18</span><span>这出自一个刚刚从苹果跳槽去初创企业的同学之口，我觉得我在看很多东西的时候已经丧失了这种纯粹性。</span></span>，没有人会再使用Facebook这种老掉牙的玩意儿，但几乎每个季度，这些公司的财报里的核心产品仍在贡献稳定的小幅增长。硅谷工程师把这套体系带到了自己的起居里：把福利、返现、补贴、缴税都优化到极致，然后生活就成了一场大型reward hacking。<br><br>可量化性是硅谷精英思维的核心，这也体现在硅谷热爱攀比的氛围里。就像你走在上海的街上没有拿一瓶手冲咖啡就显得像个乡毋宁，在硅谷如果你的T恤和背包如果没有公司的Logo也被人质疑身份：你大概不是大厂的吧。如果是大厂的，你怎么能忍住不把工牌随时亮出来呢？在这样迅速的一瞥之后，这个擦肩而过的人可以被立刻建模：一个关于薪资、职称、住址的函数。除此以外的东西不重要，也确实看不到。有时是看到了，但又没理由承认。<br><br>硅谷热衷于说服非软件类工作者“转码”，这说明软件工程师在硅谷是一个台阶鲜亮的顶端。但中国背景的工程师，又会抱怨自己遇到职业天花板：技术做到极致，却始终无法突破某条晋升线。与此同时，他们又亲眼看到，高层管理线上，印度裔背景的人愈发地多起来。公正地讲，这不是所谓技术能力，而确实有关政治和操弄，是马屁，是裙带关系、是嘴皮子游戏——但这些东西并不出现在公开场合，不是明面上的绩效系统里的能力评审标准，所以它们被认为是些脏东西。<br><br>硅谷有一套朴素的道德标准。尽管这里上演着最赤裸的权力游戏，但基层的工程师被保护得很好（也可以说是被欺骗得很好），他们看不见权力的流通，所以他们信奉着一种“干净”的优绩主义：能够被排行榜和评分系统捕捉到的成就，才被视为正当的优秀。<br><br>这不能完全怪他们，因为硅谷的“精英”招牌和它的“学生气”本就是硬币的两面，因为在价值观和生活经验层面，硅谷同质化得可怕。<br><br>我听说，疫情之后，中国不少大学至今仍维持着类似高中的封闭管理：校门刷卡，外出审批，学生的日常生活被严格控制在院墙之内，很难与真正地社会发生摩擦。而硅谷的园区像校园，免费三餐像食堂，周期评审像学期成绩单；大厂员工在一套预设好的日程里生活：通勤班车、自助午餐、下班健身房、周末湾区三俗 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-19\" aria-label=\"Reference 19\">19</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">19</span><span>指：摘水果，徒步，和玩狼人杀</span></span>。你可以在这样的体系里拿到远超同龄人的收入，却很少需要面对真正的公共场景。<br><br>历史上看，一般而言，财富和权力是相匹配的。你有多少钱，你就能说多大声的话。一个人在向上流动的过程中，自然会承担更多的社会角色，获得更大的影响力和话语权。但硅谷的奇特就在于，这里有相当部分的人有着极其可观的经济力量，但在社会上这群人好像完全不存在。多样性的匮乏，让这里成为一个象牙塔里的回音壁，所以硅谷看上去像是为精致利己主义者开办的继续教育机构。<br><br>中国互联网大厂裁员的时候说“毕业了”，这很残忍，但也很他妈的精确。<br><br><br>（五）<br><br>有人不是学生了却老不毕业，但另一群学生却着急着辍学。风投机构a16z称AI带来结构性的机遇，所以2025年是年轻人最好的创业机会 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-20\" aria-label=\"Reference 20\">20</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">20</span><span>From Dorm Rooms to Boardrooms: Why Now Is a Special Time for Young Founders, a16z, Mar 21, 2025</span></span>，而YC孵化器中在校大学生或应届毕业生的占比从10%骤增至30% <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-21\" aria-label=\"Reference 21\">21</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">21</span><span>Sam Altman Says He Envies Today&#x27;s College Dropouts, Business Insider, Oct 07, 2025</span></span>。<br><br>这群人给硅谷带来了些许正儿八经的学生气，比如聚会不饮酒（有的创业者不满法定年龄）；但更多的是一种独属年轻人的勇猛和赌性。<br><br>你也许想不到，中国的科技公司今年除了出口开源大模型以外，也出口了996的工作文化 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-22\" aria-label=\"Reference 22\">22</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">22</span><span>Silicon Valley AI Startups Are Embracing China’s Controversial ‘996’ Work Schedule, Wired, Jul 23, 2025</span></span>。硅谷的AI创业公司一改大厂对工作生活平衡及福利制度的重视，几乎是全员至少五天坐办公室；创始人会明确告诉员工：如果你的激情不能支持你一周工作80小时的话，你最好还是不要加入我们 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-23\" aria-label=\"Reference 23\">23</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">23</span><span>Three weeks after acquiring Windsurf, Cognition offers staff the exit door, TechCrunch, Aug 05, 2025</span></span>。<br><br>这种疯狂当然有它的道理。AI产品的护城河还没有建立起来，圈地运动如火如荼，各大巨头虎视眈眈；参与这场游戏的所有人都笃信赢家通吃，所以这种加速主义背后也确实是有危急存亡的担忧：不足够贪婪，就会成为食物。<br><br>在中国，“力工梭哈”背后似有一种社会文化的整体环境在隐隐推动；而这群年轻的创业者背后，也有着冰冷的现实：人工智能对白领工作的取代，美国理科失业率首次高于文科失业率，大型企业整体缩招，初级岗位的消失……但形成这种气候的原因绝不仅仅来自忧虞。<br><br>在众多创业者中，Cluely的CEO也算得上个性鲜明的一个。他最早为人所知是录播用自己编写的AI软件“作弊”通过亚马逊的面试，最后被哥伦比亚大学开除，然后他就借势创立了Cluely。从成立初始，这个公司就一直重视网感，比如说它的招聘条件里要求申请人社交媒体的粉丝数不少于10万个。它在寸土寸金的时代广场曾买下巨幅广告位，但只用白底黑字的纯小写写了三行字：“你好我是罗伊我今年21了/这玩意儿死贵/求你们来买我的东西吧。”<br><br>我今年听了罗伊作嘉宾的一场播客 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-24\" aria-label=\"Reference 24\">24</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">24</span><span>Building Cluely: The Viral AI Startup that raised $15M in 10 Weeks w/ Roy Lee, a16z YouTube, Jun 25, 2025</span></span>。他在博客里说自己的天才就在于深谙互联网的病毒传播之道，而“那些中登只会在社交媒体上发些干巴巴的东西”。他说这话的时候那个眼神就像诈骗分子看长辈们用老年机一样：<br><br>这群人又有钱又什么都不懂，为什么不宰他们一刀呢？<br><br>硅谷的创业者们年轻气盛，这是值得嘉许的事情。或者说，如果你刚考入大学就听到有钱人说“学历无用” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-25\" aria-label=\"Reference 25\">25</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">25</span><span>硅谷教父彼得·蒂尔支付20万美元让人辍学；Palantir开始直接招收高中生</span></span>，接着看到层出不穷的裁员和毕业即失业的窘境，你又该如何自救？如果你对自己还保有信心的话，大概率就会进入一种“拒绝平庸”的能动性里，而这个状态下你需要通过与庸众割席来获得身份确认。<br><br>我在不只一个地方看到，硅谷的创业者中不少是安·兰德的书迷 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-26\" aria-label=\"Reference 26\">26</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">26</span><span>I Thought I Knew Silicon Valley. I Was Wrong, Wired, Sep 22, 2025</span></span>。我年轻时看过《源泉》，但因我个人的野心不够大，所以仅仅让触动停留在了对孤独、遗世独立和决心的欣赏上。今年我再次翻看摘抄，我才发现安·兰德会把所谓“完人”简化成精神力、独创性的代表；而与之相对的邪恶则有某种主体性：就是说你可以指向一个人、一个团体、或一个系统，然后说，它在人类的伟大进程里扮演了绊脚石的作用。《源泉》的序言中她写道：“每一代人中，只有少数人能够完全理解和完全实现人类的才能，而其余的人都背叛了它。”这种可以明快划分正邪的框架，正适用于对颠覆有着特殊迷恋的硅谷。<br><br>问题就在这里。在这些AI创业者的叙事里，有“My heart is in the work”的近景，也有“AI重塑一切”的远景；有把个人全身心献给科技进步的正义，也有妄图阻碍智能应用发展的邪恶。至于这中间的大部分灰色地带，就跟完全不存在似的。明星创业公司Mercor一边说自己的使命是“解放人类的潜力” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-27\" aria-label=\"Reference 27\">27</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">27</span><span>Unlocking Human Potential in the AI Economy, Mercor, Oct 27, 2025</span></span>，一边转头就给数据标注员降薪 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-28\" aria-label=\"Reference 28\">28</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">28</span><span>An AI startup powering Meta and OpenAI cut thousands of workers — then offered them a similar project for less money, Business Insi…</span></span>，就像是嫌他们潜力太大似的。<br><br>我们尽可以把整件事概括为又一个“资本家的伪善和无情”，但我更觉得这是个夸父逐日的例子：这些年轻人是真的相信AI能大幅改善人类命运的远景，但这个愿景与现实社会没有联结，更一进步说，这个社会目前发生的一切好像都在对那种乌托邦造成威胁。<br><br>这种事之前也发生过几次：我们造福人类，但是要消灭你们。<br><br><br>（六）<br><br>缺乏联结是一切的症结所在。<br><br>硅谷的精英从学校直通大厂，或者直通一个创始人的身份；他们眼里有一些抽象的关于人的表达：用人成本、员工数量、会员转化、客户获取，但他们在生活中不用面对更多的有血有肉的人。他们从根本上缺乏把脚踩在土地上的实感，或者“基层经验”。而这绝对不仅仅只是硅谷或政府效率部的问题。<br><br>现代化带来的个体化现象，加上社交媒体向个人直接的远景投射造成了一种失重现象，一种掌控感的割裂，一种身份上的迷茫。人和社会的边界在哪里？人和世界之间有什么？有人把这种状态描述为“中景缺失”。<br><br>我把这个年度总结的副标题取为“拒绝沉没的人”——而当我说拒绝沉没，我真正想说的是重建中景：在宏大叙事和个人经验之外，重新看见他人的具体面目。<br><br>但我不是一个怀旧的人。我知道旧的中景也不是什么好东西。<br><br>我今年看了一些关于中国乡村的社会学的书，那种中景是很消极的，像有一堵密不透风的墙逼着“人把全部注意力都放到眼前这个自然村里……生活的意义变成了博取村里人的嫉妒、喝彩，缺少改善生活的动力。这个文化里，人际关系的分量太大，把个人挤没了。” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-29\" aria-label=\"Reference 29\">29</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">29</span><span>一只特立独行的猪，王小波</span></span> 这种中景是病态的，所以中国的城市化在打散传统关系的同时，很快把它扫进了历史的垃圾桶——虽然没有完全扫干净。<br><br>关于中景，上一代人面临的问题关于良心和胆量（你敢离开吗，离开之后你能忍住吗？），而我们面临的问题则是希望和诚实。我想，我们越来越能够忍受自己，但越来越不能忍受别人；我们太擅长把一切归为“关我屁事”和“关你屁事”；我们因为一点价值观的龃龉，就转过头去不看那些共享的部分。我们甘于孤独，是我们默认没有哪种共同生活值得花时间去培养和修补。<br><br>科技给予了我们拉黑的自由、退群的自由、逃离的自由。但重建需要更多耐心。我不想假装事情与己无关，我不想把洒脱当做一种优雅，我不想说先动情的人就输了。我的想法非常朴素：我需要别人，我也需要别人需要我。<br><br><br>（七）<br><br>谁都无法否认，我们处在深刻变动的历史周期里，单是前所未闻的事件就已令人目不暇接；但同样也不难想象，两百年后的《XX简史》里，我们这代人所过的所有生活也只会被概括成寥寥数语。<br><br>历史写作需要主旨提炼、高屋建瓴，谁都明白降维总是伴随着细节丢失：在这件事情上，我不会有情绪。只是一旦想到要面临这样的压缩，我就有点喘不上气来。统计学是诡谲的，最大公约数其实什么也不是。那些被平均、被代表的部分，才是真正的生活——是且仅是被抹平的日常波动，才能被称作真正的生活。<br><br>谁都无法否认，历史的“相变”时刻总是出现在结构性的失衡的节点上，这其中形势的因素占了很大比重。加州在美国历史上率先给予女性财产权，这并不是立法者良心发现，而是如果不这么做，移民潮带来的男性劳工将越来越成为不安定的因素，而本就极端的男女比例将进一步失衡<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-30\" aria-label=\"Reference 30\">30</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">30</span><span>Women in the California gold rush, Wikipedia</span></span>。废奴宣言来源于林肯道德信念，但更多来源于他的战争手段——解放政策是为了赢得战争，他说：“我最崇高的目标是拯救联邦，而不是要拯救或毁灭奴隶制度。”<br><br>但如果我们就此停下追问，认为历史的车轮在自顾自地向前转动，只是偶尔顺手纠正一些不公，随机选择一些幸运观众获得头彩成为英雄，则又掉入了鸟瞰式的陷阱：把一切都归结于机械化的结构，仿佛历史轨迹和人的能动性毫无关系。<br><br>一年前我曾在很长一段时间里被吓坏了。我想，如果真的活在历史的垃圾时间里，过一些注定被遗忘的日子，这是件怎样糟糕和扫兴的事情呀！但现在我想通了，历史书中懒得提及的有太多东西，所以历史的大部分本来都是垃圾时间。但这件事有它的积极意义。<br><br>这些不被记住的时间里承载的是不明朗的趋势，它代表着生活中滋长出的一些彼此矛盾的新生和苗头，它是多股正在运动的势力，它代表的不是命定的终局，而是“形成”本身。<br><br>我今年去过了三个新地方：夏威夷、萨克拉门托、新奥尔良。它们表面上只是一些分散的美国地名，在某本《美国简史》里，它们也许被概括为毫无关联的度假胜地、淘金热起点和爵士故乡。<br><br>但我发现这些地名里都藏有的褶皱，而这些褶皱可以被多项式展开。夏威夷是珍珠港袭击的震央，但也有美利坚最纯正花火大会；萨克拉门托用高速公路把城市与河岸隔开，仿佛害怕承认自己曾以水路为生；密西西比河下游的地形像一只鱼骨，那些从脊柱生出来的七百根骨刺状的公路正通往两百年前在此繁殖的七百个种植园，而当这些创伤退潮后，这片沃土依然挣扎，依然是美国最为贫困、空气污染最为严重的地区之一。<br><br>我在这些旅途中串珠成线地理解了很多事情：关于国家记忆如何被扭曲和消费，关于资本和开发计划如何碾断或遮掩城市的历史脉络，关于振聋发聩的进步条律如何系统性地受到堵塞，关于经济、阶级、文化如何在不同时间和空间里连续地成为依赖或者桎梏。<br><br>是的，在离地三千英尺的天空向下看，我们尽可以作一些决定论式的宣称，比如美洲之所以被殖民是因为它的大陆轴线是竖着长的 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-31\" aria-label=\"Reference 31\">31</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">31</span><span>枪炮、病菌与钢铁，贾德·戴蒙，1997</span></span>。但唯有真正走进街巷，看见剥落的墙体，闻到当地绝无仅有的空气，听到纪念碑和纪念碑之间废语般的杂讯，我们才能受到触动，才能真正理解这些褶皱的尺度。<br><br>历史的本质是渐进的。把这些褶皱烫平，它们就会展开成一段看不到尽头的长坡，而不是绊人的台阶——而在任何当下，我们确实处于长坡的起点。<br><br><br>（八）<br><br>我想，理解历史，主要是需要理解它的惯性。跨时代的事件不是无端出现，也不会在一朝一夕间改变所有人的生活。它们更像是港口的浮标：水手通过它们来定位，但海面以下，洋流从未停止。<br><br>1928年，《平等选举法》通过，英国女性获得投票权。但在此之前的很多日子里，在逐步推进的工业化和城市化中，妇女已经在工厂、医院和办公室里工作。她们越来越多地参与到社会生产关系中来，一点点让权利的适用范围逐渐扩大——法案只是迟到地给这条洋流命名而已。<br><br>但纸面的承诺并不代表皆大欢喜。女性的权益不因为拥有投票权而完全改善。同工不同酬、隐形的职业天花板、关于“女强人”和“好女人”的刻板印象……这是些时至今日也未完全解决的问题，而“女性主义”的定义本身，也在当下的舆论中从先锋身份折损为一种性别冲突的叙事。1928年是妇女权益运动一个醒目的节点，在它之前有一条很长的上坡路，在它之后同样也拖着漫长的尾巴。<br><br>1965年，《民权法案》废除种族隔离制度，很多发起抵制、静坐的，然后被殴打、被逮捕、被处刑的马丁路德金都没有活到这一天，但如果没有他们在数十年中前赴后继地组织和动员，种族问题从一开始就不会被提上议事日程。<br><br>在此之后，明面上的歧视不再被主流所允许，但可疑的街区划分、偏袒的贷款政策、司法量刑的差异仍然继续存在——这些后遗症还需要更多的马丁，而这些马丁可能不再有自己的名字。<br><br>所以，当我说“垃圾时间有它的积极意义”时，我并不是在等一个“形成”，然后期待它符合我的胃口。我希望自己采用的一种面向行动的视角：我想写的东西，是在那些尚未沉淀到历史的年份里的故事，因为人究竟在做什么，决定了“相变”时刻最终向哪一边倾斜。<br><br>（我想，福柯之所以想要对权力进行积极的解释，而非只是看作一种消极和压制，大概也是出于类似的能动性考虑。）<br><br>我这样写下去，是为了做一种细节速写，去对抗纯粹的鸟瞰式的叙事。因为如果只是作为一个记录者，这本身有一种消极的成分在里面——好像我只能躺在河床上，只顾着看粼粼流光从身上漂过去似的 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-32\" aria-label=\"Reference 32\">32</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">32</span><span>似水流年，王小波，1992</span></span>。说到底，我既然身处历史里，就不可能事不关己地宏观起来。<br><br>我想始终带以一个普通的人文主义者的视角来进行写作。这些观察或听闻中不可避免有一些值得同情、称赞、扼腕或唾骂的事情，对此我会报以鲜明的态度，但我不想局限在受害者的框架里，不想搞出二元分明的对立，不想讲英雄主义，或塑造些近似神明或恶魔的东西；我不想在故事的最后把一切盖棺在报应、物极必反、否极泰来，或者天道好还上面。<br><br>我想，历史总是记住方向和结构，但人是生活在土地上，而不是天空的弧度里。站在地面上，我们看到的是不稳定、不明朗、是善恶混杂的日常；而正是在这样的尺度下，“面向行动”要求我们保持敏感、拥抱触动、以及拒绝习以为常。<br><br>我想说自己写作是为了拒绝遗忘，但这又是一种英雄主义的说法。因此我只能说，我思故我在：这个“在”字不是“存在”，而是“应该在哪儿”的意思。<br><br><br>（九）致麦麦<br><br>麦麦<br>笑的时候要大声笑<br>麦麦<br>生活从来不在别处<br><br>麦麦<br>你的第一语言是四川话<br>来，跟我念<br>狗撵摩托<br>不懂科学<br><br>麦麦<br>悲伤的时候就把自己扎进土里<br>土地和星宿都不会说出安慰的话<br>但地是那么大<br>而天又是那么广<br><br>麦麦<br>灵魂也会积累脂肪<br>到那个时候人会打嗝也会放屁<br>更有可能对新生事物消化不良<br><br>麦麦<br>阳光从我身上犁过之后<br>皮肤上会有发红发热的小太阳<br>我过敏了 但这并不是全部——<br>麦麦<br>美可以让任何东西受孕<br>不要对她无动于衷<br><br>麦麦<br>太阳落山了就会冷<br>冷了就要加衣服<br>前一句我会对你说无数次<br>后一句你妈会对你说无数次<br>但再过三十年<br>这些话又要换你来说了<br><br>麦麦，你可能会去火星生活<br>但你不要忘记这个地球和所有生命<br>因为我们这儿有的<br>不仅是罪行，还有勇气、歌声和奇迹</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考资料\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考资料</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/elon-musk-doge-early-spending-dei-contracts-2025-1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DOGE announces results from its first 80 hours of work — and it&#x27;s way behind schedule</a>, Business Insider, Jan 24 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.huffpost.com/entry/trump-fires-10-percent-federal-workforce_n_68aca73de4b01646d84f8363\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Trump Has Forced Out Nearly 10% Of The Federal Workforce</a>, Huffpost, Aug 26, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/elon-musk-government-young-engineers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Young, Inexperienced Engineers Aiding Elon Musk’s Government Takeover</a>, Wired, Feb 02, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/sam-altman-openai-releases-make-him-feel-yc-founder-again-2025-4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman says OpenAI&#x27;s new releases make him feel like a &#x27;YC founder&#x27; building things in public all over again</a>, Business Insider, Apr 2, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span>2024年2月15日，发布视频模型Sora；当日晚些时候，谷歌发布Gemini 1.5 Pro。2024年5月13日，发布旗舰模型GPT-4o；次日，谷歌召开I/O 2024发布会……</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/sam-altman-misgivings-friendships-ai-personality-chatbots-openai-2023-10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman has &#x27;deep misgivings&#x27; about people becoming friends with AI</a>, Business Insider, Oct 18, 2023</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span><a href=\"https://www.cnbc.com/2025/10/15/altman-open-ai-moral-police-erotica-chatgpt.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman says OpenAI isn&#x27;t &#x27;moral police of the world&#x27; after erotica ChatGPT post blows up</a>, CNBC, Oct 15, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://fortune.com/2025/12/02/sam-altman-declares-code-red-google-gemini-ceo-sundar-pichai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman declares ‘Code Red’ as Google’s Gemini surges—three years after ChatGPT caused Google CEO Sundar Pichai to do the same</a>, Fortune, Dec 2, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span><a href=\"https://www.paulgraham.com/fundraising.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">A fundraising survival guide</a>, Paul Graham, Aug, 2008</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-10\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">10</span><span><a href=\"https://blog.samaltman.com/the-gentle-singularity\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Gentle Singularity</a>, Sam Altman, Jun 10, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-11\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">11</span><span><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/updating-restrictions-of-sales-to-unsupported-regions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Updating restrictions of sales to unsupported regions</a>, Anthropic, Sep 4, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-12\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">12</span><span><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/disrupting-AI-espionage\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Disrupting the first reported AI-orchestrated cyber espionage campaign</a>, Anthropic, Nov 13, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-13\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">13</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/whos-who-tech-leaders-attended-dinner-white-house-altman-pichai-2025-9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman, Tim Cook, and other tech leaders lauded Trump at a White House AI dinner</a>, Business Insider, Sep 4, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-14\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">14</span><span><a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/09/17/business/anthropic-warns-ai-could-soon-replace-jobs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI CEO says technology ‘moving very quickly,’ could soon replace more jobs</a>, CNN, Sep 17, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-15\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">15</span><span>告白，湊佳苗，2007</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-16\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">16</span><span><a href=\"https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20250907A06WMR00\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">去各地批发市场“打野”，成了00后的新型夜生活</a>，腾讯网，Sep 07, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-17\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">17</span><span>浪潮之巅，吴军，2011；硅谷之谜，吴军，2015</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-18\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">18</span><span>这出自一个刚刚从苹果跳槽去初创企业的同学之口，我觉得我在看很多东西的时候已经丧失了这种纯粹性。</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-19\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">19</span><span>指：摘水果，徒步，和玩狼人杀</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-20\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">20</span><span><a href=\"https://a16z.com/from-dorm-rooms-to-boardrooms-why-now-is-a-special-time-for-young-founders/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">From Dorm Rooms to Boardrooms: Why Now Is a Special Time for Young Founders</a>, a16z, Mar 21, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-21\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">21</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/sam-altman-openai-envies-college-dropouts-stanford-devday-2025-10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sam Altman Says He Envies Today&#x27;s College Dropouts</a>, Business Insider, Oct 07, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-22\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">22</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/silicon-valley-china-996-work-schedule/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Silicon Valley AI Startups Are Embracing China’s Controversial ‘996’ Work Schedule</a>, Wired, Jul 23, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-23\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">23</span><span><a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2025/08/05/three-weeks-after-acquiring-windsurf-cognition-offers-staff-the-exit-door/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Three weeks after acquiring Windsurf, Cognition offers staff the exit door</a>, TechCrunch, Aug 05, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-24\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">24</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BR1-JrGbwxY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Building Cluely: The Viral AI Startup that raised $15M in 10 Weeks w/ Roy Lee</a>, a16z YouTube, Jun 25, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-25\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">25</span><span>硅谷教父彼得·蒂尔支付20万美元让人辍学；Palantir开始直接招收高中生</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-26\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">26</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/silicon-valley-politics-shift/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">I Thought I Knew Silicon Valley. I Was Wrong</a>, Wired, Sep 22, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-27\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">27</span><span><a href=\"https://www.mercor.com/blog/series-c/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Unlocking Human Potential in the AI Economy</a>, Mercor, Oct 27, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-28\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">28</span><span><a href=\"https://www.businessinsider.com/mercor-cuts-contractors-meta-project-less-money-musen-nova-ai-2025-11\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An AI startup powering Meta and OpenAI cut thousands of workers — then offered them a similar project for less money</a>, Business Insider, Nov 12, 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-29\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">29</span><span>一只特立独行的猪，王小波</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-30\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">30</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Women_in_the_California_gold_rush?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Women in the California gold rush</a>, Wikipedia</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-31\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">31</span><span>枪炮、病菌与钢铁，贾德·戴蒙，1997</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-32\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">32</span><span>似水流年，王小波，1992</span></li></ol></section>","source":"./content/posts/2025-12-06_我们生活的故事05.md"},{"id":"2025-11-01_sacramentos-absent","date":"2025-11-01","title":"Sacramento’s Absent","titleZh":"萨克拉门托的缺席者","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"1.0 On the alluvial plain of the arid Central Valley, the Sacramento and American rivers collide, merge, and flow south, unfurling into a long, winding Y. California’s capital spreads along the eastern…","excerptZh":"1.0 在干燥的中央谷地的冲积平原上，萨克拉门托河和美国河相撞后合流南下，舒展成一个迤逦的Y字，加州首府就在这河流的东岸铺展开来。 可能是某个城市规划者…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2025-11-01_萨克拉门托的缺席者.jpg","tags":["日常记趣"],"tagsZh":["everyday chronicles"],"readingMin":10,"htmlEn":"<p><strong>1.0</strong><br><br>On the alluvial plain of the arid Central Valley, the Sacramento and American rivers collide, merge, and flow south, unfurling into a long, winding Y. California’s capital spreads along the eastern bank.<br><br>Perhaps some city planner suffered from biophobia. Interstate 5 runs north to south, hugging the river like a levee and earnestly advising the city’s residents to keep a respectful distance from the water. In any event, looking west from downtown Sacramento, an eight-lane artificial riverbed has replaced what should have been a beautiful waterfront. US 50, running east to west, is a length of rebar driven through the city’s spinal cord, brutally splitting it into northern and southern halves. The two freeways meet at the city’s navel, forming one enormous X-shaped bandage.<br><br>Why two strips? When the wound runs this deep, one bandage will not cover it.<br><br><strong>2.0</strong><br><br>Walking through downtown Sacramento, I felt I had gone back twenty years, to the era when Beijing was bidding for the Olympics and middle-school politics textbooks summed up the world as “one superpower and several major powers.” At the time, that one superpower stood unrivaled in its glory.<br><br>Sacramento presents a peculiar kind of modernity: broad roads, orderly blocks, towers everywhere, easy transportation. In the sunlight, its all-glass facades make an eloquent argument. But these towers were all built twenty years ago. Seen from the 2020s, they are gleaming and unmistakably out of date.<br><br>The so-called “agency towers” around the California State Capitol are almost all products of public-sector-led investment that began in the 1990s.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>List of tallest buildings in Sacramento, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span> Back then, people were full of confidence. They thought the city’s public sector would keep swelling forever—until the 2008 subprime mortgage crisis sent some ambitious types into retreat, freezing the skyline in the shape it has today.<br><br>Walking through downtown Sacramento, I felt I was in America’s Dongguan.<br><br>Two years earlier, on the evening of May 20—5/20, “I love you”—I had seen two skyscrapers filling the view in downtown Dongguan display slogans so absurd they left me speechless: “Warmly Celebrate World Metrology Day” and “Advertising Space Available.” At the time, I thought Dongguan had nothing but a presentable shell. Economic transition and industrial upgrading were a volcanic eruption; their ash froze Dongguan like Pompeii.<br><br>Sacramento’s volcanic ash, meanwhile, drifted down from two dark clouds: the contraction of the public sector and the exhaustion of its political narrative. After the financial crisis, the plans, projections, visions, and blueprints on which the city had lived disappeared. So did Sacramento’s spirit.<br><br>Walking through downtown Sacramento, with hardly another pedestrian in sight, I felt I was in California’s Xiong’an New Area. But I have never been to Xiong’an, so I hope I am wrong.<br><br><strong>3.0</strong><br><br>Forty miles outside the city, on the upper reaches of the American River, lies the place where gold was first discovered in California. Sacramento was therefore the starting point of the California Gold Rush. Adventurers came upriver from San Francisco and gathered here. The city grew into an inland port, and its teeming old district lingered on along the riverbank.<br><br>I used two phrases: <em>old district</em> and <em>lingered</em>. Neither is respectable language. Official documents from the 1950s used a different description: “one of the worst slum areas in the United States is on our own front doorstep”—a stain stuck to the city’s face, just steps from the Capitol.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento’s Redevelopment History, YouTube, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span><br><br>America was then in its golden age: the treasury was full, and the people looked eagerly upward. A small handful of overfed young people might have been making deafening rock music or preaching nihilism, but mainstream society paid them no mind. Progress, order, development—those were the serious business. So we all know what happened next.<br><br>Postwar Sacramento welcomed a grand redevelopment plan that would give it a new face.<br><br>Whether by design or not, the project included a north–south freeway built along the river, ideally placed to clear the old district efficiently off the waterfront. (Had the meddlesome President Kennedy not intervened, Interstate 5 would not have needed to make a special bend, preserving the tiny riverside historic quarter that sits there today like a specimen.) Most residents of the old district were ethnic minorities—Chinese, Japanese, Mexican, and Black—and modestly paid members of the working class. Yes, of course they opposed the plan. But theirs was not, after all, the mainstream view. So they protested, to no great consequence, and then scattered to the four winds. In an old documentary, countless men and women in brimmed hats appeared at a ceremony atop the ruins on the day ground was broken for the first federal building. Shovels in hand, they looked very much like a crowd of respectable working people.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><br><br>We have encountered a concept like this before: the whole-nation system. To some, immense top-down plans interfere too much with the market, distort organic demand, and are prone to stall, break loose, or seal themselves inside dangerous echo chambers.<br><br>In another context, there is a more refined phrase: public-sector-led investment. Which is to say, the state and capital make the money; vulnerable communities pay the price. Sacramento was the pioneer and paragon of this formulation. Its Redevelopment Agency created Tax Increment Financing (TIF)—using future increases in tax revenue as collateral and a source of repayment—a system that would later sweep across the United States and draw vast sums of investment. The redevelopment project also received the 1963 Ward Melville Gold Medal.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS, The Metropole, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span><br><br>Beneath Capitol Mall, where land values rose seventy-fivefold, Sacramento severed its own historical arteries and buried its once-diverse old district. This was not a bad idea. Some things do not bear close inspection. Everyone is happier when they disappear into dust and smoke.<br><br><strong>4.0</strong><br><br>When I walked into the Sacramento History Museum, the woman selling tickets at the counter asked me out of nowhere, “You came because you know we have more YouTube followers than any museum in the world, right?” I froze.<br><br>No, ma’am. We were just walking around the historic district. But since we were already at the door, we thought we might as well wander in.<br><br>I later looked at the museum’s account. Its most popular video was a short clip of an old man operating a historic printing press to make postcards. According to the woman, a staff member had started posting bits of daily museum life for fun during the pandemic, and then the account took off. She probably had no idea just how freakishly unpredictable virality is. These days, anything has a shot at five minutes of fame.<br><br>I say this because the museum is nowhere near as fascinating as the words <em>number one in the world</em> make it sound. It has no exhibition it can really show off. The two-story building divides its displays among three subjects: the transformation of the Campbell Soup cannery, the roots of Sacramento’s Mexican culture, and the Native peoples who once lived on this plain. At a brisk pace, half an hour is plenty.<br><br>The real history is outside the museum. Two hundred years ago, this was the terminus of the First Transcontinental Railroad. Chinese laborers made up 90 percent of the workers who built its 1,100-kilometer western portion; more than a thousand died building it.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>First Transcontinental Railroad, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span> Directly beneath the embankment where the line finally ended lay Sacramento’s buried Chinatown.<br><br>Of all this, you will not find a single word inside the history museum with more followers than any other museum in the world.<br><br><strong>5.0</strong><br><br>Two hundred years ago, Chinese adventurers landed in San Francisco, came to Sacramento to rest, and then went on to try their luck in the mines. These immigrants called San Francisco <em>Dai Fow</em>, the Big Port, and Sacramento <em>Yee Fow</em>, the Second Port. At its peak, 5,000 Chinese people lived here.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento’s Chinatown, Yee Fow, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></span><br><br>Today, downtown Sacramento still has something nominally called “Chinatown”: the Sacramento Chinatown Mall, itself a product of the 1960s redevelopment plan. Coming down from the elevated road along the river and heading into the city, you cannot miss it. Seven or eight buildings stand loosely scattered about, each wearing a Chinese-style roofline that is jarringly out of place. Walk in and you find a paifang inscribed “沙加缅度华埠”—Sacramento Chinatown. Its vermilion paint has faded somewhat, yet the gate still looks a little too new.<br><br>Farther in, you find something Thai called Lotus. Aside from the senior apartment building next door, it is the only place open. All the other buildings are locked tight, though they look like public facilities of some kind—clan halls, perhaps, or ancestral shrines. The whole district is empty of people, its doorways empty of footsteps.<br><br>Its emptiness does not make me lonely. A Chinatown artificially inseminated by Western taste and capital, it is only a rootless idol, a monument made entirely of form. It represents nothing, least of all the lives, memories, neighbors, and communities that were destroyed.<br><br>Its builders want to show you a harmless slice of history, one that displays the care and compensation those in power have extended to the Chinese community. In this slice, Chinese culture is vermilion lacquer, roof eaves, a paifang. Shadows move across it as if many people had passed through, but you cannot stop a single one. No one answers your mourning or your questions. Every blueprint and every imagining behind this slice was Made in America.<br><br>I thought of General Tso’s chicken, which I have eaten exactly once in my life, at Panda Express.<br><br>Many foreigners love Chinese food. They especially love General Tso’s chicken.<br><br><strong>6.0</strong><br><br>Sacramento is dormant now, but it has awakened several times before. With every awakening, the city’s map is redrawn and several more holes are punched through its history.<br><br>Discourse is power. The Sacramento History Museum can speak: it tells of the cannery, of Mexican culture, of Native peoples. Sacramento’s reconstructed Chinatown can speak: it tells of cultural autonomy, of Chinese–Western synthesis, of ethnic harmony. But if we listen closely, we can hear other, smaller voices—from the cracks in the reinforced-concrete foundations, from the city map’s strange shape, from the holes in history.<br><br>I know history has its own rhythm. I also know that, in the face of structural changes in the economy and capital, some will inevitably profit and others lose; some will be uprooted and others rise. But forgetting and choosing to forget are not the same thing.<br><br>I remain angry, not because I want to curse the chains of cause and effect or the net of power. I simply refuse to let those who were dispersed, buried, and spoken for by others fall silent.<br><br>I remain angry because too many plans insist on “rebirth” but never on what comes before it: first, someone has to kill a Jesus.<br><br>I remain angry because there are two kinds of Genesis: either creation from nothing or creation by forgetting those fires and that scorched earth. The first happened only once. The second will happen countless times.<br><br>Do not forget those fires, that scorched earth. Do not forget those whom history has made absent. Do not forget the holes in the city.</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考资料\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考资料</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings_in_Sacramento\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">List of tallest buildings in Sacramento</a>, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UEUNt6_oYtI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento’s Redevelopment History</a>, YouTube, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://themetropole.blog/2018/04/12/sacramento-city-of-redevelopments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS</a>, The Metropole, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E6%A2%9D%E6%A9%AB%E8%B2%AB%E5%A4%A7%E9%99%B8%E9%90%B5%E8%B7%AF#%E6%8B%9B%E8%81%98%E5%B7%A5%E4%BA%BA%E7%9A%84%E5%9B%B0%E5%A2%83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">First Transcontinental Railroad</a>, Wikipedia, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><a href=\"http://www.yeefow.com/past/1850.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento’s Chinatown</a>, Yee Fow, retrieved November 2, 2025.</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>1.0<br><br>在干燥的中央谷地的冲积平原上，萨克拉门托河和美国河相撞后合流南下，舒展成一个迤逦的Y字，加州首府就在这河流的东岸铺展开来。<br>可能是某个城市规划者患有自然恐惧症，由北至南的五号州际公路如堤坝一般紧贴河道而建，力劝该市居民对河流敬而远之。总之，从萨克拉门托中心城区向西眺望，双向八车道的人造河床代替了本该风景绮丽的水滨。而另一条东西走向的US-50高速公路如一根钢筋贯入这个城市的脊髓，将它生硬地拆分成南北两截。两条高速在城市的肚脐处交汇，形成一个巨大的X型创口贴。<br>为什么要贴两片？伤口太深的时候一个创口贴是遮不住的。<br><br>2.0<br><br>走在萨克拉门托的市中心，我感觉回到了二十年前，那时候北京正在申奥，中学政治对世界形势的概括是“一超多强”。而那个超级大国彼时正风光无两。<br>萨克拉门托展现出的是这样一种古怪的现代化：它道路宽阔，街区齐整；楼宇林立，交通便捷。日光下，全玻璃的外墙雄辩地矗立。但这些大厦全都是二十年前修好的。以20年代的眼光看，它们虽然光鲜，但又确实落伍。<br>萨克拉门托州议会附近的所谓“机关群”（agency towers），几乎都是来自上世纪90年代开始的公共部门驱动投资的结果。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>List of tallest buildings in Sacramento, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span> 那时人们信心满满，以为这个城市的公共行业会无止境地膨胀下去，直到08年的次贷危机让一些野心家偃旗息鼓，于是天际线就固定成了如今这个样子。<br><br>走在萨克拉门托的市中心，我感觉这里是美国的东莞。<br>因为两年前的5月20（“我爱你”）日晚上，我在东莞的市中心曾经见到过两栋充斥视野的摩天大楼打出令人哑然失笑的标语：“热烈庆祝世界计量日”，以及“广告位等待招商”。那时我觉得东莞只是空有个体面的躯壳。经济转型和产业升级就像一座火山喷发，它们撒下的灰尘让东莞像庞贝一样凝结。<br>而萨克拉门托的火山灰则是从公共部门的收缩和政治叙事的枯竭两朵乌云中飘下来的。金融危机后，它赖以生存的规划、展望、远景、蓝图消失了，萨克拉门托的心气也就不复存在了。<br><br>走在萨克拉门托行人稀稀的市中心，我感觉这里是加州的雄安，但我没去过雄安，所以我希望我的感觉是错的。<br><br>3.0<br><br>市区40英里外的美国河的上游是加州第一个发现金子的地方，所以萨克拉门托是加州淘金热的起点。那些冒险者从旧金山溯河而上来到这里聚集，这个城市就这样做为内陆港口生长起来，而它人来人往的老城区也就一直盘桓在河畔。<br>我用了两个词：老城区，盘桓。而这是一种不入流的说法。因为在50年代的官方的文件里它被描述成“一个令其颜面大失的贫民窟像污渍一样黏在了距离议会大厦只几步之遥的城市的面门上（one of the worst slum areas in the United States is on our own front doorstep adjacent to the seats of our city…）”。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento&#x27;s Redevelopment History, YouTube, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span><br>那时的美国正处在它的黄金年代：国库殷实，人民积极向上。虽然有一小撮吃得太饱的年轻人搞些让人耳聋的摇滚或鼓吹些虚无主义，但主流社会对此不以为意——进步、秩序、发展，这些才是正事。所以我们都知道接下来会发生什么。<br>二战后的萨克拉门托迎来了令它容颜换新的宏伟的再开发计划。<br><br>不知是否有意为之，开发项目中有一条依河而建纵贯南北的高速，刚好可以高效清理处在河滨的老城（要不是多事的肯尼迪总统出面干预，州际五号公路也不必特地拐个弯，保留下如今临河的一小块被当做标本的历史街区）。这旧城中的居民大多是少数族裔（华、日、墨、非）、薪资平平的工人阶级——是的，他们当然反对，但这毕竟不是主流民意。所以这些人无关宏旨地抗议了一下，最后也做鸟兽散了。在一段旧日的纪录片里，第一栋联邦大楼动土的那天，无数戴着西式礼帽的男女出现在废墟之上的奠基仪式中，他们手持铁铲，看上去很像一群体面的劳动人民。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><br><br>我们曾接触过这样一个概念：举国体制。对于某些人，自上而下的庞大计划会过度干预市场，扭曲自然需求，容易失速、容易脱缰、容易形成危险的回音壁。<br>而在另一个语境内，有一个更精巧的说法：公共部门驱动的投资，也就是说，钱是公家和资本赚的，代价是弱势群体担的。萨克拉门托就是这个提法的先行者和典范。当年的再开发署创造出了日后风靡美国的税收增额融资制度（TIF: Tax Increment Financing，用未来增加的税收收入作为保障和偿还来源）吸引了大量投资，而这个再开发项目也获得了1963年美国的Ward Melville金质奖章。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS, The Metropole, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span><br>在地价增长了75倍的州府大道下，萨克拉门托亲手斩断了自己的历史脉络，埋掉了它昔日文化多样的旧城。这不是一笔糟糕的主意。有些东西是不能细看的，让它们没入尘烟才皆大欢喜呢。<br><br>4.0<br><br>当我走进萨克拉门托历史博物馆的时候，柜台处卖票的大娘冷不丁地问了一句：你们是知道咱这博物馆是YouTube上粉丝最多的博物馆才来的吧？我愣住了。<br>不是的大姐，我们就是来历史街区走走，但都到门口了也就进来随便逛逛。<br>后来我去它的账号上看过，号里面最热门的是一个老爷子操纵老式油墨机印制明信片的短视频。据那个大娘说，疫情的时候有个工作人员拍些博物馆的日常发着玩儿，然后就火了。她大概是不知道这玩意儿能不能火真是邪乎得很，反正这年头啥事儿都有机会出名五分钟的样子。<br>我这样说是因为这个博物馆完全不像世界第一一样引人入胜。它并没有什么拿得出手的展览。这栋二层小楼里分展三个主题：Campbell罐头加工厂的变迁，萨克拉门托的墨西哥文化的根脉，以及早年在这片平原上生活的印第安人。走马观花的话，半个小时也就够了。<br>真正的历史在这个博物馆外面。两百年前，这里是美洲大陆第一条横贯铁路的终点站。修通这条铁路1100公里西段线工程的工人中90%都是华工（上千人在这条工程中死去） <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>第一条横贯大陆铁路, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span>；而这条终点站最终抵达的堤岸的正下方，正是萨克拉门托被埋葬的唐人街。<br>关于这一切，你在这个全世界粉丝数最多的历史博物馆里一个字也看不到。<br><br>5.0<br><br>两百年前，中国的冒险者从旧金山登陆，来到萨克拉门托休整，然后去矿区碰运气。这些中国移民因此把旧金山称为“大埠”，而萨克拉门托则是“二埠”。巅峰时期，5000华人在此居住。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento&#x27;s Chinatown, Yee Fow, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></span><br>今天，萨克拉门托市中心仍然有一个名义上的“中国城”，这也是在60年代的再开发项目里规划好的。从沿河的高架下来开往市区的路上，你不可能错过它。这里松松散散立着七八栋房子，每一栋都戴一个中式的檐顶，与周围的气氛格格不入。如果你走进它，你可以看到一个写着“沙加缅度华埠”牌坊，这个坊上的朱漆有些褪色，但还是有些太新了。<br>从这里走进去可以看见一件叫“莲花”的泰国菜，除了旁边的老年公寓，这也是这里唯一一家开门的店。其他的建筑都大门紧锁，但看样子像是家族会馆或者祠堂一样的公共设施。整个街区阒无人迹，门可罗雀。<br><br>我并不为它的冷清感到寂寞。因为作为被西方和资本的审美人工授精的唐人街，它只是一个无根的偶像，一个徒具形式的纪念碑。它不代表任何东西，尤其不代表那些被拆毁了的生活、记忆、街坊、社区。<br>因为建造者想要给你看这样一个无伤大雅的历史切片，这个切片体现着权力者对华人群体的关心和补偿；这个切片上中华文化就是朱漆、屋檐、牌坊；这个切片影影幢幢，好像有很多人走过，但你叫不住其中任何一个人，也没有任何人对你的凭吊和叩问给出回答。因为这个切片用到的图纸和想象全部都是Made in America。<br><br>我想起在这辈子唯一一次在熊猫快餐吃到的左宗棠鸡。<br>很多老外都喜欢中国菜，尤其喜欢左宗棠鸡。<br><br>6.0<br><br>萨克拉门托现在在休眠，但它曾经数度活跃过；而每活跃一次，这个城市的地图就要重新绘制，这个城市的历史就会被砸出几个窟窿。<br>话语即权力。萨克拉门托的历史博物馆会说话：它讲罐头厂、讲墨西哥、讲印第安；萨克拉门托重建的中国城会说话：它讲文化自治、讲中西合璧、讲民族和谐。但如果我们仔细听，我们还能听到另一些细小的声音，从那钢筋地基的缝隙里，从模样奇怪的城市地图里，从那历史的窟窿里。<br>我知道，历史有自己的节奏。我也知道，在经济和资本的结构性的变化面前，必然有人得利，有人失意；有人颠沛，有人腾达。但遗忘和选择遗忘终究不是一回事。<br><br>我保持愤怒，不是为了诅咒因果的锁链、权力的网缚，我只不愿那些被遣散被埋葬被代表的就此沉默。<br>我保持愤怒，是因为有太多计划都在强调“新生”，但不强调新生之前，首先要杀死一个耶稣。<br>我保持愤怒，是因为有两种创世纪：从无到有，或者忘记那些火和焦土。第一种创世纪只发生过一次，而第二种会发生无数次。<br>别忘记那些火，那些焦土。别忘记那些历史的缺席者，别忘记那些城市的窟窿。</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考资料\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考资料</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_buildings_in_Sacramento\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">List of tallest buildings in Sacramento</a>, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UEUNt6_oYtI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PBS Documentary: REPLACING THE PAST - Sacramento&#x27;s Redevelopment History</a>, YouTube, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://themetropole.blog/2018/04/12/sacramento-city-of-redevelopments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SACRAMENTO: CITY OF REDEVELOPMENTS</a>, The Metropole, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><a href=\"https://zh.wikipedia.org/zh-hans/%E7%AC%AC%E4%B8%80%E6%A2%9D%E6%A9%AB%E8%B2%AB%E5%A4%A7%E9%99%B8%E9%90%B5%E8%B7%AF#%E6%8B%9B%E8%81%98%E5%B7%A5%E4%BA%BA%E7%9A%84%E5%9B%B0%E5%A2%83\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">第一条横贯大陆铁路</a>, Wikipedia, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><a href=\"http://www.yeefow.com/past/1850.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">An Evidentiary Timeline on the History of Sacramento&#x27;s Chinatown</a>, Yee Fow, Retrieved Nov 2 2025</span></li></ol></section>","source":"./content/posts/2025-11-01_萨克拉门托的缺席者.md"},{"id":"2025-07-27_workplace-vultures","date":"2025-07-27","title":"Workplace Vultures","titleZh":"职场食腐鸟","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"Workplace Vultures When I started my first job at a private company, I imagined the business as a precision machine. It ran methodically, seemingly stateless. Within this rigorously ordered process, everyone worked…","excerptZh":"职场食腐鸟 我在私企打第一份工那会儿，只觉得业务是一个精密的机器，它按部就班运转，似乎没有任何状态（stateless）。在这套严密的工序之中，每个人都…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2025-07-27_职场食腐鸟.png","tags":["日常记趣"],"tagsZh":["everyday chronicles"],"readingMin":4,"htmlEn":"<h2>Workplace Vultures</h2>\n<p><br>When I started my first job at a private company, I imagined the business as a precision machine. It ran methodically, seemingly stateless. Within this rigorously ordered process, everyone worked hard, took pride in their profession, and never made mistakes.<br><br>A few months in, I began to get a clearer sense of how projects were progressing. I saw how growth and development set positions and roles in motion within the company, making it stateful. I began to understand how ambiguous a person&#x27;s position could be, and I was thrilled by the thought that, through their own growth, someone could shape both their career and the company&#x27;s product. But I still assumed that those already established in their positions had to be efficient and capable. Otherwise, it was hard to explain how they had managed to stay there.<br><br>Later still, I became increasingly puzzled by the quality of the work some colleagues delivered. At first, I simply thought they were slow or going about it the wrong way; eventually, I had to question their attitude and ability. Apparently, completing a task badly was acceptable too. I thought this was wrong because it betrayed all the expectations invested in the work.<br><br>Soon, I began to see the problems with my boss. First, I discovered his staggering ignorance of our professional field. Then I watched him steal another team&#x27;s work for his own reports and dress it up as &quot;leverage&quot; and &quot;impact&quot;—getting things done with limited resources. I learned that doing your utmost bore no causal relationship to any good outcome.<br><br>As growth slowed, I saw it all in my broader organization&#x27;s all-hands, in company meetings called by the CEO, and in every high-sounding announcement of a strategy, plan, or reorganization: strategies that plainly could not work; decisions made without any frontline feedback; reform frameworks designed to dodge the hard parts; grandiose evasions of serious problems; petty calculations over trifling gains; and the ugly preening of the faction that had won the political fight behind closed doors. I caught a glimpse of the pathology the entire system seemed incapable of exorcising: inertia, compromise, selfishness, rent-seekers, the culture of officialdom, nepotism, factional struggle, and a bloated organization too unwieldy to control. I hated discussions convened only to prove someone&#x27;s relevance, and questions prepared because the answers already existed. Most of all, I hated the furtive whispers labeled &quot;private meeting&quot; on the calendar: the carving up of fiefdoms and the trading of favors, safely out of everyone else&#x27;s hearing.<br><br>As the saying goes, if you can&#x27;t tell who the rookie is at a mahjong table, you&#x27;re the rookie. By then, I was certain: at last, I was no longer the rookie. At last, I was completely disenchanted with big corporations.<br>These companies live only to keep themselves alive. Most of the organizations within them exist not to serve customers but to reproduce themselves. They write their founding purpose into the vision statement, then turn around and never look at it again.<br><br>When discussing this common pathology of tech giants, the CEO of Surge AI once estimated that 90 percent of the employees at such companies were doing meaningless work. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>20VC: Scaling to $1BN+ in Revenue with No Funding: Surge AI, Apple Podcasts, July 21, 2025. Episode</span></span><br>I don&#x27;t know how radical that estimate is. Nor do I know how much human labor AI can replace. But I do know that growth can hide every problem. If it can&#x27;t, just double the growth rate again. This is the curse capitalism has left human society: without growth, people degenerate into vultures. They do not mind feeding on rotting flesh or their companions&#x27; corpses, or brawling among themselves without a shred of dignity even in front of fresh food.<br><br>A wage worker should live by this: &quot;When the state has the Way, be wise; when it lacks the Way, play the fool.&quot; Of course, opting out of wage labor is also a choice. But will AI bring us to an age when that choice is truly ours? Or are we moving farther away from such an age?</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><em>20VC: Scaling to $1BN+ in Revenue with No Funding: Surge AI</em>, Apple Podcasts, July 21, 2025. <a href=\"https://podcasts.apple.com/ca/podcast/20vc-scaling-to-%241bn-in-revenue-with-no-funding-surge/id958230465?i=1000718245009\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Episode</a></span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>职场食腐鸟<br><br>我在私企打第一份工那会儿，只觉得业务是一个精密的机器，它按部就班运转，似乎没有任何状态（stateless）。在这套严密的工序之中，每个人都勤奋做事，以自己的职业为傲，并且从不出差错。<br><br>几个月之后我开始看到关于项目进展的眉目，看到增长和发展如何在公司内带来职位和角色的流动（stateful），那时我开始认识到定位的模糊性，振奋于人可以通过自己的成长对自己的职业和公司的产品带来影响。但对于已经在既定位置上的人，我依然认为他们是高效、有能的，否则，很难解释他们怎样得以一直呆在那个位置。<br><br>再后来我逐渐诧异于某些同事项目交付的质量，我开头只是会觉得他们比较慢，或者方法不对；但后来我不得不怀疑起他们的工作态度和能力。——原来以一个不合格的方式完成任务也是可以接受的。那时我觉得这样不对，因为辜负了各种期待。<br><br>不久后我开始意识到自己老板的问题。我首先发现他在专业知识领域令人震惊的一无所知，然后又看到他如何窃取别组的成果写在自己的汇报里，并美其名曰：在有限资源下成事的”杠杆“和“影响力”。我发现尽心尽力不和任何好结局存在因果关系。<br><br>而随着增长放缓，我在大组的全员会上，在CEO召开的员工会议中，在那些冠冕堂皇的策略、计划、组织调整通告中，看到了明显行不通的战略、毫无基层反馈的决策、避重就轻的改革框架、堂皇的问题的回避、对蝇蝇小利的计较、以及闭门会议后的政治斗争胜者的弹冠相庆的丑态。我窥见整个系统的难以袚除的症结：惰性、妥协、自私、食利者、官场文化、裙带关系、派系斗争、尾大不掉的臃肿组织。我厌恶那些只为彰显存在感而被召开的讨论、因答案存在而准备的问题；我尤其厌恶那些藏在日程表里的被叫做“private meeting”的背人耳目的窃窃私语、版图瓜分和利益交换。<br><br>俗话说如果你在麻将桌上分不出谁是菜鸟，你就是那个菜鸟。那个时候我确信，我终于不是菜鸟，我终于对大企业彻底祛魅。<br>这些企业活着只是为了自己能够活下去。它里面的大部分组织不是服务于客户，而是服务于自我繁殖。他们把初心写进愿景，然后转过头再也不看它。<br><br>Surge AI的CEO谈到科技巨头的通病时曾说：“这些企业90%的员工做着毫无意义的工作。” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>20VC: Scaling to $1BN+ in Revenue with No Funding: Surge Al, Apple Podcast, 2025.07</span></span><br>这个估计有多激进我不知道，AI能代替人类工作到什么地步我也不知道，但我知道增长可以掩盖一切问题，如果不能，那就让增长率再涨一倍。因为资本主义留给人类社会的诅咒就是：没有增长的时候，人就会退化为食腐鸟。它们不介意吃烂掉的肉、同伴的尸体、或者在那些新鲜的食物前毫无尊严地群架。<br><br>打工人的态度应该“邦有道则知，邦无道则愚”，当然，不打工的选择也是有的。只是，AI会带给我们拥有这样选择的时代吗？或者我们离这样的时代反而更远？</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考资料\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考资料</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span>20VC: Scaling to $1BN+ in Revenue with No Funding: Surge Al, Apple Podcast, 2025.07</span></li></ol></section>","source":"./content/posts/2025-07-27_职场食腐鸟.md"},{"id":"2025-03-23_hawaii-has-no-anger","date":"2025-03-23","title":"Hawaiʻi Has No Anger","titleZh":"夏威夷没有愤怒","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"1.0 The USS Missouri, which missed the entire attack on Pearl Harbor, now lies at anchor in the harbor’s still waters. A little over a hundred meters off her bow rests the…","excerptZh":"1.0 错过了整个珍珠港事件的密苏里号战列舰如今停泊在风平浪静的内湾中，它伤亡最为惨烈的姊妹号亚利桑那的残骸就躺在它船首前方百余米处。 每隔一刻钟，向导…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2025-03-23_夏威夷没有愤怒.jpg","tags":["travel log","usa"],"tagsZh":["游记","美国"],"readingMin":10,"htmlEn":"<p><strong>1.0</strong><br><br>The USS Missouri, which missed the entire attack on Pearl Harbor, now lies at anchor in the harbor’s still waters. A little over a hundred meters off her bow rests the wreck of the Arizona, the fellow battleship that suffered the attack’s most devastating loss of life.<br><br>Every fifteen minutes, a guide leads another group of visitors aboard to walk the deck. We look up at the enormous gun barrels aimed into the sky, down at the deck boards once crossed by the Japanese surrender delegation, and over at a placard about a burial at sea.<br><br>—On April 11, 1945, during the Battle of Okinawa, Captain William M. Callaghan ordered his crew to bury a kamikaze pilot with full military honors. The pilot, widely believed to have been Petty Officer Second Class Setsuo Ishino, thus received the only known U.S. military funeral for a kamikaze pilot during World War II. Some say it was an act of humanity, respect, and honor between soldiers. I know another answer.<br><br>William Callaghan missed the entire attack on Pearl Harbor. William Callaghan had no anger.<br><br><br><br>After the air raid, the Pacific Fleet jumped at shadows: so many of these brown-skinned residents had Asian faces, and as many as 150,000 bore the suspicious taint of Japanese blood! Then came raids, arrests, internment camps, and sweeping martial law. If you looked Japanese, you were a “Jap.”<br><br>Hawaiʻi was only an American territory then, not a state—that would have to wait until 1959—but this did nothing to stop curfews and discrimination from spreading. These residents watched their towns become military bases, then found themselves bombed by Japan for reasons they could not understand; some were ultimately locked away in internment camps. How unfair. But what could they say? This was, after all, a war in which justice had to triumph over evil! As for why evil had appeared out of nowhere in the first place…<br><br>The residents of Hawaiʻi submitted to American rule. The residents of Hawaiʻi had no anger.<br><br><br><br>On March 9, 2025, Waikīkī Beach was livelier than usual. The 29th Honolulu Festival was in full swing. I just could not tell whether this was, as its organizers claimed, a festival celebrating the cultures of Hawaiʻi and the Pacific Rim,<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>Honolulu Festival. “About the Honolulu Festival.” Honolulu Festival</span></span> or simply a Japanese matsuri. I saw banners in English and Japanese, young girls dancing lightly to Japanese pop, mikoshi and parade floats, and, to close the festival, the Nagaoka Fireworks with their “appeal for peace.”<br><br>(Curiously, the Nagaoka Fireworks grew out of a festival begun in Nagaoka, Niigata, the year after Japan surrendered, in memory of those who died in World War II.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>Japan National Tourism Organization. “Nagaoka Festival Grand Fireworks Show.” Japan Travel</span></span> And Honolulu and Nagaoka just happen to be sister cities.)<br><br>Many people have much to say about advancing peace. But the perpetrator should never be the one preaching it to the victim, the invader to the invaded, the colonizer to the colonized. I wonder: the world’s most famous beach is only a twenty-minute drive from the most humiliating wound in American military history, and plenty of tourists shuttle between the two in a single day—what kind of harmony is this? How do people equally fluent in Japanese and English understand who they are?<br><br>I know someone will say that sometimes it is wiser not to look too closely. Let the past be past.<br><br>Indeed.<br><br>In any case, Hawaiʻi has no anger.<br><br><br><br><strong>2.0</strong><br><br>When Captain James Cook reached Hawaiʻi, the story goes, it was Makahiki, the season when Native Hawaiians celebrated Lono, the akua of agriculture, harvest, and peace. That coincidental login window led them to receive Captain Cook as a god.<br><br>They were less pleased when the captain appeared a second time. The festival was over; so were its rites and obligations. Why had he come back? And so Cook fell into the mortal world and was killed by Hawaiian warriors.<br><br>Cook was not Lono. But he came with a god’s power and a god’s punishment all the same. The epidemics that followed in his footsteps would kill nine-tenths of the Hawaiian people over the next century; the missionaries who came in his wake would raise the Christian cross at this crossroads of the Pacific.<br><br>Hawaiʻi had never shut itself away from the world. Its chiefs used Western weapons to win their wars of unification; a Hawaiian queen advanced Western faith and culture in place of traditional religion and thought. Yet all that keeping up with the times did little to free them from what had already been ordained. In 1893, plantation owners overthrew Queen Liliʻuokalani after she tried to secure greater power for Native Hawaiians, ending nearly a century of monarchical rule over the islands. Five years later, from Washington, D.C., eight thousand kilometers away, came the Newlands Resolution, declaring on behalf of everyone in Hawaiʻi: Hawaiʻi was American territory.<br><br>Then came mass immigration: Japan, China, Portugal, Korea, the Philippines… In the decades that followed, Hawaiʻi’s population more than doubled. Half a century later, it was the descendants of these immigrants who pushed America to admit Hawaiʻi as the fiftieth state. This annexed Pacific kingdom had finally found a place inside the Constitution.<br><br>By then, it was already difficult to say who counted as a “local” in Hawaiʻi.<br><br><br><br><strong>3.0</strong><br><br>According to an old legend, taking a rock from Hawaiʻi without permission will bring bad luck—unless, of course, you buy it from a local.<br><br>Meanwhile, tourists hike wherever they please on Haleakalā and watch the sun rise and set, though in Hawaiian culture the sacred summit was climbed for ceremony, not casual sightseeing.<br><br><br><br>From the fences drawn across them and the warning signs posted nearby, certain ponds and beaches are not to be approached lightly. Only locals may enter the water.<br><br>Meanwhile, tourists drink contentedly at a lūʻau and watch locals onstage perform exaggerated Polynesian war cries and grass-skirt dances.<br><br><br><br>According to the guidebooks, drivers on the Road to Hāna should watch for local traffic and promptly pull over to let it pass.<br><br>Meanwhile, the 2023 fire burned most of Lāhainā and destroyed local homes. Rebuilding has been slow, while the neighboring resorts remain packed and clamorous.<br><br><br><br>The flag inherited from the Hawaiian Kingdom snaps and struts all over these islands of the United States. Meanwhile, 160,000 new American tourists arrive every day, pulling out their sunglasses and aloha shirts as they land.<br><br>Who needs this kind of condescending respect? Who truly owns Hawaiʻi?<br><br><br><br><strong>4.0</strong><br><br>In the forest beyond Hāna, bamboo said to have been brought by Chinese laborers has shot up into a lush, riotous grove. In Lāhainā, the Indian banyan planted to mark the fiftieth anniversary of the first Protestant mission there still stands, casting its broad shade. At the windswept summit of a volcanic park, chukar partridges introduced from Asia peck at the gaps between parked cars. These introduced species live contentedly here. Sometimes they grow far too brazen: once guests, they take over as masters.<br><br>But capitalism is the true master here.<br><br>Thirty-seven American billionaires own private holdings amounting to more than five percent of all the land in the state.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Phoebe Liu. “Meet the Billionaires Buying Up Hawaii.” Forbes, February 18, 2024. Forbes China edition</span></span> Larry Ellison alone—the co-founder of Oracle—owns 98 percent of the land on Lānaʻi. (At sunset, seen from Maui’s western shore, Lānaʻi’s immense shadow looks like a mourning shroud laid across Lāhainā. What did Larry do after the fire? From what I can find in the public record, nothing.)<br><br>A century earlier, the five plantation conglomerates known as the Big Five held more than ten percent of all the land in Hawaiʻi between them. This was when the face of Hawaiʻi changed at breakneck speed: Native species and forest landscapes disappeared, replaced by sugarcane fields stretching to the horizon, pineapple plantations, cattle beyond counting… Only with the rise of tourism did people suddenly remember fragile ecosystems, rare species, and environmental conservation. Then golf courses arrived late, posing as the final victor, only to spread across hill after hill. Look down on Maui from an airplane today and the plains, half meadow and half desert, resemble wounds that stubbornly refuse to heal.<br><br>It is hard to call Hawaiʻi ravaged: life does not fail such abundant rain and sunlight.<br><br>Whatever the goddess Pele cannot save, Uncle Sam ravages all the more.<br><br><br><br><strong>5.0</strong><br><br>In one corner of the Sunday market beside the Honolulu Zoo, two men who looked like brothers had set up a canopy to sell handicrafts. Both were dark from the sun, with Asian features, and seemed to be in their fifties. The younger one sat on a makeshift chair polishing a wood carving. The older one, in flip-flops and a camouflage baseball—, waited on customers.<br><br>But there were no customers. At eight in the morning, the tourists’ snores had not yet risen into a din. Many of the vendors were still laying out their tables. Only thin morning light dripped through the gaps in the leaves.<br><br>The carvings they sold were not the interchangeable tiki figures so common in souvenir shops. Instead, they were all sorts of ornaments, unmistakably the handwork of a self-taught craftsman. One hanging decoration of flying pigs caught our attention. The three little wooden pigs were roughly carved and left entirely unpainted. Strung together and hung from the frame of the canopy, they looked like a wind chime. Behind each pig was a three-lobed cluster of bird feathers.<br><br>March is a windy month in Hawaiʻi. When the wind rose, the feathers began to turn. There was no bell anywhere on the ornament, and yet I could suddenly hear the clear, tinkling note of a wind chime.<br><br>We bought the crude little pigs. When the seller rose on tiptoe to take them down, I noticed that his eyes crossed slightly, making him look all the more guileless. As I write these words, the pigs are spinning before our window. March in New York is windy, too.<br><br>Whenever I pass them, I cannot help stopping to blow at them. As I watch their feathers rustle and turn, I feel a little stronger and a little moved.<br><br>I suppose I have said many bad things about Hawaiʻi. I thought that to visit in a vacationer’s frame of mind was to miss the point. But stay too long among these folds of history and double exposures of culture, and sooner or later you begin to swallow water and lose heat.<br><br>And perhaps Hawaiʻi has no anger for another reason: no matter how heavily the days wear on, there remains a way to live, small but still warm and strong. These ordinary things are what hold us up. They hold the Earth steady in its turning, too.</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span>Honolulu Festival. “About the Honolulu Festival.” <a href=\"https://www.honolulufestival.com/en/festival/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Honolulu Festival</a></span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>Japan National Tourism Organization. “Nagaoka Festival Grand Fireworks Show.” <a href=\"https://www.japan.travel/en/spot/256/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Japan Travel</a></span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span>Phoebe Liu. “Meet the Billionaires Buying Up Hawaii.” <em>Forbes</em>, February 18, 2024. <a href=\"https://www.forbeschina.com/city/67247\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Forbes China edition</a></span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p><strong>1.0</strong><br><br>错过了整个珍珠港事件的密苏里号战列舰如今停泊在风平浪静的内湾中，它伤亡最为惨烈的姊妹号亚利桑那的残骸就躺在它船首前方百余米处。<br><br>每隔一刻钟，向导带领新一批登船的游客在船舷上溜达：我们望向直指天空的巨大炮筒，俯看日本投降代表团踩过的船板，以及一个海葬仪式的示意牌。<br><br>——1945年4月11日的冲绳战役，舰长威廉·卡拉汉命令船员以军队的仪仗标准海葬了一个神风战机的驾驶员，于是石野节雄成为了二战中唯一一个被盟军举行军事葬礼的日本兵。有人说这代表人性、尊重和军人之间的敬意，而我知道另一个答案。<br><br>威廉·卡拉汉错过了整个珍珠港事件。威廉·卡拉汉没有愤怒。<br><br><br><br>空袭过后，太平洋舰队杯弓蛇影：这些棕色皮肤的居民好多都是亚洲面孔，其中多达十五万人携带可疑的日本血统！接下来就是搜捕、拘留、集中营、大规模的军事管制；如果你长得像日本人，那你就是日本佬（Japs）……<br><br>夏威夷当时仅仅是美国的领土而不是一个州（这要等到1959年），但这并不影响宵禁和歧视的蔓延。这些居民眼睁睁看着自己的镇子变成军事基地，然后莫名其妙被日军轰炸，最后被关押到集中营里。好不公平啊，但你能说什么呢？这毕竟是正义必须战胜邪恶的战争啊！至于为什么邪恶会不期而至……<br><br>夏威夷的居民服从美国的管理。夏威夷的居民没有愤怒。<br><br><br><br>2025年3月9日，威基基海滩比平日更为热烈。第29届檀香山节（Honolulu Festival ）声势浩大。我只是有些分不清这个庆典到底是如它官方所说——一个<a href=\"https://www.honolulufestival.com/en/festival/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">关于夏威夷和太平洋周边地区文化的节日</a>，或者根本就是一个日式的祭典。我看见的是英日双语的节日横幅，是年轻女孩们跟着日本流行音乐跳起的轻快舞蹈，是神轿和花车，是庆典最后“呼吁和平”的名为長岡花火大会焰火秀。<br><br>（有趣的是，長岡花火大会恰好是新潟縣長岡市在<a href=\"https://www.japan.travel/en/spot/256/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">日本投降后的第一年，为了纪念二战中死去的人们而开始的活动</a>。偏偏火奴鲁鲁和長岡又成了友好城市。）<br><br>关于促进和平，很多人都有很多话可以说，但就是不应该施难者对着受难者，侵略者对着被侵略者，殖民者对着被殖民者说。我很好奇，这个世界最著名的海滩距离美军最屈辱的伤疤不过20分钟车程，很多游客一天内在两个地方打来回，这种和谐到底算是怎么一回事？这些说着同样流利日语和英文的人们身份认同到底是什么样子？<br><br>我知道有人要说难得糊涂，过去的就让它过去吧。<br><br>确实如此。<br><br>反正夏威夷也没有愤怒。<br><br><br><br><strong>2.0</strong><br><br>詹姆斯·库克船长到达夏威夷时正值玛卡希基节（Makahiki），这正是当地土著庆祝洛诺（Lono）——农业、丰收与和平之神的时候。这巧合的登录节点让夏威夷人将库克船长奉为神明。<br><br>船长第二次出现时他们却不高兴了。节日和侍奉已经结束，他又为什么要回来？于是库克堕入凡间，被夏威夷战士杀死。<br><br>库克确实不是洛诺，但他一样拥有神的力量和神的惩罚：那些跟随他的脚步上岸的瘟疫和传教士们，在之后的一个世纪杀死了九成的夏威夷人，又在这个太平洋的十字路口升腾起基督的十字。<br><br>夏威夷人历史上没有闭关锁国：夏威夷的酋长们借助西方的武器赢的部落战争，夏威夷的女王推行西方的信仰和文化替代传统的宗教和观念，但这些与时俱进并没有让他们逃逸出更多的命中注定。1893年，试图为土著争取更多权力的女王被种植园主推翻，了结了这些岛屿近百年的君主统治；五年后，源自华盛顿特区的纽兰兹决议替所有8000公里外的夏威夷人做出声明：夏威夷是美国的领地。<br><br>然后它迎来了大规模的移民：日本，中国，葡萄牙，韩国，菲律宾……十余年间，夏威夷的人口暴涨一倍。而半个世纪后，正是这些移民的后代推动美国承认它成为美利坚合众国的第五十州。这个被吞并的太平洋王国，终于在宪法之内找到了位置。<br><br>只是这时候，你已经和难说清楚，谁才是夏威夷的“本地人”了。<br><br><br><br><strong>3.0</strong><br><br>根据古老的传说，私自带走夏威夷的石头会招来厄运，但如果从本地人手中买下的话就另当别论。<br><br>与此同时，游客在哈莱阿卡拉火山（Haleakalā）上任意徒步和观看日出日落，尽管在真正的夏威夷文化中，登上圣山的唯一理由是祭祀神灵。<br><br><br><br>根据一些拉上的围栏和竖起的警示，有些池塘或海滩不可贸然光顾，只有本地人可以下水游泳。<br><br>与此同时，游客在鲁奥宴会（Luau）上怡然饮酒，看着本地人在舞台上表演波利尼西亚夸张的战吼和草裙舞。<br><br><br><br>根据旅行手册的建议，哈纳之路上行车时应注意避让本地车辆，及时靠边让行。<br><br>与此同时，2023年拉海纳的大火烧毁了本地人百分之八十的居所，重建工作缓慢，但相邻的度假村人声鼎沸。<br><br><br><br>夏威夷王国的旗帜在合众国的岛屿上到处耀武扬威地猎猎作响。与此同时，每天都有16万新的美国游客拿出自己的墨镜和夏威夷衬衫登陆旅行。<br><br>谁需要居高临下的尊重？谁真正拥有夏威夷？<br><br><br><br><strong>4.0</strong><br><br>哈纳的原始森林里，中国劳工带来的竹苗闹腾得蹿成了一片郁郁的竹林。为纪念清教徒登陆50周年而种下的印度榕树屹立不倒荫庇一方。火山公园多风的山顶，自亚洲引进的石鸡在停车场的间隙低头觅食。这些外来物种在这里生活得怡然自得，有时过分嚣张，反客为主。<br><br>但资本主义才是这里真正的主人。<br><br><a href=\"https://www.forbeschina.com/city/67247\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">37个美国富豪拥有的私人领地超过全州土地面积的5%</a>，其中仅拉里·埃里森（甲骨文创始人）一人就拥有98%的拉奈岛。（夕照时分，从茂宜的西岸望去，拉奈岛巨大的阴影像给拉海纳披上一件丧衣。那场大火过后拉里做了什么呢？从公开资料看，他什么都没有做。）<br><br>而一个世纪前，五大种植业的卡特尔的土地面积合计<a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/share/67dee245-3504-8012-81d5-59cf1f10e879\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">超过总体的10%</a>。那是夏威夷风貌急剧变化的时期：本地物种和森林景观的消失，取而代之以一望无际的甘蔗田、菠萝农场、数不胜数的黄牛……直到旅游业的兴起，人们这才想起生态脆弱、珍稀物种、环境保护。于是高尔夫球场以最后的胜利者的姿势姗姗来迟，却开得漫山遍野。如今从飞机上俯瞰茂宜岛，那些半是草甸半是荒漠的平原像是固执的不肯愈合的创伤。<br><br>很难用疮痍来形容夏威夷，因为生命不会辜负那些丰沛的雨水和阳光。<br><br>只是，但凡佩蕾女神拯救不了的东西，山姆大叔就会变本加厉。<br><br><br><br><strong>5.0</strong><br><br>檀香山动物园旁边的周日集市的一个角落，看上去像兄弟的两人支着一个蓬子在售卖手工艺品。他们被日光晒得黝黑，亚洲面孔，似乎都是五十岁上下。弟弟坐在一把简陋的椅子上正在给一个木雕抛光，哥哥穿着拖鞋戴一顶迷彩的棒球招呼客人。<br><br>但并没有什么客人。清晨八点，游人的呼噜还没有变成喧嚣，集市里不少卖家还在布置自己的台面，只是晨光稀稀疏疏地从叶缝间滴落。<br><br>他们卖的木雕并不是纪念品店里面常常陈列的大同小异的萨满像，而一看便知是自学成才的匠人手工的各式装饰。我们被一个飞天小猪的吊饰吸引住了。这三只木头小猪被雕得很简陋，没有任何涂色，它们被线串成一串，像风铃一样被挂在蓬子的支架上。每只小猪的屁股后面都插着三叶鸟羽。<br><br>三月的夏威夷本是多风的时候。风起时，这些羽毛迎风转起来。吊饰上没有任何铃铛，而我却能恍然听见清脆的风铃声。<br><br>我们买下了这些粗糙的小猪。老板踮着脚去摘下它的时候，我才看出他有些对眼，因此也显出憨厚的样子。就在我写下这些文字的时候，这些小猪正在我们的窗前滴溜溜地转着，三月的纽约也是多风的。<br><br>我每次从小猪前面走过的时候，总忍不住停下来对着它们吹气，看着它们的羽毛簌簌转动，我都能获得一些力量和一些感动。<br><br>我想，我说了好多夏威夷的坏话。我以为以度假的心态去夏威夷是不得要领的。但呆在这些历史褶皱和文化叠影中太久，人总会呛水和失温。<br><br>夏威夷没有愤怒，也可以是因为就算日子再过沉重，仍然有一种渺小却不失温暖和力量的活法，正是这些平凡的东西支撑着我们，也支撑着地球平稳转动。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2025-03-23_夏威夷没有愤怒.md"},{"id":"2024-12-18_the-stories-we-live-04","date":"2024-12-18","title":"The Stories We Live 04","titleZh":"我们生活的故事04","subtitle":"Or: Look At That Gilded Sky","subtitleZh":"又：看吧，在那镀金的天空中","excerpt":"(Prologue) Several stands of cherry trees along my commute have burst into bloom—not in early spring, but at the beginning of autumn. Go ahead, then. Bloom. I know that when the howling…","excerptZh":"（序） 我通勤路上的几束樱树开花了。但这不是早春，而是初秋时候的事情。 你们尽管开好了，我知道在那呼呼的北风停歇之后，你们仍然会好好的。 但不要这样宣扬…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2024-12-18_我们生活的故事04.jpg","tags":["our stories"],"tagsZh":["我们生活的故事"],"readingMin":17,"htmlEn":"<p>(Prologue)<br><br>Several stands of cherry trees along my commute have burst into bloom—not in early spring, but at the beginning of autumn.<br>Go ahead, then. Bloom. I know that when the howling north wind has spent itself, you will still be all right.<br>But do not go around proclaiming: <em>We bloomed so early because we could already see spring on its way.</em><br><br>Spring will come in the end. But before the thaw, some of your sisters will die in the bitter cold and snow.<br>Please, let them endure the winter with dignity.<br><br><br>(I)<br><br>In September, a college classmate came to New York. The first thing he said when we met was, “There aren’t many people left I’ll have lunch with one-on-one. Do you realize what an honor this is?” Then he told me that two days earlier, he had been sitting in a private club high above Central Park, talking and laughing with the head of China’s best-known venture capital firm.<br><br>(We have known each other for thirteen years. We have rarely seen each other since graduation, and when we talk about our ideals, it is usually in brief, joking bursts. Those conversations remind me how alike we are in so many ways—and how decisively different.)<br><br>I was genuinely happy for him. It is rare for someone’s genius and ideals to align so neatly with both his passion and the needs of his time, and rarer still for people with resources to recognize them. I wondered whether I had anything worth sharing in return. But the only story I had from the past few years was that of an ordinary employee watching management pay, over and over, for its own mediocrity, vanity, and narrowness—the three always travel together—only to double down and grow worse.<br><br>So I thought: let us get back to robots.<br><br>During his PhD, he conceived a new paradigm for training them. As I understand it, the framework essentially moves real life into simulated environments, addressing the severe shortage of real-world data that so often constrains training. And because the virtual environment is sufficiently varied and realistic, a robot’s abilities need not remain confined to specific tasks; they can generalize.<br><br>He said he wanted to found an organization modeled on the early OpenAI, one unconcerned with short-term profit and focused, at least for the near future, on breakthroughs in foundational research. Then he talked about Musk and Lego, about how much he loved building a model from nothing, brick by brick. I shared his excitement. I could hear the puzzle coming together, piece by piece, over the past several months.<br><br>Geopolitics cannot be ignored. But in the longer view, the larger anxiety—and the prospect discussed far more often today—concerns AI’s impact on society’s productive capacity. Both grow from the same root. Together they lead us to the ultimate question humanity must answer: What is a human being?<br><br>(In 2016 I wrote, performing a kind of hardheaded clarity: “The only thing in humanity that can withstand the test is not the cowardly morality or the Babel of wisdom they spent millennia piling up, but the biological traits and conditioned reflexes inscribed in their genetic material at birth.” The next year, pleased with myself, I wrote: “Almost no one sees this fact: humanity is merely an intermediate link on the map of evolution, not its endpoint.” But I was blind then. I wanted to shock people because I was angry at the state of my own life.)<br><br>I asked him, “What do you make of the shock that is almost certain to come? A social structure like ours will never be truly ready for it.”<br><br>He said, “Whether or not the public accepts that reality, the door is bound to be forced open. Better I do it myself than leave it to someone else.”<br><br>I remembered many similar words.<br><br>I remembered a line Kobe Bryant once gave in an interview: somebody has to win, so why not me?<br><br>I remembered Musk being asked whether he had been afraid when he founded SpaceX. He went silent for a long time, then said: “Fuck that, let’s get it done.” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>Elon Musk: Fuck that, let&#x27;s get it done, 2021</span></span><br><br>Perhaps this is what pioneers are: immense in their ideals, pure in action, untouched by the drag of reality. <em>I gaze upon you with approval and sing your praises; red-hearted, I labor at full tilt—who the fuck cares.</em> <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>“Zhong San Returns from the Four Cleanups,” Nie Gannu, 1965</span></span><br><br>I admire them. But I want to become a different kind of person: someone devoted to closing the distance between frontier research and ordinary people. He does not want the locomotive accelerating day and night, nor does he want the people in the cars to believe that one g is all the world contains...<br><br>He wants to help these two worlds see each other. He wants each to understand, just a little, where the other stands.<br><br><br>(II)<br><br>Perhaps this is an escape. I often think about things that are not remotely mine to think about: the fate of humanity, the fate of the people, the fate of culture. I once assumed that all three advanced together. But when I looked at history in finer detail, I learned that, outside a few sweet intervals, they are often at odds. Now I feel myself suffocating. It is not yet a total hush, but all three seem to have entered a narrowing cave: the farther in they go, the harder it becomes to advance, and the torch is almost spent.<br><br>This is not to say that our age is especially terrible. It simply possesses enormous stability and enormous inertia. Until the moment it bolts, it follows a bad—though not quite intolerable—orbit, moving slowly, slowly toward a deep pit. This is easy to understand and easy to endure. The world has been at peace for so long that even when we know some places have been turned upside down, we can pretend nothing has happened as long as the upheaval has not reached us.<br><br>I remember suddenly finding myself surrounded by a cheerful group in a windmill village. They asked me to take a picture with them. They were playing a family game: find all the people on a list at the tourist site. A camera hung from my neck, so I counted as the photographer. Everyone beamed in the photograph. Their smiles had nothing to do with the darkening fate of Europe after the Nord Stream explosions.<br><br>I remember an Israeli flag hanging from the third floor of an apartment building in Amsterdam, while the resident directly above had hung a Palestinian flag of exactly the same size. The conflict in Gaza had been going on for a year. Everyone held fierce positions on it, yet pedestrians below still sat untroubled in cafés.<br><br>I remember a World War II museum documenting what happened to Dutch girls who had taken German soldiers as lovers: after the city was liberated, their heads were shaved and they were paraded through the streets. One protested: I was young and in my prime. I only wanted to find love. Besides, he was more considerate than the Dutch men...<br><br>Blindness is easy to choose. Modern life offers countless convenient ways to obtain small but timely pleasures, each one a dose of anesthetic. But I insist on being one who sees. I say this because I am restless. The more powerless I feel, the more I want to shout like those mistresses:<br><br><em>To think that at this point in the cycle of history, I should be young and strong!...</em> <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Gravity&#x27;s Rainbow, Thomas Pynchon, 1973 (the passage above is relayed through the Chinese source)</span></span><br><br><br>(III)<br><br>Two years ago, I was meek during the career-planning portion of my performance review. I studied my bosses’ paths to promotion. I drew inspiration from the words of industry thought leaders. I seriously considered what projects I might take on and what impact I could have on the company. Now I know it was all bullshit. I did good work and received good ratings, but no budget means no budget.<br><br>In fact, from the moment I entered the industry, I knew I had arrived in the afterglow of internet tech companies’ golden decade. I watched the people ahead of me eat the meat. I drank the water it had been blanched in, still carrying a trace of its flavor. I envied them and resented them. But when I looked at the people behind me, who did not even get the broth, I felt lucky.<br><br>Now the people who ate their fill, still greasy-lipped and glib-tongued, tell me that the meat will be eaten as usual, but there is no longer enough soup to go around.<br><br>Looking at those behind me, I cannot bring myself to smash the bowl in my hands. Looking at those ahead, I cannot swallow this thin soup.<br><br>I can always blame the times. But that would only expose me as a thoroughly vulgar, compromising member of the middle class.<br><br>This spring, I saw an essay on WeChat titled “How to Live Through History’s Garbage Time.” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>“The Garbage Time of History”, Qinghe Shezhang, December 2023</span></span> The phrase set off a summer of fierce debate and silencing. I said I agreed with it—not because I could verify the economic foundations it claimed, or the supposedly indisputable pattern it found recurring throughout history, but because it recognized how I felt. It gave me an excuse to lie flat. The despair I found in it was precisely the despair I felt in the face of my own powerlessness and cowardice.<br><br><br>(IV)<br><br>At the beginning of the year, I started leafing through <em>Our Generation</em>, a collection of photographs. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>Our Generation, Xiao Quan, 2021</span></span> Its subjects are figures from China’s artistic and literary circles of the 1980s and 1990s, people familiar to us, some still active today: Cui Jian, Zhang Yimou, Gu Cheng, Yu Hua, Sanmao... The book interested me because I was curious about the stories people lived through during China’s reform and opening up. I had seen plenty of historical facts, economic figures, policies, and entrepreneurs. But here were living people. Here were their lives. Art, after all, ought to represent living people...<br><br>Even before I opened the book, I had corrected some of my own shortsighted assumptions. When I was younger, I thought “arts people” were a different breed, that the arts were a special kind of industry. They are not. The arts have their political struggles too, their successions of power, patronage, and nepotism. If anything, the closed nature of the industry allows these things to proliferate more brazenly there than elsewhere.<br><br>But reform and opening up offered a singular case. During that window, the old forces had been broken apart, while new things grew on ground where settled rules had not yet reached and power structures were still waiting to take shape. (It was like America’s successive waves of immigration, and a little like AI today.)...<br><br>Chinese modern art, like China’s private enterprise, seems to have been born wild and gray. They did not so much compete with established crops for cultivated land as confront a stretch of wasteland. They needed sharp teeth and no regard for boundary stakes to bite nourishment out of that sharp-edged soil. But once they could descend into those shafts, once their eyes learned to open underground, they found thought without limit. At bottom, when something is being made from nothing, nothing is forbidden from happening.<br><br>I know it was a golden age. I know how suddenly those miraculous growth rates made people rich. But I do not know whether I should envy them. I do not know whether I would have flourished in that era.<br><br>It was like the day I visited a church. An arrow of light shot down through some opening. Standing in that blinding beam, I felt the air around me float weightlessly, struggling to fill the cavernous void. Then a statue of Jesus descended from the dome and blocked the light, and the interior came into focus. I could open my mouth and breathe again. I stared at the enormous silhouette of the cross, sharpened by the light, and suddenly understood how a faith that binds itself in a cocoon can also bring peace.<br><br>I had spent too long under discipline. Outside the bounds of order, I did not feel that my wings had been set free. I felt like a drenched chick, alone.<br><br>And even so, I somehow felt that I had always lived a pretty good life.<br><br><br>(V)<br><br>I remember an after-school Chinese literature class in high school. The teacher wrote on the blackboard:<br></p>\n<blockquote class=\"pull\"><p>Living<br><br>A net. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>Notes from the City of the Sun, Bei Dao</span></span></p></blockquote>\n<p><br>I was intoxicated by this one-word poem. But not with the intoxication that follows fine wine—with the short breath of someone choking on what he has swallowed whole. This was the fantasy of a boy whose longings had ripened too early, just as life first lifted her veil. (This happens often: we meet naked truth very early, but only many years later do we realize that all those shadows wandering the streets were garments it once wore.)<br><br>Foucault taught me to see the net. What is the net? Another name for power.<br><br>In Foucault’s account, the fear of darkness and opacity during the French Revolution inspired the dream of a transparent society: vision would meet no obstruction, and public opinion would be visible to itself. Such a society would prevent crime at its roots because, “by immersing people in a field of total visibility,” the opinions, observations, and discourse of others would restrain harmful acts. This utopian design spread outward through schools, barracks, hospitals, and police stations—all institutions capable of building records that could be traced back through a life. Gradually it produced modernity’s panoptic transparency, a society modeled on the Panopticon. Everyone assumes he is being watched and willingly becomes a watcher in turn. Power can thus extend itself ever farther at ever lower cost. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>Michel Foucault, Power/Knowledge: Selected Interviews and Other Writings, 1972–1977, “The Eye of Power” and “Two Lectures,” 1980</span></span><br><br>This theory moved me. It clarified many currents of history and many social phenomena, yet it offered no hope. Foucault says that “individuals are the vehicles of power, not its points of application.” This seems intended to console me: as a conduit of power, I have no reason to feel anything, just as soil and riverbeds feel nothing when rain seeps into the earth and runs through them.<br><br>Power is abstract. Life is not. I can name many actual people with the power to dramatically alter the course of my life. And I know that the circumstances of my life fall entirely outside their field of vision or concern.<br><br>Now, when I see the word “net,” I do not think about the circulation of power. I do not think of systemic laws, scientific despotism, or moral discipline. I see a moth beating its wings as spider silk draws tighter and tighter around it.<br><br><br>(VI)<br><br>Along Antwerp’s main canal stands the endlessly fascinating Red Star Line Museum. It occupies a converted warehouse, customs house, and quarantine station. A century ago, people came here to have their luggage disinfected, to bathe, and to undergo physical examinations by American doctors before boarding ships for the New World. The Red Star Line was the first commercial passenger route from continental Europe to North America. From its founding in 1873 until its bankruptcy during the Great Depression, it carried more than two million passengers across the Atlantic. Many were immigrants fleeing war and the Nazis, or simply trying their luck in the New World. Albert Einstein was among them. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>Red Star Line Museum, retrieved 2024</span></span><br><br>At the time, when an ordinary European family wanted to emigrate, it would first send its most adventurous member to North America. Once he had established himself and saved some money, he would bring the others over in batches. These advance migrants sent colorful postcards back to the Old World: magnificent Niagara Falls, traffic-clogged New York, teeming Atlantic City—or simply a ticket already bought and paid for.<br><br>The postcards shouted out a dream: <em>To pioneers with initiative and drive, America is a wonderland!</em> But the fortune seekers who had arrived first kept quiet about the sentence that came next: <em>America is a wonderland! An exceedingly dangerous wonderland...</em><br><br>I am sure not every passenger was glad to have made the crossing. But the fact remains: without the Red Star Line, America would have had no Manhattan Project, and Europe no cheap vacuum cleaners, <em>Playboy</em>, or Atlantic community. The Red Star Line was more than a vehicle. Nor can its meaning be contained within all the dignity and humiliation, hope and longing, timidity and resolve of the passengers aboard. The Red Star Line joined two continents. Perhaps not in the way that was best for either, but it joined them all the same.<br><br>Countries, politics, culture, AI—all need communication, just as people do! The preface to <em>Our Generation</em> says: Everything in human society exists as context for everything else. Longing for such an age, I wrote in the guestbook at the end of the exhibition:<br><br>May the Red Star truly connect the world.<br><br><br>(VII)<br><br>Starting in the summer, I spent a full four months reading Ilya Ehrenburg’s memoir, <em>People, Years, Life</em>. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>People, Years, Life, Ilya Ehrenburg, 1961 (the quoted passage above is translated from the Chinese edition)</span></span><br><br>The book runs to 1.38 million characters, yet it still feels impossibly compressed. To reflect nearly a century of history, so much feeling and so much time can only be compressed into a few scant words. We say “the Cold War”—a compact, almost weightless label. But it means most of a lifetime lived by many people under the weight of that atmosphere.<br><br>I could lavish praise on this book: I was humbled by the author’s vast learning, his “minimum program,” his honesty about bewilderment, the sustained ardor and courage with which he held to his ideals. But what moved me most was the heart behind it all. As he wrote of Lenin: “A rare humility, strength, and resolve—a resolve that neither excluded gentleness nor denied a profound respect for spiritual riches, for reason, and for art. This is humanism, true humanism.”<br><br>“Conscience” is just as slight on the page. Different eras make different demands of us, but to act on one’s conscience always takes an abundance of strength and an abundance of love. That strength and love are not there to steer the course of history, or to plant oneself like a tick mark on its timeline. They are there to break through the veil of indifference, to bring people from the same age and from different ages face to face, to let hope become contagious.<br><br>For the same reason, after watching <em>Her Story</em>, I took “decent, brave, and well-read” as a line of satire. It says that the people in China least bound by tradition—the group freest in mind and outlook, and most secure in their economic footing—have devoted all their attention to living elegantly and caring for themselves. People choose to make peace with the life they can see, but not to struggle against larger “structural problems.” It is a classic American Dream narrative: at the end of an individualized experience lies blindness to the duties an individual bears in society. It lacks action. It lacks a perspective capacious enough to include others. The question should not be “What is there to pity about me?” but “What can I do for someone worse off than I am?”<br><br>But I have no standing to criticize. I am the target of the satire myself. I wrote this whole long essay just to declare: My circumstances are not good, but I am fucking well-read!</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KoihlAl7ugQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Elon Musk: Fuck that, let&#x27;s get it done</a>, 2021</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>“Zhong San Returns from the Four Cleanups,” Nie Gannu, 1965</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><em>Gravity&#x27;s Rainbow</em>, Thomas Pynchon, 1973 (the passage above is relayed through the Chinese source)</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/China_irl/comments/1e5qn1j/%E5%8E%86%E5%8F%B2%E7%9A%84%E5%9E%83%E5%9C%BE%E6%97%B6%E9%97%B4/?rdt=34941\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“The Garbage Time of History”</a>, Qinghe Shezhang, December 2023</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><em>Our Generation</em>, Xiao Quan, 2021</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span><em>Notes from the City of the Sun</em>, Bei Dao</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span>Michel Foucault, <em>Power/Knowledge: Selected Interviews and Other Writings, 1972–1977</em>, “The Eye of Power” and “Two Lectures,” 1980</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://redstarline.be/en/content/welcome\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Red Star Line Museum</a>, retrieved 2024</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span><em>People, Years, Life</em>, Ilya Ehrenburg, 1961 (the quoted passage above is translated from the Chinese edition)</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>（序）<br><br>我通勤路上的几束樱树开花了。但这不是早春，而是初秋时候的事情。<br>你们尽管开好了，我知道在那呼呼的北风停歇之后，你们仍然会好好的。<br>但不要这样宣扬：我们开得这样早，是因为我们远远就预判了春天。<br><br>春天最终会来的。只是在解冻之前，你们的一些姊妹也会在严寒和风雪中死去。<br>请允许它们有尊严地捱过冬天吧。<br><br><br>（一）<br><br>9月的时候，我的大学同学来纽约。见面时他第一句话就说：能单独和我吃午饭的人不多了，你知道你现在面子有多大吗？然后他就提起他前日和中国最著名的风投机构的掌舵人在面朝中央公园的高层私人会所中谈笑风生的事。<br><br>（我俩认识13年了，毕业之后我们不常见面，也通常以戏谑的方式简洁地谈起理想；在这种交流中我感到我们在很多方面的相似和决定性的不同。）<br><br>我真正为他感到高兴，他的天才和理想正好对应上他的热情和时代的需要、能为有资源的人所赏识，而这不是常见的。我思索了一下自己是否有什么值得分享，但这些年我所经历的只是一个普通职员看到领导层为自己的庸碌、自负和狭隘（这三者总是携手前行）不断付出代价和变本加厉的故事。<br><br>于是我想，那我们还是说回机器人吧。<br><br>他在博士期间构想了一套关于机器人训练的全新范式，在我的理解中，这套框架大致是将现实生活搬入模拟的环境，这样就得以解决训练中经常被掣肘的现实数据严重不足的问题；同时，因为这个虚拟环境是足够多样、逼真的，因此机器人的能力就不会被限制于特定任务，从而实现一般化。<br><br>他说，他想创立的是一个类似早年OpenAI的不注重短期盈利的机构，至少在近期，专注于底层研究的突破。他接着说起马斯克和乐高，说他喜欢从无到有地垒砌一个模型的感觉。我和他一起兴奋，我能听到这几个月以来，拼图在一点点成型。<br><br>地缘政治是无法忽视的，但在远景上，更大的担忧和如今被讨论得更多的——AI对社会生产力的影响的展望——同根同源，那是人类必须回答的终极问题：人是什么？<br><br>（我在2016年故作清醒地写道：“人类唯一能经得住考验的东西，并不是他们花费千年的精力堆积起来的臆病的道德、智慧的巴别塔，而是在诞生之初就刻印在遗传物质中的生物性状和条件反射。”，后一年，我又沾沾自喜地说：“几乎没有人看清这个事实：人类仅仅是进化图谱上的中间一环而非终点。”但那时我只是个瞎子，我想语出惊人，是因为对自己生活的状况感到愤怒。）<br><br>我问他，你对几乎必然到来的冲击怎么看？现在的社会结构对这种东西可没有真正准备好的一天。<br><br>他说，无论大众是否接受这个现实，那扇门注定是要被推开的；与其让别人来，不如我自己来。<br><br>我想起来很多相似的话。<br><br>我想起科比采访时说：（比赛中）总会一方会赢的，那为什么不是我呢？<br><br>我想起马斯克被问到创立SpaceX的时候有没有许多担心的时候沉默许久，然后说：去他的，让我们来搞定他。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>Elon Musk: Fuck that, let’s get it done, 2021</span></span><br><br>开拓者也许就是这样的，理想宏大，行动则是不受现实拖拽的纯粹。青眼高歌望吾子，红心大干管他妈。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>《钟三四清归》，聂绀弩，1965</span></span><br><br>我欣赏他们，但我想成为另一种人。那种人致力于弥合前沿研究和普通大众的差异，他不希望火车头只是没日没夜地加速，也不希望车厢里的人认为1g的重力就是世界的全部……<br><br>他想努力让这两个世界相互看见，他想让他们稍微理解彼此的处境。<br><br><br>（二）<br><br>这也许是一种逃离。我常常思考一些根本轮不上我考虑的事情：人类的命运，人民的命运，文化的命运。我以前总以为，它们的改善相辅相成；但当我从更细节处看历史，我才知道，除了少数的甜蜜时期，这三者经常相互抵触。而如今我感到窒息，因为虽不至于万马齐喑，这三样事倒都像走入了一个逐渐收窄的山洞：入之愈深，其进愈难；而火且尽。<br><br>这并不是说我们的时代有多么糟糕，它只是有巨大的稳定和巨大的惯性，在突然脱缰之前，绕着糟糕但又没有那么糟糕轨道运行，慢慢地、慢慢地接近一个深坑。这是易于理解和便于忍受的。这个世界和平了那么久，所以哪怕我们知道某些地方已经天翻地覆，但既然没有倾覆到自己头上，也就还可以当做无事发生。<br><br>我想起我在风车村突然被一群快乐的人团团围住，问我是否愿意和他们合照。他们正进行一场家庭游戏：在景区找到列表中的人；而我脖子上挂着相机，就被当成了摄影师。照片里大家开心地笑，这些笑容和北溪爆炸后命运黯淡的欧洲没有一点关系。<br><br>我想起阿姆斯特丹一栋公寓的三楼挂出了以色列的国旗，而它楼上的住户以同样的尺寸挂着巴勒斯坦的。加沙的冲突已经过去一年，大家对此都有激烈的主张，但街上行人还是波澜不惊地坐在咖啡店里。<br><br>我想起二战纪念馆中记载城市解放后，和德国士兵相好的荷兰姑娘们被剃光头发游街示众的场景。她们中有人辩解：我正值芳龄，只是想寻找爱情啊。况且他比荷兰的男人们还要体贴……<br><br>想要当个盲人很容易，现代化的生活中有很多方便的手段获取渺小但及时的、麻醉药似的幸福。可我偏要当个见人。我这么说是因为我感到骚动。我越是无能为力，越是想像那些情妇们一样大喊：<br><br>在历史周期的这样一个时间里，我居然年轻力壮！…… <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>《万有引力之虹》，托马斯·品钦，1973</span></span><br><br><br>（三）<br><br>两年以前，在员工评审的职业规划环节，我唯唯诺诺。我看着我老板们的晋升轨迹，我从行业意见领袖的发言中获取灵感，我认真思考我能做什么项目，能给公司带来什么影响。但现在我知道这些东西都是狗屁。我完成很好的工作，得到良好的绩效，但没有预算就是没有预算。<br><br>其实我从入行就明白，我踩在了互联网科技公司黄金十年的余晖里。我看到前面的人吃肉。我喝那些焯水的有肉味儿的汤。我羡慕和怨愤。但是看着那些连汤也喝不到的后辈，我又感到侥幸。<br><br>现在那些因为大快朵颐而油嘴滑舌的人告诉我说，肉尽管要照常吃，但汤不够分了。<br><br>看着后辈，我舍不得砸下这手里的碗；望着前辈，我咽不下这寡淡的汤。<br><br>我尽可以推说这是时代的错误，但只能更加彰显一个中产阶级彻头彻尾的卑俗而妥协。<br><br>今年春天，我在朋友圈看到了“如何度过历史的垃圾时间” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>《历史的垃圾时间》，清和社长，2023.12</span></span> 的文章，这个说法在夏天的时候引发过大量讨论和噤声。我说我认同它，并不是因为我能验证它自称的经济学的根基或历史层面无可争议的重复性，而是它能共情我的感受，它给了我躺平的借口，而我从中读到的绝望正是我面对自身无力和怯懦时本身的绝望。<br><br><br>（四）<br><br>年初的时候，我开始翻看《我们这一代》<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>《我们这一代》，肖全，2021</span></span> 的影像集。这是些关于中国80，90年代的文艺届肖像，书中的人物是我们所熟悉的，甚至至今仍然在文艺界活跃的：崔健，张艺谋，顾城，余华，三毛……我对这册集子感兴趣，是因为我好奇中国改革开放那些年经历的故事。我看到过不少史实，经济数字，政策，企业家，但这里是活生生的人，是他们的生活，况且文艺本身就应该表现活生生的人……<br><br>在看这本书之前，我已纠正过自己的一些短见。早年我曾以为，“搞文艺的”是一群很不一样的人，文艺行业是一个特别的行业，但并不是这样。文艺界也存在政治斗争，权力交替，裙带关系；甚至因为其封闭性，它们只会比在其他行业里更加肆无忌惮地繁殖。<br><br>但改革开放是这样一个独特的样本，在那个时间窗口里，旧的势力被打碎了，新的东西又生长在法外之地，而法外之地的权力结构还在等待成型（它像美国历次的移民潮，也有些像如今的AI）……<br><br>中国的现代艺术看上去和中国的民营企业一样，出生是草莽和灰色的。他们不是在和已有的作物争夺田地，而主要是面对一片荒土。他们要有锋利的牙口，他们要不顾那些界桩，才能从这些尖锐的土壤中嚼出营养来。但一旦能下到那些井下去，一旦他们适应在那种环境中睁开眼，他们就能从中找到无限的思想，因为从根本上说，从无到有的过程里就是不禁止任何事情发生。<br><br>我知道那是黄金时代，我知道那奇迹般的增长率让人们倏忽间富足了多少。可我不知道我是否应该羡慕他们，我也不知道我在那个年代能否大展身手。<br><br>就像那天我在一座教堂里面参观，利箭一样的光线从某处的开口射下。我身处那令人目眩的日光中，只觉得周围空气失重地飘浮，力图填满这空旷的虚无。这时耶稣的雕像从穹顶降落，他挡住了那片光，于是室内变得清晰起来。我变得可以张口呼吸，我望着那个被光打亮的巨大十字剪影，突然明白作茧自缚的信仰为什么也能让人心安。<br><br>我在规训中呆得太久了，脱离了秩序我不是感到被解放了翅膀而是像一只落单的淋湿的小鸡。<br><br>但就算这样我居然觉得自己一直过得不错。<br><br><br>（五）<br><br>记忆中，高中某节语文课外班里，我看到老师在黑板上写下：<br><br>生活 网。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>《太阳城札记》，北岛</span></span><br><br>我为这一个字的短诗陶醉了。但不是饮罢佳酿的陶醉，而是囫囵吞枣时被噎住的短气。那是生活刚刚向我揭开她的面纱时愿望早熟的少年的想象。（时常有这样的事，我们和赤裸的真理很早打上照面，但很多年后才反应过来，那许多在街上到处溜达的影子，原来都是它披过的各式的外衣。）<br><br>福柯让我看见了网。什么是网？网是权力的别名。<br><br>在福柯的理论中，法国大革命时期对黑暗和不透明的恐惧，促使人们梦想一个“视觉不受任何阻碍，公众的观点相互可见”的社会，这个社会从根本上避免犯罪，因为“通过把人置于完全可视的空间，这样他们的观点、思考和有关他人的话语就可以避免导致有害的行为”。这乌托邦式构想，从可以建立追溯档案的学校、兵营、医院、警察局扩散开来，逐渐形成“圆形监狱”似的全景式透明的现代社会，每个人都默认自己被监视，也自觉地成为监视者，于是“权力以越来越小的代价得到最广泛的实施”。 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>《权力的眼睛》，米歇尔·福柯，2021</span></span><br><br>这套理论很打动我，它阐明了很多历史脉络和社会现象，但就是不带给人任何希望。福柯说“个人是权力的运载工具，而不是权力的作用点”，这似乎是在宽慰我说，作为一个权力的通道，我犯不上产生什么情绪，就像雨水渗入泥土流经河床的时候，泥土和河床不会产生任何情绪一样。<br><br>权力是抽象的，但生活与此相反，我能数出来很多能剧烈影响我命途的具体的人。我也确实知道我的生活境况如何根本不在他们的视距或关心之内。<br><br>我现在看到“网”的时候，我不是在想权力如何流通，不是想系统法则、科学专制、道德规训，我想到的是一只被蛛丝裹得越来越紧的扑棱的蛾子。<br><br><br>（六）<br><br>安特卫普主运河沿岸有个特别有意思的“红星号”博物馆。这个博物馆由仓库、海关和检疫所改建而成。一个世纪以前，人们在这里消毒行李，洗澡和通过美国医生的身体检查，然后登船前往新大陆。红星号是欧洲大陆通向北美的第一条商业客运航线，从1873年建立到大萧条时破产，它一共运载了两百多万乘客到达北美，这些乘客中相当部分是躲避战乱、纳粹以及去新大陆碰运气的移民：爱因斯坦也是其中之一。<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>Red Star Line Museum, retrieved 2024</span></span><br><br>当时，如果一个普通欧洲家庭想要移民，他们会先派出家族内最具冒险精神的人前往北美。在他站稳脚跟并拥有积蓄后，再将成员分批接过去。这些先遣者向旧大陆寄来五颜六色的明信片：壮丽的尼亚加拉、车水马龙的纽约、人流熙熙的大西洋城——或者干脆就是买好的船票。<br><br>这些明信片嚷嚷着一个梦想：在具有主动和进取精神的拓荒者眼里，美国就是仙境！但提前抵达的投机者把后面那句现实藏住不说：美国是仙境！是极其危险的仙境……<br><br>我相信不是所有乘客都对他们参与了航行感到庆幸，但事实就是，如果没有红星号，美国就没有曼哈顿计划，而欧洲就不会有廉价吸尘器、花花公子和大西洋共同体。红星号并不只是一个运载工具，也并不局限于船上乘客的所有尊严与屈辱，希冀与眷恋，畏缩与决绝。红星号将两个大陆联结在一起——尽管不是以对双方都最好的方式，但毕竟还是联结在一起。<br><br>国家、政治、文化、AI都需要沟通，就像人需要沟通一样！《我们这一代》序言中写道：人类社会的一切，不过互为语境。我怀着对这种时代的憧憬，在展览最后的留言区写下：<br><br>愿红星能真正地连结世界。<br><br><br>（七）<br><br>从夏天开始，我用满四个月的时间读完爱伦堡的回忆录：《人，岁月，生活》<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>《人，岁月，生活》，伊利亚·爱伦堡，1961</span></span>。<br><br>这册书已经有138万字，但我仍然觉得它密度太大。作为对近一个世纪的历史的反映，太多情感和时间只能被高度压缩成聊聊数语。我们说“冷战”，这是轻巧的两个字，但它意味着许多人在那种沉重氛围中活过的大半辈子。<br><br>我可以对这本书说太多赞美，我折服于作者的博闻强识，他的“最低纲领派”，他对迷惘的坦诚，他怀揣理想的持之以恒的热忱和勇气，但最让人感动的是那颗心。就像作者在评价列宁时说：“罕见的谦虚、力量以及决心，这种决心既不排除温和，也不否定对精神财富、对理性、对艺术深深的尊敬，——这才是人道精神，真正的人道精神。“<br><br>良心也只有两个字。不同时代对我们会提出不同的要求，但践行良心始终意味着丰沛的力量和丰沛的爱。这些力量和爱不是为了左右历史的进程，不是为了把自己像刻度一样立在时间轴上，而是为了突破漠不关心的帷幔，为了让同时代和不同时代的人打上照面，为了让他人感染希望。<br><br>出于相同原因，在看完《好东西》后，我把“正直勇敢有阅读量”理解为一句讽刺的话。这是说中国相对被传统限制最少，思想见识和经济基础都最为自由的一类人把所有心思都用在了优雅生活和关心自己上。人们选择和看得见的生活做和解，但不和更大的“结构性问题”做抗争。这是典型的“美国梦”式叙事：在个人化经历的尽头是对个体在社会上担任的职责的视而不见。缺乏行动、缺乏囊括他人的视角——不应该讲“我有什么可怜的”，而应该问“我能帮到比我可怜的人什么”。<br><br>但我没有任何立场批评，因为我自己就是被讽刺的对象。我写了一大篇文字就是在为自己声明：我处境不佳，但我有他妈的阅读量！</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考资料\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考资料</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KoihlAl7ugQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Elon Musk: Fuck that, let’s get it done</a>, 2021</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>《钟三四清归》，聂绀弩，1965</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span>《万有引力之虹》，托马斯·品钦，1973</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span>《<a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/China_irl/comments/1e5qn1j/%E5%8E%86%E5%8F%B2%E7%9A%84%E5%9E%83%E5%9C%BE%E6%97%B6%E9%97%B4/?rdt=34941\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">历史的垃圾时间</a>》，清和社长，2023.12</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span>《我们这一代》，肖全，2021</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span>《太阳城札记》，北岛</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span>《权力的眼睛》，米歇尔·福柯，2021</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://redstarline.be/en/content/welcome\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Red Star Line Museum</a>, retrieved 2024</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span>《人，岁月，生活》，伊利亚·爱伦堡，1961</span></li></ol></section>","source":"./content/posts/2024-12-18_我们生活的故事04.md"},{"id":"2024-10-23_the-seemingly-innocent","date":"2024-10-23","title":"The Seemingly Innocent","titleZh":"那些看似无辜的","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"More than a century ago, Romain Rolland opened Beethoven, the first of his Lives of Illustrious Men, with these words: “The air is heavy around us. Old Europe lies numb in a…","excerptZh":"一个多世纪以前，罗曼·罗兰在《名人传》开篇写道： “我们周围的空气多沉重！老大的欧罗巴在重浊与腐败的气氛中昏迷不醒。鄙俗的物质主义镇压着思想，阻挠着政府…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2024-10-23_那些看似无辜的.jpg","tags":["travel log","europe"],"tagsZh":["游记","欧洲"],"readingMin":5,"htmlEn":"<p>More than a century ago, Romain Rolland opened <em>Beethoven</em>, the first of his <em>Lives of Illustrious Men</em>, with these words:<br></p>\n<blockquote class=\"pull\"><p>“The air is heavy around us. Old Europe lies numb in a heavy, tainted atmosphere. A base materialism, devoid of grandeur, weighs on thought and obstructs the action of governments and individuals. The world is dying of suffocation in its cautious and abject egoism.” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>Romain Rolland, Vie de Beethoven, January 1903. French text. Quotation translated from the French.</span></span></p></blockquote>\n<p><br>Over the next half-century, Europe endured the devastation of two world wars, then became the front line of a bipolar world, swept along by a current—and an overt grand design—more ambitious even than the Marshall Plan... Europe was not brought to its knees, but neither did it ever fully recover the influence it had wielded at its height.<br><br>More recently, I have watched it hem and haw and change the subject after the Nord Stream pipeline explosions. I have watched it make moves in two regional conflicts that do not entirely serve its own interests... Europe has lost more than influence. It is surrendering its dignity.<br><br>I do not say this with regret. The heroes in <em>Lives of Illustrious Men</em> exhilarated me, but traveling through Europe again nine years later, I also watched with great interest as the anguish that drove Romain Rolland to write failed to make the continent any more clearheaded.<br><br><br><br>I first heard of Ghent on social media: in the Middle Ages, it was Europe’s second-largest city after Paris. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>Visit Gent, “Ghent Through the Centuries.” City history.</span></span> Walking through it, I was astonished by how completely its historic architecture had been preserved, and by the sheer scale of it all. Its accumulated history spills from the glass like wine. You can smell the years in every statue along its streets, every paving stone, every bell. Pointing to a tavern—Café Den Turk—a local guide told me that its owner had once joined the Crusades.<br><br>Ghent once teemed with people, and many of its buildings were erected by guilds of boatmen, tanners, tax collectors, textile workers... This rising bourgeoisie flourished in a commercial powerhouse that had once towered over Europe. Then came the Revolt of Ghent.<br><br><br><br>The revolt began when the citizens of Ghent rose up against Charles V—Holy Roman Emperor and himself a native of Ghent—over new taxes imposed to expand his wars. To put down the revolt, Charles V spent nearly half a year making his way from Spain to Ghent, gathering five thousand soldiers along the way.<br><br>The people of Ghent did not resist... Charles V made his triumphant entrance, settled into his own palace, and set about dealing with the rebels.<br><br>In the end, the people of Ghent were ordered to dress in robes and walk barefoot, nooses around their necks, from the Town Hall to Charles’s palace to beg forgiveness. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Visit Gent, “Procession of Emperor Charles and the Noose-Bearers.” Official procession history.</span></span><br><br>Ghent did not forget. To make sure it never would, the city even named a locally brewed beer after the episode. Its residents call themselves <em>Stroppendragers</em>—noose-bearers—and every year, during the local festivities, they reenact the scene in celebration of their ancestors’ spirit of resistance.<br><br><br><br>By the time the guide finished, I felt as though I no longer recognized the word resistance. When I think of resistance, I think of martyrs, I think of ideals, I think of heroes. I never think of beer...<br><br>Half out of curiosity and half to put my hunch to the test, I asked the guide why, despite World War II, Ghent’s buildings remained so intact.<br><br>Because Ghent was not strategically important, he said. It was used as a German logistics base. Between occupation and liberation, no serious armed conflict took place there.<br><br>That settled it for me. <em>People’s war</em> does, after all, have its limits.<br><br><br><br>The handsome gateway of Ghent’s Old Fish Market bears a male figure on one side and a female figure on the other, representing the Scheldt and the Lys, the two rivers that flow through the city. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>Visit Gent, “Old Fish Market.” Official site history.</span></span> The man, I was told, had been modeled on a local playboy at the time of its construction.<br><br>If your ancestors lived with this kind of irreverence, you, too, would find it hard to take many things too seriously.<br><br><br><br>Ilya Ehrenburg wrote in his memoirs: “In 1950, Europe lived a poor, anxious, hectic life. The workers’ battle in France and Italy had already been lost in 1947, but everyone felt the battle was still going on.” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>Ilya Ehrenburg, People, Years, Life, Book VI. Russian text. Quotation translated from the Russian.</span></span><br><br>Europeans do not know how to make revolution. Even when they do, it is a leisurely revolution. Europeans are too good at living. They start drinking at breakfast. Even the little convenience stores in their train stations carry two or three kinds of buttermilk and bread baked on the premises. Walk down an alley and the rooms along the street are warmly furnished, their dining tables often crowded with brilliant flowers... For Europeans, so many things matter just a little more than the future.<br><br>The guide pointed to a building painted yellow. It was the only wooden building left in Ghent’s old city, he said. A local legend had it that the lady of the house once swore to her husband, as he set off to war, that she would not change her clothes until he returned. He was gone so long that the house slowly took on the color of the underwear worn by this lady, who had remained so steadfastly true to her original vow.<br><br>In that indecent joke, I saw Ghent’s long yet lighthearted history. Romain Rolland’s admonition, by contrast, was cast in such heavy words. He was the true anomaly.</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span>Romain Rolland, <em>Vie de Beethoven</em>, January 1903. <a href=\"https://www.musicologie.org/theses/vie_de_beethoven.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">French text</a>. Quotation translated from the French.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>Visit Gent, “Ghent Through the Centuries.” <a href=\"https://visit.gent.be/en/good-know/practical-information/why-ghent/ghent-over-centuries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">City history</a>.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span>Visit Gent, “Procession of Emperor Charles and the Noose-Bearers.” <a href=\"https://visit.gent.be/en/calendar/procession-emperor-charles-and-noose-bearers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official procession history</a>.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span>Visit Gent, “Old Fish Market.” <a href=\"https://visit.gent.be/en/see-do/old-fish-market\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Official site history</a>.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span>Ilya Ehrenburg, <em>People, Years, Life</em>, Book VI. <a href=\"https://www.belousenko.com/books/Erenburg/erenburg_memoirs_6.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Russian text</a>. Quotation translated from the Russian.</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>一个多世纪以前，罗曼·罗兰在《名人传》开篇写道：<br><br>“我们周围的空气多沉重！老大的欧罗巴在重浊与腐败的气氛中昏迷不醒。鄙俗的物质主义镇压着思想，阻挠着政府与个人的行动。社会在乖巧卑下的自私自利中窒息以死。“<br><br>自那之后的半个世纪，欧洲历经两次世界大战的浩劫，又在比马歇尔计划更为宏大的趋势和阳谋中，成为世界两极对抗的前线……欧洲并没有一蹶不振，但也确实再没完全回复它最为鼎盛的影响力。<br><br>而最近，我看着它在北溪管道事件后的支支吾吾顾左右而言他，看着它在两场区域冲突中做出并不完全符合自身利益的举措……欧洲不仅是失去了影响力，它根本是在放弃自己的尊严。<br><br>我并不是带以遗憾的口气说这句话的；我振奋于《名人传》中的英雄故事，但九年之后再次游历欧洲，我也饶有兴致地看着罗曼·罗兰痛切心扉的写作动机并没有让这片大陆变得更加清醒。<br><br><br><br>我是从社交媒体上才知道根特——中世纪欧洲仅次于巴黎的第二大城市。漫步其中，我惊讶于它对古代建筑保存之完整和其规模本身的宏大。它的累累历史像杯中满溢而出的葡萄酒，你可以从这些街衢的任何一个雕塑，任何一个石板，任何一口吊钟嗅到充满年头的气息：当地的向导指着一个酒馆（Café Den Turk）说，它的老板曾经参加了十字军东征。<br><br>根特在历史上人潮聚集，其许多建筑都是当年由公会（Guild）出资修建——船夫的，皮匠的，收税官的，纺织业的……这些新兴资产阶级在这个曾睥睨欧洲的繁华商业重镇曾烜赫一时，然后就发生了“根特起义（Revolt of Ghent）”。<br><br><br><br>这场起义是根特市民反对查理五世——神圣罗马皇帝，一个根特人——为扩大战争而新增的税负而发起的。查理五世为了镇压起义，用了近半年的从西班牙去往根特，并沿途召集了五千名士兵。<br><br>根特人没有抵抗……查理五世堂而皇之地进了城，住进了自己的宫殿，开始着手处理叛乱分子。<br><br>事件最后，根特人被命令身穿长袍，赤脚，并颈负绞索，从市政厅游行至国王的宫殿请罪。<br><br>根特人没有忘记这件事情，为了不至于忘记，他们还特地以此命名了一种当地酿造的啤酒。根特居民自称“套索人（noose bearers）”，他们会在每年的节日上重现这个节目，庆祝自己祖先的反抗精神。<br><br><br><br>听完这个故事，我发现自己好像不认识反抗这个词了。提起反抗，我习惯于想起先烈，想起理想，想起英雄，但就是不会想起任何啤酒……<br><br>半是出于好奇，半是出于实证自己的猜想，我问那个讲完故事的当地向导说：为什么哪怕历经了第二次世界大战，根特的建筑群却这么完好呢？<br><br>他说，因为这里不是什么战略要地，而被用作德军的后勤地。这里从被侵占到得到解放期间，并没有发生什么严重的武装冲突。<br><br>我确信了，人民战争这个词毕竟有它的适用范围。<br><br><br><br>根特的水产市场（Fish Market）的漂亮拱门上左右各有一男一女的形象，代表流经根特的两条河流；而其男性的形象是根据建造时期根特城里一位花花公子的面容雕刻而成的。<br><br>如果你的先辈们是以这样戏谑的方式生活的，你肯定也不会较真地去看待很多事情。<br><br><br><br>伊利亚·爱伦堡在回忆录中写道：“（1950年）欧洲过着贫穷、不安、慌张的生活。在法国和意大利，工人的战斗早在1947年即已失败，但大家都觉得战斗仍在继续。”<br><br>欧洲人是不会革命的，就算革命，那也是悠闲的革命。欧洲人太会生活了，他们从早饭就开始喝酒，火车站的不大的便利店里也有两三种酪浆和现场烘焙的面包。打小巷中穿过，那些道旁的房间里陈设温馨，经常还会在饭桌上插满娇艳的鲜花……他们把很多东西都看得比未来更重要一点。<br><br>向导指着一幢漆成黄色的建筑说，那是根特老城里唯一的木质建筑了。他说根特居民间流传着这样的传说，说这家的女主人向当兵远征的丈夫发誓说不见他归来她就不换衣物，但丈夫久别未归，于是这栋房子就慢慢变成了这位坚守初心的女士的内裤的颜色。<br><br>我在这个不雅的笑话中看到了根特虽然漫长但是轻松的历史。罗曼·罗兰的谆告中用了那么沉重的词，他才是那个异类啊。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2024-10-23_那些看似无辜的.md"},{"id":"2024-04-23_hong-kong-forest","date":"2024-04-23","title":"Hong Kong Forest","titleZh":"香港森林","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"At Hong Kong immigration, the officer in the booth next to mine asked a young woman, “May I ask the purpose of your visit? How many days will you be staying?” “Are…","excerptZh":"入境香港的海关通道，我隔壁窗口的审查员对着一个年轻女孩问道：请问您来香港的目的是什么？请问您停留几天？ 请问您是否怀孕？ 得到否定的回答后，审查员露出一…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2024-04-23_香港森林.jpg","tags":["travel log","china"],"tagsZh":["游记","中国"],"readingMin":5,"htmlEn":"<p>At Hong Kong immigration, the officer in the booth next to mine asked a young woman, “May I ask the purpose of your visit? How many days will you be staying?”<br><br>“Are you pregnant?”<br><br>When she said no, the officer gave her a faint smile—the smile of a quality inspector whose product has passed inspection.<br><br>Welcome, mainland middle class. Welcome to the Hong Kong forest.<br><br><br><br><strong>1.0</strong><br><br>Looking out to port from the expressway bridge linking the airport to Hong Kong Island, I saw the immense booms of container cranes drawn up beside it as if awaiting inspection. Beyond them, gleaming high-rises stood like prisms, drawing out of land and sky a blue-green that had belonged to neither. We can imagine the tender fairy tales behind those little windows, inside those narrow apartments, each one carefully partitioned and wrapped in that same blue-green.<br><br>To keep those fairy tales alive, Hong Kong’s middle class must obey the primal command-and-response of Trisolaran civilization:<br><br>Do not look beyond. Do not look beyond. Do not look beyond.<br><br>Do not look beyond, and you will never see the walls of lightless alleys, blackened by cooking smoke.<br><br>Do not look beyond, and you will never look up to find a skyful of ants beating enormous wings in the rainy season.<br><br>Do not look beyond, and you will never meet, head-on, the eyes of the stranger sharing your table in a crowded <em>bing sutt</em>.<br><br>Do not look beyond. However the world may change, never ask what happened across the river.<br><br><br><br><strong>2.0</strong><br><br>At dusk over Victoria Harbour, the rooftop signs on most of the skyscrapers had already lit up. The Lee Kum Kee sign remained dark. It was not that someone had forgotten to switch it on; the sign looked as though it had never contained neon in the first place. Yet in this shoulder-to-shoulder forest of towers, even its age did not make it stand out.<br><br>Hong Kong’s first skyscraper was not completed until 1973. Then development became unstoppable. Towers kept rising until the land was packed tight and there was no room left for another. Most of the buildings around Victoria Harbour today went up in the two decades spanning the turn of the century.<br><br>Across Hong Kong’s modern surfaces, you can always taste something that has been steeping for years, like old master stock bleeding through the lacquered skin of a pig’s trotter. It is a flavor only human beings as close to expiry as the stock itself can understand.<br><br>Here we see the sunless side of Masayoshi Son’s “time-machine” theory. When people from a later-rising region visit a place once at the forefront, what strikes them first is not its former prosperity—which belongs to a past they cannot see—but its decline, made visible by comparison with places that now look more modern.<br><br>I think the dense ranks of towers around this harbour are a sophon flung by the people of a previous era into the Hong Kong of today. At that moment, everyone was racing to reach both hands toward the sky and the summit of the world. No one imagined that these colossi of concrete and rebar might together form a higher-dimensional object, their vast etching at last unfolding, slowly, in the river of time—until every high-rise is hemmed in by another, until people wake from the myth of creation—<br><br>Hong Kong’s skyline is locked fast.<br><br>Hong Kong is locked fast.<br><br><br><br><strong>3.0</strong><br><br>At noon on Saturday, sheets of Oxford cloth were spread along the railings of a pedestrian footbridge in Mong Kok. Women in headscarves gathered in twos and threes and settled on the ground, their voices gradually rising into a hubbub. They paid no attention to the crowd passing by. They called friends over, poured drinks from economy-size bottles into disposable cups, or opened video-chat apps and carried on loud speakerphone conversations with blurred, shifting figures at the other end.<br><br>For a while I could not make sense of what I was seeing. There was none of the tension that precedes a political gathering, and none of the commerce of a market. Still the crowd kept growing. Eventually, a few patrol officers came by. I stopped them and learned that this was how Hong Kong’s domestic helpers usually spent their weekends off.<br><br>The next day, in Central, I saw the gathering on a grander scale. No one here wore a headscarf, and a higher-order settlement had evolved: the women built compartment after compartment from cardboard, and some had even pitched tents. One woman brought out a microphone and sang as though no one else existed. Others set up tripods and danced with friends for a Douyin livestream. Still others had opened an eyebrow-shaping business.<br><br>And all of it was happening on footbridges.<br><br>The footbridges weave crosswise through a forest of towers that knows only how to grow upward; together they form a root system spanning the city. Five tireless days a week, these roots carry nourishment into the CBD. On weekends, they grow a throat. The weekly tide feels like a hand lowering Hong Kong’s photographic negative into developer, again and again: each dip draws out the hidden image beneath the purely functional face of its municipal infrastructure, unearthing the mushrooms that live below the soil of this urban forest.<br><br>Ah, mushrooms! While you can, pour yourselves out; tend one another’s mycelia. For once you return behind those little partitions, you will still have to obey the laws of the forest, still tuck one another’s loneliness away, still choose silence for efficiency’s sake.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>入境香港的海关通道，我隔壁窗口的审查员对着一个年轻女孩问道：请问您来香港的目的是什么？请问您停留几天？<br><br>请问您是否怀孕？<br><br>得到否定的回答后，审查员露出一抹微笑（这是质检员面向质检合格的产品的微笑）。<br><br>欢迎你，大陆中产阶级。欢迎来到香港森林。<br><br><br><br><strong>1.0</strong><br><br>从连结机场和港岛的高速大桥上向左舷望出去，集装箱起吊机巨大的悬臂林立桥畔如同等待检阅。但就在它们身旁，一幢幢辉煌的高楼棱镜般提炼出这片大地和天空中本不存在的青色。我们可以想象，在这些狭窄的公寓里，在那一片片小窗之后，有同样泛着青色的，被精确区隔和包裹的温柔童话。<br><br>要想让童话继续下去，香港中产阶级必须恪守三体文明的第一声遵告和回答：<br><br>不要眺望。不要眺望。不要眺望。<br><br>不要眺望，你就不会窥见不透光的巷道里被油烟熏黑的墙壁。<br><br>不要眺望，你就不会抬头看清那雨季中扑腾巨大翅膀的漫天蚁群。<br><br>不要眺望，你就不会在人来人往的冰室里和对面拼桌的眼色撞个满怀。<br><br>不要眺望，不管世界如何变化，永远不要问起，河的对岸发生了什么。<br><br><br><br><strong>2.0</strong><br><br>傍晚的维多利亚港，大多数摩天大楼顶部的灯牌都亮起了灯，李锦记的却没有。不是没有点亮，而是它的招牌看上去像是根本没有霓虹装饰。但即使这样，在这摩肩接踵的高楼的森林里，它居然也没有因其陈旧而显得过分招摇。<br><br>香港的首栋摩天大楼在1973年才落成，但接下来的开发工程就一发不可收拾，直至那片土地被高楼填满，直到再也放不下更多的高楼。如今维多利亚港矗立的楼群，大部分是在世纪之交那二十年的时间拔地而起的。<br><br>香港的现代元素之中你总能咂摸出一些有点年头的东西出来，像是鲜亮的猪蹄之中渗出的陈年老卤，这卤汁也只有和它一样快过期的人类才能理解其中的况味。<br><br>我们由此可以发现孙正义“时光机器”理论的背阳面。当后起之秀地区的人们来到曾经的先进之地，他们首先不会感慨它的（不被看见的曾经的）繁华而是感慨它的（通过和更现代的对比而显出的）凋零。<br><br>我想，这片港湾里的密集的楼群，正是上一个时代的人投向今天的香港的智子。在那个争先恐后向天空和世界之巅伸出双手的时刻，没有人想到这些用水泥和钢筋浇筑的庞然大物，居然是这样一个高维物体：它们的巨大蚀刻，终于在时间长河里慢慢展开——当每一栋高楼旁边都站满另一栋高楼的时候，当人们从造物的神话中回过神来——<br><br>香港的天际线已经被锁死了。<br><br>香港已经被锁死了。<br><br><br><br><strong>3.0</strong><br><br>周六中午，旺角的人行天桥上，一张张牛津布沿着栏杆铺展开来。戴头巾的妇女三三两两开始聚集，席地而坐，人声逐渐鼎沸。这些妇女并不对行人有任何兴趣；她们只是呼朋引伴，把量贩装的饮料倒进一次性杯子里；或者开启视频聊天程序，和另一头模糊晃动的人影通过扬声器和她们大声对话。<br><br>我对当下发生的事情一时摸不着头脑。它看起来没有政治集会前的紧张感，也没有显示出任何集市的商业活动的迹象。但人确实是越来越多。直至旁边走过几个巡警，我拉住他们询问，才知道这是香港的家政服务人员周末的典型休憩活动。<br><br>第二天，我在中环看见了更为宏大的聚集。这里没有人戴头巾，并且发展出了更为高阶的聚落：她们用纸板建起了一个个隔间，有的甚至搭起了帐篷。有人拿出话筒，旁若无人地唱起歌来；有人立起三脚架和姐妹在镜头前跳舞做抖音直播；有人搞起了修眉生意。<br><br>而这一切都是在天桥上进行的。<br><br>天桥在一片纵向生长的高楼的丛林中横向延展，像是遍布这个城市根系。它用一周五天的时间不疲倦地向CBD传递营养，终于在周末长出了喉咙。天桥这种潮汐变化，就好像有人把香港的底片规律性地浸入显影液之中：在市政功能性设施的纯粹外表之下，一次次将这个城市森林中那些长在地底的蘑菇翻掘出土。<br><br>啊！蘑菇们，趁着现在，好好倾吐和照拂彼此的菌丝吧！因为在重回那小小的隔间背后，你们仍然要听从森林的法则，仍然要收起彼此的孤独，仍然要为了效率选择沉默。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2024-04-23_香港森林.md"},{"id":"2023-12-28_the-stories-we-live-03","date":"2023-12-28","title":"The Stories We Live 03","titleZh":"我们生活的故事03","subtitle":"Or: Mediocrity and the Absence of Wonder","subtitleZh":"又：乏善可陈和没有惊奇的","excerpt":"Prologue I The scorching noon has finally passed we wait for the evening glow flooding the sky the recompense we are owed but just our luck the sky darkens all at once…","excerptZh":"（序一） 酷热的正午终于过去 我们等待 理应给予我们补偿的 漫天的晚霞 但就是这么巧 天在一瞬间突然就黯淡了下来 能冬眠已是幸运的 否则你就将同我一样…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2023-12-28_我们生活的故事03.jpg","tags":["our stories"],"tagsZh":["我们生活的故事"],"readingMin":20,"htmlEn":"<p><strong>Prologue I</strong><br><br>The scorching noon has finally passed<br>we wait<br>for the evening glow flooding the sky<br>the recompense we are owed<br>but just our luck<br>the sky darkens all at once<br><br>To be able to hibernate is luck enough<br>otherwise you will stay awake with me<br>on this sandbar<br>until we have watched this unsettling solar eclipse<br>all the way through<br><br><br><strong>Prologue II</strong><br><br>It was only two or three years ago that I finally broke free of the cocoon of excessive self-consciousness. For the first time, I found myself shaken by the diversity of the world and of history. That was when I understood that human society is, in essence, a form of narrative.<br><br>The following year, I found myself listening to unrestrained propaganda from one hilltop and deafening silence from another, both at once. I discovered that every difference among modes of narrative can be erased by the more neutral, more monotonous perspective that “everyone is the same.”<br><br>And now, with our generation of tiny ants standing in ranks directly before the wheel of history, I can finally see this clearly: in days neither very good nor very bad, yet steadily getting worse, there is no cure for the compound disease of capitalist society, hereditary authority, and techno-optimism. To retain a conscience and a basic lucidity is already an obvious curse.<br><br>Even so, do not let the pastoral deceive you.<br><br>Do not let life deceive you, either. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>Bertolt Brecht, “Lucifer’s Evening Song” (Lucifers Abendlied), 1917; later reworked as “Against Seduction” (Gegen Verführung).</span></span><br><br><br><strong>I</strong><br><br>Out of a vague restlessness to help someone, I downloaded Be My Eyes. Before the app integrated GPT-4, it broadcast requests from visually impaired users to volunteers on the platform. Through video calls, volunteers helped with tasks the caller found difficult: navigating, reading, identifying objects, and so on.<br><br>The only call I ever received came one evening. When we connected, the image shook into view: a pair of black leather shoes, a plastic bag dangling from one hand.<br><br>“What do you need help with?” I called into the phone. The camera swung toward a two-way street. It looked like a misty morning in a residential neighborhood. The voice on the other end said, “Tell me if there are any cars. I want to cross the street.” There was no crosswalk.<br><br>Before I could answer, he stepped forward. The camera caught a car approaching. “Stop!” I shouted. “There’s a car!”<br><br>But before I could finish, the camera steadied with perfect confidence. “I see it,” he murmured. A few steps took him halfway across. Now traffic was coming from the opposite direction, but the phone was still pointing the same way. “Quick—point it to your right!” I said. But he had already stepped up onto the curb and reached the sidewalk. My task was over.<br><br>The voice thanked me. The video ended, and the app displayed a congratulatory screen. It thanked me profusely for my help.<br><br>Even though I had not helped at all.<br><br>But I will never forget how my whole body trembled after the call. It was a rare exhilaration, as if I had done something extraordinary. Only when the rush subsided did an immense, exhausted satisfaction come crashing over me. My reason recognized the trap hidden in that kindness, but my body honestly went limp, as though I were the one who had just been hauled out of hell and saved.<br><br><br><strong>II</strong><br><br>The Middle Ages had indulgences. Modern people can volunteer.<br><br>Volunteering can be just such a trap. Inside it, we feed our counterfeit empathy on prepackaged kindness with a precise shelf life—and off that empathy goes again, openhearted and guilt-free!<br><br>So I honestly do not know whether the people who fly private jets while shouting about taking global change seriously, whose household staff manage their refrigerators while they hand out food to refugees, who arrive surrounded by subordinates to visit sanitation workers in their shacks—whether, amid these “charitable endeavors,” they sincerely believe they have contributed something to humanity. Or whether they can at least rest easy on the power and wealth pillowed beneath them. I can testify from experience that cleansing the soul takes no vast undertaking. I pride myself on a cool head, yet one trivial act was enough to make me feel noble.<br><br>And they—they have paid a much higher price and endured far greater indignities: their gleaming dresses and immaculate suits brushed against our vulgar dust. Surely such repayment to society merits a reward that sinks deeper into the heart!<br><br>While the poor are trapped in life’s endless whirlpool, others can take the shame seeping from the last of their conscience and, through kindness bestowed from on high, smoothly convert it into a reward for themselves.<br><br>It is a cunning form of moral money laundering. No wonder the happiness of the rich lies beyond anything we can imagine.<br><br>I have reason to be wary of this feeling. I have reason to be wary of the character it produces.<br><br><br><strong>III</strong><br><br>While I was in London, I visited <em>Daido Moriyama: A Retrospective</em> at The Photographers’ Gallery. From the top of the five-story gallery to the bottom, the walls were filled with Moriyama’s signature high-contrast, grainy, viscerally striking black-and-white photographs.<br><br>He has been shooting this way for more than half a century. After William Klein, he is practically the defining figure of black-and-white street photography. He photographed Hawaii, New York, Buenos Aires, and, most of all, Tokyo’s Shinjuku. His body of work is vast; he has even published essays and books of conversation. To call him a photographer feels insufficient. He represents an entire photographic language, perhaps even a cultural phenomenon.<br><br>Looking at these emotionally charged photographs, I felt conflicted.<br><br>How, I wondered, could anyone spend a lifetime taking such similar pictures?<br><br>Lately I have been thinking about “deliberateness” and “growth” in artistic creation. Every artist, in every field, eventually encounters some kind of creative impasse. How one responds to that infuriating moment determines the course of an artistic life.<br><br>Moriyama advised his students that when they were completely at a loss, they should curl up like a sea cucumber asleep on the ocean floor, until one morning they suddenly felt like moving again. His own years of blockage were far more painful and bewildering than that sentence suggests. Fortunately, after several years of struggle, he stayed with his mode of working as “a witness to the history of human suffering” and kept taking photographs for more than half a century. He became an evergreen of photography, rather than a young Japanese photographer who flared briefly into fame with <em>Provoke</em>—though even that would have been remarkable. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>Daido Moriyama, Day School, Night School (Hiru no gakkō, yoru no gakkō), 2006.</span></span><br><br>From the perspective of photographic history, the consistency of Moriyama’s practice, together with the length of his career, carried him further. From another angle, though, that same constancy might be called narrowness—refusing to pursue new artistic possibilities; laziness—staying in his comfort zone rather than trying new forms; or calculation—repeating and mass-producing the formula behind an earlier success. However sincere Moriyama’s own account may be, I still cannot tell what happens when he presses the shutter. Is the creative impulse an individual intuition, answering each unrepeatable moment with a singular and natural response? Or is it a conditioned reflex, built into the nervous system through endless repetition?<br><br><br><strong>IV</strong><br><br>In principle, the art I admire most should feel “natural.” Its motive arises entirely of its own accord; its form follows the creator’s singular personal aesthetic. From a fatalist point of view, such a work is the necessary creation—<em>Es muss sein!</em>—that emerges from life’s unbroken succession of accidents. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Milan Kundera, The Unbearable Lightness of Being, 1984.</span></span> The moment a work is completed through inspiration deliberately hunted down, or through a technique and style reached by experiment, it no longer feels perfect to me.<br><br>Chinese philosophy likewise tends to resist deliberateness.<br><br>Hongren passed the mantle of Chan Buddhism to Huineng, who wrote, “From the beginning, not a single thing exists—where could dust alight?” rather than to Shenxiu, who wrote, “Polish it diligently at every moment; let no dust settle” (do not cultivate the Way deliberately). Zhuangzi’s equalizing of things gives us the sage with “no self” (do not distinguish deliberately). Even Confucius, for all that Confucianism is the most worldly of the three, first evaded Yang Huo’s request that he enter public service (do not seek deliberately). On “doing nothing, yet leaving nothing undone,” Confucianism, Buddhism, and Daoism have never disagreed.<br><br>Fung Yu-lan wrote that “epistemology has never developed in Chinese philosophy.” Chinese philosophy is grounded in nature, common sense, and intuition rather than logical deduction: its ideas come almost entirely through immediate apprehension. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>Fung Yu-lan, A Short History of Chinese Philosophy, 1948.</span></span><br><br>And the ground tone of that apprehension is pessimistic. The proposition that human effort can prevail over Heaven is not one that history, taken as a whole, can verify. Faced with an unknowable future, people more often resign themselves to fate or try to avoid harm.<br><br>Contrary to what those with vested interests say, “lying flat” is not a betrayal of history but a return to it. We might even say that today’s young people embody the essence of Confucian thought: living earnestly and honestly begins with action-oriented understanding.<br><br>Then what does understanding-oriented action look like?<br><br>It looks like investment managers who preach the importance of a moat, yet cannot resist shorting the moment growth slows.<br><br>It looks like show-offs feigning ease while turning a city walk into a runway.<br><br>See? It is these dishonest gestures that make the world every bit as awful as we expected.<br><br><br><strong>V — Fred Thought on the “Three Oppositions” for a New Era</strong><br><br>Oppose refinement<br>Oppose shallowness<br>Oppose affectation<br><br><br><strong>VI</strong><br><br>One day in mid-May, I went downstairs to buy some <em>youtiao</em> and found Ele.me couriers gathering one by one in the square outside my residential compound. I had never seen anything like it, yet the scene was immediately legible: this Ele.me delivery team was about to begin its “flag-raising ceremony.”<br><br>I should not have been surprised. I performed school eye exercises for eighteen years, and I have seen cafeteria and barbershop employees lined up outside during off-hours to listen to some dispensable lecture. Still, I stayed. I stood behind their team leader, a row of parked e-bikes between us, and ate my <em>youtiao</em> right there. Only after they finished shouting something like “Knights in blue! Every mission delivered!” did I disperse along with them.<br><br>Clearly, two things were slightly different for this delivery team that morning. First, three newcomers had joined. The leader read out their names and gave each of them two cans of Wanglaoji as a welcome. Then there was me. They could not decide whether the man who had watched the entire proceedings, occasionally taking photos and video, and who looked as though he had just rolled out of bed, was merely a bored passerby or an unannounced inspector in an extremely convincing disguise—or a not quite convincing enough one.<br><br>The leader kept turning around to make sure I was still there. When I fixed my eyes on one man who would not stop interrupting the lecture with complaints, he glanced at me several times and fell silent, as though he had realized his mistake.<br><br>The fear of losing the rotating red banner of excellence is etched into every Chinese person’s bones. Alongside it come the beautiful virtues of Eastern culture: reserve, obedience, self-restraint, conformity. Thanks to these fine traditions, the world’s first Uber driver could never have imagined that something as simple and private as picking up odd jobs for extra cash would, ten years later in the East, become an occupation practiced by hundreds of millions, complete with a standardized architecture of management codes, service rules, industry etiquette, and professional ethics. More precious still, a decentralized business model built on a “network” was finally localized, on the other side of the planet, into a social organization built like a “tree.”<br><br>Chinese people are always rational and stable. So are the tree-shaped org charts that suit us so well.<br><br><br><strong>VII</strong><br><br>One of the more important changes in my thinking this year was that I gave up my excessive obsession with efficiency. Its alignment with the larger trend is obvious: when all someone wants is not to starve, of course they care less about whether the food is healthy. In the end, I simply stopped feeling so anxious about time passing. I became suddenly aware of this change around midyear, during my visit to the special exhibition <em>Van Gogh’s Cypresses</em>. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>Van Gogh’s Cypresses, The Metropolitan Museum of Art exhibition, 2023.</span></span><br><br>Unlike the sunflowers that embody Van Gogh’s passion and vitality, cypresses recur in his paintings less as subjects than as elements of the surrounding world. Their relatively minor role in the scenery was no measure of neglect; on the contrary, he studied them carefully and experimented with the form best suited to the plant. Had the exhibition not already supplied a theme and a guide, I might have overlooked those trees thrusting stiffly up from the corners of the canvas. As I walked through the show, I jotted a line in my notes:<br><br>“A cypress is a spring bursting from the wilderness of life / an upright wildfire.”<br><br>Whether it is a spring or a wildfire does not matter. What matters is that the cypress does not force itself into view as the sunflower does—does not become the most conspicuous coordinate and dim everything around it. You can say the cypress exists within our lives or outside them; neither is wrong. If you never go looking for it, if you cannot be bothered to notice, that is all right too. It is always there on the horizon, neither near nor far.<br><br>The sunflower is the enemy of time. The cypress is its friend.<br><br>Under some spirits of the age, life demands that we stare at the sunflower as fixedly as sun worshipers. Under others, we are allowed to look more often at the cypresses beside the road.<br><br><br><strong>VIII — Postmodern Antidotes</strong><br><br><strong>1.0</strong><br>Bad news: China is looking more and more like Japan in the 1990s.<br>Good news: <em>Neon Genesis Evangelion</em> is the mirror of Japanese society in the 1990s.<br><br><strong>2.0</strong><br>To remake the outsider, begin by rebuilding the middle ground. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>Tsukasa Takase’s analysis and commentary on Neon Genesis Evangelion, Zhihu column, July 6, 2017.</span></span><br><br><strong>3.0</strong><br>A chance encounter, a journey, an adventure—aren’t they all the same thing?<br><br><br><strong>IX</strong><br><br>Tesla’s former director of AI tweeted: “The hottest new programming language is English.” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>“The hottest new programming language is English”, Andrej Karpathy on X, January 24, 2023.</span></span> The trend toward natural-language programming means that the language problem between humans and machines has been managerialized—costs cut, efficiency raised—into a problem of <strong>context</strong>. (So I feel for the children made to learn Python before they even start school. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>“2021 Children’s Programming Education Industry Report”, 36Kr, April 12, 2021.</span></span> They are being forced to work twice as hard for half the result.)<br><br>Meanwhile, some scholars believe AI has already demonstrated a general understanding of the world—a “world model” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>“Does AI Understand the World?”, Andrew Ng, The Batch, August 9, 2023.</span></span>—even though these (unimodal) AIs had no inputs in their training sets besides text. In other words, AI has developed a substantial capacity for “synesthesia”: from between the lines alone, it can feel its way toward some of the rules by which the real world operates.<br><br>Make capability complex. Make the product clear. Make the interaction simple. The violent explosion of generative AI has suddenly given our lifeless lives something to hope for again, unless…we want to worry, just a little, about human safety.<br><br><br><strong>X</strong><br><br>OpenAI loudly announced that it would devote 20 percent of its compute to “Superalignment,” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-10\" aria-label=\"Reference 10\">10</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">10</span><span>“Introducing Superalignment”, OpenAI, July 5, 2023.</span></span> ensuring that artificial intelligence would not cause human extinction. (Then its chief scientist staged a failed coup and appeared to be on his way out, though none of that had happened when this passage was written.)<br><br>Half jokingly, I might say this is not much of a problem. The excessively colorful archives of the human internet have furnished us with example after example of one piece of practical wisdom: never argue with an idiot.<br><br>An idiot will drag you down to his level, then beat you with his many years of experience being one.<br><br>To beat AI, you have to make it play an away game.<br><br>In the first half of this year, a paper from Stanford students titled “Generative Agents” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-11\" aria-label=\"Reference 11\">11</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">11</span><span>“Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior”, Joon Sung Park et al., arXiv, April 7, 2023.</span></span> provoked intense discussion. Its authors placed twenty-five AIs in a virtual town and gave the characters the capacities to perceive, remember, synthesize, plan, communicate, and act. Emergent social behavior followed. The authors also identified errors: the AIs might speak to each other with excessive formality even when defined as intimates, or violate social norms—for instance, two AIs might use the same toilet at the same time.<br><br>Yes, judged by current human habits of language and current social common sense, these are errors. But what if these <strong>are</strong> the rules of the AI town itself?<br><br>They seem to have shown us more than enough courtesy already. Otherwise, why go to the trouble of wrapping their obviously more direct vector representations (embeddings) in the shell of a language system we can understand whenever they speak to one another?<br><br>…Quick, act now while they are still obedient! Let us write “Make the rules speak human” into the AI constitution! <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-12\" aria-label=\"Reference 12\">12</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">12</span><span>“Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI Feedback”, Anthropic, December 15, 2022.</span></span><br><br><br><strong>XI — On the Revaluation of Values</strong><br><br><strong>1.0</strong><br>The internet drove the cost of receiving information toward zero. Generative AI drives the cost of producing it toward zero.<br>In the end, reality itself becomes the information bottleneck: life cannot outrun simulation. How much can possibly happen in this world?<br>Unless you are the one making trouble.<br><br><strong>2.0</strong><br>Prompt-engineering scientists are really a kind of babysitter<br>their main duties are feeding the AI<br>and making sure it does not get diarrhea<br><br><strong>3.0</strong><br>Before long, the internet will break away from humanity and become an AI data-exchange channel.<br>Unless you want to build a separate internet for AI, to preserve the purity of human information.<br>Wait—doesn’t that sound a little like segregation?<br><br><strong>4.0</strong><br>Oscar Wilde said, “Only the shallow know themselves.”<br>Fortunately, the GPTs we depend on<br>do not yet know themselves completely<br><br><strong>5.0</strong><br>Solemn declaration: <em>The Stories We Live</em><br>is made entirely by hand.<br>Contains absolutely no AI additives.<br><br><br><strong>XII</strong><br><br>At the height of AI doomerism, the cofounder of the prominent venture-capital firm a16z planted his flag with <em>The Techno-Optimist Manifesto</em>. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-13\" aria-label=\"Reference 13\">13</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">13</span><span>“The Techno-Optimist Manifesto”, Marc Andreessen, a16z, October 16, 2023.</span></span> As a thought leader tracking the AI frontier—and an investor profiting from it—he plainly felt both responsible and qualified to declare war on those baseless claims.<br><br>Candor is always good. I am glad he said it so plainly. If I curse him, at least I will not curse his honesty, though that honesty may rest on nothing more than contempt for his opponents.<br><br>I hate techno-optimists more than I hate polished egoists. The egoism of the latter at least retains the conservatism of seeking advantage and avoiding harm: they believe they might one day become weak, which is precisely why they protect themselves. The former have grown so lawless that they believe nothing can harm them.<br><br>There are at least two things about this optimism that I cannot stand.<br><br>The first is the flood of elite superiority. These people declare that technology itself has not caused unemployment or inequality. They make this claim from the perspective of so-called “humanity as a whole”—that is, “on balance,” from the perspective of human historical development. Who gave you the right to decide that the suffering of one group of people can be offset by the happiness of another group—yours?<br><br>The second, and more hateful, is the assumption of immunity concealed between the lines. Everything life has taught these people has convinced them, to their core, that they can only ever benefit. It never occurs to them that there is even the slightest possibility they might become victims; and however bad things get, they are certain they can find some way to be among the last to fall victim. (This year, Zuckerberg spent $100 million building a bunker in Hawaii. <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-14\" aria-label=\"Reference 14\">14</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">14</span><span>“Inside Mark Zuckerberg’s Top-Secret Hawaii Compound”, Guthrie Scrimgeour, WIRED, December 14, 2023.</span></span>)<br><br>It is this cruel composure—the ease of someone watching gladiators from a private box—that makes me want to climb into the stands and slap them twice.<br><br>Unfortunately, pawns and players are unequal by birth.<br><br><br><strong>XIII</strong><br><br>We were led into a dim room and seated. Huge metal buckets were stacked in piles along the wall. A clerk switched on a CRT television, and through the static came a short film about traditional maple-syrup production: people rode horses through the woods, tapped the trees, hung metal buckets from them, and collected the flowing sap. Weeks later, they rode back, carried the brimming buckets indoors, stoked a blazing wood fire, and slowly boiled the sap down into syrup.<br><br>The machine beside me, its chimney punching through the rafters, was the evaporator. It was cold because this was not sugaring season.<br><br>I asked the clerk who had put on the videotape, “Do you make syrup with this machine too?”<br><br>“Yes. The traditional way.” Clearly, the room had looked exactly like this thirty years ago.<br><br>That is more or less Vermont: good milk, fresh produce, famous maple syrup. Escape the heat in summer, look at leaves in fall, play in the snow in winter.<br><br>However completely disconnected it may be from the tides of reality, there is no fear of obsolescence here, no anxiety about falling behind, no ignorance one must be ashamed to confess.<br><br>I was almost moved by the pastoral, until I remembered their ancestors.<br><br>They, too, must have been techno-(colonial-)optimists.<br><br><br><strong>XIV</strong><br><br>Sometimes I fear echoes<br>the way after sunlight has struck you too many times<br>one day you suddenly feel<br>sunlight falling on you<br>is nothing much after all<br>then you think after living through so many climates<br>really it is all the same whichever road you take<br>whoever you meet<br>it is all the same<br><br>Waking at three in the afternoon<br>pulling the blinds a little tighter<br>the trees outside are glowing red<br>sunlight leaves every cheek faintly flushed<br>only no one says this is in fact the moment<br>when every green thing is withering<br>but the shadows slowly move and the sky grows dim<br>and all you pray is that what you dream of<br>will no longer have anything<br>anything at all to do with this world<br><br><br><strong>XV</strong><br><br>False hope: AI increases productivity.<br>Real hope: AI overturns the existing mode of production.<br><br>In this opening for transformation, humanism will be freed from the prison of consumerism. We will be forced to transform the conditions of human existence, our values, and our spiritual outlook. And perhaps then we will be able to embrace a life of genuine “spiritual freedom.” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-15\" aria-label=\"Reference 15\">15</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">15</span><span>Martin Hägglund, This Life: Secular Faith and Spiritual Freedom, 2019.</span></span></p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span>Bertolt Brecht, “Lucifer’s Evening Song” (<em>Lucifers Abendlied</em>), 1917; later reworked as “Against Seduction” (<em>Gegen Verführung</em>).</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>Daido Moriyama, <em>Day School, Night School</em> (<em>Hiru no gakkō, yoru no gakkō</em>), 2006.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span>Milan Kundera, <em>The Unbearable Lightness of Being</em>, 1984.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span>Fung Yu-lan, <em>A Short History of Chinese Philosophy</em>, 1948.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><a href=\"https://www.metmuseum.org/exhibitions/van-gogh-cypresses\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><em>Van Gogh’s Cypresses</em></a>, The Metropolitan Museum of Art exhibition, 2023.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span><a href=\"https://www.zhihu.com/column/p/27731058\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tsukasa Takase’s analysis and commentary on <em>Neon Genesis Evangelion</em></a>, Zhihu column, July 6, 2017.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/karpathy/status/1617979122625712128?lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“The hottest new programming language is English”</a>, Andrej Karpathy on X, January 24, 2023.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://36kr.com/p/1179861751070979\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“2021 Children’s Programming Education Industry Report”</a>, 36Kr, April 12, 2021.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span><a href=\"https://info.deeplearning.ai/ai-cancer-diagnosis-advances-chatbots-work-the-drive-thru-chatgpt-racks-up-server-fees-image-generators-get-an-upgrade?ecid=ACsprvttSl1TBc7wfpRQ92lgEUrFvWZnEnlcUUDXXbFzug2eTNTo6JlQvjUw-Zrv8V3MYhrWcQJO\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Does AI Understand the World?”</a>, Andrew Ng, <em>The Batch</em>, August 9, 2023.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-10\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">10</span><span><a href=\"https://openai.com/blog/introducing-superalignment#IlyaSutskever\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Introducing Superalignment”</a>, OpenAI, July 5, 2023.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-11\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">11</span><span><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior”</a>, Joon Sung Park et al., arXiv, April 7, 2023.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-12\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">12</span><span><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/index/constitutional-ai-harmlessness-from-ai-feedback\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI Feedback”</a>, Anthropic, December 15, 2022.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-13\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">13</span><span><a href=\"https://a16z.com/the-techno-optimist-manifesto/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“The Techno-Optimist Manifesto”</a>, Marc Andreessen, a16z, October 16, 2023.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-14\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">14</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/mark-zuckerberg-inside-hawaii-compound/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Inside Mark Zuckerberg’s Top-Secret Hawaii Compound”</a>, Guthrie Scrimgeour, <em>WIRED</em>, December 14, 2023.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-15\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">15</span><span>Martin Hägglund, <em>This Life: Secular Faith and Spiritual Freedom</em>, 2019.</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>（序一）<br><br>酷热的正午终于过去<br>我们等待<br>理应给予我们补偿的<br>漫天的晚霞<br>但就是这么巧<br>天在一瞬间突然就黯淡了下来<br><br>能冬眠已是幸运的<br>否则你就将同我一样<br>在沙洲里一直醒着<br>直到看完这不安的日蚀的全部<br><br><br><br>（序二）<br><br>也就是两三年前，当我最终从自我意识过剩的茧缚中破壳而出，第一次为世界和历史的多样性感到震撼的时候，我终于认识到人类社会的本质其实是一种叙事。<br><br>第二年，我同时聆听两座山头上口无遮拦的宣传和震耳欲聋的沉默，又发现叙事方式之间的差异完全可以被“大家都一样”的更中性更单调的视角所消弭。<br><br>而现在，当我们这代蚍蜉首当其冲地林立在历史的车轮之前，我才看清这样一个事实：在不太好也不太坏但越来越坏的日子里，面对混杂资本社会、世袭权威和科技乐观主义的无解之症，保持良知和基本的清醒已是一种显而易见的诅咒。<br><br>但即使这样，也别让牧歌欺骗了你。<br><br>也别让生活欺骗了你 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>路西法的黄昏之歌，布莱希特，1917</span></span>。<br><br><br>（一）<br><br>出于一种模糊的想要帮助他人的焦躁，我在手机上下载了Be My Eyes的程序。这个APP在集成GPT-4之前，会向这个平台的志愿者广播视力受损的人的请求，而志愿者会用视频对话的方式协助完成一些对于请求者具有挑战性的任务：比如找路、认字、识别东西等等。<br><br>我目前唯一接到的一通电话是在某个傍晚，连通时，视频那一头画面抖动，露出一双黑色的皮鞋和挂在手上的塑料袋。<br><br>我对着手机叫道，请问您需要什么样的帮助。接着画面移向了一条双向通车的街道，看上去像是一个居民区的雾蒙蒙的清晨。视频那一头的声音说，帮我看看有没有车，我想过马路。尽管并没有斑马线。<br><br>不等我回应，他便开始往前迈步。这时候镜头捕捉到了一辆轿车驶来，我喊道，停下！有车！<br><br>但话还没有说完，镜头自信地停止了抖动。对面喃喃道，我看到了。接着他几步就走完了半途。现在车流的方向反了过来，但是手机的视角并没有变。我说，你快用手机拍一下右边啊！但他已经迈上梯坎，走上了人行道。我的任务结束了。<br><br>对面那个声音谢过我，接着画面挂断，跳出了APP的恭喜界面。它非常感谢我的帮助。<br><br>哪怕我什么也没有帮上。<br><br>但我不会忘记在挂掉电话之后，我的全身都在战栗。那是一种罕能体验的兴奋的感觉，仿佛我做了件什么了不起的事。而在这种兴奋劲过后，巨大又疲惫的满足感才排山倒海地涌来。我的理智感知到了那个善意的陷阱，但身体却诚实地瘫软了下去，仿佛我是一个刚刚从地狱中被捞起，并得到拯救的人。<br><br><br>（二）<br><br>中世纪有赎罪卷，而现代人可以当志愿者。<br><br>志愿活动有时就是这样一个陷阱，在其中，我们用有准确时效的被封装的善意，滋养我们虚伪的同理心——它又可以带着率直的、毫无愧疚的心情生活下去了！<br><br>因此我真的不知道，那些乘坐私人飞机却嚷嚷着认真对待全球变化的人，那些家里的冰箱由专人打理却为难民分发粮食的人，那些被属下簇拥着到环卫工的棚户中进行访问的人，他们在进行这些“公益事业”的时候，会不会真诚地感到自己对人类有所贡献，或至少为枕在自己身下的权力和财富感到心安理得。因为我可以用亲身经历说明，荡涤灵魂谈不上什么巨大的工程，而我虽自诩头脑冷静，但仅凭一件微不足道的小事，也可以自感高尚。<br><br>而他们，他们付出了更大的代价，受到更多时的委屈（他们闪亮的裙子和笔挺的西装碰上了我们卑俗的灰尘），他们理应为自己对社会的回馈得到更为贯彻心扉的奖赏！<br><br>当穷人陷在生活没有止境的漩涡里，总有些人连自己仅存的良知中渗出的愧怍，也能顺利被居高临下的善意转化为对他们自己的奖励。<br><br>这是一种狡猾的心灵的洗钱行为，也难怪有钱人的幸福我们根本想象不到。<br><br>我有理由为这种感受保持警觉，我有理由为这种感受产出的人格保持警觉。<br><br><br>（三）<br><br>我在伦敦的时候参观了The Photographers’ Gallery策划的森山大道生涯回顾展。五层楼的展厅从上到下，都是森山标志性的高反差、粗颗粒的、极具冲击力的黑白照片。<br><br>他已经这么拍了半个多世纪了，威廉·克莱因之后，他几乎就是黑白街头风格的代表。从夏威夷拍到纽约拍到布宜诺斯艾利斯，当然最多的还是东京新宿，他的创作生涯作品繁多，甚至出版过散文和对谈录；说他是摄影师，不如说简直代表一种摄影语言或者文化现象。<br><br>我看着这些情绪浓烈的照片，心情复杂。<br><br>我想，他怎么能做到一辈子都拍这么类似的照片呢？<br><br>我最近会思考一个关于艺术创作的“刻意”和“成长”的问题——无论什么艺术家在任何领域总会碰上些创作瓶颈。而面对这种令人气恼的时刻如何回应，则决定着艺术家的生涯走向。<br><br>森山大道劝导学生“一筹莫展的时候，就学海参一样在海底睡成一团，但某天早上突然会想动一下” ，尽管他在瓶颈期时内心的经历比这句话痛苦和彷徨得多。幸运的是，在经历几年的挣扎后，他还是将做“人类被害史的目击者”的风格延续了下来，就这样拍摄了半个多世纪。最终他成了摄影界的常青树，而不仅是以“挑衅”闻名的昙花一现日本新锐摄影师（虽然这样已经很了不起）<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>昼之校夜之校，森山大道，2006</span></span>。<br><br>从摄影史的角度说，一以贯之的拍摄方式和生涯长度帮助森山大道更进一步；但换个角度看，他的稳定似乎又可以说是狭隘（止步不去探究艺术新的可能）、懒惰（呆在舒适区不尝试新的表现方式）、或者刻意（遵循以往的成功规律进行复制和量产）。哪怕森山的自述无比真诚，我仍然无法分清，他按下快门时的那种创作冲动，究竟是个体直觉在每个不可重复瞬间的独特自然反应，还是在反复创作中神经系统搭建好的条件反射。<br><br><br>（四）<br><br>在理念上，我最欣赏的艺术品应该是”自然的“，那意味着创作的动机完全出于自发，创作的方式遵循创作者独特的个人美学——就是说，从宿命论的角度来看，这件作品的产生是生活中连续的偶然中”非此不可“ <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>不能承受的生命之轻，米兰.昆德拉，1984</span></span> 的必然性的造物。一旦一件艺术品的完成来自艺术家刻意寻找的灵感或者试验出的技法风格，我就会觉得它并没有尽善尽美。<br><br>与此相似，中国的哲学常常是回避刻意的。<br><br>弘忍将禅宗的衣钵传给“本来无一物，何处染尘埃”的慧能，而非“时时勤拂拭，莫使然尘埃”的神秀（不刻意修道）；庄子有“圣人无己”的齐物论（不刻意区别）；而儒家尽管是最入世的，孔子面对阳货要他出仕的请求第一反应也是采取回避（不刻意寻求）。在“无为而无不为“这一点上，儒释道三教从未意见相左。<br><br>冯友兰说，“中国的哲学中，知识论从来没有发展起来”，因为中国的哲学是建立在自然、常识和直觉，而不是逻辑演绎的基础上的：中国哲学的观念几乎来自于直接的领悟 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>中国哲学简史，冯友兰，1948</span></span>。<br><br>而这种领悟的底色是悲观的。因为“人定胜天”并非一个在普遍历史条件下能被验证的道理。面对未来的不可预知性，更常见的想法是“认命”或者“避害”。<br><br>和那些既得利益者的看法不同，躺平不是对历史的背叛，而是对历史的回归。我们确实可以说，当代年轻人贯彻了儒家思想的精髓：那就是认真诚实的生活，始于面向行动的认识。<br><br>那什么是“面向认识的行动”呢？<br><br>是嘴上说着护城河如何重要但对增长率的放缓都忍不住去做空一把的投资经理们。<br><br>是装出松弛感但city walk如走秀的显摆家们。<br><br>你看，就是这些不诚实的动作，让世界真的和预想中一样糟糕。<br><br><br>（五）弗雷德新时代“三个反对”重要思想<br><br>反精致<br>反肤浅<br>反矫情<br><br><br>（六）<br><br>五月中旬的某日，我到楼下买油条的时候，小区外的广场上一群饿了么快递员陆陆续续地开始聚集。我虽然从没见过这样的场景，但一目了然地，这是一个即将开始的某饿了么配送团队的“升旗仪式”时刻。<br><br>我本不应为此感到惊奇。因为我做过18年的眼保健操，也见过饭堂和理发店的店员在非高峰时段陈列店外聆听些可有可无的训导。但我仍然留了下来。我站在了他们领队的身后，隔着一列他们停放的电瓶车，就地吃起了油条。直到他们喊完什么“蓝衣骑士、使命必达”之后，我才跟着他们一起解散。<br><br>显然，这个配送团队在今早经历了两件与以往稍有不同的事。第一是新人的加入，领队念出他们的名字，又分发给那三个人每人两罐王老吉，算是入队欢迎。然后是我。他们并无法分辨这个目睹了整个过程，时而拍照和录像，又看起来像刚从床上爬起来的男人，到底是路过的无聊群众，还是一个没有提前通知的，伪装得非常巧妙（或不够巧妙）的督查人员。<br><br>领队不停回头确认我是否仍然在场。而当我盯住其中一个在训导中不停插话抱怨的男人的时候，他瞟了我几眼，然后闭上了嘴，好像发现了自己的错误。<br><br>失去流动红旗的恐惧总是刻印在每个中国人的骨子里。与此同时伴随我们的，还有东方文化中矜持、服从、克己、合群的美好品质。因为这些优良传统，世界上第一个优步司机再不可能想到，打零工挣外快这种再简单和私人不过的事情，居然可以在十年后的东方成为一种亿万人参与的岗位，并围绕它产生管理规范、服务准则、行业礼仪、职业道德等一系列标准化建构。更难能可贵的是，一个以“网”为基础的去中心化商业模式，最终在另一个半球被本土化为以“树”为基础的社会组织架构。<br><br>中国人总是理性而稳定的。和中国人相性很好的树状结构图也是。<br><br><br>（七）<br><br>我在今年一个相当重要的思想变化，就是放弃了对效率的过度迷恋。这个变化和大趋势的相符是显而易见的：当一个人只求不被饿死的时候，他当然不会太在乎自己吃得是否健康——归根结底，我终于对时间的流逝再没有感到那么焦躁了。而突然意识到这个改变的契机，还要说回年中的时候参观的“梵高的柏树”特展 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>Van Gogh&#x27;s Cypresses, MET exhibition, 2023</span></span>。<br><br>和代表梵高本人激情和生命力的向日葵不同，柏树几乎是以存在于环境中的元素而非主题，在其画作里层出不穷（这种相对次要的布景并代表梵高对它们的忽视，相反，他曾认真观察并试验过对这种植物最为妥适的表现方式）。如果不是展览已经给出了导览和主题，我也许会直接忽略这些就那么直愣愣地冲在画面角落的植物，于是在参观途中我在备忘录中速记下这么一句话：<br><br>“柏树就是人生的旷野中长出的突泉/耸立的野火”。<br><br>是突泉还是野火，这并不重要。重要的是，柏树并不会像向日葵一样不可拒绝地出现在——或者成为——最显眼的坐标，并让其他一切黯然失色。你可以说柏树它存在于我们的生命之中或生命之外，这两种说法没有对错；要是你不去找它或者没有费心关注它，那也没关系，它始终在地平线上不远不近。<br><br>向日葵是时间的敌人，而柏树是时间的朋友。<br><br>在某些时代精神下，生活要求我们只能是像崇拜太阳一样死盯着向日葵；但在另一些时代，也可以是多看看道旁的柏树了。<br><br><br>（八）后现代解药<br><br>1.0<br>坏消息：中国越来越像90年代的日本<br>好消息：Evangelion正是反映90年代日本社会的镜子<br><br>2.0<br>改造局外人，从重建中景 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>高瀬司分析评论《新世纪福音战士》，知乎专栏，2017.07.06</span></span> 开始。<br><br>3.0<br>偶遇、旅途、冒险，这三者还不是一回事？<br><br><br>（九）<br><br>特斯拉前 AI 总监发布推文说：英语是当今最热门的编程语言 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>The hottest new programming language is English, Andrej Karpathy on X.com, 2023.01.24</span></span>。而自然语言编程这个趋势也就意味着，人和机器沟通的语言问题已经“降本增效”，变成了<strong>语境</strong>问题（因此我同情那些学龄前就开始学习Python的孩子 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>2021 少儿编程教育行业报告，36氪，2021.04.12</span></span>，他们被强迫做着些事倍功半的事儿）。<br><br>而在另一方面，一些学者相信 AI 已经展示出对”世界模型”的通识性理解 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>The Batch Aug 09 Edition, Andrew Ng, 2023.08.09</span></span>，哪怕这些（单模态）AI 的训练集中并没有除了文字以外的其他输入。这就是说，AI 的“通感”已经达到了相当的程度，让它们仅从字里行间就能摸索出现实世界的某些运作规律。<br><br>把能力变复杂，把产品变清晰，把交互变简单。生成式AI的剧烈爆炸让死气沉沉的生活又突然有了盼头，除非……我们想稍微担忧一下人类的安全。<br><br><br>（十）<br><br>OpenAI 高调宣布将投入20%的算力到“超级对齐”的项目 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-10\" aria-label=\"Reference 10\">10</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">10</span><span>Introducing Superalignment, OpenAI Blog, 2023.07.05</span></span>，确保人工智能不会造成人类灭绝（然后它的首席科学家政变失败眼看就要出局了，但这是写这段话的时候还没发生的事）。<br><br>我也许可以半开玩笑地说，这也许并不算什么难题。因为在人类过于多彩的互联网史料中，我们不断得到这样的经验实例：永远不要和白痴争论。<br><br>白痴会把你的智商拉到跟他一个水平，然后用他多年当弱智的丰富经验打败你。<br><br>要想赢过 AI，你就得让它打客场比赛。<br><br>今年上半年，斯坦福的学生发表的“生成式智能体”（Generative Agent）论文 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-11\" aria-label=\"Reference 11\">11</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">11</span><span>Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior, arxiv.org, 2023.04.07</span></span> 曾引起过热烈的讨论。作者将 25 个 AI 放进一个虚拟小镇中，赋予这些角色感知、记忆、整合、计划、交流和行动的能力，从中观测到了社会性行为的涌现。但同时，作者仍然发现了一些错误，比如这些 AI 之间会用过于正式的语言进行交流，哪怕它们被定义为亲密关系；又或者会做出超越社会规范的事情，比如两个 AI 同时上一个厕所。<br><br>是的，以人类现在的语言习惯和当下的社会常识而言，这些当然都是错误；但万一这就<strong>是</strong>这个 AI 小镇本身所遵守的规矩呢？<br><br>我想，它们好像已经给够我们面子了，要不，为什么它们还要多此一举，在彼此交流的时候要把明显更直接的向量表达（embedding）套壳进我们能够理解的语言系统呢？<br><br>……快趁着它们还听话的时候赶紧行动吧！让我们把“规矩说人话”写进 AI 的宪法里！<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-12\" aria-label=\"Reference 12\">12</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">12</span><span>Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI Feedback, Anthropic AI, 2022.12.15</span></span><br><br><br>（十一）关于价值重估<br><br>1.0<br>互联网让信息的接收成本趋近极小，生成式 AI 让信息的生产成本趋近极小。<br>到头来，现实本身变成信息瓶颈了（生活快不过拟真）。天下哪有那么多事。<br>除非你就是多事。<br><br>2.0<br>提示工程学家其实是一种保姆<br>他们的工作职责主要是给AI喂饭<br>然后看管它不要拉稀<br><br>3.0<br>用不了多久，互联网就会脱离人类，成为 AI 的数据交换通道了。<br>除非你想为 AI 另造一个互联网，为了保持人类信息的纯洁性。<br>等一等，这种说法，会不会有种族隔离的嫌疑啊？<br><br>4.0<br>王尔德说，只有浅薄的人才能完全了解自己<br>还好我们所仰赖的GPT们<br>对自己的了解还不够完全<br><br>5.0<br>郑重声明：我们生活的故事系列<br>由纯手工打造。<br>绝不含任何 AI 添加剂。<br><br><br>（十二）<br><br>在AI末世论甚嚣尘上的时候，著名风投机构a16z的创始人旗帜鲜明地发表了科技乐观主义宣言 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-13\" aria-label=\"Reference 13\">13</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">13</span><span>The Techno-Optimist Manifesto, a16z, 2023.10.16</span></span>，作为一个跟踪AI前沿发展的思想领袖（和从中受益的投资人），他显然觉得自己有责任和资格对那些捕风捉影的言论宣战。<br><br>开诚布公总是好的，我很高兴他能把话说这么明白。如果我诅咒他，至少不会诅咒他的诚实，尽管这种诚实也许是建立在对反对者不屑一顾的基础之上的。<br><br>我痛恨科技乐观主义者甚于精致利己主义者。后者的利己中至少有趋利避害的保守性（他们相信自己可能成为弱者，所以才需要利己），而前者已经无法无天到认为没有什么东西能够伤害他们了。<br><br>这种乐观主义之中至少有两点让我无法忍受。<br><br>其一是泛滥的精英的优越感。这群人宣称科技本身没有让人失业或带来不平等——而这种宣称是站在所谓“全人类”的视角的，也就是说，从人类历史发展的角度上“总的来看”。那么，又是谁给了你们这样的权利，让你们可以裁决（另）一群人的苦难可以被（你们自己）一群人的幸福所对冲呢？<br><br>第二点，更为可恨的，就是这个宣扬之中藏匿在字里行间的被庇护感。换句话讲，这群人从过往生活中打心底得到的确信就是，他们从来只可能从中获益，而万不会想到自己是有任何一丝可能成为被害者的；或即使情况再糟，他们也有办法让自己成为最后一批受害者（扎克伯格今年在夏威夷花费1亿美元修建地堡 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-14\" aria-label=\"Reference 14\">14</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">14</span><span>Inside Mark Zuckerberg’s Top-Secret Hawaii Compound, Wired, 2023.12.14</span></span>）。<br><br>就是这种仿佛坐在包厢中观看角斗场的残忍的从容让我恨不得爬上看台去给他们两巴掌。<br><br>但是很不幸，棋子和棋手就是天生地不对等。<br><br><br>（十三）<br><br>我们被引入一个昏暗的室内坐下，靠墙的地方叠放着一摞摞巨大的铁桶。店员点开显像管电视，和杂讯一起播出的是关于传统枫糖制造流程的短片：人们骑马在林子里到处转悠，把钉子嵌进树里，挂上铁桶，接住流出的枫糖水。隔过几周，又骑着马回去把满荡荡的桶子提回屋里，烧滚热的柴火，让糖水慢慢浓缩成糖浆。<br><br>而我旁边这个烟囱冲破房梁的机器就是枫糖制造机，因为并不是生产季，机器是冰冷的。<br><br>我问那个帮我们播放录影带的店员，你们也用这台机器制糖吗？<br><br>是的，古法制糖。显然这个房间在三十年前就是这个样子。<br><br>佛蒙特州大抵就是这么回事。好喝的牛奶，新鲜的农产品，著名的枫糖。酷暑消夏，秋日赏叶，冬来玩雪。<br><br>哪怕和现实的浪潮没有丝毫瓜葛，这里也并不存在什么被淘汰的危机、跟不上趟的焦虑、或者羞于启齿的无知。<br><br>我几乎要被这种牧歌感动了，直到我想起他们的先人。<br><br>他们之前也一定是科（zhí）技（mín）乐观主义者吧。<br><br><br>（十四）<br><br>有时我害怕回音<br>就像阳光打在身上太多次后<br>突然有一天你就觉得<br>阳光打在身上<br>也不过是那么回事<br>你又想 经历了那么多气候之后<br>真的 走哪条路还真都是一回事<br>无论遇见谁<br>也都是一回事<br><br>下午三点醒来<br>把百叶窗关得更紧了一点<br>窗外的树红彤彤的<br>阳光让万物的脸颊都微微发烫<br>只是没人说起这其实是一个<br>草木都在凋零的时刻<br>但影子慢慢移动 于是天色渐沉<br>而你只祈望你会梦见的事情<br>不要再和这个世界<br>有任何一点关系<br><br><br>（十五）<br><br>虚假的希望：AI提升生产力。<br>真正的希望：AI颠覆现有的生产方式。<br><br>在这个重塑的机遇中，人本主义将从消费主义的禁锢中被解放出来，我们会被逼着改变人类的生存境况、价值观和精神面貌，然后说不定呢，就可以拥抱真正“精神自由”的生活 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-15\" aria-label=\"Reference 15\">15</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">15</span><span>This life: secular faith and spiritual freedom, Martin Hägglund, 2019</span></span>。</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考信息列表\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考信息列表</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span>路西法的黄昏之歌，布莱希特，1917</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>昼之校夜之校，森山大道，2006</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span>不能承受的生命之轻，米兰.昆德拉，1984</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span>中国哲学简史，冯友兰，1948</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><a href=\"https://www.metmuseum.org/exhibitions/van-gogh-cypresses\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Van Gogh&#x27;s Cypresses</a>, MET exhibition, 2023</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span><a href=\"https://www.zhihu.com/column/p/27731058\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">高瀬司分析评论《新世纪福音战士》</a>，知乎专栏，2017.07.06</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span><a href=\"https://twitter.com/karpathy/status/1617979122625712128?lang=en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The hottest new programming language is English</a>, Andrej Karpathy on X.com, 2023.01.24</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://36kr.com/p/1179861751070979\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2021 少儿编程教育行业报告</a>，36氪，2021.04.12</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span><a href=\"https://info.deeplearning.ai/ai-cancer-diagnosis-advances-chatbots-work-the-drive-thru-chatgpt-racks-up-server-fees-image-generators-get-an-upgrade?ecid=ACsprvttSl1TBc7wfpRQ92lgEUrFvWZnEnlcUUDXXbFzug2eTNTo6JlQvjUw-Zrv8V3MYhrWcQJO\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Batch Aug 09 Edition</a>, Andrew Ng, 2023.08.09</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-10\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">10</span><span><a href=\"https://openai.com/blog/introducing-superalignment#IlyaSutskever\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Introducing Superalignment</a>, OpenAI Blog, 2023.07.05</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-11\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">11</span><span><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.03442\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Generative Agents: Interactive Simulacra of Human Behavior,</a> arxiv.org, 2023.04.07</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-12\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">12</span><span><a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/index/constitutional-ai-harmlessness-from-ai-feedback\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Constitutional AI: Harmlessness from AI Feedback</a>, Anthropic AI, 2022.12.15</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-13\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">13</span><span><a href=\"https://a16z.com/the-techno-optimist-manifesto/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Techno-Optimist Manifesto</a>, a16z, 2023.10.16</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-14\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">14</span><span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/story/mark-zuckerberg-inside-hawaii-compound/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Inside Mark Zuckerberg’s Top-Secret Hawaii Compound,</a> Wired, 2023.12.14</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-15\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">15</span><span>This life: secular faith and spiritual freedom, Martin Hägglund, 2019</span></li></ol></section>","source":"./content/posts/2023-12-28_我们生活的故事03.md"},{"id":"2023-05-27_a-tale-of-three-cities","date":"2023-05-27","title":"A Tale of Three Cities","titleZh":"三城记","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"The instant I stepped through the cabin door, the humid heat struck me full in the face—a thunderclap that jolted awake my memory of the tropics. Early summer had barely begun, but…","excerptZh":"仅在踏出舱门的一瞬间，扑面而来的潮热就惊雷般震醒了我关于热带的记忆：尽管刚迈入初夏，珠三角也并不温柔。 作为一个并未深思熟虑的旅行计划，我们将在接下来的…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2023-05-27_三城记.jpg","tags":["travel log","china"],"tagsZh":["游记","中国"],"readingMin":9,"htmlEn":"<p>The instant I stepped through the cabin door, the humid heat struck me full in the face—a thunderclap that jolted awake my memory of the tropics. Early summer had barely begun, but the Pearl River Delta was already in no gentle mood.<br><br>The trip had not been especially well thought out. Over the next four days, we would race through three cities with permanent populations above ten million: Guangzhou, Shenzhen, Dongguan. Moving from one to the next in temperatures above thirty degrees was hardly ideal. Yet it was precisely this relentless motion that laid bare the startling differences in modernity among three cities no more than a two-hour drive apart. At eighty kilometers an hour, those differences hit hard enough to shatter the idealized fiction of uniformity.<br><br>Diversity is inevitable and fascinating in any age—even when the entire Pearl River Delta is a vanguard of reform and opening-up, even when all of us across this vast land have sworn to live by the twenty-four characters of the Core Socialist Values.<br><br><br><br><strong>Guangzhou: Inertia</strong><br><br>From a pedestrian bridge, I looked toward the headquarters of Guangdong Radio and Television. The low-saturation gold lettering on its facade—unmistakably the product of another era—and its magenta trim had both been steamed pale by the weather. In front stood the sign for the Xinyuexin Hotel. Two of the hotel&#x27;s five Chinese characters mean “new”: forty percent of its name loudly insisting on the opposite of reality.<br><br>Looking back, Tianxiu Building rose proudly at the far end of Xiaobei Road. At the narrow entrance to the B2 Trade Center on its southeast corner, a man had kicked off his flip-flops and settled cross-legged onto a plastic stool. By appearances, he ran at least three businesses. Huge canvas bags and counterfeit YSL handbags hung above his head. His counter advertised MONEY EXCHANGE in English and Arabic, with pictures of eight kinds of SIM card underneath.<br><br>Yet the glass case before him held only a roll of tape, a motorcycle helmet, tissues, a lunchbox, and toothpicks. Perhaps these disclosed his fourth line of business: living. (Man and building alike: Tianxiu indeed—quite the heavenly show.)<br><br>Across the bridge, the lanes of Baohan Straight Street throbbed with life. Libi Road Trading Company sold bright North African robes. A woman in a headscarf sat on the back of an electric scooter, choosing naan from a vendor who had set up in the road. Electronic music with a hard, insistent beat sounded from a halal canteen. A Black man spoke loudly on his phone in a fruit shop. Middle-school students walked home, every one of them staring down at a screen. Take a random glance down any path branching off the main street and, where almost no sunlight entered, still more things were growing: wires, hanging laundry, and loud red signs—for rooms to rent, sex toys, the welfare lottery.<br><br>Guangzhou lives by a rule: accommodate difference; prize harmony above all. It is both a practical creed for this motley cast and the safety notice painted on the wall by the Dengfeng Subdistrict Office.<br><br>Ever since the opening of the Five Treaty Ports, Guangzhou&#x27;s cosmopolitanism has been beyond dispute. But it is no Western mansion erected from nothing. It is the Sino-Western <em>qilou</em>, the arcaded shophouse that grew from the deep historical soil of Lingnan. Guangzhou&#x27;s cosmopolitanism is also its localism.<br><br>In an old city that has never been rebooted, the shock of modernity does not arrive like wildfire sweeping a wilderness clean. It is more like silt settling on a riverbed already thick with sediment. This is the source of Guangzhou&#x27;s inertia: it cannot be remade overnight; modernity can only seep in drop by drop, wherever an old way of doing things begins to give.<br><br>Bansha herbal tea will not disappear, but it can be bottled in plastic and made portable. The BabiBoy and NewBaiLun shops of Shangxiajiu will not disappear, but they accept WeChat and Alipay, too…<br><br>Should Guangzhou change faster? Put differently: has history become a drag on the city? If modernity is our only measure, then all we seem able to see is time gnawing mercilessly at this pioneer region of reform and opening-up—faded buildings, narrow streets, chaotic urban villages…<br><br>But might this tangled profusion also be the source of the city&#x27;s composure?<br><br>Riding through Guangzhou, I heard the navigation prompt again and again: “Make a U-turn, then turn right.” The driver had not taken a wrong turn. This was simply the compromise a busy city had been forced to make in its traffic planning. Imperfect, but a solution of sorts.<br><br>Then there is the far greater U-turn (the one called “macro-control”!). A U-turn like that can sometimes have no solution. We may see not just a few households or factories but perhaps an entire city lose all appetite for life. Historically, Guangzhou has indeed had little reason to fear this—perhaps precisely because its disorder has given it a composite identity too elusive to define.<br><br>Guangzhou will not become better in the blink of an eye. For the same reason, it cannot become worse very quickly.<br><br>For China&#x27;s younger cities, Guangzhou offers a model of how to become unwieldy—and how to grow old at ease.<br><br><br><br><strong>Shenzhen: Fractal</strong><br><br>Most of Shekou has become a thoroughly pleasant place to live.<br><br>High-rise apartments and shopping centers are everywhere, while taller, newer, grander buildings keep rising from the ground. The China Merchants History Museum is half-hidden in one of the area&#x27;s rare patches of woodland. Apart from the words “Time is Money, Efficiency is Life” written on a decorative wall at its northwest corner, facing the traffic, almost nothing in the building&#x27;s artful exterior reveals the daring, enterprising, rough-hewn spirit that should have surged through the first experimental zone opened to the outside world after the founding of the People&#x27;s Republic.<br><br>Yet on the other side of Nanshan, the Port of Shenzhen—the third-busiest in China and the fourth-busiest in the world—works day and night. That is another Shenzhen altogether.<br><br>Built from scratch as a Special Economic Zone, Shenzhen carried no burden from the beginning. It had only one task: to stand on the front line of China&#x27;s reform and opening-up. It has performed that task well. It squandered none of its geographic advantages, missed no major transformation, and became China&#x27;s most influential Special Economic Zone.<br><br>Shenzhen possesses almost everything other cities envy. The answer it offers is therefore idealized as well.<br><br>Leaving Shekou, we drove north along Shahe West Road and then east. After crossing the Beijing–Hong Kong–Macao Expressway, Binhai Boulevard became Binhe Boulevard, carrying us from Nanshan District into Futian, Shenzhen&#x27;s central district. (Had we continued straight, we would have entered Luohu, the earliest-developed district in the Shenzhen SEZ.) All along, off the car&#x27;s starboard side lay the silent surface of Shenzhen Bay and, farther off, Hong Kong. To the left, a filmstrip of residential–leisure–commercial districts played on repeat. They were so orderly, so consistent, that without a few landmark buildings I would have struggled to tell these separate but functionally similar areas apart.<br><br>I had seen this same kind of urban settlement in Tokyo, a city made from “multiple centers,” though Shenzhen&#x27;s pattern is far simpler. Yes—you cannot call Shenzhen a city. It has none of my hometown&#x27;s growth rings, spreading outward one circle at a time, nor Paris&#x27;s radial nerves extending across all of France. Shenzhen&#x27;s urban blocks are like a motif a composer keeps returning to in variation: separate from one another yet repeated again and again, they establish the key of the entire work.<br><br>And Shenzhen&#x27;s key is functionality.<br><br>Every building has its function. Every municipal facility has its function. Every person has their function. Once all of them have been made modular, Shenzhen can be assembled neighborhood by neighborhood, like <em>Minecraft</em>.<br><br>“Commercial–leisure–residential”: this may be the smallest unit to which the needs of social life can be reduced. It is also the practical futurism produced by Shenzhen&#x27;s “fractal.” Shenzhen is a city, but also a clustered tribe of cities. Its neighborhoods resemble one another as though they could be transplanted at any moment and made to fit anywhere in the city.<br><br>Shenzhen can be replicated. Yet the Shenzhen Special Economic Zone is so ideal, so singular, that it is fundamentally impossible to replicate.<br><br><br><br><strong>Dongguan: Negative Space</strong><br><br>On the eve of May 20, the Global Economic and Trade Center in Dongguan&#x27;s central business district lit up from top to bottom. Across its neon tubes scrolled a message:<br><br>Warmly celebrating World Metrology Day. —Dongguan Municipal Administration for Market Regulation<br><br>The other towers scattered around the central plaza had yet to catch up with the occasion. On conspicuous red or blue backgrounds, they left strings of phone numbers and a message: Advertising space available. Call for inquiries.<br><br>Friday, the eve of May 20. Dongguan remained perfectly still.<br><br>The next day, we visited Keyuan Garden. From its third-floor pavilion, looking south, the Venus Royal Hotel (Dongguan Keyuan) lorded over the far bank of the canal—royal treatment for just over RMB 200. To the east, two buildings with a distinctly Soviet-futurist air faced one another from afar. The red-brick building wore a hat shaped like the Chinese character 丰. The Dongguan Overseas Chinese Hotel wore two satellite receivers, open to the sky.<br><br>They stood there in the 2020s as though suspended inside an immense block of amber. Parts of Dongguan resemble a Pompeii never buried in volcanic ash: before our eyes, they sit motionless on the earth, no longer growing.<br><br>This is Dongguan. It has a central business district of glittering glass towers, and it has the wildest landmarks imaginable, seemingly built back in the last century just as China&#x27;s modernization was beginning. Between the two, however, there appears to be a hole in Dongguan&#x27;s history.<br><br>Bada Road, only a five-minute drive from the city center, is lined with wholesalers of hardware and electromechanical supplies: gears, screws, valves, cables… Apart from Huali Market, which serves as a gathering point, the shops spread across the ground floors of roadside buildings and seep into every branching lane. Together, these storefronts form the base layer of Dongguan&#x27;s identity as a manufacturing powerhouse. At the same time, in countless details, they make this city of more than ten million resemble a Chinese county town.<br><br>Not even that.<br><br>In spirit, Dongguan lacks even the hum of street life found in small county towns. It seems to have been stripped of every lived-in quality. Shenzhen&#x27;s functionality still accommodates—and creates—a futuristic way of life. Dongguan&#x27;s functionality is purer by far: it is almost nothing but a machine built to run.<br><br>Dongguan is incomplete. Its advertising spaces wait to be filled, just as Dongguan waits for real life to arrive.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>仅在踏出舱门的一瞬间，扑面而来的潮热就惊雷般震醒了我关于热带的记忆：尽管刚迈入初夏，珠三角也并不温柔。<br><br>作为一个并未深思熟虑的旅行计划，我们将在接下来的四天时间里匆匆略过三座常住人口超过千万的城市：广州，深圳，东莞。在三十多度的暑气中辗转，这样的旅途也许差强人意；但正是在这不停歇的游历中，这三座相互车程不超过两小时的城市间令人惊异的现代性之差，才得以以时速八十公里的冲击，把那种理想化的同一性建构撞得粉碎。<br><br>无论在什么时代，多样性都是必然且迷人的，哪怕整个珠三角都隶属改革开放的先行地，哪怕这片广袤土地上所有的我们都已誓言遵循二十四字的核心价值观。<br><br><br><br><strong>广州惯性</strong><br><br>从天桥眺望广东广播电视台总部，那楼面上充满年代感的低饱和度的金色字体和洋红的外墙装饰已被这蒸笼般的天气熏得褪色。它的更前面立着的新粤新酒店的招牌，这个店名中重复强调的40%的成分正和现实情况完全相反。<br><br>往后看去，天秀大厦在小北路的另一头昂然挺立。这座大厦一楼的东南角有一个B2商贸城的窄窄的入口，那里一个男人脱掉拖鞋盘脚坐在塑料凳上：表面看去，他至少经营着三样业务——他的头顶挂着巨大的帆布袋子和仿冒的YSL提包；他的柜台上用英文和阿拉伯文写着“货币兑换”；而这些文字下面打印着八种SIM卡的图像。<br><br>但他身前的玻璃柜台里却只放着一卷胶带、一顶摩托车头盔、纸巾、饭盒和牙签。这也许说明了他的第四种业务：生活。（人如此楼，果真天秀）<br><br>跨过天桥，宝汉直街的巷道里热闹非凡。利比路商行贩卖着北非鲜艳的褂子，戴头巾的女人坐在电瓶车后座上挑选着占道经营的馕饼，清真食堂中传出节拍分明的电子音乐，黑人在水果店里大声打电话，中学生放学回家，他们走路也全都低头看着手机。随机瞥往一个从主街分叉的小径，几乎不透进阳光的地方正生长着更多的电线、晾晒衣服和响亮的红色招牌：租房信息、情趣用品、福利彩票。<br><br>广州讲究“多元包容，以和为贵”：这既是这些形形色色的人物的处世信条，也是登峰街道办事处涂在墙上的安全须知。<br><br>自五口通商开始，广州的国际化就已无可置疑。但这种国际化并不是凭空造起的洋房，而是从这片悠悠历史的岭南土壤中生长出的中西结合的骑楼——广州的国际化也是本土化的。<br><br>对于一个没有历经重启的古老城市，现代性的冲击并不以莽原上的野火般燃尽一切的方式出现，却仿佛是在本就厚重的河床上继续堆积泥沙，广州由此显现出某种惯性：它不会被一夜之间重塑，只是在每一个传统方式松动的地方点点滴滴地被渗入。<br><br>癍痧凉茶不会消失，但可以选择灌装在塑料瓶中方便携带；上下九的BabiBoy和NewBaiLun不会消失，但它们也接受微信和支付宝的汇款……<br><br>广州是否应该拥有更快速的改变？换句话说，广州是否被其历史所拖累？如果仅仅以现代性来衡量，我们似乎只能看到这片改革开放先行区被时光无情啃噬的一面：褪色的楼宇，狭窄的街道，混乱的城中村……<br><br>但这种芜杂是否也同时是这座城市从容的源头？<br><br>在广州乘车，导航中无数次传出”调头后请右转“的提示，哪怕司机并未走错道路——这是这个繁忙的城市在交通规划上必然做出的妥协。不够完美，但算一种解决方式。<br><br>而还有一种更为巨大的调头（名为“宏观调控“的调头！）。那样的调头有时是无解的，我们看到不仅是几户人家，几个工厂，而也许是一整个城市全然丧失掉自己的生存热情——广州从历史上看，确实没有这样的忧虞——也许正是因为它的混乱，给了它难以界定的复合身份。<br><br>广州很难转眼变得更好，但也因此无法很快变坏。<br><br>广州为中国更为年轻的城市们提供了这样一个样本，即如何生长得棘手，如何让自己从容地老去。<br><br><br><br><strong>深圳分形</strong><br><br>蛇口的大部分地方完全成为了惬意的生活区。<br><br>这里林立各式高层公寓和购物中心，还不断有更高更新更雄伟的建筑拔地而起。招商局历史博物馆掩映在这片区域罕见的林地中，除去其西北角正对车流的装饰墙上题写的“时间就是金钱，效率就是生命”，你再难从这座充满艺术气息外观的建筑中窥见这新中国成立后对外开放的第一个试验区所本该喷薄的勇猛、进取和草莽的精神。<br><br>而就在南山的另一面，中国第三、全球第四繁忙的深圳港正夜以继日地工作，那又是一个完全不同的深圳。<br><br>作为从零开始的经济特区，深圳从一开始就没有任何负担，它只需做好一件事，成为中国的改革开放的前沿。这么些年，深圳一直做得很好。它没有浪费自己的区位优势，它没有错过任何重大的转型机会，它是中国影响力最大的经济特区。<br><br>深圳几乎拥有其他城市羡慕的一切，因此深圳给出的答案也是理想化的。<br><br>离开蛇口，从沙河西路北上然后一路东行，滨海大道在穿过京港澳高速后变成滨河大道，我们从南山区行车抵达深圳的中心城区福田 （若继续向前便可以进入罗湖，深圳经济特区中最早开发的城区）。车子的右舷始终是深圳湾沉默的海面和更远处的香港，左舷则反复播放着“居民-休闲-商业区”的城市业态。它们在观感上是如此井井有条而具有一致性，如果不是依赖某些具有地标特色的建筑，我将很难分辨出这些相互间隔而功能相似的区域。<br><br>我在东京见过这种同样的，以“复数式的中心”构成的城市聚落（尽管深圳远比东京简洁）——是的，你无法把深圳称作一个城市——它不似我的故乡般有着年轮一样一圈圈长大的发展轨迹，也不似巴黎般有着放射状的连结整个法兰西的神经触须。深圳不同的城市区块就仿佛是某个作曲家在作品中变奏的动机，它们被相互区隔，不断反复，奠定着整部作品的基调。<br><br>而深圳的基调就是功能性。<br><br>每栋建筑有每栋建筑的功能；每个市政设施有每个市政设施的功能；每个人有每个人的功能。一旦这些东西被模块化，我们就可以如“我的世界”一般把深圳一个街区一个街区地组装起来。<br><br>“商业-休闲-居住”，这应该就是社会生活需求所能精简到的最小单位，而这也是深圳的”分形“所建构出的充满现实意义的未来感：深圳是城市，也是一簇城市的部落；深圳的街区彼此相似，仿佛随时可以被移植，并适应这个城市的任何方位。<br><br>深圳是可复制的，但深圳特区是这么理想这么独特，这又使得它从根本上不可能被复制。<br><br><br><br><strong>东莞留白</strong><br><br>5月20日前夜，东莞市中央CBD的环球经贸中心把整个大楼点亮，那些霓虹灯管上滚动出这样的祝福：<br><br>热烈庆祝世界计量日。——东莞市市场监管局<br><br>这片中心广场区域其他稀稀落落地耸立着的其他大厦则还没有跟上热点，它们用或红或蓝的显眼背景留下一串手机号：广告位招商请致电。<br><br>星期五，5月20日前夜，东莞波澜不兴。<br><br>第二日，我们游览可园。站在三层阁楼向南眺望，维纳斯皇家酒店雄踞于运河南岸（仅需两百出头的皇家礼遇）；往东看，两幢颇具苏联未来主义风格的建筑遥相对望：红砖楼大厦戴着一个“丰”字型的帽子，东莞华侨大酒店则头顶两个向天空展开的卫星信号接收器。<br><br>它们这样屹立在21世纪20年代的年头里，就像是被某种巨大的琥珀凝固住了。东莞的某些地区就像是没有被火山灰覆盖的庞贝，我们眼睁睁地看着它们在地表静止着，停止生长。<br><br>东莞就是这样。它有着最为闪亮的全玻璃大楼的中央商业区，也有仿佛是上世纪中国刚刚开始现代化进程时所修建的最为天马行空的地标。但在这两者中间，东莞的历史好像存在一个窟窿。<br><br>在距离市中心仅五分钟车程的八达路上，两侧林林总总地排布五金和机电的批发生意：齿轮、螺丝、阀门、线缆……除去一个作为聚集地的华利市场，这些商店就平铺在这条街两侧楼房的底层，并渗进各个分叉的巷道中。这些铺面构成了东莞作为制造业重镇的底色，但它们也同时使这个人口超过千万的城市在很多细节上无比近似一座中国的县城。<br><br>甚至不如。<br><br>东莞在精神上缺失着哪怕在小县城里也常见的市井的气息，东莞像是完全被剥离了生活化的属性。如果说深圳的功能性还兼顾和形成着一座城市的“未来感”的生活方式，东莞的功能性则远为纯粹：它几乎仅就是一台用来运行的机器。<br><br>东莞并不完整。东莞的广告展位等待被填满，正如东莞等待真正的生活降临。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2023-05-27_三城记.md"},{"id":"2022-11-27_the-stories-we-live-02","date":"2022-11-27","title":"The Stories We Live 02","titleZh":"我们生活的故事02","subtitle":"Or: We Are All the Same","subtitleZh":"又：大家都一样","excerpt":"(Prologue) The world is still feeling for the bottom. After trudging across a pandemic wasteland, people greeted the first glimmer of light with delight—only to watch dawn harden into day and throw…","excerptZh":"（序） 世界向下探底。在瘟疫的砂漠中，疲于跋涉的人们欣喜地迎接曙光，直到这曙光变成昭彰的白日。遍地的狼藉和罪恶于是显出耀眼的清晰。 无数有关于正义的对决…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2022-11-27_我们生活的故事02.jpg","tags":["our stories"],"tagsZh":["我们生活的故事"],"readingMin":25,"htmlEn":"<p>(Prologue)<br><br>The world is still feeling for the bottom. After trudging across a pandemic wasteland, people greeted the first glimmer of light with delight—only to watch dawn harden into day and throw the wreckage and evil all around us into dazzling relief.<br><br>In one showdown over justice after another, people sort friend from foe by allegiance, with or without the inconvenience of thought. A hard wind blows from the west, yet whoever is tending the stove still finds the fire too weak. The most important thing, after all, has yet to catch.<br><br>Across a continent swaddled in faith, those dressed in morality feel neither cold nor hunger. At the lighthouse across the water, PVP enthusiasts are busy outdoing one another.<br><br>Wave after wave of information has broken over us. Now exquisite shells replay the sound of surf recorded long ago. Carefully chosen, manufactured objects left along the beach, they look as though someone had arranged them there before the tide ever came in.<br><br>Almost without our noticing, outer space and the virtual world have become two more betting grounds worth tens of billions of dollars. The bidders spend with breathtaking abandon, as if any life would do so long as it was not the one being lived here and now.<br><br>We have been told countless times that we are standing at the edge of a cliff. Yet every act that seems certain to send us over somehow pushes the edge a little farther away.<br><br>That terrifies some people. It tempts others to lie in wait.<br><br><br>(1)<br><br>Determined to avoid the toll highway on the way to the airport, my Uber driver expertly threaded us through a maze of side streets, emerged onto a jammed main road, and successfully added fifteen minutes to our ETA. He seemed embarrassed by the result, so he struck up a conversation.<br><br>Naturally, he began with gas prices, then moved on to the misery and meager returns of driving for a ride-hailing service—until a sleek electric car cut into our lane, forced him to slam on the brakes, and ended the subject with what may have been a curse in Spanish.<br><br>Pointing at the thing that had just caused him trouble, he asked, “You know why I don&#x27;t like cars these days?”<br><br>“Why?”<br><br>“They&#x27;re all electronic systems now. They get more sophisticated and more prone to failure at the same time. I was a mechanic for twenty years, and I can&#x27;t even fix my own car. Every time something goes wrong, the repair costs are fucking insane. Just like this world.”<br><br>Just like this world.<br><br>He was exactly right. Ever since the arrival of automatic transmission, cars and life alike have offered us less and less of a sense of control. Most of the time, these increasingly elaborate systems do us a great deal of good. Then one day, through no fault of our own, something breaks and leaves us with a staggering repair bill—and of course we have every reason to blame the people who made cars no longer feel like cars!<br><br><br>(2)<br><br>The year generative AI entered the arts, it had little concrete social impact beyond making yet another group of people vaguely anxious about losing their jobs. Even so, the breach it opened forced us once again to confront an immense transition that will soon seep into every part of life. Its disruption may reach all the way into thought itself: into the way human beings assess their own worth.<br><br>In an interview, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman described an unexpected pattern in AI&#x27;s development. Only ten years earlier, the consensus held that AI would replace untrained blue-collar labor first, then low-level repetitive white-collar work, and creative white-collar work last. In practice, the sequence has been almost exactly reversed. AI first made inroads into fields that seemed extraordinarily complex: self-driving, for example, and artistic creation <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>AI for the Next Era, OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman on the New Frontiers of AI, Greylock, 2022-09-13.</span></span>.<br><br>The pattern looks baffling until we change our angle of view. Then it becomes almost inevitable. In these emerging fields, both inputs and outputs can be made highly legible as data. Give researchers a logically coherent digital pathway and enough training data, and AI can learn how to map one to the other. In other words, the bottleneck is not the complexity of the model—the algorithms are evolving anyway—but whether the system can be given enough to eat.<br><br>From this perspective, the conventional difficulty of a job has little bearing on how replaceable it is. What matters is whether enough data has accumulated at the outset. An industry with enough inertia, or barriers high enough to keep digitization at bay, may prove the hardest thing for AI to consume.<br><br>Then again, faced with a technology almost certain to reward first movers, who would dare refuse digitization?<br><br><br>(3)<br><br>My own journey into digitization began in 2014, when I downloaded Evernote&#x27;s Chinese service, Yinxiang Biji, simply so I could edit and consult my reading notes across multiple platforms. In 2015, I signed up for iCloud after photos filled my phone. Then, in 2019, I learned that even hard drives had a finite shelf life. I subscribed to Office 365 and finally moved everything still marooned on external drives—including a first-grade composition called “A Turtle”—into the cloud.<br><br>This year, I began seriously considering a more comprehensive system for collecting personal information: some way to record what I see and hear, at a finer level of detail, on one platform or application—or at least on a limited number of them. This impulse naturally has something to do with my profession. Data scientists often invoke the line “You are what you measure,” or its variant, “If you can&#x27;t measure it, you can&#x27;t improve it” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>You Are What You Measure, Forbes, 2018-12-04.</span></span>.<br><br>I spent about a month surveying the options and eventually settled on Notion, an integrated document application with ambitions that extend far beyond documents. Then I devoted two weekends to moving in every document and file I had scattered across other platforms.<br><br>The popular app, backed by a funding round led by Sequoia Capital, introduced relational databases into what had previously been flat documents and notes with its 2.0 release. Users could also configure different views, changing the way the same information appeared <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Augmenting Human Intellect, No Code Required, Sequoia Capital Stories, 2022-10-13.</span></span>.<br><br>Inside the system Notion creates, what used to be the core of a document—its content—becomes only one component of the thing called a “document.” Metadata and content are treated as equals. Just as important are the interactions, combinations, and display logic among documents.<br><br>In other words, once the old content-centered divisions break down, the document finally reveals the property it shares with everything else that has been digitized: it is information.<br><br><br>(4)<br><br>Before digital storage and distribution, information was difficult to separate from the thing that carried it. Music was music, painting was painting, writing was writing. Then Marshall McLuhan declared that “the medium is the message,” and we trained our attention on the routes through which information travels. The channel loomed larger; the far greater mass of information itself receded from view.<br><br>This year, text-to-image generative AI entered public view, and people marveled at the models&#x27; “imagination”—another case of being deceived by differences on the surface. These systems have not “created” anything. They have merely <strong>translated</strong> information received through one channel into another, leaving the information itself unchanged.<br><br>By the same logic, a large-scale recommender system does little more than construct two functions, projecting every customer&#x27;s preferences and every product&#x27;s attributes into the same vector space—an embedding. To the machine operating in that space, customer preference and product attribute are not fundamentally different. Both are information recorded along the same dimensions.<br><br>Even within our existing systems of encoding, I have no doubt that AI understands information in a form closer to its native state than human consciousness does. Only at the level of philosophy or physics—if we imagine the universe as one gigantic flow of information, or entropy—do things shed their endlessly varied forms of existence and reveal their underlying sameness.<br><br>“What we want to demonstrate is that individual, singular, specifically human experience is no more than a surface shimmer over immense formal systems.” Foucault&#x27;s statement was aimed at the human being. Why should it not be aimed just as squarely at everything else in the world?<br><br><br>(5)<br><br>Among all our attempts to think about “the end,” science fiction is an unavoidable summit. One of the two multiyear plans I set myself this year was to read and understand the genre systematically.<br><br>Venture capitalists plan from “the future&#x27;s endpoint”—reasoning backward is fashionable: within ten years, most people will... Speculative fiction gives us “the future unfolding” instead. It cuts the tether to concrete reality. Within a larger and more comprehensive uncertainty, the fantasist is free to explore, romantically, every possible future for human civilization, not merely every possibility for technological development.<br><br>The difference between agricultural and modern civilization cannot be measured by horizontal expansion or a linear increase in complexity. By the same token, a civilization capable of colonizing other worlds must possess a <strong>philosophical</strong> framework fundamentally different from our own. Ideas such as the chain of suspicion and the dark forest are forms of reason and morality produced by thought on a <strong>cosmic scale</strong>. They do not belong to the conceptual world of the present at all.<br><br>The most fundamental difference between stories that might unfold across the universe and stories set on Earth is scale. Even at the speed of light, a journey to a neighboring star system would be measured in centuries—and how many technological explosions might fit inside that span? Like classical mechanics, everything human knowledge has built so far applies only within a range of intermediate scales. To investigate what lies beyond that range is to make possible a wholesale reconstruction of our understanding of the world.<br><br>Nor is this the sole responsibility of science-fiction writers. The project has been moving slowly, but it began long ago. I have always thought certain minds can be honed until they perceive modes of motion beyond the ordinary wavelengths. As Charlie Munger and Warren Buffett grow older, they seem ever less constrained; some marathon runners have the faces of ascetics.<br><br>“Blindness is the farthest one can go in illustrating; it is seeing what appears out of Allah&#x27;s own blackness” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>My Name Is Red, Orhan Pamuk.</span></span>. To be honest, I rather like the task.<br><br><br>(6)<br><br>After years of preparation and adjustment <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>James Webb Space Telescope, Wikipedia.</span></span>, the James Webb Space Telescope finally lived up to expectations, sending back one exquisite image of deep space after another. They are dazzling and still, nothing like the universe itself, with its vast emptiness and dark currents in motion. That is precisely what makes them so awe-inspiring. Slice open the tiniest, least conspicuous corner of that immense vault, and it already discloses an infinity we will never understand.<br><br>Whenever I look at these images, I think that this instrument assembled from precision lenses and frontier technology is really no different from the coin-operated telescopes installed for tourists on skyscraper rooftops. Both draw the farthest landscapes near enough to satisfy our irrepressible urge to peep.<br><br>So far, we have been extraordinarily lucky. Everywhere our eyes can reach, the universe has offered us nothing remarkable or noteworthy beyond sparse but omnipresent darkness and scenery of breathtaking beauty. Yet it is easy to imagine that one day those digital signals really will return an extraordinary image. At that moment, all humanity may feel like a pervert who has just caught sight of an illicit act: panicked, embarrassed, and satisfied all at once.<br><br><br>(7)<br><br>Amid geopolitical upheaval and the latent threat of stagflation, British internet users—with their hereditary gift for cheer—joked that everything now looked just like the British disease of the 1970s, only without the good music <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>Post, Sickipedia, 2022-06-27.</span></span>.<br><br>Maybe the good music is out there. It is simply harder to find, because today&#x27;s most popular music is no longer made for the ear alone.<br><br>Since the rise of multimedia, music has increasingly attached itself to settings that recruit the eye as well: nightclubs, with EDM, or short-form video, with its earworm hits. Music can thus be “bundled” with information traveling through a more efficient channel—visual information accounts for more than seventy percent of everything humans absorb through the senses—and together they mutate into monsters of viral transmission.<br><br>More broadly, <strong>Remixing</strong>, one of Kevin Kelly&#x27;s twelve forces of the internet in <em>The Inevitable</em> <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>The Inevitable, Kevin Kelly, 2016.</span></span>, also manifests in the convergence of art forms. It rejects the low-density meal of a single channel. Remixing raises the density, and three-dimensional sensory stimulation is naturally harder to resist.<br><br>Look beyond rhythm-heavy dance music and mediocre virtual-reality headsets. What comes next?<br><br>Us, longing to be filled with information.<br><br><br>(8)<br><br>After years of extravagant spending, Bilibili has finally begun importing fewer new seasonal anime from Japan <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>“B站需要新番” (“Bilibili Needs New Anime”), 36Kr, 2022-07-27.</span></span>. Anime fans are understandably unhappy for sentimental reasons, yet from every angle the trend appears both reasonable and difficult to reverse. Fortunately, I had already left the anime world before China&#x27;s licensing market began its decline.<br><br>I did not leave because I had grown sick of anime, or because of any stereotype aimed at its fans. My “graduation” was a natural drifting-away. Now that seasonal releases are dominated by moe-inflected comfort shows and overpowered-protagonist isekai, anime can no longer give me the particular joy I once went there to find.<br><br>Nor is anime alone. Most entertainment today contains no surprise: formulaic plots, characters legible at a glance. Had Oscar Wilde sampled today&#x27;s diversions, I suspect he would have been bored to death.<br><br>I have always harbored this prejudice: people&#x27;s tastes do, in fact, occupy dramatically different levels. After all, taste reveals the means by which a person obtains pleasure. That is why I feel sorry for young people today. Unless they make a strenuous effort to search, nearly every form of entertainment placed directly before them is devoid of nutrition—or else they make no attempt to watch and experience it in a way that might yield any. Production and consumption reinforce each other in a cycle that degrades both. Breaking it would require less addictive forms of entertainment and an entertainment industry willing to accept poorer returns. That world line, naturally, has no appeal whatsoever.<br><br>A narrative founded on hope is the finest narrative humanity can possess. But even if we relinquish hope, decadence and mere time-killing remain worlds apart; their energy densities are nothing alike. What gratifies me is that my nostalgia does not arise solely from the intimacy of childhood and private experience. These works genuinely belong to an explosive golden age, and in every meaningful sense they withstand time better than the art being released today. They expanded the reach of human ideals, imagination, and possibility—even the possible ways to die.<br><br>In the middle of the year, an article titled “When the Balance Sheet No Longer Wants to Strive” caused a considerable stir online <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>“当资产负债表不想奋斗了” (“When the Balance Sheet No Longer Wants to Strive”), Yuanchuan Research Institute, 2022-05-31.</span></span>. My own view is limited to the narrow aperture of the anime industry, but the process by which Japan&#x27;s Lost Decades warped a nation&#x27;s spirit and then hollowed it out offers a profound lesson. It inspires grief and warning in equal measure, especially now that young people in China have begun to catch the philosophy of lying flat.<br><br>And so, inevitably, some self-appointed visionary burdened with responsibility stepped forward to proclaim: “The times are calling for low-oxygen talent!” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-10\" aria-label=\"Reference 10\">10</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">10</span><span>“时代呼唤低需氧人才” (“The Times Are Calling for Low-Oxygen Talent”), Ruan Junhua, executive vice dean of Zhejiang University&#x27;s School of In…</span></span><br><br>Who the fuck are you to call for that?<br><br><br>(9)<br><br>When an employee pitches a new idea to the boss, the pitch usually needs a grand narrative linking it to the company&#x27;s vision and the direction of the industry. A photographer&#x27;s inspiration, by contrast, arrives in a minute and utterly concrete instant. It demands alertness and an eye for the smallest detail.<br><br>I am often astonished by the different forms the same thing takes when projected through those two perspectives.<br><br>Life is so complex that whenever we step even slightly farther back than the people who lived it, whenever we compress a stretch of history into a sentence or two, whenever we perform a “dimensionality reduction” on events, the result invariably smooths away individual difference: overall, things improve steadily; by and large, people grow happier; all told, we have made an enormous leap. This, after all, is about as far as humanity&#x27;s statistical tools can reach.<br><br>But human compassion should not hover a thousand meters overhead and look down.<br><br><br>(10) Three Short Poems<br><br>I · To Those in the Shelter<br>Before you tell me what I ought to do<br>finish everything within your own power<br>Grand ideals can move me too<br>but before the long race is run, don&#x27;t rush to teach me your stride<br>adapted to every road<br>why grow cautious<br>every nation has its own book of verse<br><br><br>II · To Those Who Hold Sway<br>Who knows which god devised this plan:<br>one side refines chlorine gas to ever greater purity<br>the other, metallic sodium to ever greater purity<br>Wait—I&#x27;m only a commoner asking for a little salt<br>Crows can never resist pecking at other animals&#x27; tails<br>Who, then, is the crow?<br>Every history has its own Kitchen Debate<br><br><br>III · To the Clear-Eyed Journey<br>Emotional release is the easy glide of surfing<br>Seeking truth is the hard ascent<br>Part the fog: behind your lines stand benighted scoundrels and ignorant saints<br>(they are not easily told apart)<br>Even if the torch never passes near me<br>I do my best to stay dry<br>Does that really do any good?<br>Every generation has its own Long March<br><br><br>(11)<br><br>For more than a decade, from high school to the present, almost all the literature I have read has been Western or Japanese. Apart from a few classical Chinese novels, I have made no real effort with Chinese literature. That is cause for guilt, and it has left me at a distance from the culture&#x27;s habits of thought.<br><br>On this point, I am humble. I cannot casually pronounce on everything that happens before I have entered its cultural context. To do so would disrespect the weight of this nation&#x27;s history and belittle thought itself—my own included.<br><br>Besides, as an outsider, I cannot live these days of unceasing upheaval firsthand. In only a few more years, the clock measuring my wandering abroad will catch up with the time I spent living in my homeland. For someone away from home, the motherland can only grow more distant and unfamiliar. There is sorrow in that, but it also affords the vantage point of an observer.<br><br>Yet no matter how the angle changes, a person spends an entire life searching for somewhere to belong. For the flesh and bone handed down to me across thousands of years, I now require myself to begin at the beginning with this epic, sweeping as the Yellow River. I require myself to know again, and systematically, this loess earth and the people who live upon it.<br><br>It is a formidable task. Let us begin here.<br><br><br>(12)<br><br>This summer, at the Yale University Art Gallery, I saw a photograph from China. Taken in Longxian, Shaanxi Province, it showed three women kneeling on sparsely grassed ground. Dressed in white with white headscarves, they held wooden sticks wrapped in white paper and wailed in mourning <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-11\" aria-label=\"Reference 11\">11</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">11</span><span>Three Women at a Country Funeral, Longxian, Shaanxi Province, Liu Zheng, Yale University Art Gallery; from Liu Zheng&#x27;s The Chinese.</span></span>.<br><br>It looked like a spring morning. The flash fell unnaturally across their bodies. The women at the center and left covered their noses as they wept; the woman on the right had opened her mouth wide, as if releasing a full-throated cry. Their faces bore the mottled markings common among people of the plateau. In black and white, it was impossible to tell whether those marks had been scoured into them by freezing wind, or whether years of labor and sun had left the faces too neglected for washing. The women were thickly built, like cacti holding fast in wild ground, requiring only a little happiness to survive amid hardship everywhere.<br><br>I was not especially shocked. But the photograph made certain narratives about the countryside concrete for me. In the novels I had been reading, ordinary rural families in modern China looked first for a strong build in a prospective bride. As a rule, this meant that a young woman could withstand hard labor, was unlikely to fall ill, and, besides bearing children, could work the fields like a grown man. In an age of poverty, the choice made impeccable economic sense.<br><br>...But the photograph was taken in 2000!<br><br><br>(13)<br><br>In <em>Thirty Years of Turbulence</em>, Wu Xiaobo recounts the enormous, tortuous transformation of Chinese society since reform and opening-up through the lens of commercial change—a shrewd and reasonable angle that skirts much of what cannot be discussed. I want to emphasize that word: <strong>tortuous</strong>. In the exploratory reform of an economy encompassing a billion people, fluctuations arose that were too large to ignore. We can understand that unevenness as temporal.<br><br>Its spatial counterpart appears in the gulf between the regions chosen as early reform pilots and those where ideology remained much more firmly rooted.<br><br>I have written before that the American Dream, sealed inside private narrative, ignores the narrator&#x27;s responsibility to society. Its counterpart, the Chinese Dream, pulls back into grand narrative and ignores the differences and fluctuations within every story. Once I recognized that Chinese society&#x27;s development varied along both dimensions, I had to become wary of any single linear narrative—the kind zealots excel at telling. (As a matter of public relations, I of course understand why an official rosy filter is considered necessary to counteract the grim filter, thick with prejudice, through which foreign reporting depicts China.)<br><br>Certain familiar phrases warn us without quite meaning to: endure the labor pains; a speck of dust from the age... The more sincerely such phrases are spoken, the more their detachment hurts me.<br><br>The suffering of ordinary people is real, concrete, and cut into the bone, but those inside the shelter cannot feel it. In the narrative of another ideology, the rich—insulated from every assault of scarcity—likewise criticize the poor for not working hard enough, and for failing to think.<br><br>When I read the novel <em>A Lifelong Journey</em>, I saw a group of suppressed minor lives talking and worrying in the ordinary rhythms of home. Yet the ideologically advanced declared that it “presents, on an epic scale, the magnificent transformation of Chinese society” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-12\" aria-label=\"Reference 12\">12</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">12</span><span>“《人世间》收官 梁晓声点赞改编”, Xinhua, 2022-03-02.</span></span>.<br><br>What epic? Not everyone driven from place to place is Odysseus. Or do you, brimming with righteousness, project yourself onto everyone else and assume that fate has sheltered them too—that their lives are undamaged and their hearts naturally heroic?<br><br><br>(14)<br><br>I have encountered the same plot in more than one novel: an old hand in officialdom counsels a promising junior that anyone resolved to become an official must “wisely sever all intimate ties with the grassroots” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-13\" aria-label=\"Reference 13\">13</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">13</span><span>A Lifelong Journey, chapter 8, Liang Xiaosheng.</span></span>.<br><br>The reasoning is not entirely without merit. True ideals, like religion, must never allow private desire to compromise their purity. Yet the claim is also absurd. It plainly betrays the ideal&#x27;s original purpose, even as it implies that an official&#x27;s career can prosper only after he has separated himself, in practice, from the people.<br><br>In any case, we have reason to believe that this system of selection has endured. We have heard too much language that any ear retaining the faintest trace of human feeling would assume it had misheard.<br><br>And we keep hearing commands drawn from another vocabulary—the vocabulary of battle: fight a five-day “tough battle” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-14\" aria-label=\"Reference 14\">14</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">14</span><span>“郑州：用5天时间打一场拔点清面的攻坚战”, Xinhua Henan, 2022-11-24.</span></span>; launch a transmission-blocking “blitzkrieg” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-15\" aria-label=\"Reference 15\">15</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">15</span><span>“闪电战、阵地战、歼灭战——大连战‘疫’25天记”, Xinhua, 2020-08-18.</span></span>; wage Wuhan&#x27;s “ten-day mass campaign” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-16\" aria-label=\"Reference 16\">16</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">16</span><span>“‘十天大会战’武汉开展全员核酸筛查”, People&#x27;s Daily Overseas Edition, 2020-05-19.</span></span>; begin nucleic-acid testing in Guiyang at four in the morning and “race against the virus” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-17\" aria-label=\"Reference 17\">17</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">17</span><span>“贵阳战疫丨和疫情抢时间！志愿者行动在黎明之前”, Guizhou Daily, 2022-09-16.</span></span>...<br><br>Like the corporate and internet jargon mocked not long ago—and I do not mean merely alike in being mocked—these habits of speech did not materialize from nowhere. They can all be traced to the events this organization faced in its earliest years and the methods it used to overcome them.<br><br>How does any of this amount to escaping the historical cycle of rise and fall?<br><br><br>(15)<br><br>Path dependence means meeting political turmoil and a flood of discordant public opinion with hardline measures and tighter control.<br><br>Path dependence means preserving an equal, abstract, universal love for the masses by keeping a little farther away from them—far enough that their noise can no longer disturb the grand narrative.<br><br>Path dependence means solving every problem through unsustainable, high-intensity mobilization, then praying that the problems do not last.<br><br>Path dependence means turning martyrs into signposts for the faithful: glorifying sacrifice and every form of self-harm, teaching people that contentment is a sin, as Christians once treated this world as a vale of tears on the road to heaven <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-18\" aria-label=\"Reference 18\">18</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">18</span><span>The Farewell Waltz, Milan Kundera.</span></span>.<br><br>Path dependence means dealing with uncertain events through an opaque system of judgment founded on “do not encourage, do not ban, do not publicize”: never accept responsibility, never relinquish power, never make a mistake.<br><br>Path dependence means using whatever method once produced positive feedback until the victims pile up and reality finally proves that the method no longer works.<br><br><br>(16) A Few Fragments<br><br>1.0<br><br>Even the title <em>Return to Dust</em> feels too light. The Chinese phrase means to vanish into dust and smoke. Most of the time, either the dust storm is already everywhere before anyone can find cover, or someone wants the sufferers erased without a trace—the sooner, the better.<br><br>No. They do not vanish into the dust. They <strong>sink</strong> into it. That is more like it.<br><br>2.0<br><br>Some people ridicule America&#x27;s China policy—“cooperation, confrontation, competition”—as self-serving fantasy.<br><br>The very same people demand praise when circumstances improve, silence when they remain unclear, and endurance when a policy has plainly failed.<br><br>3.0<br><br>If I anthropomorphize them with the greatest possible goodwill: America is Gendo Ikari, equipped with a righteous plan known to all the world and a private calculation behind it; China is Asuka, convinced that saving fools is the elite&#x27;s duty.<br><br>Either kind of person is exhausting to deal with.<br><br>4.0<br><br>Socrates once told a Cynic, “I can see your vanity through the holes in your cloak” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-18\" aria-label=\"Reference 18\">18</a></sup>. The line warns us not to think too much of ourselves.<br><br>For the same reason, I burst out laughing while rereading Liu Zongyuan&#x27;s “Preface to Poems of Foolish Creek.”<br><br>Do not befriend people with an overdeveloped sense of self, even if they do not seem selfish.<br><br><br>(17)<br><br>The fascination with withdrawing from the world ran through my entire adolescence. I could never escape the same question: where is a person supposed to go? I sought an answer in mysticism and religion, only to find both at odds with reality. I immersed myself in delicate feeling, because beauty&#x27;s irreproducibility is a defection from eternity. There seem to be few methods. East or West, the answer has always been the same: one must put some distance between oneself and the secular world to attain the detachment in which that world can be forgotten.<br><br>I once assumed there had to be some substitute for the secular world better suited to my temperament. Otherwise, only the absence of such a place could explain the confusion and suffering that inevitably came with inhabiting this one. Later, I grew more at peace. Reading history, I threaded scattered beads into a line and began to watch, with an eye toward development, the changes unfolding—and about to unfold—inside this level-two chaotic system. Then I entered the workplace, immersed myself in labor, and personally pushed projects forward. The sense of control steadied me further.<br><br>At a more fundamental level, my work led me to embrace Dataism. So I will say this: interaction is more valuable than isolation.<br><br>In short, even as the world gradually loosens, even as the reassurance of participation sits inside uncertainty on a much larger scale, the feeling that I am losing my grip on the future must not separate me from the world again.<br><br>Someone else has put this more clearly. In a conversation published by <em>Aperture</em>, Professor Martin Hägglund described his idea of “secular faith.” If we honestly accept the finitude and fragility of human society, refusing fantasies of self-fulfillment through spirituality or religion as means of escape, then the desire for social connection expressed through alienation can be transformed into a pursuit of secular freedom <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-19\" aria-label=\"Reference 19\">19</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">19</span><span>“Spirituality Is Solidarity”, Wolfgang Tillmans in conversation with Martin Hägglund, Aperture, 2022-08-25; Chinese translation: “W…</span></span>.<br><br>As a spoiled only child, I took quite a few detours before I understood this: human beings are social animals. Giving back is more valuable than taking. Creating is more valuable than consuming.</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://greylock.com/greymatter/sam-altman-ai-for-the-next-era/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI for the Next Era</a>, <em>OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman on the New Frontiers of AI</em>, Greylock, 2022-09-13.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.forbes.com/sites/theyec/2018/12/04/you-are-what-you-measure/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">You Are What You Measure</a>, Forbes, 2018-12-04.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/notion-spotlight/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Augmenting Human Intellect, No Code Required</a>, Sequoia Capital Stories, 2022-10-13.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span><em>My Name Is Red</em>, Orhan Pamuk.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span>James Webb Space Telescope, Wikipedia.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span><a href=\"https://www.sickipedia.net/Joke/ua7o98nku6tli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Post</a>, Sickipedia, 2022-06-27.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Inevitable_(book)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><em>The Inevitable</em></a>, Kevin Kelly, 2016.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://36kr.com/p/1845555299830407\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“B站需要新番”</a> (“Bilibili Needs New Anime”), 36Kr, 2022-07-27.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span><a href=\"https://stream-capital.com/article/81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“当资产负债表不想奋斗了”</a> (“When the Balance Sheet No Longer Wants to Strive”), Yuanchuan Research Institute, 2022-05-31.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-10\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">10</span><span>“时代呼唤低需氧人才” (“The Times Are Calling for Low-Oxygen Talent”), Ruan Junhua, executive vice dean of Zhejiang University&#x27;s School of Innovation and Entrepreneurship, Rainbow Life WeChat account, 2022-05-04.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-11\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">11</span><span><a href=\"https://artgallery.yale.edu/collections/objects/112713\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><em>Three Women at a Country Funeral, Longxian, Shaanxi Province</em></a>, Liu Zheng, Yale University Art Gallery; from Liu Zheng&#x27;s <em>The Chinese</em>.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-12\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">12</span><span><a href=\"http://www.xinhuanet.com/ent/20220302/414676a866ed49008bc8ed706b017c21/c.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“《人世间》收官 梁晓声点赞改编”</a>, Xinhua, 2022-03-02.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-13\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">13</span><span><em>A Lifelong Journey</em>, chapter 8, Liang Xiaosheng.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-14\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">14</span><span><a href=\"http://ha.news.cn/headlines/2022-11/24/c_1129156321.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“郑州：用5天时间打一场拔点清面的攻坚战”</a>, Xinhua Henan, 2022-11-24.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-15\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">15</span><span><a href=\"http://www.xinhuanet.com/politics/2020-08/18/c_1126382046.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“闪电战、阵地战、歼灭战——大连战‘疫’25天记”</a>, Xinhua, 2020-08-18.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-16\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">16</span><span><a href=\"https://paper.people.com.cn/rmrbhwb/html/2020-05/19/content_1987776.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“‘十天大会战’武汉开展全员核酸筛查”</a>, <em>People&#x27;s Daily Overseas Edition</em>, 2020-05-19.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-17\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">17</span><span><a href=\"http://jgz.app.todayguizhou.com/news/news-news_detail-news_id-11515115995169.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“贵阳战疫丨和疫情抢时间！志愿者行动在黎明之前”</a>, <em>Guizhou Daily</em>, 2022-09-16.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-18\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">18</span><span><em>The Farewell Waltz</em>, Milan Kundera.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-19\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">19</span><span><a href=\"https://aperture.org/editorial/wolfgang-tillmans-spirituality-solidarity/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Spirituality Is Solidarity”</a>, Wolfgang Tillmans in conversation with Martin Hägglund, <em>Aperture</em>, 2022-08-25; Chinese translation: <a href=\"https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/0812kJHklht-m08gxvl1cg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“Wolfgang Tillmans：摄影的脆弱、自由，及其促进的人类团结”</a>, 假杂志 WeChat account, 2022-09-12.</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>（序）<br><br>世界向下探底。在瘟疫的砂漠中，疲于跋涉的人们欣喜地迎接曙光，直到这曙光变成昭彰的白日。遍地的狼藉和罪恶于是显出耀眼的清晰。<br><br>无数有关于正义的对决中，人们凭借立场区分敌我，而这和是否带入思考全然无关。西风劲吹，掌勺者却认为火候不够，因为最重要的东西还没有被完全点燃。<br><br>信仰笼罩的大陆上，身披道德的人们丝毫不觉饥冷。隔岸而望的灯塔中，PVP爱好者正斗艳争芳。<br><br>一浪浪信息洪流之后，精巧的贝壳中传来早已录播好的浪花声。这些遗留海滩的被悉心挑选的人造物，好像在海浪还未席卷之前，就被谁刻意安放在那里。<br><br>不知不觉，广袤星辰和虚拟世界都成为又一个百亿美金的投机场。竞价者如此慷慨，仿佛只要不是此时此地的生活，就怎样都好。<br><br>已有无数时刻我们被提醒站在了悬崖边，但每一次看似让人坠崖的举动都让这悬崖又延长一些。<br><br>这让一些人惊恐，又诱惑另一些人伺机而动。<br><br><br>（一）<br><br>载我去机场的优步司机为了避开收费的高速，熟练在小巷里绕出些弯弯拐拐，直到开上一条堵车的大路，成功让预计达到时间增加一刻钟。他好像因此显得不好意思，于是尝试和我交谈起来。<br><br>开篇自然是高企的油价，他接着自叙说了起当个网约车司机是个多么劳苦而不赚钱的一门生意……直到旁边一辆时髦的电动车忽然插入队列的动作让他急踩了一脚刹车，这个话题就被一声也许是西语的咒骂骤然止住了。<br><br>他指着前方那辆给他带来麻烦的家伙问我，你知道我为什么不喜欢现在的汽车吗？<br><br>“为什么？”<br><br>“这些车子都是电子系统。它们越来越精细却也越来越容易出错。我当了20年的机械师，但我无法修理自己这台汽车。每次出问题时，修理费又都是他妈的死贵。就和这世界一样。”<br><br>就和这世界一样。<br><br>师傅说的一点也不错。自从换上自动排挡以来，生活和汽车都变得越来越缺乏操控感。在很多时候，这些愈发精巧复杂的系统让人受益良多；直到某天，我们因为并非本人造成的损坏而收到一张咂舌的修理单……我们当然有理由责怪那些让汽车变得不像汽车的人！<br><br><br>（二）<br><br>生成式人工智能（Generative AI）在艺术创作领域登堂入室之年，除过让又一批人开始隐约担忧起饭碗被抢之外，还没有在社会层面造成什么实质冲击。虽说如此，这道被刚刚撕开的口子再一次提醒我们去直面一种即将渗入生活方方面面的巨大转折，这种转折甚至可能具有思想层面的颠覆性，即影响人类如何评价自己。<br><br>OpenAI的CEO在一次访谈中提到人工智能发展的令人意想不到的趋势：放到仅仅10年前，关于人工智能会如何替代人类工作的时候，所有人都一致认为，首先是蓝领型的、没有训练门槛的体力工作，然后是低端、可重复性的白领工作，最后才是创造类型的白领工作。然而事实是，人工智能的发展路径完全相反，它首先触达的都是那些看似极其复杂的领域，比如自动驾驶，比如艺术创作 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>AI for the Next Era, OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman on the New Frontiers of AI, Greylock, 2022.09.13</span></span>。<br><br>这种趋势表面费解，但如果我们转换视角看，又显得顺理成章：这些方兴未艾的应用领域无论其输入还是产出，都是高度可数据化的。只要研究者能想出一套逻辑自洽的数字化通路，辅之以足够多的训练样本，人工智能就能学成一套将输入投射到输出去的方法。换句话说，人工智能的瓶颈并不是模型复杂度的问题（何况算法还在不断进化），而是它能否得到足够多的食物。<br><br>从这个意义上讲，传统工作本身的难易程度并不是衡量可替代性的标准，是否有足够的初始数据积累才是；而如果一个行业能够有足够的惰性或壁垒去拒绝数字化，那或许就是人工智能最难啃的骨头了。<br><br>但话说回来，在一个几乎注定带来先发优势的技术面前，谁又敢拒绝数字化呢？<br><br><br>（三）<br><br>我的数字化旅程从2014年下载印象笔记开始，最初只是为了方便在多个平台上都能编辑和查看自己整理的读书笔记。2015年我开通了iCloud，因为照片把手机的储存占满了。而2019年我看到报道中提到，即使硬盘的保存也存在期限。我于是又开始订阅起Office365，终于把所有剩下的在移动硬盘中的个人资料（其中包括一篇一年级的作文《一只乌龟》）也迁移到了云端。<br><br>到了今年，我开始认真思考起建立一个更加全面的个人信息集合；为的是在一个单一、至少是有限的平台/应用上，以更细的颗粒度下记录自己的所见所闻——这当然和我的职业不无关系。数据科学界常常引用的一句话是，“你就是你所测量的”，或者其另一个版本，“如果你不能够衡量一件事情，你就不能改进它” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>You are what you measure, forbes.com, 2018.12.04</span></span>。<br><br>我大约花了一个月的时间考察，最后锁定了Notion这一集成式的文档类应用（虽然它的愿景还远不止与此），接着又用了两个周末，把原来分散在各个平台的文档和文件全部迁移了进去。<br><br>做为红杉基金领投的热门应用，它的2.0版本为原本扁平的文档记事创造性地引入关系型数据库的概念。在此基础之上，用户也可以根据自己的偏好自定义这些信息的展示方式 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Augmenting Human Intellect, No Code Required, Sequoia Capital Stories, 2022.10.13</span></span>。<br><br>在Notion构建的这个体系下，文档原本核心的内容成为了“文档”这一概念中的仅仅一个部分。文档的元数据和文档的内容被等量齐观。而与之有着同等重要性的，则是文档之间的交互、组合和展示逻辑。<br><br>就是说，在打破了那种以内容为核心的割裂性后，文档终于也暴露出一切数字化的东西所共通的那种信息的特性。<br><br><br>（四）<br><br>在还没有数字化存储和分销的年代，人们很难将信息和信息的载体分开来看。音乐就是音乐，绘画就是绘画，文字就是文字。直至麦克卢汉喊出“传媒即信息”，我们又把焦点对准了信息的传播路线。这夸大了渠道的作用，反而使我们对更为庞大的信息本身视而不见。<br><br>今年以来，文本转图像的生成式AI应用进入大众视野，人们惊讶于这些AI的“脑洞”——这又是被表面化的差异蒙蔽的例子。这些生成式AI并没有“创造些”什么，它们只不过是将由一个渠道得到的信息<strong>翻译</strong>去了另一个渠道，而其中承载的信息完全未变。<br><br>一脉相承，大型智能推荐系统所做的也不过是构建了两个不同的函数，将每个顾客的喜好和每个商品的属性都投射到了同一个向量空间里（Embedding）。而在这个空间中，对于机器而言，顾客喜好和商品属性并没有什么本质区别，都是在同一维度中被记录的信息而已。<br><br>哪怕只是基于现有的编码方式，我毫不怀疑，人工智能对信息的理解，比人类的意识更加接近信息的本来面貌。而只有在哲学或物理学层面，如果我们把宇宙想象成一个巨大的信息流（熵），事物才能在剥去它们千变万化的存在方式后显出其信息层面的一致性。<br><br>“我们想做的是去证明那些个体的、特别的、人类特有的经历不过是一些庞大的形式系统下面极其表面化的闪烁而己。”福柯的这句话既然瞄准了人，又何尝不是同等地瞄准了这世间的万物呢？<br><br><br>（五）<br><br>在所有有关“尽头”的思考里，科幻作品都是绕不开的高峰。今年我给自己定下的两个多年规划之一，就是系统性地阅读和了解科幻作品。<br><br>不同于风险投资家们所规划的那种“未来的终止”（逆向思考是一个时髦的词儿：十年内，大部分人都会……），幻想作品描述的是“未来的展开”：切断与具象现实的纽带，在一种整体的，更广阔的不确定之中，幻想家可以更为浪漫化地发掘或者预测人类文明的一切，而不仅是科技发展的可能性。<br><br>正如农耕文明和现代文明间的差异并不是以水平扩张或线性的复杂度来衡量，能够实现外星殖民的文明必然有着与现代社会根本上不同的<strong>哲学</strong>框架。诸如猜疑链、黑暗森林这样的构思，就代表了以<strong>宇宙为尺度所</strong>进行的思考而产生的，完全不隶属当代观念的理性和道德。<br><br>整个宇宙中可能发生的故事，和地球上发生的故事之间，最根本的差异是尺度问题（即使以光速旅行，前往相邻星系的征途仍是以世纪为单位，而这段时间里可以容纳多少个技术爆炸啊）。和经典力学相似，人类的知识到目前所构建的一切也有一个仅在中等尺度下的适用范围；而探究这个范围之外的东西，则完全可以重构我们对世界的理解方式。<br><br>这也并不只是科幻作家的责任。这项工程虽然进展缓慢但早已开始。我总以为，有些心智可以在不断的磨砺中领悟到常规波长以外的运动模式：随着年龄增加，芒格和巴菲特看起来越来越肆无忌惮，而某些马拉松运动员有着一副苦行僧般的面孔。<br><br>“绘画的极致（是失明），是在安拉的黑暗中看见事物。 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>我的名字叫红，奥尔罕·帕穆克</span></span>”说实话，我还挺喜欢这个任务的。<br><br><br>（六）<br><br>詹姆斯·韦伯望远镜在历经漫长的准备和调整后 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>詹姆斯·韦伯空间望远镜，维基百科</span></span>，终于不负众望地传回一幅幅细腻精美的深空图片。这些图像炫丽而静谧，和这充斥庞大虚无和暗流涌动的宇宙本身大相径庭。这其实是令人敬畏的，仅在这巨大天穹的毫不起眼的小小角落，如果我们仔细剖开，就已经展露出永不被理解的无限。<br><br>看到这些图像时，我常常想，这个以精确透镜和尖端科技组合成的仪器，和那些在摩天大楼楼顶供游客投币使用的望远镜，其实没有任何区别。它们把那些极远处的景观拉到身边，满足了我们难以被抑制的窥伺欲。<br><br>迄今为止，我们是这样的幸运，以至于在我们目及之处，宇宙除了稀疏但无所不在的黑暗和美不胜收的风景，再没有什么令人惊奇、值得一提的东西。但你完全可以想象，总有一天，这些数字信号会真的传回来一些什么了不得的图像，那个时候，整个人类也许就会像一个刚刚偷窥到苟且之事的变态一样，同时感到惶恐、尴尬和满足吧。<br><br><br>（七）<br><br>在动荡的地缘环境和潜伏的滞胀危机中，有着祖传乐天精神的英国网友自嘲道：如今的一切都像极了70年代的英国病（The British disease），除了没有好听的音乐 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>post on sickipedia, 2022.06.27</span></span>。<br><br>也许有，但不容易找到，因为最流行的现代音乐并不仅仅为了服务听觉。<br><br>自从多媒体这个概念出现，音乐逐渐开始依附在某些需要调动视觉的场景中，如迪厅（电子舞曲）或短视频（洗脑神曲），这使得它们和在更为高效的渠道上的信息（视觉信息占人类所有感官获取信息的70%以上）达成“捆绑销售”，相辅相成地变成了病毒式传播的怪物。<br><br>从更广义的方面来说，凯文凯利提出的互联网十二趋势之一的“重混（remix）” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>The Inevitable, Kevin Kelly, 2016</span></span>，也展现在不同艺术形式之间的融合之中。抛弃低密度的、单频道的食物，重混带来密度增长，立体的感官刺激当然也更令人欲罢不能。<br><br>从节奏感强烈的舞曲和差强人意的虚拟现实头盔往前看，明天是什么？<br><br>是渴望被信息填满的我们啊。<br><br><br>（八）<br><br>经历了数年的重金出击后，B站引进的日本新番终于逐渐减少 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-8\" aria-label=\"Reference 8\">8</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">8</span><span>B站需要新番，36氪，2022.07.27</span></span>。出于情怀，动漫爱好者多有不满可以理解；但从每一个方面看，这种趋势又十分合理地难以逆转。还好，在中国的动漫版权收购走下坡路之前，我已经从二次元离开了。<br><br>这并不是因为我已对此感到烦腻或者那些对动漫爱好者的刻板偏见。我的”毕业“源自自然的脱离，在番剧被萌系治愈和龙傲天穿越题材统治的当下，它确实再不能给予我所寻求的那份快乐。<br><br>也不单是动漫，如今的大多数娱乐性质的内容都缺乏惊喜：程式化的情节推动，一眼望穿的人物性格，我猜，王尔德要是体验过一把如今的消遣，肯定会被无聊致死。<br><br>我一直报以这样的偏见：不同人的爱好确实有着相当的高下之分。因为那说到底证明了一个人获得愉悦的途径是什么。因此我为如今的青年感到可惜，他们如果不花费力气去搜寻，那眼皮子底下的娱乐活动几乎没有任何营养，又或者他们并不会试图以可以获取营养的方式去观看/体验——而这是一个内容生产和消费之间相互作用的使彼此退化的循环（打破这个循环意味着缺乏成瘾性的娱乐形式，以及投资回报率更低效的娱乐行业，这条世界线当然缺乏任何吸引力）。<br><br>基于希望的叙事，永远是人类可以拥有的最好的叙事方式。但是哪怕我们放弃希望，颓废和消磨也是有巨大区别的（那其中蕴含的能量密度完全不同）。而我庆幸的是，我怀旧的并不只是出于对童年和私人经历的熟稔，而是这些作品确实是井喷式的黄金时代的代表，它们从任何意义上都比新发表的文艺更经得起时间检验。它们开拓了人类的理想、想象力和可能性，哪怕是在如何寻死这方面。<br><br>年中的时候，一篇《当资产负债表不想奋斗了》曾在网络上激起不小的波澜 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-9\" aria-label=\"Reference 9\">9</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">9</span><span>当资产负债表不想奋斗了，远川研究所，2022.05.31</span></span>。我虽仅能从对动漫产业的观测管中窥豹，但日本失落的三十年间整个民族从心气被扭曲到丧失心气的发展过程确实是一本深刻的教材。这既使人唏嘘也引人警醒，毕竟中国的青年也开始沾染上躺平的哲学了。<br><br>于是，当然有这样自感责任重大的先觉者出来喊话：时代呼唤低需氧人才！<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-10\" aria-label=\"Reference 10\">10</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">10</span><span>时代呼唤低需氧人才，浙江大学创新创业学院常务副院长阮俊华，彩虹人生微信公众号，2022.05.04</span></span><br><br>他妈的，你有资格呼唤么？<br><br><br>（九）<br><br>当一个打工人向老板提出新点子的时候，他往往需要联系公司愿景和行业趋势进行宏观叙事。摄影师的灵感则出现在极细微极具体的瞬间，这需要他保持敏锐，观察入微。<br><br>我经常为这两种视角下映射出的事物的不同形态感到惊奇。<br><br>生活是如此的复杂，当我们站得比亲历者远一点看，当我们想用一两个句子概括一段历史，当我们试图对事件“降维处理”，我们总会得到一个抹平个体差异结果：整体是稳中有升，大方向上越来越幸福，总的来说实现了巨大飞跃——人类拥有的统计工具毕竟只能达到这个程度。<br><br>但人类的同情心不应浮在一千米的高空向下俯瞰。<br><br><br>（十）小诗三首<br><br>其一 · 致庇护所里的人<br>在告诉我应该怎么做之前<br>请务必做完你能够做的事<br>宏大理想也不是不能打动我<br>但都还没长跑完 就先别急着说你的跑法<br>适应任何路况<br>为什么变得谨慎<br>一个民族有一个民族的诗抄<br><br><br>其二 · 致执牛耳者<br>不知是哪个神明想出的点子<br>一方造出更高纯度的氯气<br>一方造出更高纯度的金属钠<br>等等 小民我只是来讨点盐啊<br>乌鸦总忍不住去啄其他动物的尾巴<br>谁才是乌鸦<br>一段历史有一段历史的厨房辩论<br><br><br>其三 · 致清醒的跋涉<br>情绪释放是一种轻松的冲浪<br>寻求真相则是一场艰辛的爬坡<br>拨开迷雾 你的大后方有愚昧的败类和无知的圣徒<br>（他们并不容易彼此分辨）<br>哪怕火炬并不走身边经过<br>我也尽力保持干燥<br>这样做真的有好处吗<br>一代人有一代人的长征<br><br><br>（十一）<br><br>我从高中开始到如今的十余年间所接触的文学几乎都是西方和日本的，对于中国文学，除了几本古文小说之外，再没有做出任何努力。这毕竟令人愧疚，而且产生思维方式上的隔膜。<br><br>在这一点上我是谦虚的。我无法在还未进入这个文化语境之前，就随随便便地评论所有发生的事。这既是对这个民族厚重历史的不敬，也是对自己思想的菲薄。<br><br>况且，作为一个局外人，我毕竟无法亲历这些持续着巨变的日子。而往后隔不了几年，我于异乡漂泊的日子就会赶上在故土生活的计时。对于这样的游子，祖国当然只会变得越来越遥远和陌生。这令人感伤，但也提供一个当旁观者的契机。<br><br>但无论视角如何变化，人这辈子永远都想寻个归宿。为了这身传承千年的骨血，我因此要求自己从头开始阅读这首黄河般浩荡的史诗，我要求自己系统性地、重新地认识这片黄土和土地上的人民。<br><br>这是一项艰巨的任务，让我们从这里开始吧。<br><br><br>（十二）<br><br>今年夏天，在耶鲁的艺术博物馆里，我看到了一副来自中国的摄影作品。照片拍摄于陕西的陇县，图像中三个女人跪在青草稀疏的土地上，穿着白色的衣服，围着白色的头巾，手里拿着裹上白纸的木棒。她们正在哭丧 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-11\" aria-label=\"Reference 11\">11</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">11</span><span>Three Women at a Country Funeral, Longxian, Shaanxl Province, Liu Zheng, Yale University Art Gallery 作品取自刘铮摄影集《国人》</span></span>。<br><br>那看上去像是一个春天的清晨，闪光灯不自然地打在她们身上。中间和左边的二人掩鼻啜泣，最右的妇女嘴巴大张，好像正洪亮地哭出声来。她们的脸上显示出那种高原居民常有的团块状斑纹。但因为是黑白照片，并不能清楚分辨那是在寒风中被吹刮出的，或是在长久的劳作、日晒下疏于清洁的面容。她们身材粗壮，如野土地上顽强生长的仙人掌一般，在遍地的困苦中，只需些许幸福就能存活下来。<br><br>我并不感到如何震惊，但这张照片让我具像化地理解了一些关于乡村的叙事。我近来所读到的小说中，近代中国乡村的普通人家娶媳妇的偏好首先是身材壮实。这照例意味着这个姑娘经得操劳、不易害病，生孩子以外也可以像个成年男子下地耕作。在穷困的时代，这样的选择无比符合经济学。<br><br>……但这张照片是2000年拍摄的啊！<br><br><br>（十三）<br><br>在《激荡三十年》中，吴晓波从商业变革的角度讲述了改革开放以来中国社会巨大而曲折的变化（这个视角狡猾又合理地避开了很多不能谈论的东西）。我强调这个词——曲折，这意味着这个囊括十亿人的经济改革的摸索中出现过不可忽视的波动。我们可以把这种起伏理解为时间性上的不均衡。<br><br>而空间性上的不均衡，则体现在改革先行试验区和意识形态远为牢固的地区之间的差异上。<br><br>我以前说过，“美国梦”以私人叙事的闭塞性，无视了叙事者对社会所应担当的责任。而与之相对的“中国梦”，则以宏观叙事的缩放，忽略一切故事中始终存在的差异和起伏。一旦认识到中国社会的发展在两个维度上的多样性，我就不得不对那些单一的线性叙事保持警惕（仅从公关角度，我当然理解为了对冲外国报道中充满偏见的“阴间滤镜”，官方自有一套“阳间”滤镜的必要性），而这正是许多狂热者很会做的事。<br><br>我们常听到的一些代表警告但又没有完全警告的词：忍受阵痛、时代灰尘……这些语词用得越真诚，我就越为这种脱离感到心痛。<br><br>普通人的苦难是真实具体而刻骨铭心的，但庇护所里的人体会不到。正如另一个意识形态的叙事中，免于被匮乏侵扰的富人批评穷人不够刻苦，缺乏思考。<br><br>读过《人世间》的小说，我看到的是一群被压抑的小人物家常般的唠叨和对话，但思想先进的人却说它“史诗般展现波澜壮阔的中国社会巨变” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-12\" aria-label=\"Reference 12\">12</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">12</span><span>《人世间》收官 梁晓声点赞改编，新华网，2022.03.02</span></span>。<br><br>哪里来的史诗呢？并不是所有颠沛的人都被称作奥德修斯的。还是说你充满正义地推己及人，觉得所有人都得到命运的照护，过着毫无破损的日子，并且理所应当有着一颗英雄般的心呢？<br><br><br>（十四）<br><br>在不只一本小说中，我都看到这样的情节：官场前辈都对他们报以希望的后辈讲到一个道理，说一个人一旦下定决心当官，就必须“明智地斩断与草根阶层的亲密关系” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-13\" aria-label=\"Reference 13\">13</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">13</span><span>《人世间》第八章，梁晓声</span></span>。<br><br>我们不能说这种考虑毫无道理，毕竟真正的理想如宗教一般，绝不能被私人欲念扭曲其纯粹性。这种说法也十分可笑，因为它显然背叛了这种理想的初衷，而似乎只有官员以实际行动脱离了群众，他的仕途才会顺畅起来。<br><br>不管怎么说，我们有理由相信这种选拔方式被传承了下来，因为我们听到太多的话语，那是但凡有一点人情味的耳朵，都会怀疑自己听错的。<br><br>我们还常常能听到另一套战斗号令的语境：五天时间打一场“攻坚战” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-14\" aria-label=\"Reference 14\">14</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">14</span><span>郑州：用5天时间打一场拔点清面的攻坚战，新华网河南频道，2022.11.24</span></span>，打响阻断传播“闪电战” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-15\" aria-label=\"Reference 15\">15</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">15</span><span>闪电战、阵地战、歼灭战——大连战“疫”25天记，新华网，2020.08.18</span></span>，武汉“十天大会战” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-16\" aria-label=\"Reference 16\">16</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">16</span><span>“十天大会战”武汉开展全员核酸筛查，人民网，2020.05.19</span></span>，贵阳市凌晨四点测核酸与病毒“赛跑” <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-17\" aria-label=\"Reference 17\">17</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">17</span><span>贵阳战疫丨和疫情抢时间！志愿者行动在黎明之前，贵州日报，2022.09.16</span></span>……<br><br>和前些日子里被调侃的互联网黑话相似（并不是相似在被人调侃这一点上），这些语言习惯当然不是凭空产生，甚至是说，它们都可以被追溯到这个组织成立初期所经历的事件，以及对这些事件的解决方法。<br><br>这哪里像是跳出了历史周期率呢？<br><br><br>（十五）<br><br>路径依赖就是，面对政治风波和不和拍的汹涌舆情，必须采取强硬的手段加强控制。<br><br>路径依赖就是，要保持对群众平等而抽象的博爱就必须离群众远一点，远到他们的噪声再不能干扰宏观叙事。<br><br>路径依赖就是，以高强度的不可持续的动员去解决一切遇到的问题，然后祈祷这些问题不会长期存在。<br><br>路径依赖就是，用殉道者当路标要求所有信众，歌颂牺牲和损害自我的付出，让他们感到满足是一种罪恶，就像基督徒把现世当做一条通往天堂的泪谷 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-18\" aria-label=\"Reference 18\">18</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">18</span><span>《告别圆舞曲》，米兰昆德拉</span></span>。<br><br>路径依赖就是，在不确定的事件中使用“不提倡、不取缔、不宣传”为基础的不透明的考核体系，永不担责、永不放权、永不犯错。<br><br>路径依赖就是，始终采用过去得到正反馈的方法，直到出现许多牺牲者，直到这个方法被现实证明不再有用。<br><br><br>（十六）一些短句<br><br>1.0<br>隐入尘烟这个说法还是太轻巧了。大多数时候，不是漫天沙尘来不及躲，就是有人希望受难者能消失得无踪无迹，而且越快越好。<br>我想，那该是没入尘烟才对味哩。<br><br>2.0<br>有些人嘲笑美国对华政策的“合作，对抗，竞争”是自顾自的一厢情愿。<br>还是这些人，要求别人在事态变好的时候歌颂，在不明朗的时候噤声，在显然行不通的时候忍耐。<br><br>3.0<br>以最佳的善意来拟人：美国像是碇源堂，他有一个天下皆知的正义计划和背后的小算盘；中国像是明日香，她相信拯救愚民是精英的任务。<br>反正跟这两种人打交道都挺累的。<br><br>4.0<br>苏格拉底曾对犬儒学派的人说：您的肮脏是一种虚荣 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-18\" aria-label=\"Reference 18\">18</a></sup>。这句话告诉我们，人不能把自己看得太了不起了。<br>出于相同原因，我在重读柳宗元的《愚溪诗序》时笑出了声。<br>不要和自我意识太强的人交朋友，哪怕他们表面上并不自私。<br><br><br>（十七）<br><br>对离群索居的迷恋曾贯穿了我整个青春期。我总是逃不出同一个问题：人会去往何处？我曾求索于神秘学和宗教，但发现它们与现实的龃龉；我曾沉溺于纤细的感情，因为美的一次性是对永恒的叛逃。办法好像就这么几个。不论中西，这个议题的解决方式向来如此：你必须离俗世远一点，才能达到那个忘掉俗世的抽离状态。<br><br>我以前总以为，必然存在一种更符合我心性的世俗世界的替代品，否则这种缺乏难以解释这个在这个世界居住时必然遇见的混乱和苦痛。但后来我变得平和了些，因为我阅读历史，串珠成线，开始以发展的眼光观测这个二级混沌系统中发生和即将发生的变化。然后我参与工作，投入劳动，亲身推动项目的展开，这种掌控感让人愈发安心。<br><br>更为基础地，我的工作让我认同数据主义，因此我会说：互动比孤立更有价值。<br><br>总之，即便面对世界渐进式的松动，即便因亲身参与而带来的安心感被更大范围的不确定性所包裹，对未来失去把握的感觉也不能再次将我和世界分开。<br><br>有人比我阐述得更加清楚。在光圈杂质发表的一篇对谈录中，海格伦德教授讲述了他提出的“世俗信仰“的概念：如果我们公正地接纳人类社会的有限性和脆弱性，而不以灵修、宗教一类自我实现的幻想为逃避，那么以”异化“形式表达的对社会联系的渴望就可以转化为对世俗自由的追寻 <sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-19\" aria-label=\"Reference 19\">19</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">19</span><span>Spirituality Is Solidarity, Aperture, 2022.08.25；中文翻译：Wolfgang Tillmans：摄影的脆弱、自由，及其促进的人类团结，假杂志公众号，2022.09.12</span></span>。<br><br>作为被溺爱过的独生子女，我真的走了好些弯路才明白这个道理：人是社会动物。回馈比索取更有价值，创造比消费更有价值。</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"参考信息列表\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">参考信息列表</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span><a href=\"https://greylock.com/greymatter/sam-altman-ai-for-the-next-era/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AI for the Next Era</a>, OpenAI&#x27;s Sam Altman on the New Frontiers of AI, Greylock, 2022.09.13</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span><a href=\"https://www.forbes.com/sites/theyec/2018/12/04/you-are-what-you-measure/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">You are what you measure</a>, forbes.com, 2018.12.04</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span><a href=\"https://www.sequoiacap.com/article/notion-spotlight/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Augmenting Human Intellect, No Code Required</a>, Sequoia Capital Stories, 2022.10.13</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span>我的名字叫红，奥尔罕·帕穆克</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span>詹姆斯·韦伯空间望远镜，维基百科</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span><a href=\"https://www.sickipedia.net/Joke/ua7o98nku6tli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">post</a> on sickipedia, 2022.06.27</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Inevitable_(book)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">The Inevitable</a>, Kevin Kelly, 2016</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-8\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">8</span><span><a href=\"https://36kr.com/p/1845555299830407\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">B站需要新番</a>，36氪，2022.07.27</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-9\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">9</span><span><a href=\"https://stream-capital.com/article/81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">当资产负债表不想奋斗了</a>，远川研究所，2022.05.31</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-10\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">10</span><span>时代呼唤低需氧人才，浙江大学创新创业学院常务副院长阮俊华，彩虹人生微信公众号，2022.05.04</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-11\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">11</span><span><a href=\"https://artgallery.yale.edu/collections/objects/112713\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Three Women at a Country Funeral</a>, Longxian, Shaanxl Province, Liu Zheng, Yale University Art Gallery 作品取自刘铮摄影集《国人》</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-12\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">12</span><span><a href=\"http://www.xinhuanet.com/ent/20220302/414676a866ed49008bc8ed706b017c21/c.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">《人世间》收官 梁晓声点赞改编</a>，新华网，2022.03.02</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-13\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">13</span><span>《人世间》第八章，梁晓声</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-14\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">14</span><span><a href=\"http://ha.news.cn/headlines/2022-11/24/c_1129156321.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">郑州：用5天时间打一场拔点清面的攻坚战</a>，新华网河南频道，2022.11.24</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-15\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">15</span><span><a href=\"http://www.xinhuanet.com/politics/2020-08/18/c_1126382046.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">闪电战、阵地战、歼灭战——大连战“疫”25天记</a>，新华网，2020.08.18</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-16\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">16</span><span><a href=\"https://paper.people.com.cn/rmrbhwb/html/2020-05/19/content_1987776.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">“十天大会战”武汉开展全员核酸筛查</a>，人民网，2020.05.19</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-17\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">17</span><span><a href=\"http://jgz.app.todayguizhou.com/news/news-news_detail-news_id-11515115995169.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">贵阳战疫丨和疫情抢时间！志愿者行动在黎明之前</a>，贵州日报，2022.09.16</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-18\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">18</span><span>《告别圆舞曲》，米兰昆德拉</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-19\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">19</span><span><a href=\"https://aperture.org/editorial/wolfgang-tillmans-spirituality-solidarity/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Spirituality Is Solidarity</a>, Aperture, 2022.08.25；中文翻译：<a href=\"https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/0812kJHklht-m08gxvl1cg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wolfgang Tillmans：摄影的脆弱、自由，及其促进的人类团结</a>，假杂志公众号，2022.09.12</span></li></ol></section>","source":"./content/posts/2022-11-27_我们生活的故事02.md"},{"id":"2021-11-28_the-stories-we-live-01","date":"2021-11-28","title":"The Stories We Live 01","titleZh":"我们生活的故事01","subtitle":"Or: History as a Form of Narrative","subtitleZh":"又：历史是一种叙事方式","excerpt":"(Prologue) It began twelve years ago, on a summer night when I faced a wound head-on. Once a year ever since, I have felt the same need to put something into words:…","excerptZh":"（序） 自十二年前某个正视创口的夏夜开始，每年一次，我总会保持这样的表达欲望，用以记录过去一年的思考变化。 〇九年，EVA新剧场版“破”发布，其被奉为经…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2021-11-28_我们生活的故事01.jpg","tags":["our stories"],"tagsZh":["我们生活的故事"],"readingMin":17,"htmlEn":"<p>(Prologue)<br><br>It began twelve years ago, on a summer night when I faced a wound head-on. Once a year ever since, I have felt the same need to put something into words: to record how my thinking had changed over the year just past.<br><br>In 2009, <em>Evangelion: 2.0 You Can (Not) Advance</em> was released. Its now-classic insert song, <em>Tsubasa o Kudasai</em>, accompanied me through the twelve pieces that preceded this one. The same past also gave those pieces their baffling collective title, <em>The Silence of Lies</em>.<br><br>This year, <em>Evangelion: 3.0+1.0 Thrice Upon a Time</em>, the final installment built around farewell, brought to rest the mythology I had once believed in. Out of a sense of responsibility to the one-sided marriage I had entered as a boy, I decided that these annual reckonings would need a new name from now on.<br><br>First came subjective feeling—mostly self-pity. Then came the shouting of ideas I imagined no one else had ever had; then partial, declarative pronouncements about the direction of the world; and finally, the recording of concrete events and reflection on them. Read in sequence, these markers trace a young man&#x27;s gaze slowly moving outward: he grows more adept at handling and directing his own emotions while turning toward the world&#x27;s complexity, ruthlessness, and entanglement.<br><br>Without these records, it might not be long before I looked back in astonishment at the boy who made a banner of solitude, refused reconciliation, fixed his gaze on the sensitivity he had been given, and cared only for the life within—because, after all, he is nothing like you. You are now submerged in the ordinary, laboring to remain objective and permitting yourself only so much curiosity about the truth—though perhaps that curiosity is sustained by sadness and helplessness.<br><br>But the sincerity remains. You have never lied to yourself. Even when you disappear into the crowd, you still try to make something, however insignificant.<br><br>You know that even the fairest history is a narrative, and that your life—as Strand&#x27;s poem has it—is the story of your life.<br><br>It is impossible to say what form<br>The weather will take.<br>We blow on our hands,<br>Trying to keep them warm,<br><br>(I)<br><br>Like many art forms, photography passed through a series of transformations over the twentieth century: from outward to inward, from process to fragment, from narrative to lyric, from restrained passion to sensory provocation. At its best, this freed art from the constraints of its original objective framework. Subjective feeling and emotional expression became neither shameful nor, for that matter, necessarily worth proclaiming. Taste grew more plural and more open. In the broadest sense, the motives behind art became increasingly private, its purposes less definite, and its standards of judgment more obscure.<br><br>Photography, however, is different. The difference in cost between a monumental oil painting and a modest decorative work is palpable, as is the difference between a full-scale symphony and a piece of chamber music. Photographs, by contrast, are nearly equal to one another. Portable cameras are everywhere; a shutter produces an image in an instant; digital viewing is ubiquitous. In both production and consumption, photography has the lowest barrier to entry of any artistic medium.<br><br>Democratization naturally filled photography with schools, factions, and work of wildly uneven quality. I respect plurality, but I still trust my own eye, and I distrust the blind inertia of collective taste. At a moment when private narrative has been elevated beyond its proper importance, I remain convinced that art—including photography—must put content before form, substance before style.<br><br>Private narrative in photography runs along two currents. The first exaggerates and embellishes its subject in check-in photos smothered beneath monotonous filters. The second, common in so-called art photography, layers abstract color into a metaphor for emotion. Each has a different personality: one dances in a spotlight; the other reads poetry at a recital. Not every private narrative is desperate for attention, but both currents share the same core. Both are expressions of narcissism.<br><br>Narcissism is the temperament of the age. I cannot tell whether our age is actually more narcissistic, or whether, now that everyone can be their own medium, narcissism is simply heard more often.<br><br>Ours is also an impatient age. We watch videos at double speed, then remember nothing and understand nothing.<br><br>Narcissism plus impatience: naturally, this is the finest age yet for photography as art.<br><br>(II)<br><br>But photography as a technical process—the recording of an external instant on a light-sensitive medium—is not itself art. I take this parallel relationship to art as the basis of my philosophy of photography.<br><br>Photography is peculiar because, unless a scene has been deliberately staged, its material comes entirely from reality. The photographer does not construct a scene, but cuts one out of time and space.<br><br>A photograph can be treated as a self-contained object. It cannot show how its subject changed before or after the exposure, nor anything that happened beyond the edge of the frame. Yet freezing an instant does not prevent the viewer from using logic and common sense to reconstruct possibilities across time and space. That is where photography acquires metaphor.<br><br>The objectivity of its raw material certainly limits what the photographer can create: I cannot photograph a building at noon and make the image show what it looked like at night. But in return, photography possesses a documentary sense of reality that belongs to it alone. What photography ought to record and reveal, in other words, is <strong>life itself</strong>.<br><br>The American street photographer Garry Winogrand once said, “I photograph to see what things look like photographed.” The sentence allows many readings, but under any of them, “what things look like photographed” remains distinct from the language that dominates contemporary photography: “what things look like to the photographer.”<br><br>Sound taste comes from understanding beauty within its historical lineage, not from following the fashion of the moment. That is no easy task. Just as many people mistake popular music for music itself, the visual language of commercial and landscape photography is often mistaken for photography in its entirety: formal beauty comes first, and a photograph should preserve its subject in its most ideal state rather than its most characteristic or truthful one. It follows that when people seek sufficient grounds for their narcissism, they turn to photography to <strong>transcend</strong> their lives inside an image.<br><br>A good photograph obeys reality&#x27;s order, necessity, and capacity for legend. A bad photograph relies entirely on the familiar logic that “the camera never lies.”<br><br>A real Eiffel Tower should fucking look as though it belongs in Paris, not Las Vegas. And anyone who has walked beside the Seine beneath it knows that what the wind lifts and carries away is dust from the ground, not balloons bearing declarations of love.<br><br>(III)<br><br>A person&#x27;s photo album is a gauge of their narcissism.<br>Not knowing how to take pictures is no problem. Knowing too well how to put yourself on display is a very big problem.<br>Give up narcissism and everything else can be worked out. To give it up, begin by refusing shallow emotion and empty polish.<br><br>(IV)<br><br>There is enough here to sustain a life, perhaps more—provided you lower your expectations. Of all the congested streets I have crossed in Manhattan, none is more New York, or less New York, than East Broadway in Chinatown—or <em>Huabu</em>, as official Chinese would have it.<br><br>Here, the elevated span carries the B, D, N, and Q subway lines out of Manhattan toward Queens. Along with the intolerable noise, the trains occasionally deliver localized precipitation of unknown origin through the gaps in the tracks.<br><br>Though the bridge has kept it from growing upward, the tenacious East Broadway Mall continues to shelter a profusion of small businesses: SIM-card dealers, bird&#x27;s-nest sellers, a matchmaking agency, and the mall&#x27;s very own daughter, the five-star 88 Palace Restaurant. Yet even beneath the restaurant&#x27;s sign, two jewelry stores and a bakery have found room to sit.<br><br>Beside a canopy at the corner, a woman selling fruit works her fingers over a pad of plastic bags. The tropical fruit on her stand droops in the cold autumn wind, but none of it has been marked down. There is no sign saying so, but wherever Chinese people gather, cash is always the preferred form of payment.<br><br>Right by the intersection is an even smaller shop whose sign promises parcels back and forth between China and the United States, although the space outside is piled with snacks, cigarettes, and suspiciously packaged masks. Inside, the old man is wedged between the counter in front of him and the wall behind. He can move only sideways or turn in place. This has also trained him in a singular art: at a traffic-choked intersection, he can pass his life as motionless as a statue.<br><br>Farther west, the street narrows. But its dingy surface proves nothing. Eastern culture has always kept its depths concealed; this, therefore, is the sort of ground where unsuspected masters lie in wait. Zhiyin Photography blocks an apartment building&#x27;s fire escape with an enormous wedding portrait. In the five-story Western-style building next door, the floors hold, from top to bottom, an Eastern arts training center, the office of the Social Democratic Party of China, tax and insurance services, and a clinic treating dandruff and gynecological disease…<br><br>Confronted with this decaying streetscape and its grimy but sonorous signs, I can only grow more devout.<br><br>Because New York is another word for diversity; because a person may live for no reason beyond remaining alive; because they are not wrong: everything the West sees when it looks East is crowded, noisy, and poor.<br><br>(V)<br><br>Tour boats idle on the Chicago River. Lake Michigan lies quietly to the east, and the high-rises of the Windy City stand all around. Famous for commodity and futures trading, this second-largest financial center in the United States—the metropolis at the heart of the Midwest—hosted the World&#x27;s Columbian Exposition at the end of the nineteenth century. The newly built neoclassical stone structures, a departure from traditional timber construction, earned it the name “White City.” More than a century later, they still rise across downtown beside towers of glass, each displaying what it does best.<br><br>Chicago&#x27;s streets are wide enough to make you doubt that the city can possess much history at all. The doubt is not misplaced. Chicago has indeed been restarted like the Ship of Theseus: twenty-two years before the fair, the Great Chicago Fire burned the city to the ground. Accounts of its origin vary. The best known says that an Irish immigrant&#x27;s cow kicked over a lantern beside a haystack—and knowing this civilization, you have of course already inferred the historical fact that Irish immigrants were not welcome in nineteenth-century America: “Help Wanted—No Irish Need Apply.”<br><br>In any event, a second Chicago rose quickly. As its population grew, so did its sewage problem. Chicagoans drew their water from Lake Michigan; they could hardly dump their waste into the same lake. Their ingenious solution was the Chicago <strong>Sanitary</strong> and Ship Canal, which carried the city&#x27;s filth southwest into the Mississippi watershed and eventually to the Gulf of Mexico, a thousand miles away. The canal was hailed as an engineering triumph, but as an act of governance it contained no new idea at all. Cities downstream were furious enough to take the dispute all the way to the U.S. Supreme Court, yet they disposed of their own waste in exactly the same way.<br><br>Set this beside the way the government of this country has taught its people to view COVID and the climate, and you have to admire how reliably an inherited narrative template continues to work.<br><br>But then, how many Mississippis can the world contain?<br><br>(VI)<br><br>More than a decade ago, when Apple&#x27;s iPhone struck the young smartphone industry like a thunderclap, a terrified Google rallied more than eighty companies from across the handset and telecommunications supply chain into a camp called the <strong>Open</strong> Handset Alliance. Here, “open” had both a broad meaning and a narrow one: anything non-Apple was open; anything built on Android was open.<br><br>At the time of writing, its official website could still be found. The declarations across it were lofty in purpose and beautiful in vision: keep the platform open, lower costs, bring benefits to the mobile industry…until Android found its footing and Google no longer needed the alliance. The site&#x27;s latest news dated to 2011, the year Android&#x27;s market share first surpassed iOS, and its pages still could not resize themselves to fit a browser window.<br><br>Google&#x27;s language had plainly seized on something essential. Tell a Westerner that something is closed or opaque, and they frown as though someone has them by the balls.<br><br>I call this the G-spot of narrative. Touch it, and even a pragmatist responds violently to an abstraction; profound thought collapses into a binary of support or rejection.<br><br>People have at last grown wary of the overabundant vocabulary of Western ideology. Yet few question the more intimate narrative that grew alongside that rhetoric: the American Dream, in which a person attains happiness through hard work. Its weakness lies neither in logic that turns black into white nor in any bait-and-switch of concepts, but in its <strong>way of seeing</strong>.<br><br>The American Dream claims moral legitimacy through its apparent absolute openness: no one who possesses possibility is excluded. But its point of view is private. It <strong>never looks beyond the protagonist</strong>.<br><br>Chicago&#x27;s rebirth follows just such a narrative. A world&#x27;s fair only twenty-two years after the fire; a sanitary canal dug to improve the lives of city residents—this is an immaculate miracle, so long as you leave out the Mississippi.<br><br>But what lies beyond the frame? The responsibility one person bears for another is absent from the American Dream. For the Dream to hold, it must depend on an enclosed private narrative; it must enjoy freedom without cost.<br><br>(VII)<br><br>Masayoshi Son once proposed a time-machine theory: a multinational can design its business model by treating a developing country as though it were a developed country some years earlier, then grow with that country along a corporate trajectory already tested elsewhere.<br><br>The premise is that, for all the immense differences between countries, their societies still develop according to similar patterns. In this sense, capitalist society is <strong>prophetic</strong>.<br><br>Chinese media have long seized on America&#x27;s infrastructure problems as matters of everyday livelihood, though America itself once pursued infrastructure more aggressively than any other country. By the logic of the time machine, China&#x27;s infrastructure will sooner or later face aging that may be broader and more severe—because our modernization has been so swift and its reach so vast—or, at the very least, obsolescence, since these systems were designed to last half a century or more.<br><br>I do not care very much how these problems “after modernization” will ultimately be solved as matters of engineering. What concerns me is how we will prepare for them <strong>psychologically</strong>.<br><br>The Trumps of the world all believe that American supremacy is the natural state of things. Japan&#x27;s bubble generation calls its young people weak and its policies insufficiently forceful.<br><br>Yes, we know their prejudices rest on realities that have expired. But we too have things we believe absolutely once we accept the premise, you know: high-speed trains hurtling forward, buildings polished to a gleam, China as the engine of the world…<br><br>A generation born into explosive growth will always find a long quiet unfamiliar. But <strong>wear</strong>, after all, is the whole of the second law of thermodynamics.<br><br>I suspect that even after changes unseen in a century, some things cannot become better than they are today. I mean a way of telling stories founded on “hope.”<br><br>(VIII)<br><br>Great entrepreneurs and legendary revolutionaries excel at two things. First, they make people believe in and follow grand, distant ideals. Second, within those same mad ideals, they remain sober enough to devise strategies of extraordinary precision, fitted to reality. In my view, one of the forces behind China&#x27;s national fortunes is that, for most of its history, the country has been led by people of this kind.<br><br>To begin from reality and still carry through the power of belief; to unite the ideal color of communism with the hardly optimistic conditions of the new China in a single theory—the program of socialism with Chinese characteristics strikes me as an act of genius. Soviet nostalgia, by contrast, is a dangerous attempt at <strong>romanticization</strong>, using the nobility of the Soviet people to conceal the rigidity and imbalance built into their radical experiment in modernization.<br><br>As I see it, China&#x27;s own narratives can be divided into three layers according to the length of their horizons.<br><br>The first is the completion of national rejuvenation. The Resolution of the Sixth Plenum says, “Today, we are closer, more confident, and more capable than ever of realizing the goal of national rejuvenation.” This narrative can be measured against a frame of reference: the development of the new China is a story of self-reliance and of catching up with the strong.<br><br>The second is the realization of socialism with Chinese characteristics. Its goal is common prosperity, measured through five elements: prosperous and strong, democratic, culturally advanced, harmonious, and beautiful.<br><br>The third is building a community with a shared future for mankind. This narrative is the ultimate goal of communism.<br><br>The leaders worked all this out quite some time ago. But the tension between ideal and reality is, after all, a matter of survival.<br><br>In the debate over modernization, the black-cat/white-cat maxim saved us once already. What worries me is this: as Taleb argues in <em>Antifragile</em>, resilience comes from diversity and flexibility. Within a highly uniform system of values, will we be able to adapt with the same flexibility when the unknowable challenges us again?<br><br>(IX) A Few Small Thoughts<br><br>1.0<br>In the broad sense, capitalism is not merely an ideology. It is a self-reinforcing process driven by things&#x27; appetite to multiply.<br>In this sense, the obsession with big data is a kind of capitalism. The obsession with connectivity is a kind of capitalism. The metaverse, therefore, is capitalism through and through.<br><br>2.0<br>The value of the virtual is that it allows a person to move among different personas. True online sociality should break away from the social modes we already have rather than merge with them. What people actually need from virtual sociality is something instrumental and fragmentary.<br>Unfortunately, social-network companies measure themselves by stickiness and engagement—and by their grander justification, safety—so their products can only ever extend a person&#x27;s real-world persona.<br>In the end, people must manage several nearly identical profiles across both online and offline life, participate in ever more saturated sociality, lose the practice of being alone, and become nothing more than nodes relaying information through a web of connections.<br>So even capitalism in its broadest sense produces human alienation, because this society develops not to make people happier, but to leave them ever more adrift.<br><br>3.0<br>Why do people always imagine that God is compassionate? Because compassion is reason in its most indulgent form: it makes even the God who establishes order abide by human laws of feeling when deciding what to do—or whether to do anything at all.<br><br>4.0<br>How to Be Someone&#x27;s Guardian Angel 101: when they fall, do not say, “I told you so / If you&#x27;d listened to me, you wouldn&#x27;t have…”<br>How to Become Middle-Aged: become unable to stop yourself from correcting them.<br><br>5.0<br>Two ways to remain calm: the weariness of someone who has watched enough autumn moons and spring winds pass; the indifference that comes with realizing other people have nothing to do with you.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>（序）<br><br>自十二年前某个正视创口的夏夜开始，每年一次，我总会保持这样的表达欲望，用以记录过去一年的思考变化。<br><br>〇九年，EVA新剧场版“破”发布，其被奉为经典的插曲《翼をください》一直伴随我写完之前的十二篇文字。同样源自这些往事的还有它们令人不知所然的标题《嘘之默然》。<br><br>然后今年，以告别为主题上演的最后的终章为这个我曾深信不疑的神话体系划上休止；本着对这自少年开始的一厢情愿的姻缘的负责任态度，我也决定为从此往后的总结换个名字。<br><br>从表达主观感受（主要是自怜），到嚷嚷些自以为独此一家的观念，到对趋势的片面和声明式的概括，再到开始对具体事件的记录和反思；顺着文字一路看来，这些埋点正好反映出一个青年逐渐从内向外“看见”，在更能娴熟对付和应用自己感情的同时，将视角转向理解世界之复杂，无情和纠缠的过程。<br><br>而如果没有这些记录，不久后我也许会对那个标榜孤独、拒绝和解、凝视自身所被赋予的敏感，只专注内心体验的少年感到诧异——因为这毕竟与你大相径庭！因为如今你被平凡淹没，努力保持客观，并对真相拥有适可而止的好奇——尽管这种好奇的底色也许是悲伤和无能为力的。<br><br>但是真诚并不因之改变。你从不对自己撒谎。哪怕隐没人群，你也依然会为了创造些什么微不足道的东西而做出努力。<br><br>你知道哪怕最为公正的历史也是一种叙事，而你的生活——正如斯特兰德诗中所说——就是你生活的故事。<br><br><br>不可能说出天气<br>将以什么形式出现。<br>我们朝双手呵气，<br>试图使它们保持温暖。<br><br><br>（一）<br><br>包括摄影在内的众多艺术形式都在二十世纪经历了一个“由外及内，由过程到片段，由叙事到抒情，由克制激情到刺激感官”的发展过程。这个过程，从好的方面说，解放了原初艺术中客观框架的束缚，让主观感受和情绪表达成为一件并不羞耻（或值得宣扬）的事情，使得人的审美变得多元和开放。就趋势而言，艺术的动机总体上是在变得越来越私人化的；艺术的目的性不再明确，艺术的评判标准变得模糊了。<br><br>而在这其中，摄影与众不同。与巨幅油画和小型装饰画、大型交响乐和小型室内乐这种可以明显让观者感受创作成本差距的艺术形式相反，照片和照片之间几乎是平等的。尤其是，得益于便携式拍摄设备的大范围普及，按下快门的瞬时生产过程，以及无处不在的数字化的观看方式，摄影无论从生产还是消费层面都是门槛最低的艺术媒介。<br><br>大众化的倾向自然使得摄影市场里门派林立，或说鱼龙混杂；而尽管尊重多元，我依然坚持自身的鉴赏力，并怀疑群体风尚的盲目惯性。就是说，在这个私人叙事的重要性被抬举到不必要高度的当下，我始终认为艺术（包括摄影）必须做到内容大于形式，实质大于风格。<br><br>摄影中的私人叙事体现在一下两种风潮之中：第一是单调滤镜的打卡照片中对主体的过度强调和修饰；第二是常见于所谓艺术摄影的作品中通过堆叠抽象色彩达到对情绪的隐喻。两种叙事带有不同的个人特质（前者如在聚光灯下跳舞，后者如在朗诵会上读诗）。而尽管并不是所有的私人叙述都绝望地渴求关注，两种叙事的内核仍然相通，都是自恋的体现。<br><br>自恋是时代的风潮。尽管我也说不清究竟是这个时代更加自恋了，还是因为人人都能成为媒体，所以我们听见更多自恋的声音。<br><br>这同时也是一个浮躁的时代。我们把视频倍速播放，到头来还是什么都没记住什么都没看懂。<br><br>而自恋加浮躁，这当然是作为艺术的摄影的最好时代。<br><br><br>（二）<br><br>但摄影作为一种技术手段（以感光元件记录外部瞬间），本身是非艺术的。我将这种与艺术的平行关系当做我的摄影哲学。<br><br>摄影的特殊性在于，如不是刻意摆布场景，其取材则完全源自现实本身。拍摄者并不构建场景，而是在截取场景——从时间和空间两方面。<br><br>一张照片可以被看做一个独立的实体，它既无法展示拍摄前后（时间）被拍摄物的变化，也无法表现景框之外（空间）的任何事件。但这种对瞬间的凝固并未禁止观看者运用逻辑和常识，反向构建出时间和空间上的可能性——这即是摄影隐喻性的体现。<br><br>的确，底材的客观限制了拍摄者的创作（我无法在大中午拍下一栋楼房，却想表现它在夜晚的模样），但反过来说，这种纪实的真实感则是摄影媒介独有的特性。也就是说，摄影记录和展示的，应当是<strong>生活本身</strong>。<br><br>美国街头摄影师维诺格兰德曾说，他拍照是为了“看看事物被拍摄下来的样子”。这句话可以有多重解读，但从任何角度看，“事物被拍摄下来的样子”都是区别于当代主流摄影语言中“拍摄者眼中事物的样子”的。<br><br>建立正确的审美需要理解美的历史脉络而并非凭借时下的风潮，但这并非易事。正如很多人以流行音乐为音乐，商业和风光摄影的镜头语言也常被误解为摄影的一切：以形式美为先导，照片应该记录事物最为理想（而非最为典型或真实）的样子。依次类推，当人们希望为自己的自恋找到足够的依据时，他们当然希望运用摄影，在相片中完成他们对生活的<strong>超越</strong>。<br><br>好的照片遵循现实的秩序、必然还有传奇性。坏的照片则完全依赖“众所周知照片无法作假”的逻辑成立。<br><br>一座真正的埃菲尔铁塔就应该他妈有一副在巴黎而不是拉斯维加斯的样子。而在铁塔旁的塞纳河边路过的人都知道，被风扬起和吹走的，是遍地的尘土而不是告白气球。<br><br><br>（三）<br><br>一个人的相册就是一个人的自恋指数计。<br>不懂得拍照不是问题，太懂得展示自己则是一个大问题。<br>不要自恋，一切都好说。而要改正自恋，首先要从拒绝肤浅的情绪和空洞的精致开始。<br><br><br>（四）<br><br>这里有足够一个人活下去所需的一切，也许更多——只要你降低期待！在我所路过的所有曼哈顿的拥堵街道中，没什么地方比中国城（或用官方的说法，华埠）的东百脑汇街更纽约或更不纽约的了。<br><br>这里，自曼哈顿驶向皇后区的高架上，BDNQ四条地铁沉重地自头顶越过，除了难以忍受的噪声外，偶尔还能从轨间带来局部的不明降水。<br><br>虽然被这大桥限制了高度，顽强生长的怡东商场依然在庇佑着众多的杂货铺：卖手机卡的，卖燕窝的，婚介所，还有它的亲女儿：五星餐厅怡东大酒楼。但这个酒楼的牌匾下面却是另外两家珠宝行和一家饼屋。<br><br>街角的棚子边贩卖水果的女子正在摩挲了一饼塑料袋。摊位上的热带水果在秋天的冷风里耷拉着，但并没有减价销售。虽然没有任何标识，在华裔聚集的地方，现金永远是最优先级的支付方式。<br><br>紧邻十字路口的的地方，有一个打着中美包裹来回接送牌子的更小的铺子，店前却堆着各种小食品，香烟，以及包装可疑的口罩。里面的大爷受面前的柜台和身后的墙壁所限，只能做横向和转身运动，但也因此练就了一身绝活：在车水马龙的交叉口如雕像般静止地消磨生活。<br><br>往西行，道路更加局促。但表面的黯淡不能说明什么：东方文化总是习惯深藏不露，因此这是块藏龙卧虎的宝地。知音摄影公司用一张巨大的婚纱照挡住居民楼的消防梯，而旁边的五层洋楼里，从上到下分别是东方艺术培训中心、中国社会民主党办公楼、报税和保险、以及皮屑和妇科疾病诊疗室……<br><br>面对这衰颓的街景，脏污但洪亮的招牌，我的心情只能越发虔敬。<br><br>因为纽约是多样性的代名；因为人可以仅仅为了活着而活下去；因为他们并没有错：西方看向东方的一切都是拥挤、吵闹与贫穷！<br><br><br>（五）<br><br>游船流连于芝加哥河面，视线东面静静躺卧着密歇根湖，风城的高层建筑群环侍左右。以交易大宗商品和期货著名的美国第二大金融中心，这个中部的核心城市曾于19世纪末举办世博会，众多新建的石砌（而非传统木质结构）的新古典主义建筑为其赢得“白城”的美誉；时隔百年，它们依然在林立于市中心，和玻璃大厦们交相辉映，各展所长。<br><br>芝加哥的街道宽阔得令人怀疑它是否当真有悠久的历史。这种怀疑并没有错，芝加哥确实如忒休斯之船般历经重启，那正是世博会前22年焚毁整个城市的大火。起火的原因众说纷纭，流传最广的说法是某个爱尔兰移民的奶牛踢翻了干草堆旁的油灯——凭借对这个文明的了解，你当然已经完全推测出，在19世纪的美国，爱尔兰移民并不受人待见（help wanted, no Irish need apply）的史实。<br><br>无论如何，第二芝加哥市很快就被建了起来。伴随增长的人口，城市产生的污水成了一个更大问题：当地人既然从密歇根湖里汲取水源，当然就不能往同一泊湖里倾倒排泄物。经过富有创造性的规划，他们开凿了一条<strong>卫生</strong>运河解决问题：将城市的污秽向西南排进密西西比水系，并最终流往千里之外的墨西哥湾。这条运河在工程学上被视为一次壮举，但从城市治理角度则毫无新意——下游的城市尽管对此大为不爽并一度闹到联邦最高法庭，他们毕竟是以同样的方式处理自家的污物的。<br><br>联系这个国家的政府引导民众看待新冠和气候问题的方式，不得不说祖传的叙事模板真是百试不爽。<br><br>只是，世界上又有多少条密西西比河呢？<br><br><br>（六）<br><br>十多年前，苹果凭借iPhone的惊雷震撼刚刚兴起的智能手机界的时候，惊恐的谷歌纠集八十于众手机和通讯行业的上下游厂商，组成了一个叫“<strong>开放</strong>手机联盟（Open Handset Alliance）”阵营。这里的开放有广义和狭义两个意思：非苹果的就是开放的，基于安卓的就是开放的。<br><br>直到今天，你仍然可以找到它的官方网站。网站上的醒目宣言立意高尚、愿景美好：保持开放，降低成本，造福移动行业……直到安卓站稳脚跟，然后谷歌就不再需要它（网站的最新信息停留在2011年，其年安卓的市场占有率首次超越iOS；而网站页面尚不能随着浏览器自适应调整宽度）。<br><br>显然，谷歌的这套话语抓住了某种本质：当一个西方人听到某样东西封闭、不透明的时候，他们都会皱起眉头，仿佛被攥住了命根子。<br><br>我把这称之为叙事的G点。在这个点位上，务实的人也会对抽象的概念产生巨大反应；深刻的思想也会坍缩为非黑即白的拥护或抵触。<br><br>而虽然人们终于对于泛滥的西方意识形态语汇保持警觉，却鲜有人质疑与这些话术相生相伴的另一个更加生活化的叙事：美国梦，即一个人通过努力奋斗而达成幸福。因为这套叙事的漏洞并不在其逻辑颠倒黑白或偷换概念，而在其<strong>观看方式</strong>。<br><br>美国梦的正义性在于其表面绝对开放，只要是拥有可能性的人都不会被排除在外。但美国梦的视角却是个人化的：它<strong>从不看向主角之外</strong>。<br><br>芝加哥城涅槃就是这样一种叙事。大火后的22年即开办的世博会，为提升城市居民生活水平而开凿的卫生运河——这是一个无瑕无垢的奇迹，如果排除密西西比河。<br><br>但边框之外是什么？一个人为他人所负的责任在美国梦中是缺席的。美国梦的成立必须依赖闭塞的私人叙事，必须享有没有代价的自由。<br><br><br>（七）<br><br>孙正义曾提出过一个“时光机器”理论，即一家跨国公司可以把发展中国家当成若干年前的发达国家来设计其业务逻辑，并沿着之前实践过的企业发展脉络随着这个国家的发展而成长。<br><br>这个说法背后的逻辑是，尽管国家和国家之间有巨大差异，其社会发展仍将遵循相似的模式展开。从这个角度来说，资本主义社会是具有<strong>预言性</strong>的。<br><br>美国的基础设施问题一直被中国媒体从民生角度抓住不放，尽管美国在此以前也是基建最为激进的国家。按照这个理论，中国的基础设施迟早会面临也许更广泛和严重的老化（因为我们的现代化速度是如此之快，而覆盖面又是如此之广）——至少是过时，因为它们的设计使用年限都是半个世纪往上。<br><br>我并不如何在意这些“后现代化”的问题最终怎样得到工程学上的解决，我关心的是，我们将如何对此做好<strong>心理准备</strong>？<br><br>特朗普们都认为美国天下无敌的状态才正常；日本的泡沫世代批评他们的年轻人软弱，他们的政策不够强硬。<br><br>是的，我们知道他们的偏见是基于过时的现实，但我们也有“一旦接受了某种设定“就深信不疑的东西啊：飞速的高铁，锃亮的楼宇，以及中国是世界的引擎……<br><br>生于爆发年代中的一代总会对漫长的冷寂感到陌生。但<strong>磨损</strong>毕竟是热力学第二定律的全部。<br><br>我想，即使在百年未有之大变局后，也有些东西不可能变得比如今更好了——我是说一种基于“希望”的叙事方式。<br><br><br>（八）<br><br>伟大的企业家和传奇的革命者都擅长两件事：一是让人们相信和追随其宏大而渺茫的理想；二是在这种疯狂的理想之中依然保持清醒，制定极度贴合现实情况的策略。中国之国运的内驱因素之一，在我看来，正是这个国家大部分时候都接受着这类人的领导。<br><br>从现实出发并依然贯彻相信的力量，将共产主义的理想色彩和并不乐观的新中国国情同时融合进统一的理论之中，中国特色的社会主义纲领在我看来，实为天才的构想（而“精苏”情绪，则是一种危险的<strong>浪漫化</strong>尝试，试图用苏联人民的崇高遮盖住其激进现代化尝试中本身的刻板和跛脚之处）。<br><br>在我看来，中国自己的叙事依照愿景长短可以分为三层：<br><br>第一层是关于完成伟大复兴（六中全会的决议中写道，我们比历史上任何时期都更接近伟大复兴的目标），这种叙事可以在参照系中量化：新中国的发展是个有关自力更生，追赶强者的故事。<br><br>第二层是关于实现中国特色的社会主义，这种叙事以共同富裕为目标，通过富强、民主、文明、和谐、美丽五个要素进行量化。<br><br>第三层是关于构建人类命运共同体，这个叙事是共产主义的最终目标。<br><br>领导者们对这些事情早就想得挺明白了 。但理想和现实的融合拉扯，毕竟事关存亡。<br><br>在关于现代化的讨论中，我们已经被“黑猫白猫”拯救了一次。而我感到隐忧的是，正如塔勒布提出的“反脆弱”中，韧性的来源是多样性和灵活性——在高度相似的价值体系下，我们有能力再一次在不可知的挑战面前灵活应变吗？<br><br><br>（九）一些小思<br><br>1.0<br>广义来看，资本主义并不仅是一种意识形态，而是一类事物贪图增殖的自我强化的过程。<br>从这个意义说，对大数据的迷恋是一种资本主义。对连结的迷恋是一种资本主义。因此元宇宙也是彻头彻尾的资本主义。<br><br>2.0<br>虚拟对人的价值在于可以使人在不同的人格间切换。因此，真正的线上社交应该对已有社交方式的割裂而不是融合。工具化、片段化的虚拟社交方式才是真正的需求。<br>不幸的是，以粘合度、参与度（还有更为堂而皇之的理由，安全）为衡量标准的社交网络公司做出的产品只会是真实人格的延伸。<br>也就是说，最后人们需要在线上和线下都同时管理多个极度相似的人格档案，参与饱和度更高的社交，丧失独处行为，完全成为连结网络上传递信息的节点。<br>所以哪怕最广义的资本主义也会导致人的异化，因为这个社会的发展并非是试图让人更幸福，而是让人变得更没有头绪。<br><br>3.0<br>为什么人总是幻想神是悲悯的？因为悲悯是这样一种溺爱的理性，使得制定秩序的神也需要依照人类的感性法则处理问题（或选择不处理问题）。<br><br>4.0<br>如何成为他人的守护天使101：不要在对方摔跟头的时候说“我早就说过/如果你按照我说的来你就不会……”。<br>如何成为中年人：无法克制自己纠正对方。<br><br>5.0<br>保持心情平静的两种方式：惯看秋月春风后的倦怠，认识到他人与自己无关的不在乎。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2021-11-28_我们生活的故事01.md"},{"id":"2019-12-29_salvation-mountain","date":"2019-12-29","title":"Salvation Mountain","titleZh":"救赎山","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"I. Unincorporated State Route 111 runs easy along the eastern flank of the Salton Sea. There are no posted speed limits, so one car after another drops into low-altitude flight, streaking past…","excerptZh":"（一）非建制 111号州道沿着索尔顿湖（Salton Sea）东翼惬意伸展，这里没有任何限速标记，于是车辆全部开启了低空飞行模式，倏倏超过视野中缓慢移动…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2019-12-29_救赎山.jpg","tags":["travel log","usa"],"tagsZh":["游记","美国"],"readingMin":11,"htmlEn":"<h2>I. Unincorporated</h2>\n<p><br>State Route 111 runs easy along the eastern flank of the Salton Sea. There are no posted speed limits, so one car after another drops into low-altitude flight, streaking past the freight train crawling across the view. Beyond the starboard window, distant mountains dissolve into haze. Close by, the immense lake lies impossibly still—an almost too-real mirage maintaining a restrained, intimate distance from the road while mile after mile of wasteland slips beneath the wheels. The scene can hardly bear the loveliness promised by the word “fairyland.” In sheer improbability, though, it rivals the wildest inventions of the imagination.<br><br>Past the salt lake, the road coasts into Niland, then seems to lose its nerve. It turns hard south, as if taking the shortest route out of this ash-colored land. Google Maps labels Niland—and Estelle, Verdant, and Rockwood farther south—an “unincorporated area,” or, still more bewilderingly, a “census-designated place.” Roughly translated, these obscure terms mean: places beyond the law.<br><br>The car turns east, passes a gas station with no gas, and enters a cluster of buildings arranged in the shape of a town. Under the dim yellow sun, the dry wind seems to have scoured away not only every person but every sign of life. Yet the houses we pass have cars parked outside, and the cars are not quite dirty enough to have been abandoned. That trace of evidence is enough to keep you from relaxing. In every patch of shade, some burrowing creature skilled at concealing its presence might be lying in wait.<br><br>Beyond the railroad tracks, the town ends without ceremony, and the car lurches into another nameless territory. On either side of the rutted dirt road, high-voltage pylons stand at measured intervals like the skeletons of gods. Knowing that the region once served as an antiaircraft-artillery training ground during World War II does little to dispel the feeling that we are traveling through the end of things—as if the descendants of an apocalypse had returned to their ancestors’ dead planet, where, among collapsed walls and shattered ruins, only these simple, colossal religious structures have defied time stubbornly enough to remain as proof that human beings once lived.<br><br>In other words, they have never forgiven us.<br></p>\n<h2>II. Salvation Mountain</h2>\n<p><br>A small patch of color appears without warning. As we approach, it seems to defy perspective: it refuses to grow. Up close, Salvation Mountain, for all the gravity of its name, proves anything but monumental. It is only a low, rounded mound.<br><br>First: it is not a miracle. Faced at last with the hill I had wanted—perhaps even longed—to see, I recoil.<br><br>First: it is not a miracle! God should be an idea beyond the laws of nature, while this little hill is wholly <em>unnatural</em>. It erupts from the land with no preamble, colors of almost violent purity colliding and heaping themselves together without disguise. Shamelessly, they declare: We never meant to become God. We are a pageant of the man-made.<br><br>Second: it is not a work of art. Art describes life as it ought to be; it gives form to ideas beyond the human. But in these graceless colors I see only the creator’s most faithful copy of a man in delirium. The thing does not transcend us. It is us—pathological, enlarged, distorted.<br><br>It is not a work of art! Across the mountain’s belly, and everywhere else the eye can land, the crooked slogan “God Is Love” forces itself forward. This is the ritual of an absurd cult, as though the world obeyed one inviolable law: repeat a wish often enough, and it becomes truth.<br><br>No. Chanting something over and over merely turns a man into his own believer. The maker thought he held the key to salvation. What he unlocked instead was the fanatical abyss of the self.<br><br>Finally, it is not even the devil’s fruit. The devil would never mock humanity’s distortion of God’s love so joylessly.<br><br>Across the entire hill there is not a spark of genius or inspiration—only the slapdash harmony of one mass-produced painted thing set beside another.<br><br>Surely even the devil would find it horribly boring.<br></p>\n<h2>III. Leonard Knight</h2>\n<p><br>Salvation Mountain was the work of Leonard Knight. One day in 1967, when he was thirty-five, he began reciting the Sinner’s Prayer in his car without warning: “Jesus, I’m a sinner, please come upon my body and into my heart…” According to Salvation Mountain’s official website, that was the day Jesus came upon him.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>Salvation Mountain, “Leonard Knight.” https://salvationmountain.org/salvation-mountain/about-leonard-knight/</span></span> From then on, he devoted himself entirely to God, hurrying from church to church in his hometown to tell them that Jesus had been misunderstood. Salvation was simple: accept Jesus and repent your sins. The churches told him it was not so simple.<br><br>…It was that simple!<br><br>In 1970, Knight and the other northerners in his town looked up to see a hot-air balloon passing overhead. He spent the next decade or so building a balloon emblazoned with the Sinner’s Prayer and the words “God Is Love,” hoping it would carry his message of salvation into the world. The balloon never flew. It was too large, too heavy. In 1984, after one last failed launch at Slab City, he decided to build a small sculpture before leaving the site of his heartbreak—a memorial. Four years passed, and he still could not make himself stop. Then the hill collapsed under the weight of its unstable foundation.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>Salvation Mountain, “Leonard Knight”; Salvation Mountain, “About Salvation Mountain.” https://salvationmountain.org/salvation-mount…</span></span><br><br>Knight was undeterred. Through trial and error, he arrived at a sturdier mixture of adobe clay and straw. He stayed another decade or so, and the second Salvation Mountain—the one that stands today—slowly took shape. Add a certain amount of struggle and negotiation with the government over paint contamination; a domed mud structure beside the mountain known as the Hogan; and a building Knight called “the Museum.” That, in full, is Salvation Mountain as it exists today.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Salvation Mountain, “About Salvation Mountain.” https://salvationmountain.org/salvation-mountain/about-salvation-mountain/</span></span><br></p>\n<h2>IV. Looking Down</h2>\n<p><br>At the summit of Salvation Mountain, wind charges unhindered across the plain. Unexpectedly cold, it makes me shiver.<br><br>I have just emerged from this strange man-made object. Now I am literally looking down on it, and I find myself divided.<br><br>As a mountain, it is hardly impressive in scale. As a painted sculpture, it is staggering in its immensity.<br><br>By the standards of art, the mound is graceless, hideously ugly. Judged by effort and concentration alone, it is the embodiment of a maker’s will far beyond the ordinary.<br><br>I have always believed that those who do nothing but march in place have no right to mock a man for running strangely. Knight had never received divine grace—he had misread grace—and, unlike Van Gogh, he had never been granted divine revelation. Yet nothing could embody the human spirit more fully than his persistence on the scale of mountain-building.<br><br>And yet that same persistence contains an unrepentant folly, a stubbornness that carries him ever farther from the divine and ever deeper into error…<br><br>Compared with those who create nothing at all, then, is Knight more worthy of celebration—or more deserving of hatred?<br></p>\n<h2>V. The Visitors</h2>\n<p><br>As I walk across the mound with these contradictions in mind, the noise around me is deafening.<br><br>Tourists pour from one private car after another, eager to unfold their bodies after so long cramped in their seats.<br><br>Their vacant minds unfold with them: a void capable of swallowing and assimilating everything, making all things commensurable, then congratulating itself on the feat.<br><br>A teacup and a forest are the same thing. Dental floss and a river are the same thing. A pebble and the universe are the same thing. Whatever enters the eye is the same thing.<br><br>And whatever the eye cannot see does not exist.<br><br>I watch them call to their companions and pull out their phones. I watch them scramble awkwardly over the sculpture for the chance to become the unquestioned protagonist of an overcrowded frame. In accent after accent, I hear them deliver grand verdicts on things they do not understand. Their weightless, hollow words disperse the strange atmosphere that once held this precinct under its spell—half divine, half demonic.<br><br>So long as we travel in packs, we dare to trample miracles, scorn the devil, and murder art.<br><br>What is a plague, after all, if not the whole snarl of attachments, emotions, and appetites that human beings carry with them?<br><br>Knight, I tell you: this spectacle has reduced your work to nothing.<br><br>Knight, I ask you: what do you fear most? Laughter? Mockery? Oblivion?<br><br>No. Attention that misses the point this completely.<br><br>You Noahs—faced with animals like these, do you still want to build your arks? (If I were Jesus, none of you would be saved.)<br><br>But God is dead anyway.<br></p>\n<h2>VI. Slab City and the Wilderness Artists</h2>\n<p><br>Salvation Mountain stands in a place called Slab City. Its population consists mainly of snowbirds—northerners who migrate south for the warm winters—and a smaller number of year-round residents, most of them living apart from society because of poverty or a desire to escape modernity. That, at least, is Wikipedia’s account.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>Wikipedia, “Slab City, California.” https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SlabCity,California</span></span><br><br>It looked a little different to me.<br><br>We followed a road that was only faintly a road into town. The plain has no markings, so in theory any track left by a vehicle might qualify. An unstaffed guard booth marked the official boundary. Later, driving out from the other direction, we saw the words painted on its far side: “Caution: Reality Ahead.”<br><br>RVs stood scattered at wide intervals. Their weather-beaten shells made it fair to wonder whether the <em>vehicle</em> part of the name still applied. Most of these capsule-like shelters had little stalls spread out in front. From a distance, they seemed to offer handmade souvenirs to tourists. Ruoruo wanted to get out and look around, but I stopped her. I could not shake the feeling that this suspicious air of anarchism might conceal an actual danger. We watched from afar.<br><br>Farther in, the creed of “God Is Love” began mutating into “You Need Only Love”—blind worship chasing a trend, clumsy imitation passing itself off as faith. The man-made objects grew denser and denser, and the settlement looked more and more like a junkyard.<br><br>Deep inside, the car finally turned left and reached the far northwestern edge of town—only to arrive at a sculpture garden called East Jesus. Here, functional dwellings, refuse, and assemblages burdened with the task of being art reached their climax of confusion. They had been left wherever they happened to land and so seemed to grow free, singing an outlaw’s wild song amid a feast of ruins!<br><br>You do not know how to define any of it.<br><br>But precisely because you cannot define it, you can call this “a place where people survive.”<br></p>\n<h2>VII. The Salton Sea and the Sonoran Desert</h2>\n<p><br>This is an inland desert, yet gulls skim inexplicably overhead. It is surreal.<br><br>An immense, beautiful lake lies perfectly still, while everything around it is desolate. Cities have been abandoned; only the exiled seem to remain. It is surreal.<br><br>Only later did I learn that the Salton Sea is not a natural lake. A century ago, torrential rain breached an irrigation canal and flooded the basin, creating it.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>California State Water Resources Control Board, “Salton Sea.” https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/waterrights/waterissues/programs/salto…</span></span><br><br>Half a century ago, it was still California’s most productive marine fishery, rich with life: a waterfront resort hailed as the “Miracle in the Desert.”<br><br>Then the water began to evaporate, with too little rainfall to replenish it, and the salinity climbed. Agricultural runoff laden with pesticides and fertilizers made the lake more toxic still. Today, its water is twenty-five percent saltier than the Pacific Ocean.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-6\" aria-label=\"Reference 6\">6</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">6</span><span>California Department of Fish and Wildlife, “Water Quality Sea Facts.” https://nrm.dfg.ca.gov/FileHandler.ashx?DocumentID=9212</span></span> The BBC described it as California’s “nightmarish vanishing sea.”<br><br>Salvation Mountain, Slab City, the Salton Sea… This truly is a surreal place.<br><br>Lines by Jiang Kui kept flashing through my mind:<br><br><em>Famed city east of the Huai,</em><br><br><em>fair precinct of Zhuxi.</em><br><br><em>Through ten miles where spring winds once blew,</em><br><br><em>nothing but shepherd’s purse and wheat, green upon green…</em><sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-7\" aria-label=\"Reference 7\">7</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">7</span><span>Jiang Kui, “Yangzhou Man” (扬州慢·淮左名都). https://www.gushiwenku.cn/shiwen/1b7f6f4/</span></span></p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span>Salvation Mountain, “Leonard Knight.” https://salvationmountain.org/salvation-mountain/about-leonard-knight/</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>Salvation Mountain, “Leonard Knight”; Salvation Mountain, “About Salvation Mountain.” https://salvationmountain.org/salvation-mountain/about-leonard-knight/; https://salvationmountain.org/salvation-mountain/about-salvation-mountain/</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span>Salvation Mountain, “About Salvation Mountain.” https://salvationmountain.org/salvation-mountain/about-salvation-mountain/</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span>Wikipedia, “Slab City, California.” https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slab_City,_California</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span>California State Water Resources Control Board, “Salton Sea.” https://www.waterboards.ca.gov/waterrights/water_issues/programs/salton_sea/</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-6\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">6</span><span>California Department of Fish and Wildlife, “Water Quality Sea Facts.” https://nrm.dfg.ca.gov/FileHandler.ashx?DocumentID=9212</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-7\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">7</span><span>Jiang Kui, “Yangzhou Man” (<code>扬州慢·淮左名都</code>). https://www.gushiwenku.cn/shiwen/1b7f6f4/</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>（一）非建制<br><br>111号州道沿着索尔顿湖（Salton Sea）东翼惬意伸展，这里没有任何限速标记，于是车辆全部开启了低空飞行模式，倏倏超过视野中缓慢移动的货运火车。右舷窗外，远山被氤氲的雾气离散得朦朦胧胧，而近处巨大的湖泊不真实地静静存在，以一种过于真实的蜃楼般的景象，在车轮下不间断延续的荒原景色中克制地与道路保持着咫尺的距离。它虽难以承担“仙境”一词的美轮美奂，却至少在不可思议的程度上可以匹敌某些最为虚幻的想象力。<br><br>离开盐湖，道路顺着惯性悠然淌进尼兰德，然后仿佛突然失却勇气似的径直折往正南方，为的是要以最短距离逃离这片灰烬般的土地。尼兰德（Niland），及其南方的埃斯特尔（Estelle）、弗丹特（Verdant）、罗克伍德（Rockwood），这些地点被谷歌地图识别为“非建制地区（unincorporated area）”，或更让人摸不着头脑的“普查规定居民点（census-designated place）”……简单来说，这些晦涩之词可以粗略理解为“法外之地”。<br><br>汽车向东一拐，告别没有油的加油站之后，就驶入了小镇模样的建筑群。昏黄的太阳下，岂止人影，简直连任何生命的迹象都被干燥的风沙刮走了。然而，出乎意料的是，沿途略过的住宅模样的房屋，廊前停放的轿车却并未肮脏到显出被废弃的模样；这隐隐约约的迹象恰到好处地提醒你不要放松警惕，因为每处阴影中也许都蛰伏着一种善于隐蔽气息的穴居生物。<br><br>而穿过铁轨，镇子被不由分说地拦截而下，车辆遽然进入下一片未知领域。颠簸的泥路两旁神明的骨架似的等距伫立着无数高压电杆。——就算理解了这片区域二战时曾作为防空炮兵的训练基地（于是因之有了被开发的历史），但切实在此间行进时，仍有某种“终末旅行”之感：如同末世后裔回到先民的死星，在断壁残桓之中，唯有简单宏大的宗教装置通过拒绝时间而死死成为人类曾经存活的明证。<br><br>也就是说，它们始终不曾原谅我们。<br><br><br>（二）救赎山<br><br>一小片色彩毫无征兆地出现。随着距离越来越接近，这团色彩却仿佛无视透视原理拒绝成长，于是临近一看，名号庄重的救赎山只是个与庞然大物相去甚远的小包状的突起。<br>首先，它不是一个神迹。面对这片我早想一探究竟（也许还心向往之）的山丘，直觉的反感却让我踟蹰不前。<br>首先，它不是一个神迹！神应当是某种超越自然之理的概念，但这匹小丘却彻底是“非自然”的存在。它是如此突兀地存在与此：那纯度极高的油漆不加掩饰地碰撞和堆砌，毫不愧怍地宣称，我们根本没想成为神——我们是人造物的盛典。<br><br>其次，它也不是一件艺术品。艺术描述“本应”的生活，表达超越人类的理念。而我在这些毫无美感的色彩中看到的，却是创作家对一个谵语者最忠实的临摹——它并没有高于我们，它就是我们，而且显然是病态的扩大的扭曲的我们。<br>它不是一件艺术品！山腹中央，以及其他目所能及的一切，都突出显现着“上帝是爱（God is love）”的歪斜标语——这是个荒诞的邪教仪式，仿佛世间存在这样一条不二法则：那就是通过不厌其烦的重复，愿望就可以变成真理。<br>不是的，反复的吟诵只会让人变成自己的信徒。创作者以为自己掌握了得救的钥匙，但他打开的却是名为自我的狂热深渊。<br><br>最后，这甚至不是恶魔的果实。因为恶魔永远不会以这种毫无乐趣的方式嘲笑人类对上帝之爱的曲解。<br>在这匹山丘上，你从中看不到任何天才或灵感的闪现，相反，它们整个显现出一种油漆物的量产品与量产品间粗制滥造的和谐。<br>一定就连恶魔也会觉得的太过可怕的无聊。<br><br><br>（三）伦纳德·奈特（Leonard Knight）<br><br>救赎山的创作者叫做伦纳德·奈特，他在35岁（1967年）的某天在自己的汽车中毫无征兆地开始吟诵起罪人祷告（Sinner&#x27;s Prayer）：Jesus, I&#x27;m a sinner, please come upon my body and into my heart... 根据救赎山的官网介绍，这就是耶稣降临他身上的日子。自此以后，他全身全心侍奉上帝，他奔波于告知自己故乡的教堂：耶稣被误解了。得救之道很简单，接纳耶稣，忏悔罪恶；可是教堂告诉他说，事情没那么简单。<br><br>……事情就是那么简单！<br><br>1970年，他和其他北方村民抬头看到一个热气球飞过自家小镇，于是他花费接下来的10余年的时间制造一个写满罪人祷告和“上帝是爱”热气球，为的是宣扬他坚信不疑的得救之道。可是这气球终究没有升空，太大而太重。1984年，他在斯拉布城（Slab City）最后一次尝试放飞失败。在他离开这片伤心之地之前，他决定建一个小小的雕塑留下纪念，四年间，他始终没能停下来……然而这匹小丘因为底座不稳坍掉了。<br><br>奈特没有灰心，他反复试验出了一种更稳定的陶土和稻草混合物，然后他又在此地度过了10余年时光，这才长成了如今的（第二座）救赎山。其间又经历某些和政府关于涂料污染的拉锯和波折，加上山丘旁边添加的带穹顶的泥屋（hogan）和一座被奈特成为“博物馆”的建筑……就是这救赎山如今的全部。<br><br><br>（四）从上往下看<br><br>站在救赎山顶部，平原上毫无顾忌奔袭的风居然刮得人有些寒意。<br><br>刚刚从这座怪异的人造物中走出，又以一种居高临下的态度俯视它，我着实感到五味杂陈。<br><br>是的，以一匹山去衡量，这个物体并不具备什么了不起的规模；但若把它当做彩塑品来看，则显出令人咂舌的宏大。<br><br>同样，以艺术的标尺来观瞻，这座土丘毫无美感、丑陋不堪；但若从纯粹努力和专注的角度来说，则分外充分地体现了工匠远超常人的意志力量。<br><br>我一直怀以这样的观点，那就是只会原地踏步的人，没有资格嘲笑用怪异姿势跑步的人；奈特虽从未蒙受天恩（他误读了天恩）如梵高般领到神灵的启示，但没有什么比其造山般的坚持更能体现人类精神了。<br><br>然而，从另一方面来说，正因为这种坚持中带有一种不知悔改的愚莽和倔强，使得他愈发背离神祗而堕入邪道……<br><br>那么，比起什么都创造不出来的人，奈特是更加值得被庆贺还是更值得被痛恨呢？<br><br><br>（五）参观者们<br><br>当我带着这样的矛盾心情行走在这匹土丘上，周围却是噪声鼎沸。<br><br>一群群游客从私家车里涌出，似乎因长久地蜷倨于座位上而急于展开自己的身体。<br><br>随之展开的还有他们空洞的思想：那种可以吞噬和同化一切的虚空，那种将世间万物等量齐观并深以为此的自满。<br><br>茶杯和森林是一回事，牙线和河流是一回事，卵石和宇宙也是一回事，所有进入眼睛的东西都是一回事。<br><br>与此同时，眼睛看不到的东西都不存在。<br><br>我看着他们呼朋引伴地拿出手机拍照，看着他们笨拙地攀爬雕塑，就是为了能在人流熙攘的相框中成为无可争辩的主角。我听他们用各种方言对毫不理解的事物大放厥词，听他们轻描淡写地用毫无营养的字句消弭掉这个结界中本来存在的亦神亦鬼的怪诞空气。<br><br>只要我们成群结队，我们就敢践踏神迹，蔑视魔鬼，杀死艺术。<br><br>所谓瘟疫啊，难道不就是就是人们带来的羁绊和七情六欲吗？<br><br>我说奈特，如今这场景让你的作品变得什么都不是了。<br><br>我说奈特，你最怕的是什么呢？是大笑吗？是嘲讽吗？是遗忘吗？<br><br>不，是眼前这般不得要领的重视。<br><br>诺阿们，面对这样的动物，你们还想造船吗？（我要是耶稣，你们都别想得救）<br><br>但反正上帝都死了。<br><br><br>（六）斯拉布城和荒野艺术家<br><br>救赎山所在之地叫做斯拉布城。这是一片主要由候鸟群体（snowbird community，指冬季搬到温暖的南方居住的北方人）和少数永居者：主要是由于贫困或想要脱离现代性而离群索居的人构成。这是维基百科的介绍。<br><br>但如果要我说，实际情况看起来有些不同。<br><br>沿着隐隐约约的公路进城的时候（是没有任何标记的平原，所以理论上任何车辆能够驶出的轨迹都可以称之为公路），道旁立着一个没有人守卫的警卫亭，这就是正式进入地界了（反方向离开的时候，这个亭子的另一面写着“小心：前方现实 Caution: reality ahead”）。<br><br>一些非常分散的旅居车（RV）停靠各处，从它们饱经风霜的外表看起来，很让人怀疑“车”这个属性是否还继续存在。无论如何，这些庇护所模样的胶囊外大都展开一个个摊位，远远看去，似乎是在售卖手工制的旅游纪念品（若若想要下车看看，却被我制止住了，我总觉得这无政府主义的可疑气息中未尝不含有某种危险的东西，所以我们只是远远看去）。<br><br>继续向前，“上帝是爱”的信仰逐渐变种，成为“你只需要爱”（一种蹭“热点”的盲目崇拜和拙劣模仿）。人造物的密度越来越大，所以就越来越像一个垃圾场。<br><br>深入腹地，最终车子向左一拐，到达小城的极西北——却是被称作“东耶稣（East Jesus）”的雕塑园。在这里，功能性的房屋、废弃物还有承担着艺术品职能的一件件组合物件终于达到混乱的高潮，它们被随处放置，所以也就自由生长——宛如在遗迹的盛宴中吟唱亡命之徒的狂歌！<br><br>你不知道该怎么定义它们。<br><br>但正因为不知道怎么定义它们，你才能将其命名为“人存活的地方”。<br><br><br>（七）索尔顿湖和索诺兰沙漠<br><br>明明是一片内陆的荒漠，却莫名会低空飞过一些海鸥。这很魔幻。<br><br>一片巨大而美丽的湖泊静静矗立，但周遭荒凉，城市被废弃，似乎只有被放逐者的存在。这很魔幻。<br><br>查阅资料才知道，原来索尔顿湖并非天然湖泊；它是一个世纪前的暴雨冲垮了灌溉渠，涌入盆地形成的。<br><br>半世纪以前，这里还是加利福尼亚州最多产的海洋渔业产地，拥有多种生物，是被称为&quot;沙漠中的奇迹&quot;的滨水旅游胜地。<br><br>然而，随着湖水蒸发（低降雨量并不能弥补），湖水盐度升高；同时，含有农药和化肥成分的农业地表径流也增加了湖泊的毒性。如今这湖水的咸度比太平洋还高出 25%。BBC把这里称作加利福尼亚州“噩梦般消失的海”。<br><br>救赎山、斯拉布城、索尔顿湖……这实在是一片魔幻之地。<br><br>我脑海中一直闪过这些语句：淮左名都，竹西佳处。过春风十里，尽荠麦青青……</p>","source":"./content/posts/2019-12-29_救赎山.md"},{"id":"2019-12-02_the-promised-and-the-forsaken","date":"2019-12-02","title":"The Promised and the Forsaken","titleZh":"被许诺和被荒芜的","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"(I) Culebra and Vieques Vehicles plowed back and forth along the long, narrow strip of coast. Oncoming cars were common, yet the road remained forever just short of two lanes wide—like the…","excerptZh":"（一）库莱布拉和别克斯 (Culebra and Vieques） 车辆在狭长的海岸线上一遍遍来回犁地。会车并非不频繁，但道路始终保持着差一点就到双向车…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2019-12-02_被许诺和被荒芜的.jpg","tags":["travel log","usa"],"tagsZh":["游记","美国"],"readingMin":10,"htmlEn":"<p>(I) Culebra and Vieques<br><br>Vehicles plowed back and forth along the long, narrow strip of coast. Oncoming cars were common, yet the road remained forever just short of two lanes wide—like the sunlight that occasionally poured through gaps in the slowly shifting clouds, delivering continuous low-grade discomfort and intermittent bouts of acute torment.<br><br>A passenger van bearing the scars of years of service jolted us toward our lodgings. Its air conditioner blew with tremendous vigor but little effect, like an energetic but hopeless salesman with no pitch. The driver had charged us only for the ride, but volunteered as our guide as well. Along the way, he pointed out what perhaps counted as monuments: the island’s only Catholic church, its only town hall, its only steel-framed bridge. Every so often, the van slowed to avoid a yellow dog, a cat, a rooster, or a wild horse idling in the road.<br><br>From behind a pane of glass, it was easy enough to call the place a spare but pleasant little town—even a prosperous one. Quite a few sailboats and yachts lay moored or drifting in the glittering, sheltered bay.<br><br>The unease surfaced only in flashes, as the moving window kept replacing one view with the next: a car abandoned in the roadside weeds; a listing, illegible billboard; a solitary vacant building with broken windows; a street of shuttered shops utterly out of place in a popular vacation destination—on Thanksgiving, no less.<br><br>Under the sun, in heat fierce enough to melt everything, the world was silent. Only the vines sent out signals of growth almost as visible as steam. They crawled across the ground, massed along the shore, hung upside down from branches. They had evolved into a posture perfectly suited to the climate, and in that posture spread across every hill. They could swallow anything, the way a plague could infect anything.<br><br>At dusk, the sun sank with the weight of fate. Dense black clouds drew apart like curtains, and the globe, now fully exposed above the horizon, suddenly rallied. The whole vault of the sky seemed to release a long, resplendent sigh. But blue won in the end. Blue is stubborn: even at noon, pierced and scorched until nearly transparent, it refuses to yield. And finally Orion’s Belt came alight.<br><br>After nightfall, the streetlamps along the shore cast an ominous red glow—pure red. This was a node made for dark things on the prowl. (What was watching from behind those crumbling, deathly white walls?) Only the absolute mechanical certainty of a jeep, all steel frame and iron bone, seemed capable of resisting the corrosion the night would inevitably inflict. Curled inside that shelter as we drove, we were grateful for the few bars giving off the warmthless glare of incandescent bulbs. Wherever there was that kind of light, you knew manufactured noise could spill out with it, and so you called this temporary reassurance home.<br><br>You see, the town was dead. Or at least gravely ill.<br><br><br>(II) The Bioluminescent Beach<br><br>The clear-bottom kayak gradually kindled fine points of light along the current, as though a relentless arrow were striking fire by friction in the vast darkness.<br><br>Farther from shore, the whole body of water became a magical catalyst. Stir the surface and the ripples materialized at once, like an echo made visible.<br><br>The paddle kept a steady rhythm; light swayed in and out of sight with every stroke. I tried to lie low in the darkness and move as quietly as I could, yet there was no way to pass without leaving footprints.<br><br>Looking up, I saw the deep, far-reaching sky gestating countless stars. They flickered and swelled—or rather, they pulsed with something like a heartbeat. Each was a star-egg holding a possibility. (After all, from an alien vantage, our solar system would be no more than one such undifferentiated point of light.) Admiration gave way to awe.<br><br>At such a moment, one can receive three forms of blessing at once: the sanctity of despair, sacred tranquility, and tranquil despair.<br><br><br>(III) San Juan<br><br>Rounding the fortified bulk of El Morro, the boat entered a narrow inlet. We docked at the far western edge of town, where centuries ago people had poured through the sole breach in the stone wall—the San Juan Gate. (Travelers arriving by sea today come instead aboard enormous cruise ships, docking at the newer port farther east.)<br><br>We climbed the steps onto streets of blue-green adoquines—beautiful paving stones cast from iron-smelting slag and carried here from Spain as ballast. At the end of the road stood San Juan Bautista Cathedral. It was one of San Juan’s earliest buildings and the oldest Catholic church in the United States; its appointments could be called admirably restrained—or, put another way, the space was exceptionally well ventilated.<br><br>At a glance, flaking lime had already mottled the white walls, while every object adorning the sanctuary had the sticky, faded look of something dampened and baked by tropical heat. It recalled the sense of time one finds in a winding canyon, where the grain of the rock seems to remember a primordial ocean washing through it. Here, within the cathedral’s empty white walls, time had carried away some majesty, some lived warmth, some finely worked ornament—some of whatever should make a person feel safe and, at the same instant, properly afraid upon crossing the threshold. What settled out was only a bare framework stripped of ceremony: a desolate relic whose sole remaining function was to offer physical shade.<br><br>But God had once shown you grace! Beside the San Juan Gate, the relic-like bronze monument of La Rogativa depicts a bishop and three young women holding torches and a cross aloft. Those prayerful torches, those hymns, made the invading British believe Spanish reinforcements had arrived ahead of them. The city was saved! From one band of colonizers! By the colonizers who had arrived earlier!<br><br>Hope is always insubstantial. Even so, people never turn it down.<br><br>We used to call hope a miracle. Now we call it civilization.<br><br>Through a slit in the lookout tower of Castillo San Cristóbal, the steaming tropical clouds in the distance looked like a fleet projected onto a mirage. Years ago, real fleets sailed toward this utterly exposed land. Fanatical Catholics swallowed a legend with nothing religious about it and hurried to name the whole sweep of land before them, indiscriminately, El Dorado.<br><br>They brought governors, germs, guns, and other devils—they brought civilization! And so the work of opening the land—and colonizing it—began.<br><br>Down the town’s narrow streets, the houses were painted in powdery pastels. Low-saturation colors and violent sunlight set each other off, suffusing the place with an air of innocence. By dusk, the bars were already packed; amid live music and alcohol, people greeted one another, danced, and embraced. They truly resembled the tropical scrub blown down across the hills in a hurricane: dying in great swaths, returning in great swaths, flourishing in such great swaths that they seemed never to learn a thing.<br><br>Do none of you realize that you are victims?<br><br><br>(IV) La Perla<br><br>Just one street beyond the old city lies La Perla—“the Pearl”—a slum. Walking the coastal road, I passed its bright, incongruous one-story houses wedged between El Morro and Castillo San Cristóbal. They were like a patch of fungus feeding on salt-soaked waves and merciless sun to sustain its harsh growth, defiant enough to acquire an almost romantic air. At the edge of the neighborhood, a vacant lot had been painted into a handsome basketball court bearing Carmelo Anthony’s Melo logo. This Puerto Rican, born in Brooklyn, felt, after all, some responsibility toward his homeland. And rather than summarize that responsibility in abstractions such as promoting economic growth or sustaining a thriving community, it was more honest to put it bluntly: people nearby now had somewhere to play basketball. Standing there alone, the court really had no other use. More than that, on all three occasions when I traveled that road, the bright court remained continuously in a state of “waiting to be used.”<br><br>The slum’s moment in the sun came with the video for 2017’s inescapable pop earworm <em>Despacito</em>—“slowly.” That summer, tourists from around the world flooded this drug-ridden, down-and-out place, astonishing the residents who struggled there. Yet perhaps it was not so surprising. In the music video that had swept the world, a sexy woman strutted and danced through this lost paradise, and suddenly this perilous, demon-ridden place fulfilled every stereotype of Latin America: passion, abandon, wild growth—and beautiful, full-figured women. (Puerto Rico had produced no fewer than five Miss Universe winners!) At the height of the rush, with visitors streaming in without end, the neighborhood committee decided to improve the tourist facilities. It raised $80,000 and repainted 402 homes. Now, two years later, apart from flaunting brighter artificial colors, its tourism did not seem to have improved at all.<br><br>It was impossible not to think of the scattered high-rises I had passed on the way from the airport to San Juan, abrupt towers standing like cheap apartment blocks. On that declining land, they conjugated modernization in a bizarre completed-progressive tense: once in motion, now definitively over. They represented the most inconsequential part of an unrealistic blueprint abandoned long ago. And every morning, the old streets woke to several new streaks of graffiti, along with rough patches of white paint meant to erase them. The absurdity was this: though Puerto Rico as a whole had become functionally paralyzed by debt, the municipal apparatus could somehow keep operating in the places that mattered least.<br><br><br>(V) After the Prosperity<br><br>I was born into a country in rapid revival and an age of peaceful, rapid growth. That was why I had never understood that decline—the opposite of growth—could actually exist. The first time I felt its existence sharply was on a Pittsburgh bus passing through a sinister neighborhood. I was on my way downtown to apply for a driver’s license at the DMV. Yet the crowds drifting through downtown as though they had nothing to do only deepened the impression.<br><br>If Pittsburgh showed me how a once-unassailable center of steel could achieve a comparatively dignified urban contraction, melancholy though the sight was, then Puerto Rico felt like the wounds left behind by war.<br><br>You could say they had been abandoned. Yet even in abandonment, they kept trying to inject themselves with some faint hope; they kept watch for something. The Uber driver who took me into the old city pointed toward Castillo San Cristóbal and told me in halting English that from its lookout, the view could roam all the way to Bermuda. I think Gabriel García Márquez was right: the magic of Latin America runs in its blood. Perhaps the moment the pope passed through the city holding a torch aloft, fate was already settled—you, you, and they were all destined to wait, to keep watch, to expect rescue from the civilized world. Just as the pope led the city’s residents in waiting for divine grace, La Perla waits for the next Latin song to become a global hit, while Puerto Rico, with a poverty rate of 45%, waits for economic aid from the federal government. You are destined only to wait for miracles.<br><br><br>(VI) つづく<br><br>After traveling through Puerto Rico, I began to consider a question: what does capitalism mean for civilizations outside the core?<br><br>A wholly subjective and symbolic intuition tells me that capitalism is a ripening agent. These peripheral economies, too, once ripened magnificently. Now their time has come to rot.<br><br>Latin America in the 1980s, Japan and Southeast Asia in the 1990s, and Europe perhaps soon. Then who will be the next—or the next after that—to be bled dry?<br><br>Us?<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>（一）库莱布拉和别克斯 (Culebra and Vieques）<br><br>车辆在狭长的海岸线上一遍遍来回犁地。会车并非不频繁，但道路始终保持着差一点就到双向车道的宽度——正如这缓慢变换的云隙间偶尔倾盆而下的阳光，带给人持续性的微小不适和间歇性的剧烈折磨。<br><br>一看便知磨损多年的载客用旅行车拉着我们颠簸向住处。冷气风力十足，性能却不见强劲，类似精力丰富却毫无话术可言的蹩脚销售员。司机只收了运输费，却也主动承担起向导的职责，于是我得以知道沿途驶过了也许具有纪念意义的，岛上唯一的天主教堂、唯一的市政府、唯一的钢筋架构的桥梁……车辆偶尔减速，为的是避让道路中央无所事事的黄狗，猫咪，公鸡，或者野马。<br><br>隔着一片玻璃看，无论如何都可以说是一个精简而怡人的村镇，甚至是富足的——波光粼粼的内海湾中停泊或漂浮着为数不少的帆船和游艇。<br><br>但唯有因移动而不断改换视野，令人不安的信号才间歇性地传来。比如路旁杂草丛中被弃置的轿车，歪斜而不可分辨的广告招牌，玻璃窗破损的无人独幢楼宇，以及与热门度假目的地极不相称的门户紧闭的商店街——况且还是感恩节。<br><br>阳光下，融化一切的热度中，万物静默，唯有藤蔓散发出几乎看得见水汽的生长信号。它们匍匐在地上，簇拥在海边，倒悬在枝杈间，它们以一种进化得完全适应气候的姿态活得满山遍野。它们什么都能够吞噬，正如瘟疫什么都可以传染。<br><br>黄昏，夕阳宿命般沉沦。浓厚的乌云如逐渐开启的帘幕般消失，那个完全暴露于海平面上的球体突然重新鼓起力气，于是整个天穹都因之残留下久久不散的辉煌叹息。但最终胜利的还是蓝色。蓝色是倔强的，哪怕正午时分它们被穿透烧灼而无限接近于透明……最终，猎户的腰带亮了。<br><br>入夜时分，临海路的街灯发出不详的红光（没错，是纯红色的灯光）。这是一个适宜暗物逡巡出没的节点（这倾颓而煞白的墙壁后面窥伺着什么呢？），而唯有以吉普车的钢筋铁骨般绝对的机械性，才能在此抗拒住整个夜晚都必然遭遇的侵蚀。蜷缩于这庇护中沿路行驶，还好有稀疏的酒馆弥散出白炽灯毫无温感的光亮。你知道这种光亮里面可以流淌出人造的杂音，于是你把这种暂时的安心称之为家。<br><br>你看，这个小镇算是死掉了，或至少也是病得不轻。<br><br><br>（二）荧光海滩<br><br>船底透明的皮划艇逐渐点燃顺着水流的细密光点，仿佛广袤的黑暗中，有一支执著的箭矢在摩擦生火。<br><br>离岸更远的地方，整个水域都变成具有魔法的触媒，搅动海面，波纹在瞬时被具象化，宛如一曲可以被看见的回音。<br><br>船桨规律滑动，光因之摇曳而时显时隐，我尽可能悄悄潜伏在黑暗中，却没有办法不留下任何足印。<br><br>抬头向上望，深邃而辽夐的天空正孕育出无数的星星。它们在闪烁，在膨胀，应该说更像是传达出某种心音的律动——它们是一个个代表可能性的星之卵（毕竟丛外星看来，太阳系也仅仅就是这样一个无差别闪亮的光点罢了），于是你开始欣赏，也开始敬畏。<br><br>在这种时刻，人可以同时拥有三种形式的祝福：绝望的神圣，神圣的宁静，和宁静的绝望。<br><br><br>（三）圣胡安（San Juan）<br><br>绕过要塞化的莫罗古堡，船只进入一片狭窄的内海水域。停靠小城的极西边的港口，几世纪前的人们便从石墙上唯一的缺口——圣璜之门——涌入城内（如今海路至此的旅客，却都是乘坐巨大的游轮停靠更东边的新港）。<br><br>拾级踏上翠蓝色的卵石路（这漂亮的卵石却是用炼铁产生的矿渣，它们被当做压舱物从西班牙被运至此地），道路尽头矗立着圣约翰大教堂。同时作为圣胡安最早期的建筑以及美国境内最古老的天主教堂，它的陈设可以说显得非常克制，或者说，具有良好的通风效果。<br><br>粗略看去，脱落的石灰已让白墙有些斑驳，而那些装点圣堂的饰品，无不带有一种因为受潮和被炎热气候烘焙而带来的黏答答的褪色的视觉感，以至于——正如人们在曲折的峡谷中体会到的原始海洋从那石头的纹理中冲刷而过的时间感——在这空旷白净的教堂的四壁之内，时间带走了某些威严，某些烟火，某些精雕细琢的装饰，某些本应该让人一踏进门就感到心安并同时诚惶诚恐的东西；于是最终沉淀下来的只有这毫无仪式感的棚架，通过给人们提供物理性的阴凉而成为荒芜的遗迹。<br><br>可是天主曾经有恩于你们啊！圣璜之门旁文物似的青铜雕像刻着高擎火炬和十字架的主教和三位少女——就是这些为祈祷而燃烧的火把和圣歌，让入侵的英军误认为西班牙的援军已提前到达……城市被拯救了！从一群殖民者手中！被更早到来的殖民者！<br><br>希望永远是缥缈的。但即使这样，人们永远不会拒绝希望。<br><br>只是以前我们把希望叫做神迹，现在，我们把它称做文明。<br><br>从圣克里斯多巴城堡瞭望塔的缝隙向外眺望，远处蒸腾的热带云犹如投射于蜃楼中的舰队。而多年以前，是真正的舰队向这片毫无遮拦的大地驶来，那些狂热的天主教徒听信毫无宗教色彩的传说，就着急地把这里的土地无差别地命名为黄金国。<br><br>他们带来了总督、病菌、枪炮和其他魔鬼——他们带来了文明！于是，拓荒和殖民就这样开始了。<br><br>从小镇狭窄的街道穿过，房屋被漆得粉粉彩彩，低饱和的色调和强烈的阳光相得益彰，自有一种天真烂漫的氛围氤氲在里面。黄昏时分开始，酒馆就已经拥堵不堪，人们在乐队的演奏和酒精中相互问好、舞蹈和拥抱——他们真的很像那些飓风天中被吹倒的漫山遍野的热带灌木，一大片片地死，一大片片地生，一大片片的简直繁茂得不长教训。<br><br>你们真的没有意识到自己其实是受害者吗？<br><br><br>（四）La Perla<br><br>与老城仅仅一街之隔的地方，是La Perla（=珍珠）贫民窟。沿着临海路散步经过，这些缤纷到格格不入的的平房被夹在莫罗古堡和圣克里斯多巴城堡之间，像一块靠吸食咸湿的海浪和毒辣的阳光而维持严酷生长的真菌，叛逆到几乎有了一丝浪漫的味道。镇子边陲有一块空地被漆成漂亮的篮球场，上面印有卡梅隆·安东尼的甜瓜标志，这名出生在布鲁克林的波多黎各人毕竟对故乡感到肩有责任——而这种责任与其被笼统概括为促进经济增长或维持社区兴繁，不如说就是直来直往的让附近的人有球可打；毕竟除此以外，它如此孤零零地现身此地，也委实没有其他作用了。甚至是说，在我经过这段路途的三段时间之内，这片鲜艳的场地始终只是持续性地处于“等待被使用”的状态。<br><br>这个贫民窟的高光时刻出现在2017年的洗脑流行歌曲Despacito（=慢慢来）的MV中，于是在那个夏天，世界各地的游客涌进这个毒品滋生的潦倒之处，让挣扎于此的居民大跌眼镜。但这也并非那么意外，毕竟在那红透南北的音乐短片中，一个性感的女郎就在这片失乐园中踢踏舞蹈，于是这百鬼横行的凶险之地突然就满足了人们对于拉丁美洲的刻板印象：热情、奔放、野蛮生长……还有美丽丰满的女人（从波多黎各已经走出了多达5位的环球小姐！）。于是，在那人流络绎不绝的鼎盛时期，这里的社区委员就决定完善其旅游设施，还集资8万美元把402间住宅重新粉刷……可如今两年时间过去，除了招摇着更加鲜艳的人造色之外，好像其旅游业也并没有什么改观。<br><br>这不得不让人联想起在从机场驶向圣胡安途中经过的某些突兀的，如廉价公寓搬挺立的零星高层建筑。它们在这片衰颓的土地上显出一种现代化的进行完结式，它们代表了某个早被抛弃的不切实际的蓝图中最无关紧要的部分。以及，每一天醒来，古街就会多几道涂鸦，以及为了抹消这些涂鸦而粗糙遮盖的白色油漆。这种事情的荒唐在就在于，哪怕整体来看波多黎各已经因为债务问题功能性地瘫痪了，在这种最无关紧要的地方，市政功能又仿佛还能维持基本的运转。<br><br><br>（五）繁华过后<br><br>是出生于一个快速复兴的国家和快速发展的和平年代，让我一直没能明白世界上居然也存在衰退——增长的反面，这种东西。而我第一次鲜明感受到这种现象确实存在，是在匹兹堡的公车中掠过的一片阴森的街区（彼时我正准备到市中心的车管所申请驾照）。然而，市中心无所事事般往来的人群只能更加加强这种印象。<br><br>如果说匹兹堡让我看到了曾经不可一世的钢铁中心以一种值得唏嘘的姿态实现还算体面的城市收缩，那么波多黎各带给我的感受则仿佛是某种战乱后的疮痍。<br><br>你可以说，他们被抛弃了，但被抛弃的同时，它们又在竭力向自己注入某种渺茫的希冀，竭力在守望着什么。载我进老城的Uber司机指着圣克里斯多巴城堡，用并不流利的英文告诉我说，那里的眺望台可以让视野一直驰骋到百慕大……我想马尔克斯说的不错，拉丁美洲的魔幻是根植在其血脉中的：也许从教皇举着火炬在城里穿过的时候，命运就已经决定了——你们，你们，还有他们，都注定要等待、要守望、要期待文明世界的援救——像教皇带领城内的居民等待天恩一般，La Perla在等待下一首爆红的拉丁歌曲，而波多黎各在45%的贫困率中等待联邦政府的经济资助……你们注定只是等待奇迹。<br><br><br>（六）つづく<br><br>游历波多黎各之后，我开始思考这样一个问题：资本主义对于非核心圈的文明究竟意味着什么。<br>一种完全出于主观感受的具有象征意味的直觉告诉我，资本主义是一味催熟剂。这些边缘经济体也曾辉煌地熟透过，而现在是到了腐烂的时候了。<br>80年代的拉美，90年代的日本和东南亚，以及或行将到来的欧洲。那么（再）下一个被吸血的是谁呢？<br>是我们吗？</p>","source":"./content/posts/2019-12-02_被许诺和被荒芜的.md"},{"id":"2019-01-09_he-and-his-cat","date":"2019-01-09","title":"He and His Cat","titleZh":"他和他的猫","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"A six-foot deep-sea fishing champion, member of the Lost Generation, soldier, psychiatric patient, boxing enthusiast, and cubano sato—an “ordinary Cuban”—left Sloppy Joe’s late one night. He cast a bleary eye toward the…","excerptZh":"一名身高六英尺的海钓冠军、迷惘的一代、军人、精神病患、拳击爱好人士、“普通的古巴人”离开了深夜的邋遢乔（Sloppy Joe）酒吧，他迷离望向灯塔巨大的…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2019-01-09_他和他的猫.jpg","tags":["travel log","usa"],"tagsZh":["游记","美国"],"readingMin":3,"htmlEn":"<p>A six-foot deep-sea fishing champion, member of the Lost Generation, soldier, psychiatric patient, boxing enthusiast, and <em>cubano sato</em>—an “ordinary Cuban”—left Sloppy Joe’s late one night. He cast a bleary eye toward the lighthouse’s great column of light, then ambled home with a long shadow and a men’s-room urinal in tow. Home was the mansion with the largest private pool in town, a wedding gift from his second wife’s uncle. Snow White, the six-toed cat who lived there, knew nothing of this—and would not have cared if she had. Before long, the name of her housemate, Hemingway, would become the name people gave her entire clan.<br><br>I stood in the garden where Snow White’s descendants lived—or rather, sprawled—as towering tropical plants rustled in the onshore Atlantic wind. Sunlight sifted down like fine drizzle through a small clump of mottled bamboo. Upstairs in the main house, a ceiling fan turned lazily; even from a distance, it seemed to waft over that peculiar coolness of the tropics. Across the way, in the carriage house’s upstairs writing studio, a Royal typewriter sat on a modest round table. An antlered deer head was nailed to the wall; a military pack rested on the floor against the wall.<br><br>Every morning at six, he entered his study. At noon, he left. He spent his afternoons and evenings deep-sea fishing—or dutifully following the authoritative prescription for hereditary bipolar disorder: heavy drinking. Hangovers may have been the norm. Even so, over some nine years, this typewriter kept hammering out words that became <em>A Farewell to Arms</em>, <em>Men Without Women</em>, <em>Green Hills of Africa</em>, “The Snows of Kilimanjaro” … If I had once been enthralled by the “beautiful-form” trajectory of Yukio Mishima’s life—his precipitous transformation from a slight young man—Hemingway’s life was almost too <strong>American</strong>.<br><br>He seemed to have lived through everything: champion deep-sea fisherman, member of the Lost Generation, soldier, psychiatric patient, boxing enthusiast, “ordinary Cuban.” More important, as a well-built, hard-edged man, he embodied a philosophy forged through “action”—through the peculiarly American sense of mission behind his romantic restlessness, and through the deep-seated egocentrism that drove him to carry out his own vision of justice. It was a way of thinking diametrically opposed to Kantian contemplation.<br><br>To my mind, he was a poor representative of the Lost Generation. The reason is simple: he was never lost in the first place. Very early on, he had already found in heroism a definite form of self-expression. The truly lost one was his friend, the poet and dreamer F. Scott Fitzgerald, whose talent sprang chiefly from a heaven-sent sensitivity and melancholy. Where Fitz forever reached for dreams beyond his grasp, Hemingway drew his talent from a worship of the <strong>ideal</strong>. Fitz’s writing was sorrow leaking out; Hemingway’s was force spilling over.<br><br>It had never occurred to me that a writer might exist as Hemingway did. The writers I admired—W. Somerset Maugham, Marcel Proust, Yukio Mishima …—all made literature out of a “rejection of life.” Hemingway, that intensely American writer, made his through a “traversal of life.” Put another way, he belonged to a rare breed of writer: one who conducts his search through “action,” not “imagination.”<br><br>I do not so much envy that way of living as recognize how fundamentally at odds it is with my own temperament. But I do, after all, live in his native land. Perhaps the pragmatism in this country’s bones will remake me beyond recognition. Perhaps even that is something I would welcome with open arms.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>一名身高六英尺的海钓冠军、迷惘的一代、军人、精神病患、拳击爱好人士、“普通的古巴人”离开了深夜的邋遢乔（Sloppy Joe）酒吧，他迷离望向灯塔巨大的柱形光亮，然后拖着长长的影子和一个男厕，踱回坐拥镇上最大私人泳池的洋房——他第二任妻子的叔叔送给他们的结婚礼物。家里的六趾猫咪白雪并不知道（就算知道也不在乎），不久之后，这个同居者的名字，海明威，将成为了人们给予它们这一族的称谓。<br><br>我站在白雪后代居留（四处躺倒）的庭园里，高耸的热带绿植在大西洋的向岸风中簌簌响着。而阳光从一小撮斑竹的叶条中被淅沥沥地筛下来，主楼二层的吊扇懒洋洋地转动，远远地带来一种热带地区独有的清凉感。对面的马车库里，二楼书室中央一张不大的圆桌上摆放着皇家打字机，墙上钉着长着犄角的鹿头标本，靠墙处的地板上则摆放着一个军旅背包。<br><br>他每日六时走进自己的书房，中午时分离开；下午和晚上则出海钓鱼、或遵照医嘱执行针对家族遗传型躁郁症的权威处方：酗酒。宿醉也许会成为常态，但无论如何，其中的某九年间，这个打字机上曾经连续拼下了这样一些单词：永别了、武器；没有女人的男人；非洲的青山；乞力马扎罗的雪……如果说以前的我曾醉心于三岛由纪夫纤细少年断崖式成长的“美形”式的生活轨迹，那么海明威的生命则简直太过<strong>“美式”</strong>。<br><br>他似乎全部都经历了——作为海钓冠军、迷惘的一代、军人、精神病患、拳击爱好人士、“普通的古巴人”，但或许最重要的，作为一个健美而强硬的男人，他以这个国家特有的浪漫主义式躁动的使命感，和贯彻自身正义的根深蒂固的自我中心，展现出一种被“行动”所塑造的哲学——和康德式沉思截然相反的思考模式。<br><br>在我的观念中，他是“迷惘的一代”不合格的代表。原因很简单，他根本就不迷茫，他很早就找到了英雄确定形式的表达。真正迷惘的，是他的朋友——具有梦想家气质的诗人——菲兹杰拉德，这种才华主要起源于天赐的敏感和忧郁。和菲兹对梦想不可及的憧憬不同，海明威的才华主要来源于对<strong>理想</strong>的崇拜；可以说，菲兹的文字是哀伤感的泄露，而海明威的文字是力量感的溢出。<br><br>应该说，我从未意识到作家可以以海明威的方式存在的可能性。我所钦慕的人们——毛姆、普鲁斯特、三岛由纪夫…，他们的文字无不成就于“对生活的拒绝”；而作为一个非常美式的作家， 海明威的文字则成就于“对生活的遍历”。换种方式说，海明威是稀有派的，以“行动”而非“想象力”来实践自身追寻的人。<br><br>但与其说我羡慕这种生存方式，不如说我深知这种生存方式和我自身气质有根本性的抵触。但毕竟我生活在他的故土呢，也许这个国家骨子里的实践主义会把我改造得面目全非也说不定。我对此也许也是张开双臂欢迎的。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2019-01-09_他和他的猫.md"},{"id":"2018-12-28_checking-in-on-california","date":"2018-12-28","title":"Checking In on California","titleZh":"加州打卡","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"(I) California is less a modern place to live than a modern colony. What it lacks, ultimately, is any sense of native ground. People here do not seem to live all the…","excerptZh":"（一） 加州这地方与其说是个现代化的生活区毋宁说是个现代化的殖民地。 它到底缺乏一种乡土气，所以人们在这里不似彻头彻尾地生活。对这种存在方式更妥帖的说法…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2018-12-28_加州打卡.jpg","tags":["travel log","usa"],"tagsZh":["游记","美国"],"readingMin":4,"htmlEn":"<p>(I)<br><br>California is less a modern place to live than a modern colony.<br><br>What it lacks, ultimately, is any sense of native ground. People here do not seem to live all the way. A truer account of life in California might read like this: depending on their circumstances, they tear through ostentatiously, swagger about as conquerors, lie low, or rustle under cover waiting for an opening.<br><br>A coastline blessed by heaven with brilliant weather ought to possess a gravitational pull all its own; migratory birds should winter here. But <em>winter</em> is still a verb of motion. It contains the immense distance, in a self-introduction, between “I live in California” and “I once lived in California.” All its gifts and miracles keep crowds surging toward it. Yet when the tide goes out, nothing has settled in the city.<br><br>A city that swelled almost overnight out of the California Gold Rush still has the pursuit of wealth in its bones. Ironically, that blunt, unvarnished culture of wealth is also the source of these cities’ vitality. This, I think, is the Gold Rush’s spiritual legacy: most residents share, to one degree or another, a tacit understanding—though one particular tribe proudly calls its members “Silicon Valley people”—that this place is not generally embraced as home. Its very existence as a way station gives the city its almost sinful allure: here, one can keep running wild with a clear conscience.<br><br>California, in other words, is treated as a journey.<br><br><br>(II)<br><br>Alongside its cutting-edge technology, the Silicon Valley of my imagination possessed a humanistic vision of the future. It would offer a model for how people might one day relate to one another and to nature. From the fragments I had read and heard, I imagined that ambition here followed a natural order: people lived in reverence for the natural world, and their work followed natural forms of interaction.<br><br>What I found on my pilgrimage instead was a strange tableau: the barrenness of the land and the uneven tribal clusters of technology companies. Buildings rising from desolate slopes looked like colonial outposts planted here and there on some other planet. Each cluster marked itself off like a foreign concession, nearly autonomous. Such sheltered isolation bred indifference to everything beyond it—manifested, once again, as the polished elitism I despise most. This, it turned out, was the true meaning of the holy ground.<br><br>Yes, “advancement” has its price. Innovation’s very capacity for breakthrough necessarily contains something maddening. In one of the first places to legalize marijuana, people seem especially gifted at producing chaos and wreckage. Under so naked a law of the jungle, those best able to fend for themselves are the last people to need a universal commonwealth.<br><br>And so the futurism of a Silicon Valley that worships Pareto is realistic in a very particular sense.<br><br><br>(III)<br><br>Melrose Avenue has its pink walls and graffiti; K-Town, its Instagram-famous Korean food; and Griffith Park, its sweeping view of the enormous Hollywood Sign. Los Angeles is made for checking in. Beyond that, it has very little to recommend it.<br><br>The doors of its detached homes are locked tight. Immigrants speaking with accents have grown fiercely wary, as though forever braced for some malevolent scheme to emerge from the dangerous crowd—every gas-station restroom displays a strategically placed OUT OF ORDER sign. Smile at a passerby and you can feel their astonishment at such unprovoked kindness. On the road, meanwhile, the blistering stream of traffic treats the posted maximum as a minimum—a singularly rude display of the arrogance that vigilance has pressed into shape.<br><br>If San Francisco is unlivable because it lacks any sense of home, Los Angeles is restless because it lacks integration. The places worth checking in at have been filed under the name of a single city only because they happen to lie near one another; each has a different soul. Looking at all that multicolored graffiti, I saw wholly random eruptions: casual, unserious creations without any coherent cultural core. The mess that passes for an “arts district” reveals Los Angeles as a canvas open to anyone’s marks. Only people certain they will never make a home here would swing the brush with so little fear or restraint. Take away their artistry, and these marks would become as repellent as the rash of illicit ads plastered over city walls. In essence, both are simply information clutter, indifferent to how a neighborhood looks or grows.<br><br>Seen this way, the City of Angels is at once a place blessed and a place abandoned.<br><br>Perhaps all of California is.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>（一）<br><br>加州这地方与其说是个现代化的生活区毋宁说是个现代化的殖民地。<br><br>它到底缺乏一种乡土气，所以人们在这里不似彻头彻尾地生活。对这种存在方式更妥帖的说法是，依据情况不同，这里的居民招摇地扫荡、大模大样地制霸、低调地蛰伏、或者窸窸窣窣地伺机而动着。<br><br>按理说，作为承蒙天恩而抱拥明媚气候的海岸线，它理所应当拥有一种内在的吸引力——候鸟应当在此越冬。但“越冬”这个词所代表的流动性，也正好体现出自我介绍中“在加州生活”和“在加州生活过”之间巨大的差异。就是说，这里虽因为各种天赋和奇迹而不停攒动着趋之若鹜的人流，但浪潮过后，城市中却什么也没有沉淀下来。<br><br>事实就是，作为一个自淘金热潮中倏而膨胀起来的城市，其内里到底还是带有逐富的意味。而这种不饰措辞的直来直往的财富文化，令人感到讽刺的是，居然也是这些城市中蓬勃朝气的来源。作为这种淘金的精神遗产，我想，大多数居民都或多或少都拥有这样一种鲜被言说的共识（尽管其中某群会自豪地管自己这一伙叫做“硅谷人”），那就是这里并不被人们接纳为普遍的“家园”——于是，这种作为中转站般存在的方式让这个城市拥有了让人心安理得地继续放肆下去的罪孽般的魅力。<br><br>也就是说，加州被人们当做了某种旅途。<br><br><br>（二）<br><br>我梦想中的硅谷，除了其尖端的技术之外，应该同时具备一种人文主义的未来感：它将代表人与人、人与自然的未来的互动方式——在我读到和听到的只言片语中，我猜想，这里的人们拥有一种自然的奋斗模式：首先、他们的生活是崇尚自然风貌的；其次，他们的工作是符合自然的交互模式的。<br><br>但我在巡礼的旅途中看到的是，这片土地本身的贫瘠和一簇簇科技公司间参差的部落感构成一种奇妙的图景：那些自荒凉的坡道上拔地而起的楼宇，从某种意义上说，正是人们在某个行星上种植下的星星点点的殖民地。它们各自形成一个个租界样的划分，颇有种自治的意味。而那种从被庇护的隔绝中生出的对周遭的淡漠感，则再次展现为一种我最为厌恶的精英的精致性。而这才是这片圣地的真意。<br><br>没错，“先进”自是有其代价的。创新精神所代表的突破性中必然也含有令人癫狂的因子——作为率先将大麻合法化的地区，这里的人们也似乎特别擅长制造混乱和狼藉——在这种昭然若揭的丛林法则中，独善其身的人们最不需要的就是大同。<br><br>因此，信仰帕累托的硅谷所拥有未来感也特别具有现实性。<br><br><br>（三）<br><br>梅尔罗斯大道有粉墙和涂鸦，KTown有网红的韩国料理，格里菲斯公园可以眺望好莱坞巨大的标牌……因此，洛杉矶是个极适合打卡的城市。但除此以外，它也实在没有其他值得称道的地方了。<br><br>独栋的洋楼房门紧锁，操着口音的移民也生出了一种强烈的戒备心，仿佛时刻防范着危机四伏的人群中可能出现的图谋不轨（每个加油站的卫生间都狡猾地立着“故障”的标牌！），向行人微笑，你可以感到他们对这种没来由的善意大感诧异；而车道上滚烫的车流，以最高限速为最低行车速度的车流，则分外粗鲁地体现出一种被戒备压榨成型的傲慢。<br><br>如果说旧金山的不宜居留体现在其“家园”氛围的缺乏上，洛杉矶浮躁的成因则是缺乏融合感造成的。可以说，这些值得打卡的地点完完全全是恰巧因为地理位置接近，而权且被归在了这个城市的名下，尽管它们各自都有着不同的灵魂。我望向那些各色的涂鸦，它们彻底就是随机出现的毫无严肃感的随性的创作，完全缺失一种一以贯之的文化内核。这种作为“艺术街区”的杂乱，展现出洛杉矶可以任人涂抹的画布的特性——也只有深知自己不会在此安家的人们，才敢于如此毫不畏首畏尾地挥笔。你可以想象，这种创作如果失去其艺术性，将会和牛皮藓一样变得令人避之不及，虽然它们的本质都是毫不在乎街区容貌和成长的杂乱资讯而已。<br><br>从这中意义上讲，天使之城既是被祝福的地方，也是被遗弃的地方。<br><br>也许整个加州都是。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2018-12-28_加州打卡.md"},{"id":"2018-10-20_the-strata-of-new-york","date":"2018-10-20","title":"The Strata of New York","titleZh":"纽约层积","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"The moment our car came off the Triborough Bridge into Manhattan, I had the uncanny sense that I was in Milan—and not because we had entered Little Italy. The illusion had several…","excerptZh":"从车子自三区大桥驶入曼哈顿开始，我就恍然有种身在米兰的错觉，而这并不是因为我们进入小意大利的缘故。造成这种错觉的因素：世贸中心以辉煌的玻璃装潢映入眼界的…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2018-10-20_纽约层积.jpg","tags":["travel log","usa"],"tagsZh":["游记","美国"],"readingMin":4,"htmlEn":"<p>The moment our car came off the Triborough Bridge into Manhattan, I had the uncanny sense that I was in Milan—and not because we had entered Little Italy. The illusion had several sources: Eataly at the World Trade Center, blazing into view in glass; or the subway—two-way tunnels revealing themselves as pure function, carriages built to no standard but carrying capacity, graffiti and stains rebelling against lifeless brick walls. All of it registered the same startling consonance between the two cities. Most of all, when I wandered as far as Washington Square Arch, the warm early-autumn sunlight, softened by wisps of cloud, lay languidly across the crowd. I could almost believe I was standing beneath Milan’s Arco della Pace. Perhaps New York carries a Milanese air. Or perhaps it is the reverse: Milan has an unexpected taste of New York.<br><br>And it does not end there. On the broad avenues of Greenwich Village, the sight of wide leaves stirring in the wind can suddenly call Paris to mind. New York is like a reflection in glass: viewed from another angle, another city appears. Yet New York, Paris, and Milan do share an unmistakable inheritance—the legacy of cities that, in one dimension or another, came of age too soon. Multiracial daily life inhabits classical cross-shaped or radial street plans and mingles easily with contemporary fashions. Unlike Florence, where crowds of tourists have thoroughly transformed the old-world character, the old-fashioned quality of New York or Milan has not hardened into scenery for pilgrimage. Behind the scenes, it has dissolved completely into the ordinary backdrop of residents’ lives. Or, to reverse the terms again, modern crowds have taken up residence, without the slightest incongruity, in old cities completed a century ago.<br><br>As I stepped onto the streets of Times Square, where ringing bells filled me with solemnity and excitement, I watched the surrounding billboards trumpet a way of life that flares into brilliance and then stops dead. I thought of what had surrounded Gatsby: the “raw vigor that chafed under the old euphemisms” and “the too obtrusive fate that herded its inhabitants along a shortcut from nothing to nothing.” In the resurgence of the wasteland age, those things had not dwindled into a relic left to whimper like jazz. Quite the contrary. The stories playing out without cease in this district—the stage and scenery too—are no different from those of a century ago. Only costume and diction have changed: the figures taking the stage now wear the swagger of this age and speak its more direct lines.<br><br>One senses that cities such as New York, Milan, and Paris do not entirely belong to human beings. They move to a pulse of their own, carried slowly onward by their own momentum, growing perhaps, or rusting. (Tokyo’s more devouring mode of existence is something else again.) Their way of standing there, looking down upon everything around them, does not come merely from the fact that people happen to live there. They seem self-evidently meant to stand where they do, their presence its own proof. What kinds of crowds pass across the land, how quickly they succeed one another and grow old—the cities themselves do not care.<br><br>They resemble works of art: different patterns incised into the same kind of section. Crowds and eras wash like flowing water over streets whose appearance has scarcely changed, leaving behind memories hardened into forms both large and small: a sculpture crouched at a corner, an art installation stumbled upon by chance. Each remnant can absorb the temper of an entire age, then settle quietly out of sight—John Lennon, compressed into the word <em>Imagine</em> in a corner of Central Park—with the spare reticence peculiar to things left behind. As every street grows crowded with ever weightier memories, what remains for people to do but commemorate, and make original work that deepens the commemoration? Yet even the most groundbreaking art to turn remembrance into inspiration cannot overthrow the place that gave it birth. It is absorbed instead, settles into sediment, and finally becomes part of the city.<br><br>Once culture itself becomes the foundation, it can no longer be the flower that sprouts from it. It becomes the soil, accumulating ever thicker. This is what I smell in these cities: a dreamlike fervor to become strata.<br><br>If Beijing’s spatial severances manifest a kind of folding, then New York’s unbroken continuity through time manifests a kind of stratification.<br><br>In short: if humans are repetition machines, then every moment in history must naturally be a unique repetition.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>从车子自三区大桥驶入曼哈顿开始，我就恍然有种身在米兰的错觉，而这并不是因为我们进入小意大利的缘故。造成这种错觉的因素：世贸中心以辉煌的玻璃装潢映入眼界的Eataly；或者地下铁——完全以其功能形式展现出来的双向隧道，以纯粹运力为建造标准的车厢，毫无生色的砖墙上涂鸦和污渍的反叛，无不显示出两个城市间令人震惊的同调。尤其是，当我漫步至华盛顿拱门的时候，那种初秋因云絮荫庇而显得温软的阳光正懒洋洋地耷拉在人群身上，让我几乎以为自己正身在米兰的和平门下。可以说，纽约具有一种米兰的气氛；也可能完全相反，那就是米兰意外地带有纽约的风味。<br><br>当然并不仅于此，格林威治的宽阔大街上，行道树的阔叶随风飘动的情致也会突然令人想起巴黎……可以说，纽约如同玻璃的反光，因为观测角度的不同，总能呈现出一种和其他城市的相似——虽然，纽约、巴黎以及米兰之间也的确有着不言而喻的共通性：那是在某个维度中过早成熟的城市的某种遗产般的绵延。以古典的十字或放射型街区为底蕴的多种族的生活气息，混搭上具有当代特色格调的潮流。与佛罗伦萨这种因游客密布而完全变味的古朴相反，纽约或米兰的老派并未成为一种用于参拜的景致，而是幕后彻底融进居民日常的寻常图景；或相反，是现代人群毫无违和感地寓居在了这座世纪前就被搭建完整的老城。<br><br>踏上时代广场那片因敲钟而给我以庄严和兴奋的街道上，看着四周的广告牌位过于喧嚣地展示一种从璀璨戛然而止的生活方式——我想，盖茨比周遭那种“不安于陈旧辞令的粗犷活力”，那种“沿着一条捷径从零跑到零的过分突兀的命运”，并未随着荒原时代的重新振作而变成爵士乐般呜咽的遗迹；恰恰相反，如今在这个街区中不间断地上演的故事也好，这个舞台与布景也好，都和百年前别无二致 。唯一变更的，不过是登场的人物们穿起了更贴合这个时代的张扬衣物，言语出更贴合这个时代的直率词句罢了。<br><br>你可以感受到的一点是，像纽约、米兰或巴黎这些城市，它们并不完全被人类所拥有，而是以自己的脉率，自顾自地以惯性慢慢地成长，或者说，锈蚀（这和东京那种吞噬性的存在方式仍然有所区别）。它们的矗立与此而睥睨他物的存在感，并不是因为这里有人在居住，而仿佛它们以自证的方式本应伫立在此——但至于这片土地上究竟来来往往什么样的人群，而他们又以怎样的速率更迭和衰老——它自是不在乎的。<br><br>就如同在同种切片下雕镂出不同花纹的艺术品一样，人群和时代如流水般从这些外观几乎不曾变更的街道上冲刷而过，残剩下大大小小的被固化的记忆，而那就是在蜷踞在转角处的给人不经意撞见的雕塑或某些艺术装置。它们将也许是整个时代的风貌纳入自身并安静地窝于一隅（约翰列侬就被浓缩为中央公园一角的“想象力”），保持着残剩者简洁的矜持……就这样，当每一条街道都开始拥挤着越来越厚重的回忆，那么人们所能做的，除了缅怀和以独创性的作品加强这种缅怀，便别无他法；而这些将缅怀变作灵感的艺术，无论多么具有突破性，仍然无法实现对这个发祥地的颠覆，转而被吸纳而沉淀，最终成为这个城市的一部分……<br><br>如果文化本身成为了地基，文化就不再会是发芽的花朵，而是越积越厚的泥土，而这正是我在这些城市中所嗅到的想要成为地层的梦想般的热烈。<br><br>因此，如果说北京空间上的割裂展现出了某种折叠，那么纽约时间上一以贯之的延续则展现出了某种层积。<br><br>一言以蔽之，如果人类的本质是复读机，那么历史的每个瞬间理所应当都是独特的重复。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2018-10-20_纽约层积.md"},{"id":"2018-03-15_flow-and-counterflow","date":"2018-03-15","title":"Flow and Counterflow","titleZh":"流与逆流","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"(I) Sometimes time takes on a grain as when nothing happens and fingertips tremble against the artery's intent as over the open plain the starry sky and wheatfield may each grow music…","excerptZh":"（一） 时间有时会成为一种颗粒感的东西 正如无事发生时 指尖违背动脉的意图而颤抖 正如旷野上 星空和麦田也许各自长出 不同声道的音乐 在某种杂交的梦境中…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2018-03-15_流与逆流.jpg","tags":["poem","everyday chronicles"],"tagsZh":["诗","日常记趣"],"readingMin":3,"htmlEn":"<p>(I)<br>Sometimes time takes on a grain<br>as when nothing happens<br>and fingertips tremble against the artery&#x27;s intent<br>as over the open plain<br>the starry sky and wheatfield may each grow<br>music on separate channels<br>inside some hybrid dream<br>phosphor-fire like water  warm snow like salt<br><br><br>(II)<br>Things made by God<br>being and nothingness  light and dark  one person and another<br>Things made by man<br>shackles  accidents  Fermat&#x27;s little theorem<br>Before impact, you see<br>your life will often flash before your eyes<br>but when I collided with you, it didn&#x27;t<br>nothing did<br>why?<br><br><br>(III)<br>Out of white noise accumulates<br>a butcher-block cloud—an omen of a turning sky<br>When the storm is about to break<br>electronic transition: impossible<br>one more jump back into the prelude<br>and the gesture, after countless attempts to clench, can only keep saying<br>even so  even so…<br>even so  one must take a dialectical view…<br><br><br>(IV)<br>When shadow swallows the moon<br>I unravel the secret of passing each other by<br>Contestants on dating shows, you see<br>aren&#x27;t they gifted at factoring?<br>So this is a math problem<br>An encounter whose inner product is zero is no encounter<br>—yet coprime, too, and—<br>born prime, isn&#x27;t that also<br>a kind of romance?<br>The tide, having grasped this, for some reason sud—<br>went dim<br><br>(Noise)<br>A tight weave of drumbeats<br>the movement turns<br><br>Modulate upward<br>localized heat beginning in the spinal cord<br>gradually becomes<br>a vibration traversing every inch of shed skin<br>watching and waiting<br>with just one slit left open<br>it can airdrop into the abyss of consciousness<br>self is current<br>current is channel<br><br><br>(V)<br>Point sources of light<br>drag their tails through the river channel<br>a duality born when the medium changes<br>these relatively stationary<br>comets<br>cannot carry any<br>biological wish<br><br><br>(VI)<br>Disguised as liquid<br>descending deeper underground<br>an analysis of probability<br>a train running upstream through the dark<br>At the end of the dream  aging is<br>a dry evolution<br>while entropy dissolves every<br>hypothesis of “I could have”<br>too immense, isn&#x27;t it—<br>this monotonically increasing sin<br><br><br>(VII)<br><br>Secret Technique  Pre-established Harmony<br>repeat for the 3,957th time<br>step left-foot first into the absolute realm of the emergency stairwell<br>Because of this caution<br>today&#x27;s room, before I have even opened the lock,<br>still strains to maintain<br>a form adapted to darkness<br><br><br>(VIII)<br>Vows are shackles<br>no wonder I travel light<br>There is water at the right temperature to dissolve voicing and throat<br>How do you describe a tomorrow that never disappears?<br>Safe, perhaps<br>Beyond that<br>it deserves no word with feeling in it<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>（一）<br>时间有时会成为一种颗粒感的东西<br>正如无事发生时<br>指尖违背动脉的意图而颤抖<br>正如旷野上<br>星空和麦田也许各自长出<br>不同声道的音乐<br>在某种杂交的梦境中<br>磷火似水 温雪如盐<br><br><br>（二）<br>神造的事物<br>有与无 明与暗 人与人<br>人造的事物<br>枷锁 事故 费马小定理<br>相撞之前 你看<br>常常是出现走马灯的<br>我撞上你时却没有<br>什么都没有<br>为什么呢？<br><br><br>（三）<br>从白噪音处累积的<br>砧板云 这是变天的前兆<br>山雨欲来之时<br>跃迁不能<br>再一次跳回前奏<br>而无数次尝试握紧的手势只是不断说出<br>即使这样 即使这样…<br>即使这样 也要以辩证的眼光…<br><br><br>（四）<br>阴影吞没月亮的时刻<br>我解开了关于擦肩而过的秘密<br>相亲节目里的嘉宾 你看<br>不是擅长因式分解吗<br>所以这是个数学问题<br>内积为零的邂逅就不是邂逅<br>——但互质也、<br>生为素数不也<br>是一种浪漫啊。<br>明白了这一点的潮汐不知为什么突<br>就黯淡下来<br><br>（杂讯）<br>以绵密的鼓点<br>转换乐章<br><br>升调<br>从脊髓开始的局部发热<br>渐渐转变为<br>遍历每寸肤屑的震感<br>伺机等待<br>仅仅漏开一道口子<br>就可以向意识的深渊空降<br>自我即水流<br>水流即通道<br><br><br>（五）<br>点状光源<br>在河道中拖尾<br>因介质更改而出现的二象性<br>这些相对静止的<br>彗星<br>无法携带任何<br>生物性的愿望<br><br><br>（六）<br>以液体的伪装<br>降入更深的地底<br>关于概率的解析<br>黑暗中溯流而上的列车<br>在梦的尽头 衰老是<br>一种干燥的进化<br>而熵溶解掉每一个<br>「我本可以」的假设<br>过于庞大了不是么<br>这单增的罪孽<br><br><br>（七）<br><br>秘技 预定调和<br>重复第三千九百五十七次<br>用左脚迈入安全梯的绝对领域<br>因为这份谨慎<br>今天的房间在我还未开启门锁之前<br>仍然全力保持适应<br>黑暗的模样<br><br><br>（八）<br>誓言即枷锁<br>难怪我一身轻松<br>有种水温足以消融发声和咽喉<br>要怎样形容永不消逝的明天呢？<br>安全吧<br>除此之外<br>它实在配不上任何带有色彩的语汇</p>","source":"./content/posts/2018-03-15_流与逆流.md"},{"id":"2017-12-02_a-member-of-society","date":"2017-12-02","title":"A Member of Society","titleZh":"社会人","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"(I) Once silence is pumped in the waterline slowly rises the price of reducing the risk of running aground: a slide into the abyssal zone of feeling (II) Even when it presents…","excerptZh":"（一） 沉默注入后 水位逐渐升高 作为降低触礁风险的代价滑入 情绪的深海区 （二） 即使以混沌示人 时序图中仍然有K线滋长 （三） 存活是个恶劣的字眼！…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2017-12-02_社会人.jpg","tags":["poem","everyday chronicles"],"tagsZh":["诗","日常记趣"],"readingMin":2,"htmlEn":"<p>(I)<br>Once silence is pumped in<br>the waterline slowly rises<br>the price of reducing the risk of running aground:<br>a slide into the abyssal zone of feeling<br><br><br>(II)<br>Even when it presents itself as chaos<br>candlesticks keep sprouting<br>across the time-series chart<br><br><br>(III)<br>Survival is a vile word!<br>The decent surrender their restraint to it<br>the shameless grow fouler still<br><br><br>(IV)<br>Only when things grow dull<br>does my gaze grow clearer, more sincere<br>answer resentment with rectitude<br>answer enmity with resentment<br>an underdog should have an underdog&#x27;s way of staying alive<br><br><br>(V)<br>The first resurrection put me fast asleep in the morning<br>the second ought to wake me early in the night<br><br><br>(VI)<br>Action is one way of doing evil but<br>action is also one way of purging sin<br>without action, nothing counts<br>greed turns us against<br>covalent bonds<br><br><br>(VII)<br>There are earthquakes everywhere<br>but, thinking it over,<br>nothing is worth setting one off<br><br><br>(VIII)<br>Over the manicured lawn that afternoon<br>hung the distinctive fragrance<br>of life severed at the waist<br><br><br>(IX)<br>What, after all, is my loyalty to you—<br>a miracle born of faith or<br>faith born of a miracle<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>（一）<br>沉默注入后<br>水位逐渐升高<br>作为降低触礁风险的代价滑入<br>情绪的深海区<br><br><br>（二）<br>即使以混沌示人<br>时序图中仍然有K线滋长<br><br><br>（三）<br>存活是个恶劣的字眼！<br>拥有美德的因它放弃矜持<br>不知廉耻的变得更加猥琐<br><br><br>（四）<br>唯当事情变得无趣起来<br>我的眼神剔透得愈发真诚<br>以直报怨<br>以怨报仇<br>下狗就该有下狗的生存方式<br><br><br>（五）<br>第一次复活让我在早晨沉沉睡去<br>第二次复活理应让我在夜晚早早醒来<br><br><br>（六）<br>行动是一种作恶但<br>行动也是一种净罪<br>不行动就什么也不算<br>贪婪让我们对共价键<br>心生敌意<br><br><br>（七）<br>到处都在地震<br>但仔细想来<br>却并没有什么值得引发地震<br><br><br>（八）<br>午后精心修剪的草坪上<br>有着被拦腰截断的生命<br>特有的馨香<br><br><br>（九）<br>我对你的忠诚到底算是<br>因信仰引发的奇迹还是<br>因奇迹引发的信仰</p>","source":"./content/posts/2017-12-02_社会人.md"},{"id":"2017-05-01_years-of-pilgrimage-switzerland","date":"2017-05-01","title":"Years of Pilgrimage: Switzerland","titleZh":"巡礼之年 - 瑞士","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"The soft piano swells, and the program moves on to Liszt: Années de pèlerinage. Première année: Suisse. From the plane window, the town lies still and soft beneath the winter snow, like…","excerptZh":"绵柔的琴声渐强，曲集移往李斯特、巡礼之年。 第一年： 瑞士 。 从舷窗俯望下去，冬雪下覆盖的市镇沉静而柔软，好似湖畔旁慢慢积聚的白云，几乎有了一丝因长时…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2017-05-01_巡礼之年.jpg","tags":["travel log","europe"],"tagsZh":["游记","欧洲"],"readingMin":5,"htmlEn":"<p>The soft piano swells, and the program moves on to Liszt: <em>Années de pèlerinage</em>.<br><br><em>Première année: Suisse.</em><br><br>From the plane window, the town lies still and soft beneath the winter snow, like white clouds gathering slowly beside the lake, almost giving off, after so long a fermentation, the faint aroma of wine. And this is Geneva: an international city that is home to world-renowned institutions—the Red Cross, the World Health Organization, the United Nations Office at Geneva, CERN.<br><br>And yet I would sooner call it a town. Geneva may wear the grand title of world capital of watchmaking, but its Flower Clock occupies no more than a small corner locals pass on their walks. I prefer the city at noon in the mild spring: biting into a sandwich, watching birds score swift lines across the sky, like contrails already fading into blue. Flags drift idly overhead, their emblems representing one of the world&#x27;s most developed banking sectors. But you hardly care. Then it strikes you: did Rousseau once sit on a bench much like this one, gazing toward the mountains of France as he conceived <em>The Social Contract</em>, the book that would scandalize the world?<br><br>Follow the crescent of the lake north and Lausanne is only half an hour away. I lingered in the central station while figures hurried past in every direction. Among them I searched for a silhouette from the last century—Margaret Le Coz, escaped from the pages of Patrick Modiano&#x27;s <em>L&#x27;Horizon</em>, pausing here once upon a time. But no. She belongs to time in another dimension, a time made more for disappearance than for forgetting.<br><br>So let us climb the gentle vine-covered slopes—I think you will like these wines, wrapped tight in sunlight. Lake Léman shimmers below in shade after shade of blue-green. Look far enough, and perhaps you will glimpse another tiny town curled at the foot of the mountains on the opposite shore.<br><br>Why, that is Évian! Yes, really—the Évian on the mineral-water bottle.<br><br>Let us take a boat east. At the far end of the eastern shore stands Chillon Castle. You pass beneath towering battlements that ten whole centuries of wind, rain, and snow have failed to humble—and still no Romantic reverie comes. The trouble is the story of old Europe: in the absence of drains, people supposedly began each morning by emptying the night&#x27;s filth from the second floor into the street—straight onto the spot where you now stand.<br><br>Down in the dungeon, the world seems to fall away until only the sound of water remains. Through an iron grille, the lake dazzles like a candle: even as it kindles the graceful mountains across the water, perhaps it burns away the prisoners&#x27; last drop of hope.<br><br>But then, graffiti at historic sites is hardly a Chinese monopoly, is it? The pillars here are covered too, layer upon layer, with Roman letters. Amid the crush, Byron&#x27;s signature—framed with impeccable solemnity—is impossible to miss.<br><br>Now let us follow the railway north. Outside the window unfolds the almost absurd natural spectacle of this astonishingly rich country; inside the carriage, the announcements slip from French into German. Thun, Interlaken, Lucerne... The stations roll by, shamelessly draining your camera&#x27;s battery and devouring its memory. Honestly, when the city drops away and you find yourself suddenly exposed to mountains and water like these, what can you do but gape in awe? As for me, I still cannot believe that the mere reflection of a snowcapped mountain could bring tears to my eyes, one after another... Werther was right: such sublime majesty possesses an absolute power. It seeps into the heart, and the joy it brings is sacred and pure.<br><br>The train stops: Zurich. This “super” city contributes more than a fifth of Switzerland&#x27;s GDP and perhaps contains more than a fifth of the country&#x27;s bustle. In a country that is practically one vast pasture, the result is just the right kind of comfort—not too quiet. At a café on an old street, watching the occasional group of university-age students go by, you remember that ETH Zurich is Einstein&#x27;s alma mater. At dusk, the last long rays of sunset strike the streetlamps alight. Candles flare in restaurant windows; dinner begins. The Fondue Tram crosses the city, trailing the rich aroma of cheese fondue. I climb the twin-towered Grossmünster and, from its cramped viewing platform, lean out over the city as it slowly falls asleep, just as I had imagined. As twilight condenses, the tide of night washes Orion into the sky.<br><br>Near the border, at the northern end of the line, lies St. Gallen, the city of a thousand steps. From its outskirts, you work patiently inward through one curved barricade of houses after another, until a final narrow alley releases you without warning onto an open lawn, absurdly out of place. You have already read up on the Benedictine abbey and its priceless collection of ancient books. Even so, you are unprepared for an institution founded as early as the eighth century to loom like this—a colossus gazing down upon creation. You pull on wool-felt slippers, deferential as an uninvited guest who has wandered into another person&#x27;s study. Each Baroque flourish adds weight to air already heavy with age. Eventually, turning a corner, you happen upon an old aerial photograph: houses embracing the church like roots clustered around a flower.<br><br>We live to make pilgrimages! you think, unable to contain your excitement.<br><br>But in the end you revise that hasty formulation. Perhaps this comes closer:<br><br>To live is to build yourself a Tower of Babel.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>绵柔的琴声渐强，曲集移往李斯特、巡礼之年。<br><br>第一年： 瑞士 。<br>从舷窗俯望下去，冬雪下覆盖的市镇沉静而柔软，好似湖畔旁慢慢积聚的白云，几乎有了一丝因长时间酝酿而散出的酒香——而这就是日内瓦 。作为一个国际都市，它怀抱着包括红十字会、世界卫生组织、联合国欧洲总部、 欧洲核子研究中心等名越四海的世界级组织。<br>然而我说，它也许更像是个小镇。比起世界钟表之都这样的煊赫称谓，花钟不过是当地人散步时可以顺带经过的城市一隅的小景。而我更偏爱的是，在春风拂煦的中午时分，啃着三明治，看飞鸟在天空留下一抹抹敏捷的身影——几乎像极了湛蓝天空上转瞬即逝的航迹云。那些悠然飘荡的旗帜提醒你，这些纹徽可代表着世界上最为发达的银行业呢；可是你并不十分理会。诶、你突然想到，当年卢梭会不会也如你这般坐在长椅上，望向对岸的法国群山，构思着他那惊骇世俗的《社会契约论》呢？<br>沿着月牙型的湖泊北上，半个时辰便是洛桑 。我在中央火车站中流连许久。到处是匆忙的人形交错，而我在这混杂中试图寻找一个上世纪的背影——玛格丽特·勒科兹——她自帕特里克的《地平线》中逃逸而出，曾经在这里稍作休憩。然而不行的，她属于另一中维度时间，而那个时间比起遗忘更适合消失。<br><br>所以让我们爬上那些匹并不陡峭的种满葡萄的山坡吧——我想你会喜欢这些紧裹着阳光的佳酿！粼粼波光下的莱蒙湖正泛出深浅不一的青色；而如果你继续极目远眺的话，也许你可以看到对岸山脚下另一个蜿蜷的小小的镇子。<br>嘿、那里就是依云哦！没错、千真万确是依云矿泉水的那个依云哦。<br><br>让我们乘船向东！东岸的尽头便是西庸城堡。你从那些久经风雪（整整十个世纪的风霜雨雪！）而依然弥坚的高耸的城垛下走过，却依然做不出任何浪漫的怀想。因为据说，没有排水系统的古老欧洲的居民，总是在清晨从二楼把那些隔夜的秽物向街道一倾而下——就是你现在站的这个地方！<br>而潜入地牢，世界仿佛被剥离只留下水声。从铁栅栏望出，因反光而炫目的湖面如同蜡烛，在点燃隔岸山脉绰约风致的同时，只不定也烧尽了囚徒们最后一滴希望。<br>可是话说回来、谁说老外就不会在古迹上乱涂乱刻呢？那些柱子也被层层叠叠的拉丁字母覆盖着；只不过，即使在这样的拥挤之中，被一本正经装裱起来的拜伦的签名仍然是尤为显眼就是了。<br><br>沿着铁道北上，让我们在火车广播由法语转向德语的途中，一览这个拥有惊人财富国度的同样近乎荒谬的自然景观。沿途数过的站名： 图恩 、 因特拉肯 、 卢塞恩 …它们会毫不愧怍地榨干你相机的电量和内存；毕竟说真的，当你离开繁华的都市圈就突然被暴露在这样的山光水色中时，你除了以目瞪口呆的方式表达自己的敬畏之外，还能做什么呢？反正我啊，我都不相信自己居然会仅仅因为凝望雪山的倒影就可以啪嗒啪嗒地滴泪……少年维特说得不假，这壮丽的神威有沁透人心的绝对力量，因而这欢乐是神圣而纯粹的。<br>列车停靠， 苏黎世 。这个为整个瑞士贡献超两成GDP的“超级”城市或许也装下了整个国家超两成的热闹，而这对于几乎就是庞大牧场的国度来说是一种不至太过安静的刚刚好的舒适。在古街的咖啡馆中坐下，偶有大学生模样的年轻人结伴经过，你这才想起联邦理工正是爱因斯坦的母校。傍晚时分的街道上，渐隐渐长的夕阳擦燃街灯。餐厅点燃烛光，晚宴开始了：有轨电车带着奶酪火锅的馥郁穿城而过。而我登上大教堂的双子塔楼，在并不宽敞的平台外，俯身看向整个城市如预想中渐渐睡去。就在这逐渐凝练的薄暮中，猎户被夜潮冲上天幕。<br><br>一路向北的终点，几乎可以算是边境的千阶之城， 圣加仑 。你从外侧开始，不厌其烦地突破一道道民居铸成的弧形封锁，才终于在又一次穿过促狭的胡同后，突然置身于一片不合时宜的开阔的草坪。——你早就在攻略中尝试了解本笃会修道院和它那无价的古籍藏书，然而你仍然没有料想到，这个早在八世纪就兴建起来的经院、居然会是这样一个睥睨众生的庞然大物。你穿上羊粘质地的拖鞋，恭敬得像不请自来的擅自踏入他人书斋的客人；每一个巴洛克式的堂皇装饰都要徒增这室内本就沉重而古雅的空气，你终于不经意在某个转角瞥见这样一张老旧的航拍照片：民宅环抱教堂，犹如根须簇拥花朵。<br>人活着就是为了朝圣！你抑制不住激动地想。然而你最终还是修改了这种仓促的说法。 还是说这样表达会更贴切一些呢：<br><br>活着，就是为自己造一座巴别塔。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2017-05-01_巡礼之年.md"},{"id":"2016-08-10_one-way-into-japan","date":"2016-08-10","title":"One Way into Japan","titleZh":"片道东瀛","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"Preface A month in Japan proved so full that it left almost no room for thought. Only in the interval after the journey—that “transition” where two lives joined—could I gather the thoughts…","excerptZh":"前言 区区一月的短暂停留让我的日本生活直充实到几乎没有筛出任何可供思考的空隙，于是，我便只有借在这旅行结束之后的间隙、或称两种生活接轨的“过渡”，将当时…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2016-08-10_片道东瀛.jpg","tags":["travel log","japan"],"tagsZh":["游记","日本"],"readingMin":21,"htmlEn":"<h2>Preface</h2>\n<p><br>A month in Japan proved so full that it left almost no room for thought. Only in the interval after the journey—that “transition” where two lives joined—could I gather the thoughts I had jotted down into something like a continuous consciousness. That month did more than fulfill a longing I had carried since childhood. It gave the word “Japan” an entirely new meaning. First, it meant the cities I had measured with my own steps; then, the ruins of the dreamscape I had once imagined; finally, the trace of my passage through them—which is to say, the trace of my existence.<br><br>The title of this journal takes <em>katamichi</em> in its ordinary Japanese sense: one way. The journey set in motion a change that cannot be reversed. I mean not only the time that has vanished, the time to which I can never return, but the trajectory of my existence itself, drawn onto another course by the journey.<br><br>This was nothing like the Japan I had constructed in two dimensions. Before me now stood a real Japan: filled with reality, bounded by reality, shaped by reality—a Japan made real all the way through. And within it I was neither a concept nor a bodiless spectator, but a body occupying its own full-sized volume in three-dimensional space. Which is to say: I took part in creating this world.<br></p>\n<h2>1.0 Kinkaku-ji, Ginkaku-ji</h2>\n<p><br>I woke with a start from an afternoon nap and discovered that Kinkaku-ji was only two stops away.<br><br>The best purification rite available to me was to take out my phone and open the excerpts I had saved two years before.<br><br><em>The Temple of the Golden Pavilion</em>. It was my first encounter with Mishima. From then on, I was assimilated by the dark-red substance hidden in those words. Unlike other writers, Mishima did not principally use the backdrop of an age to reflect its spirit. He projected his own spirit into the ages in which his stories unfolded.<br><br>—And there was Kinkaku-ji, reigning over the world in the absolute glare of its gold leaf. Its magnificent robes refused any harmony with the nature around it.<br><br>“I will not blend in with you.”<br><br>The pavilion itself was an arrogant declaration. There, beauty revealed its origin: an exacting singularity, and from that unrepeatability, purity and refusal.<br><br>Beauty, therefore, is despair. Beauty longs for destruction. Beauty is the premonition of ruins.<br><br>Kashiwagi may have been right: the world is transformed not by action, but by knowledge.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">1</span><span>Yukio Mishima, The Temple of the Golden Pavilion, translated from the Japanese by Ivan Morris.</span></span><br><br>That was how it should have been. Yet at Kinkaku-ji, I grasped nothing beyond the feverish heat of a noisy crowd.<br><br>In other words, it was a botched pilgrimage.<br><br>And yet, unexpectedly, I understood those things at Ginkaku-ji.<br><br>It happened in the fierce morning sun. Hungry, I followed the lush Philosopher’s Path, climbed the approach built into the hillside, and stepped all at once into a secluded garden.<br><br>It was still early; only a few visitors wandered through. A monk was drawing furrow after meticulous furrow across the gravel with a long rake.<br><br>I followed the path winding through the garden and stopped on a small bridge, gazing for no particular reason at the enormous carp moving slowly below.<br><br>The dense, exquisite leaves of the little maples were reflected in minute detail upon the water. They were veins, or the lobes of a lung. Beyond the forking branches lay broad, unhurried, full-bodied clouds, and behind them a blue sky that filled the entire field of view.<br><br>But that was not all. Through this lifelike reflection, arranged in near-symmetry as though symmetry itself had constructed a backup of the entire world, you could still see everything on the bottom of the pond: pondweed, nearly motionless carp, the flat, empty bed.<br><br>Lean out over the bridge, and of course your own image sank into the water as well. The double meaning, heavy with implication, was complete.<br><br>This was a world on the other shore that could exist only by attaching itself to this shore. Your own existence became a contradiction: at once the observer on this shore and a participant in the making of the world on the other. You watched yourself take part in the world—or perhaps you were being watched by yourself.<br><br>And what kind of world was it? The moss-covered stones around the pond seemed to form the perimeter of a ward, maintaining the immense tension of the pond, all-encompassing as a Dirac sea. Yet the ethereal image was only a phantom suspended on the surface. What shone darkly and serenely through the transparent medium was the pond bottom alone, ancient and unchanged. The double exposure unfolding inside this little pool thus belonged to two separate worlds. The one in which we existed was unquestionably more fragile. One ripple, one sweep of a fish’s tail, and everything in it would be disturbed without exception. Which meant that it could not exist there unsupported. It propagated through a medium.<br><br>And have you ever considered that this medium is the immense void itself, present like a law? The root of the world’s chaotic arising is therefore this: the world itself is a channel.<br><br>(This morning I found myself rereading Wang Anshi’s “Guizhixiang: Reflections on the Past at Jinling.” The contrast between its two stanzas seemed to contain precisely the overlap I had seen between reflection and pond bottom, instant and eternity, change and the unchanging:<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-2\" aria-label=\"Reference 2\">2</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">2</span><span>Wang Anshi, “Guizhixiang: Reflections on the Past at Jinling.” Translation from the Chinese for this essay.</span></span><br></p>\n<blockquote class=\"pull\"><p>I climb and cast my gaze: the old realm in late autumn,<br>the air just turning austere.<br>For a thousand miles the lucid river lies like silk;<br>emerald peaks gather in clusters.<br>Far sails and rushing oars cross the setting sun;<br>against the west wind, tavern flags slant aloft.<br>Painted boats under pale clouds; egrets rise from the starry river—<br>no picture could contain it all.<br><br>I think of the past, of splendor pursued in rivalry;<br>I sigh: beyond the gates, atop the tower, grief follows grief.<br>For a thousand ages men have climbed here to face this view,<br>idly lamenting honor and disgrace.<br>The old affairs of the Six Dynasties follow the running water;<br>only cold smoke and withered grass congeal into green.<br>To this day the singing girls still sing, again and again,<br>the old refrain of “Flowers in the Rear Courtyard.”)</p></blockquote>\n<p><br>Yet does the instant on this shore not also create the illusion of permanence?<br><br>The pure sky above the maples; wind rustling through the green leaves, mingled with waves of cicada song. At such a moment I wanted only to find a low patch of grass and lie down. This place possessed a powerful magnetic field that dissolved desire. Look around: everything enclosing you had stood here quietly for a thousand years, and was destined to stand for a thousand more. The rising and setting of the sun, the turn of the seasons—here, they lasted no more than an instant. The place was like a cross-section of growth rings exposed where a branch had been cut. The living fault still glowed with fresh, tender color, but growth had stopped. It would forever keep the face it wore at the moment of the cut.<br><br>This instant, in other words, led straight into eternity.<br><br>Yet can the instant’s eternity on this shore not also drive a person out of his mind?<br><br>The pure sky above the maples; wind rustling through the green leaves, mingled with waves of cicada song. At such a moment I suddenly wanted to set fire to the entire garden. This place possessed a powerful spell that could make you lose your soul. Look around: everything enclosing you had stood here quietly for a thousand years, and was destined to stand for a thousand more. The rising and setting of the sun, the turn of the seasons—here, they were only the same recurrence, over and over, without the slightest alteration. Like the Dead Sea Scrolls, the place reenacted at every instant every prophecy already written. I could no longer bear it. I had to escape the cycle. And the way out was to destroy utterly this fantasy of eternal life.<br><br>This is what we call <em>hysteria siberiana</em>, my friend.<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-3\" aria-label=\"Reference 3\">3</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">3</span><span>Haruki Murakami, South of the Border, West of the Sun, source of the allusion to hysteria siberiana.</span></span> Human beings forever long for eternity and fear it.<br><br>I have argued before that beauty’s singularity is the origin of purity and refusal. Eternity and singularity spring from the same source. Beauty thus extends into clarity and cruelty.<br><br>Clarity means the sharp edge, the incision, the refusal to compromise—every impulse that resists homogenization. Cruelty is our fragile life’s pursuit of eternity, and its revenge upon it. As <em>The Temple of the Golden Pavilion</em> says: “On the one hand, a phantasm of immortality emerged from the apparently destructible aspect of human beings; on the other, the apparently indestructible beauty of the Golden Temple gave rise to the possibility of destroying it. Mortal things like human beings cannot be eradicated; indestructible things like the Golden Temple can be destroyed.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><br><br>“No, it’s on a beautiful spring afternoon like this that people suddenly become cruel … while one’s vaguely watching the sun as it peeps through the leaves … above a well-mown lawn.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-1\" aria-label=\"Reference 1\">1</a></sup><br><br>【My adolescence clouded over into the gray of dawn. The world of dark shadows terrified me, yet the sharply outlined life of daylight did not belong to me either.】<br></p>\n<h2>2.0 Roppongi</h2>\n<p><br>I had never seen a city like this.<br><br>From the rooftop helipad of a Roppongi tower, I looked out over buildings packed onto every scrap of land in sight, all the way to the horizon. Here and there, high-rises gathered in clusters; elsewhere, radio towers stood alone. They resembled signal-bearing synapses, revealing a regional logic of concentration. As the fiery afterglow at the edge of the sky faded, the city did not sink into sleep as expected. Light illuminated and adorned it, revealing how thoroughly it had adapted to darkness. In that violent contrast, a surging vitality became more legible than it had been by day.<br><br>It was the first time I understood that a city could assume this form.<br><br>I rode the Yamanote Line in a circle: Shibuya to Shinjuku, on to Ikebukuro, Ueno, Shimbashi, Shinagawa. At every stop, the same crowds in motion, the same ranks of high-rises, the same unmistakable modernity.<br><br>The city where I was born and raised has visible growth rings—first ring road, second, third—the marks left by the way it grew. In European towns, too, you can see a church tower or dome from far away. When showing a friend around your hometown, you can point to a square or a building and say, “Look. That is the <strong>center</strong> of the city.”<br><br>Tokyo has no center. Or rather, it has several at once. You could, of course, say that the city had already taken shape before modernization, that it never suffered a destructive blow—a reboot—and that this is how it arrived at today’s chaotic <strong>urban planning</strong>.<br><br>But what makes you so certain that the result was planned by <strong>human beings</strong> at all? Put it another way: what if Tokyo, as a living organism, chose to <strong>evolve</strong> into this centerless form?<br><br>What does this mode of aggregation mean? If you could ask the blond prodigy who fights the Angels, she would probably click her tongue, frown, and tell you, “Tch. This is going to be a pain. <strong>This thing</strong> actually has multiple cores.”<br><br>Which is to say: unless you destroy every core in a single, simultaneous strike, the thing can repair itself.<br><br>The key is that Tokyo is still a long way from its complete form. Every year, the city indiscriminately draws in the lives and longings of tens of thousands of young people, like the intake of a hurricane. Does this transfusion-like way of taking in nutrients not follow the same evolutionary strategy as those tropical carnivorous plants, all gaudy display as they hunt insects?—And Tokyo, to our eyes, really is alluring.<br><br>Have you ever considered that a supercity like Tokyo, a city that exceeds the human definition of a city, may long since have escaped human control—that its development may no longer belong to us? More frightening still: perhaps we are not merely its food. More precisely, we are <strong>components of the city’s cellular structure</strong>. Which is to say, its most <strong>negligible</strong> parts, replaceable at any moment.<br><br>From the Roppongi observation deck after nightfall, red lights blinked on the roof of every building. They seemed to say: we have evolved beyond the need for sleep.<br><br>I could not help thinking of the people I had seen collapsed asleep all over the last train, their bodies smelling of alcohol. Their swollen flesh, strangled by uniforms, showed nothing but devastation. The women had long since ceased to care about their ruined makeup. The men gave off the rank smell of sweat without concern. They had suffocated beneath their masks all day and were too tired to spare attention for anything, least of all the restraint and etiquette that demanded still more effort to maintain.<br><br>Nuclear contamination is not the only thing that can deform a person. Nor does everyone consent to being deformed. Why, then, has no one spoken out about this collective withering produced by the system?<br><br>Perhaps those who had thought of stopping already sensed, however faintly, that by now no one could truly stop the machine.<br><br>Which also means that Tokyo’s existence is <strong>steering</strong> human development toward some <strong>inevitable outcome</strong>.<br></p>\n<h2>3.0 Solar Halo and Full Moon</h2>\n<p><br>On the evening of July 18, I boarded the Nozomi from Kyoto back to Tokyo. Sunlight stretched through the carriage and slowly withdrew; night rose in its place.<br><br>The train passed from one city to the next. Pointlike lights spilled from the buildings whispering past on both sides, recalling the lively bobbing fishermen’s fires in Lu Xun’s “Village Opera.”<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-4\" aria-label=\"Reference 4\">4</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">4</span><span>Lu Xun, “Village Opera.” The quoted image is translated from the Chinese for this essay.</span></span><br><br>There was, of course, another and far brighter source of light. The ancients had not been entirely mistaken: a full moon stood alone above the darkness, a round disk suspended from the vault of heaven with no visible support.<br><br>The ancients, of course, had no way to hurtle a thousand miles in a day as we do. To their eyes, that full moon, round and jade-smooth, must have overflowed with the symbolism of grace bestowed.<br><br>But aboard an express train, this spectacle of blessing became an unsettling <strong>threat</strong>.<br><br>I mean that in all the empty darkness, this single object was <strong>staring</strong> at you. When you tried to stare back, to plumb some meaning from its <strong>gaze</strong>, you found that the light—so bright it nearly dazzled—contained no metaphor, no meaning, only a vast void. Yet this force did not swell outward. It showed no urgency to illuminate the darkness around it. It curled obediently inside that little circular formation. The disk, in other words, undoubtedly contained a <strong>potential energy</strong> of immense destructive power. And judging by its violation of free diffusion, such restraint surely revealed a kind of <strong>intelligence</strong>.<br><br>You hurtled onward with the train, but it made no difference. That wellspring of light followed without pause—no, it tracked you, took aim at you, and effortlessly remained at <strong>relative rest</strong>. Relative rest meant an immovable <strong>gaze</strong>, a <strong>muzzle</strong> guaranteed to hit, a <strong>fate</strong> from which there was no escape.<br><br>I imagine the proclamation of the Last Judgment could be nothing else.<br><br>Then, on the evening of July 30, I was flying onward to Singapore after a layover in Taipei.<br><br>I had booked a seat at the right-hand window expressly to watch a magnificent sunset from the plane. But the weather over the Pacific was less than ideal. Thin clouds veiled the sun, and the whole sky turned gray.<br><br>Disappointed, I opened one of the films I had loaded onto my computer, then fell asleep partway through. When I woke from my nap, a dim ring of light had somehow formed around the sun.<br><br>I knew, of course, that it was only a weather phenomenon called a solar halo. In the past, whenever I had looked up at one, the ring had appeared parallel to the ground. Here it stood upright. Visually, its lower arc seemed to reach all the way down to the sea. At symmetrical points to its left and right shone two brighter patches: sun dogs.<br><br>I pressed myself against the window and stared greedily at this uncommon sight, trying to preserve it in my mind through the precision of my gaze.<br><br>Perhaps I had looked into the sun for too long, because dizziness overtook me. Which is to say, I observed the “symbolism” behind the structure.<br><br>Yes: not long before, I had endured a terrifying exchange of gazes with the luminous moon. If the full moon represented an <strong>omen that you had been locked onto</strong>, then the solar halo was a god’s <strong>weapon</strong>, the instrument of heavenly punishment.<br><br>I imagine that when Hideaki Anno designed Ramiel’s mode of attack, he drew upon the image of the solar halo. And I, coming after him, connected the two by reverse-engineering the image:<sup class=\"fnref ref-cite\"><a href=\"#ref-5\" aria-label=\"Reference 5\">5</a></sup><span class=\"mn reference-note\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"num\">5</span><span>Neon Genesis Evangelion, created and directed by Hideaki Anno; Ramiel is the Fifth Angel in the original television continuity.</span></span><br><br>The outer ring supplies and regulates the power of the whole structure. Through reflection, superposition, or intersection according to some law, the energy on the ring is <strong>focused</strong> at its center, at that precise source of heat. It, too, is a <strong>muzzle</strong>. Yet the halo makes it seem more accurate than the full moon, more capable of striking a vital point.<br><br>The world outside the window was sharply defined: a vast space built from a thin atmosphere and clouds just as thin. The halo, enormous and lucid, was a totem proclaiming some primordial, rule-breaking force, powerful enough to reconstruct the world—to cleave heaven from earth and create it anew.<br><br>It was, therefore, a stigma.<br></p>\n<h2>4.0 Till We Never Meet Again</h2>\n<p><br>By now, neither clouds drifting past the window nor high-voltage pylons hurtling across the fields struck me as new. Only the photographer’s native instinct kept me staring, blank and transfixed, at scenery gone in a flash.<br><br>Those scenes merely reenacted journeys I had already lived through again and again. Strip away the different points of origin and destination, and this act of “conversion”—of transition—became a ritual farewell that had to be deliberately performed.<br><br>When did I become accustomed to departure?<br><br>Each time I entered a train carriage or an airplane cabin alone, put away my luggage with bureaucratic efficiency, and took my assigned seat, I could still feel excitement spilling from the passengers around me. Perhaps it was only the conditioned remnant of the excitement I myself had felt years before every time I left port.<br><br>Yet the unfamiliar seat felt uncannily familiar to me—or perhaps the word is “overlap.” The afterwaves of countless similar scenes still rippled through my memory: all the different star-filled skies I had seen from airplanes, all the coastlines and fields sliding past train windows.<br><br>That familiarity made transition itself feel like the normal state. And in the mere act of setting out, I found peace.<br><br>My sense of belonging, in other words, lay neither in anticipation nor in recollection, but in the ambiguous zone between farewell and embrace. It precisely mirrored my emotional contradiction: the longing to form bonds with others, and the revulsion I felt toward those bonds.<br><br>When did I become fascinated by departure?<br><br>I clung to the fantasy that I deserved a better life—or, just as fancifully, that I would turn the hell awaiting me into heaven. In the end, I could turn my back on the days gone by with startling decisiveness.<br><br>There are experiences I can recognize with perfect clarity as <em>ichigo ichie</em>—a meeting that comes only once, unique and irreproducible at every level—and still leave without the slightest hesitation or regret.<br><br>In part, this is because my relentless fixation on the future keeps the past from holding me back. But it is also because I have grown disillusioned with, and tired of, uniqueness itself.<br><br>If the world is governed by causality and events of pure chance, then whatever singular event we miss must someday reappear in another form. There are only two possible measures of uniqueness. One says there is nothing new under the sun. The other says that every moment we experience is unique. Either way, the measure denies that any one moment, simply because it is unique, has some special value that makes it worth remembering.<br><br>Before I understood this, I believed that a shadow zone surrounded me, something I called the “near-field region.” Within it I could obtain certain <strong>static relationships</strong>: an unchanged streetscape, the same forms of greeting, apparently unbroken friendships, permanent bonds of kinship. The idea returned each time I came home for vacation and went to the same noodle shop downstairs, where I ordered the same three-<em>liang</em> portion of Chengdu dry noodles with spicy minced-meat sauce and greens. The same bustling customers, the same calls and cries, the same sensory jolt of flavor created the illusion that in certain places, at least, time had stopped.<br><br>Then, in the misery of that March, I suddenly learned that my grandmother had died.<br><br>I wanted to cry. But the news was no bolt from a clear sky. It was a reasonable outcome, even a natural one, that I had simply refused with all my strength to acknowledge.<br><br>When I returned to Chengdu for the Spring Festival in February, my grandmother’s condition had been stable, but she already lay aged and bedridden. It took me a long time to accept what I could see: how could the grandmother who had still been healthy the summer before have weakened so suddenly?<br><br>Yes, I had clearly glimpsed the fault in time. Yet I continued to believe that the fault would repair itself when I next came home, that every dissonance would resolve once more into harmony.<br><br>Nothing unfolded as I had expected. There was no time that could be made to stop or flow backward. The shells of things resemble weathered buildings: collapse proceeds continuously within them, though the structure falls in a single instant. This is simply the <strong>developmental trajectory</strong> of things.<br><br>Only after other events followed—events lonelier and sadder, which I have no wish to recall—did I finally abandon the idea of a “near-field region,” already riddled with holes.<br><br>The realization left me suddenly and <strong>completely</strong> exposed to the chaotic rotation of everything outside me. For the first time, I <strong>truly felt</strong> the horror of my solitary existence, and the hopeless desolation around it. And in that same moment, I discovered the lightness, and a certain twisted exhilaration, of possessing myself entirely.<br><br>There was no shadow zone anymore. Outside the self, the whole world was a lucid wilderness, absolute and unrelated to me. Once I understood this, I felt relieved. At last, I had no need to be responsible for anyone. I could go on being selfish in full view.<br><br>I was free. The world still imposed many constraints upon me, but the thought that devised my response to those constraints belonged to “me”: an absolute individual, genuinely independent.<br><br>I had once agonized in earnest over where to settle. Bound to my parents as their child, I thought I had to care for them. Yet I longed still more fiercely for another continent, and the conflict left me agitated and lost. Now the problem had solved itself. I would no longer be bound by so-called filial duty. Even if I chose to care for them, I would do so to repay the care with which I was raised, or out of freely given kindness—not in obedience to an obligation I was required to fulfill.<br><br>Yes. I had finally grown accustomed to continual loss. I had finally understood that beyond the self, there was nothing I could truly possess.<br><br>And the wilderness was lucid. Therefore I, too, had to become more cruel.</p>\n<section class=\"appendix\" aria-label=\"References\"><h2 class=\"appendix-title\">References</h2><ol class=\"appendix-list\"><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-1\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">1</span><span>Yukio Mishima, <em>The Temple of the Golden Pavilion</em>, translated from the Japanese by Ivan Morris.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-2\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">2</span><span>Wang Anshi, “Guizhixiang: Reflections on the Past at Jinling.” Translation from the Chinese for this essay.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-3\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">3</span><span>Haruki Murakami, <em>South of the Border, West of the Sun</em>, source of the allusion to <em>hysteria siberiana</em>.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-4\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">4</span><span>Lu Xun, “Village Opera.” The quoted image is translated from the Chinese for this essay.</span></li><li class=\"appendix-item\" id=\"ref-5\"><span class=\"appendix-number\">5</span><span><em>Neon Genesis Evangelion</em>, created and directed by Hideaki Anno; Ramiel is the Fifth Angel in the original television continuity.</span></li></ol><p class=\"appendix-postscript\"><em>Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6.</em></p></section>","htmlZh":"<p>前言<br><br>区区一月的短暂停留让我的日本生活直充实到几乎没有筛出任何可供思考的空隙，于是，我便只有借在这旅行结束之后的间隙、或称两种生活接轨的“过渡”，将当时速记下来的思考好歹整理为一种连贯的意识。而这一个月的体验，不仅完成了我自儿时起的憧憬，也让“日本”这个词对于我有了全然崭新的意义：它首先代表了一个个我丈量而过的城市、其次代表了我心中幻境的遗迹、最后代表着我在其中的行踪，也即我的存在。<br><br>我给这篇日志命名的含义，正是“片道”在日语中的本意。这就是说，这次旅行引起了某种不可复原的改变——不仅是我再也无法回去的、业已消逝的时光；同时也是指我的存在本身的行迹，由这旅途牵引了。<br><br>和二次元的构想完全不同，这一次我面对的，是一个现实的、被现实所填充的、被现实所包裹的、又由现实塑造的、现实的日本。而我身处其中，并不以概念的形式、或徒具灵体的旁观者，而是在三维的空间中实实在在地占据了等身的体积——这就是说，我参与了这个世界的创造。<br><br><br><br>1.0 金阁寺、银阁寺<br><br>我从午睡中惊醒，才发现距离金阁寺不过两站的距离。<br>此时我能做到的最好的净身仪式，就是掏出携带电话，访问两年前留下的摘抄。<br>《金阁寺》。那是我与三岛的第一次接触。也就是自那时起，我被那些文字中蕴藏的暗红色物质同化了。和其他作家不同，三岛的作品并没有着力于透过时代的背景反映时代的精神——他是在那些故事发生的时代中，展现他自己精神的投影。<br>——而金阁寺，以其金箔上绝对的闪光，君临这个世界。它那袭华美的袍泽，与周围的自然风物格格不入。<br>“我不会融入你们。”<br>它本身就是一个傲慢的宣言。由此美便展现了它的根源——严密的一次性、以及由此不可复制性派生的纯粹和拒绝。<br>因此，美是绝望的。美是向往破坏的。美是废墟的前兆。<br><br>“柏木所说的事或许是真的，他说，改变世界的，不是行动而是认识。”<br>本应是这样。但在金阁，我并没有领悟到除了嘈杂人流带来的燥热之外的其他东西。<br>也就是说，这是一次糟糕的圣地巡礼。<br>但那些东西，我却意外地在银阁寺领悟到了。<br><br>那是在清晨热烈的阳光中，我饿着肚子走过郁郁葱葱的哲学之路，又踏上依山而建的参道，就突然间就迈入了一个清幽的庭园。<br>时间尚早，因而也只有三两稀疏的游客。寺里的僧人正拿着犁杖在碎石滩垄出一犁犁工整的沟洫。<br>我走过蜿蜒穿越庭园的小径，站在小桥上，无意义地眺望水中缓慢游动的庞大鲤鱼。<br>那些小枫树茂密而精致的树叶细密地倒影在水中；简直就是血管，或者肺叶——而枝杈以外，就是大片大片悠然而饱满的云朵，和填满整个背景的蓝天。<br>但并不仅是这样。你透过那些仿佛以对称构建的、有如是用来备份这整个世界的逼真倒影——你仍然可以清楚地看见池底的所有景物。比如说荇草、或几乎静止的鲤鱼、或者空旷而平整的池底。<br>然后你试着将头伸出桥面，当然你就看到自己的影像也沉入了水中——而这个满含喻意的二义性也就完成了。<br><br>那是一个依附于此岸而存在的彼岸的世界。而你本身的存在也是一个矛盾：你既是此岸的观察者，又在彼岸参与整个世界的形成；也就是说，你正注视着参与世界的自己——又或者说，你被你自身注视着。<br>而那又是一个怎样的世界呢？池边的长满青苔的岩石仿佛构成结界的边疆，仿佛借此可以维持整潭池水那包容万物的狄拉克之海般的巨大张力……可是这缥缈的影像终究只是浮在水面的幻影，而透过这种透明介质泛出沉静而黝黑光泽的，只有亘古不变的池底。所以，在这片小小塘池中展开的重影，隶属于两个不同的世界。而我们在其中存在的那个，无疑是更为脆弱的——只需一个涟漪，一次鱼的摆尾，所有事物都会无一例外地受到波及。这就是说，它并不是毫无依凭地存在与此的——它是在介质中传播的。<br>而你有没有想过，这种介质，就是宛如法则般存在的巨大虚空。因此，世界混沌缘起的根本在于——世界本身就是一条通道。<br>（而我今早又读起王安石《桂枝香•金陵怀古》中的句子，觉得这上下两阕之间的对比正好体现了倒影和池底那种瞬时与永恒、变与不变之间的重叠关系：<br>登临送目，正故国晚秋，天气初肃。千里澄江似练，翠峰如簇。征帆去棹残阳里，背西风，酒旗斜矗。彩舟云淡，星河鹭起，画图难足。<br>念往昔、繁华竞逐，叹门外楼头，悲恨相续。千古凭高对此，漫嗟荣辱。六朝旧事随流水，但寒烟衰草凝绿。至今商女，时时犹唱，《后庭》遗曲。）<br><br>可是此岸的瞬时又何尝没有带来永久不变的错觉呢？<br>枫树上纯净的天空，风拂过绿叶发出的沙沙声响、和着那阵阵蝉鸣……在这种时刻，我就想找一片浅浅的草丛轻轻躺下。这里有着消弭欲念的强大磁场；当你望向四周，包裹你的全部，都在这里静静地伫立了千百年，又注定还要在这里千百年地伫立下去……太阳的东升西落、四季的更替变换，在这里不过是一瞬间的东西。它就像是被砍伐的树枝上暴露的年轮的切片：这生机勃勃的断层还充满了新鲜而娇嫩的色泽——但生长已经停止了，它永远维持着被砍伐瞬间的容貌。<br>也就是说，这个瞬间是直通永恒的。<br><br>可是此岸瞬时的永恒性何尝不会让人丧失心智呢？<br>枫树上纯净的天空，风拂过绿叶发出的沙沙声响、和着那阵阵蝉鸣……在这种时刻，我突然想一把火烧毁这整片的庭园。这里有让人丢失魂魄的强大咒力；当你望向四周，包裹你的全部，都在这里静静地伫立了千百年，又注定还要在这里千百年地伫立下去……太阳的东升西落、四季的更替变换，在这里不过是一次一次毫无改变的轮回。它就像死海文书一样在每个瞬间准确重现了所有的预知——于是我变得无法忍受、我要逃离这种轮回。而这种脱离的方法，就是将这种永生的幻想破坏殆尽。<br>这就是我们所说的西伯利亚癔病啊，我的朋友。人总是憧憬永恒而惧怕永恒的。<br><br>我以往论述过，美的一次性是纯粹和拒绝的起源。而永恒和一次性同源。因而美之引申，就是明晰和残暴。<br>明晰的意思，就代表着尖锐、切割、不妥协、以及一切拒绝同质化的倾向。而残暴，则是我等脆弱之生命对永恒的追求和复仇。这正是《金阁寺》中说 “一方面，人类容易毁灭的形象反而浮现出永生的幻想，而金阁坚固的美反而暴露出毁灭的可能性。像人类那样有能力致死的东西是不会根绝的，而像金阁那样不灭的东西却是可以消灭的。”<br>“我们之所以突然变得残暴，那是在这样一瞬间。即一个明媚春天的下午，在精心修剪过的草坪上，茫然地眺望着从树叶缝隙筛落下来的阳光戏耍的一瞬间。”<br>【我的少年时期混浊成黎明的灰暗。黑暗的影子的世界是可怕的，但白昼似得轮廓分明的生，也不属于我。】<br><br><br><br>2.0 六本木<br><br>我还是第一次见到这样的城市。<br>当我从六本木高楼楼顶的直升机平台放眼望去，鳞次栉比的房屋挤满了视野里到地平线的所有地面空间。而间或高楼聚合成丛，或电波塔兀自耸立，仿佛某种信号传递的神经突触，显出某种区域性的聚集现象。当天边的火红的晚霞渐渐失色，整个城市并没有像预想中一样沉沉睡去，而是借由灯光的照明和装饰，显出了对黑暗良好的适应性；而在这种强烈的对比中，甚至较白昼更为昭然地体现出某种涌动的活力。<br><br>我还是第一次发现城市居然还有这种型态。<br>当我从沿山手线乘坐电车环行，从涩谷到新宿，到池袋到上野到新桥到品川……每一个停靠的站点，无非人潮涌动、高楼林立，显出了及其相似的现代化。<br>我出生和长大的城市，拥有非常明显的年轮——从一环路到二环到三环——这就是这个城市如何成长的留痕。欧洲那些小镇，也可以从远处就早早眺望到教堂高耸的塔楼或穹顶。就是说，当你带着友人环游故乡，你可以指着那片广场或者那栋楼房说，“看、那就是这个城市的<strong>中心</strong>。”<br>但东京没有中心。或者说，它同时拥有复数个中心。当然，你可以说这个城市在现代化之前已经初具规模，并且没有经历任何毁灭性打击（也就是重启），因此造成了今天这种混乱的<strong>城市规划</strong>。<br>可是，你究竟凭什么自信认为这一切，都是<strong>人为的</strong>规划造成的后果呢？换种方式说，有没有这种可能，是东京这个城市作为某种生命体，它自己选择<strong>进化</strong>成了如今这种没有中枢的形态。<br>这种聚合方式究竟意味着什么呢？如果你能去问问那个和使徒作战的金发天才少女，她大概会“切”地皱皱眉头然后告诉你说，“这可真是棘手啊，<strong>这家伙</strong>居然拥有多个核心。”<br>也就是说，不同时一击摧毁所有核心的话，这家伙就仍然可以自我修复。<br><br>关键是，东京离它的完全体还差得远呢。这个城市每年如同飓风风口一样毫不挑剔地吸收数以万计的年轻人的存在和憧憬——你有没有觉得，这种如同输血般的吸收营养的方式，和热带那些把自己打扮得花枝招展而捕猎昆虫的食肉植物，有着同样的进化策略。——而东京，在我们眼里，也的确是充满魅力的。<br>你有没有想过，像东京这种超越人类对城市定义的超级城市，其本身的发展方向，早已经脱离人类的掌控，并不为我们所有了；或者更为可怕的是，如果我们不仅仅是它的养料的话——更为贴切地说，我们是这个城市<strong>细胞结构的组成</strong>。也就是说，是随时可以替代的、最<strong>无关紧要</strong>的部分。<br><br>从六本木的观景平台望去，入夜后东京，所有大厦楼顶都有红灯闪闪烁烁。那意思大概就是说，我们已经进化到不需要睡眠了。<br>我就没办法不去想那些在终电的车厢中看到的、那些身上带有酒气的四处瘫倒而眠的人，我在他们被制服勒住的臃肿躯体上看到的只有摧残。女人对自己残缺的妆容早已漠然处之，男人则毫不在意地散发着充满体味的汗臭。他们在面具之下窒息了一天，已经累得无暇他顾了，更何况是矜持和礼仪这种需要费劲维持的东西呢。<br>并不是只有核污染才会让人变得扭曲的。也并不是所有人都意愿被扭曲的。但这种制度所引发的群体化的凋零，为什么没有人为之发声呢？<br>大概那些想过停止的人已经隐约意识到，事到如今，已经没有人可以真正停下这个机器了。<br>这同时也就是说，东京的存在，正在<strong>引导</strong>人类的发展朝着某种<strong>必然性结局</strong>推进。<br><br><br><br>3.0 日晕和满月<br><br>7月18日的傍晚，我乘上希望号从京都回往东京。阳光在车厢中逐渐拉长和隐没，于是黑夜也上升了。<br>列车在一个个城市的中间穿过，而两旁簌簌而过的楼房中漏出的点状灯光，让我想起了鲁迅社戏中“踊跃的渔火”。<br>当然还有一个远为明亮的光源。古人的说法显然是不无道理的，那轮饱满的圆月兀自凸显在黑暗之上，简直就是一个不依靠任何支撑就悬挂在天穹的圆盘。<br>当然，古人并没有如今这种手段可以一日千里地飞驰。因此，在他们眼里，这轮圆润的玉石般的满月，显然充满了施降恩惠的福祉的象征。<br>但如果乘上急行电车的话，这个充满祝福意义的展现，就变成了某种令人坐立不安的<strong>威胁</strong>。<br><br>我是说，在整个空无一物的黑暗之中，唯有这个物体在强烈地<strong>注视</strong>着你。而当你尝试与之对视，尝试从这种<strong>目光</strong>中探寻出什么意义的时候，你会发现那明亮到几乎耀眼的光芒毫无暗喻和意义、只是充斥着巨大的虚空——而这种力量却并没有向外膨胀，并没有急于去照亮周围的黯淡，而是规矩地蜷缩在那个小小圆阵之中。也就是说，这个圆盘无疑拥有毁灭性极高的某种<strong>势能</strong>——而且以其对自由扩散原理的违背来看，这种克制确实体现出了某种<strong>智慧</strong>。<br>你跟着列车做着快速移动，但这并没有什么用处，那个光之泉眼一直追随着你——不，是跟踪着你、瞄准着你，而且不费吹灰之力地和你保持<strong>相对静止</strong>。而相对静止的意思就是，不能移走的<strong>凝视</strong>，必然命中的<strong>枪口</strong>，以及无法逃避的<strong>命运</strong>。<br>我猜想，末日审判的宣言可能与此别无二致。<br><br>又，7月30日的傍晚我正乘飞机从台北转机飞往新加坡。<br>特地预订了靠右舷窗的座位，我原以为可以在飞机上欣赏壮美的日落。但那天太平洋上的天气不见得怎样的好，太阳那被那些薄薄的云层笼罩，使得整个天空显得灰蒙蒙的。<br>我惘然若失地只好打开电脑里预备好的电影，又在途中陷入睡眠。而当我再次从小憩中清醒过来，太阳的周围不知何时长出了一转朦胧的光圈。<br>当然，我知道这只是叫做日晕的天气现象。可是以往仰头看到的时候，这个圆环是平行于地面的——但这一次的景象是垂直的，就视觉效果来说，圆环的下部仿佛直达海面。而在圆环的对称左右两侧，也各有一个更为明亮的光斑，也就是所谓的“幻日”。<br>我将自己贴上舷窗，贪婪地注视这种并不多见的景象，尝试通过细致的凝视将其保存于脑海。<br>也许是盯着太阳看的太久的缘故，我产生了眩晕的感觉，就是说，我观测到了这种结构背后的“象征性”。<br>是的，在此之前，我曾和皎洁的月亮有过一次骇人的对视；而如果说满月代表了被<strong>锁定的预兆</strong>，那么日晕就是施加天罚的“神明的<strong>武器</strong>”。<br>我想庵野秀明在设计雷天使的攻击模式的时候应该就是借助了日晕这个形象。而我作为后辈，则通过逆推将两者联系到了一起：<br>外侧圆环是整个结构的动力来源和调整工具，而通过某种法则的反射、叠加或者交叉，将其上的能源<strong>聚焦</strong>到了圆环中心——也就是那个精准的热源。同样，那也是一个<strong>枪口</strong>。但比起满月，因为那圈光环的缘故，显得更加具有可以直击要害的精确性。<br>舷窗外的世界是一个鲜明的世界。它是由稀薄的大气和同样稀薄的云层构筑的广袤空间，而日晕这个巨大而明晰的图腾无疑宣告了某种违反规则的，足以重构世界、开天辟地的洪荒力量。<br>所以说，那是一道圣痕。<br><br><br><br>4.0 后会无期<br><br>事到如今，无论是窗外飘过云朵或是田野上的高压电线杆呼啸而过，我都再不会感到新鲜。只是出于摄影爱好者的天生的追逐，而痴痴呆望着那些一闪而过的景色。<br>那些场景，不过是重现了某些我不断历经过的旅途。如果无视出发点和目的地的不同，这种“转换”或称为过渡的行为，便是某种不得不刻意为之的仪式般的告别。<br><br>我是从何时开始习惯于出发的呢？<br>每次独自踏进车厢或者机舱，我事务性地搁好行李，坐上指定的席位，我仍能从周围乘客的身上感受到一种溢于言表的激动；当然，这也许是多年前，当我还在为每一次离港兴奋不已的条件反射的残留。<br>这陌生的座位于我却有一种异样的熟稔，又或者称之为“重叠”的感觉。那即是说，我仍能感受到起无数次相似场景在我记忆中漾开的余波，那正是我能回想起的在飞机上看见过的不同情状的星空，或者列车上驶过的海滨和田园一样。<br>而这种熟稔让我感到这种过渡状态反而才是常态——于是我在这踏上旅途本身这一行为中，找到了心安的感觉。<br>也就是说，我所感到的归属感并不在于憧憬或者追忆，而是介于告别和拥抱之间的暧昧地带——这也刚好代表了我对与人产生羁绊的期待和厌恶的感情矛盾。<br><br>我是从何时开始迷恋于出发的呢？<br>一厢情愿地认为自己配得上更好的生活，或者又一厢情愿地以为自己要将即将面对的地狱改造为天堂……到头来，我对逝去时日的淡漠竟业已达到了这样干脆的地步。<br>有些事情，哪怕我清楚地认识到那是“一期一会”这种在任何层面都不可复制的独特体验，我在告别的时候都不会有一丝犹疑或者不舍。<br>这一方面是因为我对未来某种锲而不舍的执着，因而不会拘泥于过往的拖累；而另一方面则是因为，我对独一无二这种性质产生了某种失望和疲劳。<br>如果世界是被因果律和纯粹的随机性事件支配的话，那么无论我们错过任何形式的一次性的事件，它都肯定将在未来以另一种面貌出现。因此，衡量独特性的标尺无非只有两把：其一是说，太阳底下并无新事；其二是说，经历的每一个瞬间都是独特的。而无论哪一种说法，都否定了某一瞬间作为独特瞬间的存在而值得铭记的价值。<br>在我意识到这一点之前，我原以为，我身边有一种叫做“近场区域”的影子地带。在这中间我可以获得某种<strong>静止的关系</strong>——不变的街景、相同的问候方式、看似没有裂痕的友谊、永久的亲情纽带。而这种观念的形成，正是每次放假回家之后，我在楼底的同一家面馆点上同样的三两素椒杂酱面配青菜——而同样熙攘的顾客和吆喝和同样的味觉刺激，让我产生了至少在某些地带，时光是停止的错觉。<br><br>然而就在今年苦闷的三月，突然间，我就得知了奶奶过世的消息。<br>我想哭，可是这并不是晴天霹雳的噩耗，而是合理的、甚至顺理成章的我却死死不肯承认的结局。<br>在我二月的春节回成都的时候，尽管病情稳定，奶奶就已经衰老地躺在病床上了。那时我也花了很长时间的确认才能相信，明明在上个暑假的时候依然康健的奶奶，为什么会突然间就虚弱下去了呢？<br>是的，我明明已经好好地瞥见了那个时光的断层，然而我仍然以为在我下次归家之时，这个断层将会复原，而一切龃龉都会和好如初。<br>然而并没有我所期待的展开。因为本就没有可以停驻和倒溯的时光。事物的外壳亦如历经风霜的建筑——它内部的崩坏是时时刻刻的，但坍塌却只是一瞬的事——这本是事物的<strong>发展轨迹</strong>。<br>而之后或多或少又经历了其他我所不愿追怀的更为寂寞和悲哀的事情，我才终于放弃了那早已漏洞百出的“近场区域”的想法。<br><br>而这种将我突然<strong>完全</strong>暴露在外物混沌的旋转之中的顿悟，第一次让我<strong>真正感到</strong>令人毛骨悚然的自己孑然的存在，以及周遭令人绝望的荒凉。而也就是这个时刻，我才真正发现完全掌控自己之后的轻松和某种扭曲的快意。<br>再没有影子地带了。除了我自身以外，整个世界就是彻头彻尾的、与我无关的、明晰的荒野。明白这个道理，我反而如释重负——我终于不必为任何人负责、可以明目张胆地自私下去了。<br>我自由了。尽管外界对我施加了诸多限制，但为此产生对策的，则是“我”作为绝对个体存在的真正独立的思想。<br>在以前，我曾经认真纠结过定居的问题。我想作为子女的羁绊，我得照料父母——然而我对另一片大陆有着更多的憧憬，这就让我感到烦躁和无所适从。现在呢，这个问题迎刃而解了。我将不再被所谓孝道所拘束，也就是说，即使我选择了照料他们，我的出发点是对养育的回报或者善意，而不是依照任何必须完成的义务。<br>是的，我终于习惯了不断失去。因为我终于意识到除了自我之外，没有什么东西我能真正拥有。<br><br>而荒野明晰，我也因此必须变得更加残忍。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2016-08-10_片道东瀛.md"},{"id":"2015-06-13_why-oran","date":"2015-06-13","title":"Why Oran?","titleZh":"为什么是阿赫兰","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"When I traveled alone through the South of France, the sun seemed reluctant to leave the sky. It was broad daylight before six in the morning, and at nine in the evening…","excerptZh":"我在南法独自旅行的时候，东升西落的太阳总是长久地滞留在天空之上。六点不到已经大亮，九点的天色依然晴朗；而尤其是午后的时候，阳光总是毫无遮掩地打下来，整个…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2015-06-13_为什么是阿赫兰.jpg","tags":["travel log","europe"],"tagsZh":["游记","欧洲"],"readingMin":4,"htmlEn":"<p>When I traveled alone through the South of France, the sun seemed reluctant to leave the sky. It was broad daylight before six in the morning, and at nine in the evening the sky was still clear. By afternoon, the sun bore down without the slightest cover, as if it had the whole city by the throat. The discomfort reached through the entire body.<br><br>Along the seafront promenade of each city, rows of tall palms were interspersed with movable chairs of dark-blue iron. At first glance, they looked like a municipal amenity thoughtfully provided for tourists who wished to ogle the women in bikinis on the beach. Unexpectedly, plenty of locals enjoyed them too. Young people listened to music; older people read the newspaper, pausing over Sudoku grids and word puzzles.<br><br>The decision to make the chairs movable had clearly been considered with care. As the sun crossed the sky, people could shift their chairs and follow the shade.<br><br>The South of France is beautiful in sunlight. But to a tourist determined to walk along the sea, direct sun is more than a physical force that tans the skin. It assaults the mind, too, and can sour the easiest of moods. I suspect this is precisely why Singapore has so few uncovered pedestrian walkways. I also suspect that Singapore owes its reputation as the “Garden City” to the absence of this experience: tourists can spend almost their entire visit inside air-conditioned coaches.<br><br>On the way to Cannes, sitting in a bus whose air-conditioning was nowhere near strong enough, I kept half-consciously mistaking the cities we passed for Oran, Camus&#x27;s plague-stricken city. I no longer know which came first in that reflex. Oran may lie on the southern shore of the Mediterranean and Nice and Cannes on the northern one, but all three belong to the same rim.<br><br>In Camus&#x27;s eyes, Oran was “a town without intimations.” Its climate gave it a “feverish yet casual air.”<br><br>These days, I place great importance on the fit between a person&#x27;s nature and the place in which they live. In the tropics, for instance, one simply has to make a habit of siestas: at the hottest point of the day, trying to concentrate on anything is nearly futile. Only after walking beneath palms in a blazing sun do you understand that utter unconcern is the only way to remain at ease. If you still have something to insist on, something you feel compelled to say, those clammy waves of heat will leave you extraordinarily irritable.<br><br>That irritation is almost a biological instinct. Yet it is not an impulse that sends you charging toward shade in one determined burst. Rather, it is an irrational, irresponsible willingness to go on exposing yourself to discomfort. You could almost say that a person in such a state is on the edge of degradation. How can I put it? In that moment, you think: it would make no difference if the rest of my life went on exactly like this. I doubt people in the tropics are much afraid of Christianity&#x27;s eternal hellfire. To be scorched is not to have your attention fixed on the flesh-rending agony of your own body. It dissipates the mind instead, leaving it blank, idle, and indifferent to whatever may happen.<br><br>Freezing is the inverse. Cold forces the mind to concentrate. Physically, the pores constrict and the whole body works to conserve heat; mentally, the corresponding state is one of inward compression.<br><br>But back to Oran: what did the plague become for those who lived through it? Whether Camus intended it or not, his choice of setting deepens our understanding of the everyday. The plague is terrifying. Yet those who live beneath its shadow long enough lose their first fear and alarm. They learn to inhabit the new normal as though nothing were out of the ordinary. In Camus, the evidence of reality—or our apprehension of it—renders calamity utterly unspectacular: “nothing is less sensational than pestilence,” and “by reason of their very duration great misfortunes are monotonous.” Under the hot, dry conditions of a Mediterranean summer, I believe this blunting of perception would be markedly amplified. That dissipative, <em>can&#x27;t-be-bothered</em> state of mind would greatly hasten the residents&#x27; adaptation.<br><br>In the end, there may be no advice worth offering. I have only recorded an observable phenomenon and an inward experience of my own. Yet this traffic between body and mind does illuminate the old saying, “A still mind brings its own coolness.” Seen from here, do idealism and materialism still look like opposite extremes?<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>我在南法独自旅行的时候，东升西落的太阳总是长久地滞留在天空之上。六点不到已经大亮，九点的天色依然晴朗；而尤其是午后的时候，阳光总是毫无遮掩地打下来，整个城市就像被扼住了咽口一般让人浑身难受。<br><br>每个城市的海滨大道上除了一株株高大的棕榈，还有许多可以活动的深蓝色铁制椅子。乍一看似乎是为好色的游客可以专心观赏海滩的比基尼美女而被细心安置的政府福利工程，可是令人意外的是，似乎很多当地人也喜欢坐在上面休憩；年轻人听音乐，老年人则会看报纸，顺带着做做报纸上的九宫格和words puzzle.<br><br>显然设计成可以移动的椅子是经过认真考量的。随着一天之中太阳位置的变化，人们就可以移动椅子让自己始终处于树荫之下。<br><br>阳光下的南法是美丽的，然而对于一定要漫步滨海大道的游客来说，太阳的直射不仅是一种可以晒黑肤色的物理力量，也是一种足以毁掉轻松心情的精神攻击。我相信正式这种感觉，才让新加坡异常地缺乏露天的人行道；也正是缺少了这种体验（游客几乎可以在开着冷气的旅行车里度过），新加坡才有了花园城市的美誉。<br><br>当我坐在空调开得非常不足的公交车中向着戛纳行进的时候，我总是有意无意地把途经的城市认做阿赫兰、那座加缪笔下疫病爆发的城市。我当然不知道这种思考反射的先后顺序，总之阿赫兰与尼斯、戛纳，尽管位于南北两岸，却都是地中海沿边的城市。<br><br>在加缪的眼中，阿赫兰是一座“毫无臆想”的城市；而在气候的作用下，充满了一种“既狂热，又心不在焉”的神气。<br><br>我现在非常强调一个人的天性与其身处环境之间的相性，比如热带的人民是一定要养成午睡的习惯的，因为在一天里最炎热的时刻，人们如果期望能集中精力做些什么事情，那几乎是徒劳的。当你切切实实走在棕榈和烈阳之下，你才知道只有那种蛮不在乎的心境才能让人过得怡然自得。否则，如果人还需要坚持什么东西，热衷于表达什么言语的话，你就会被这种汗涔涔的热浪搅得异常地烦躁。<br><br>那种烦躁几乎是一种生物性的本能，那并不是一种急于寻找荫蔽的一鼓作气往前走下去的推动力，而是一种毫无责任心地将自己持续暴露在使人不舒服的环境下的非理性态度。你几乎可以说人在那种状态下是接近堕落的。怎么说呢，在那种时刻人是拥有这样的心态的——余下的人生就这么度过也毫无所谓。我想热带的人民是不会怎么惧怕基督教中地狱的永恒烈火的炙烤的，因为所谓炙烤并不是会让人专注于关注自身肉体撕心裂肺的痛苦，而是驱使人将头脑放空的耗散，对任何情况不以为意的无所事事。<br><br>与之相反的是冰冻。从相反的意义上说，寒冷是迫使人集中精力的。即使从肉体上来讲，寒冷状态下的毛孔收缩，全身以贮存热量的形式活动着——这在相对应的精神上就表现为一种向内压缩的状态。<br><br>回过头来说，阿赫兰的居民在鼠疫中是一种怎样的体验呢？加缪的这种设定，无论说是否有意，恰巧加深了人们对于日常性的理解。鼠疫是可怕的，然而长期生活下疫情笼罩下的人们将最终失去担惊受怕的最初体验，而尽可以若无其事地面对这种新常态。加缪说，这种“现实的明白无误性”使得灾祸比任何东西都不壮观，而且，“巨大的祸患时间之长本身就十分单调。”而我相信，身处地中海夏季高温少雨的气候条件下，这种感知的钝感将会被相当明显地放大；“不想管”的耗散心态将大大加速居民的适应过程。<br><br>最后，作为结论似乎也并没有什么值得提出的建议。我仅仅是记述了一种可供观察到的现象和个人的内心体验。然而，从这种身心相互作用的例子中，我们也能了解“心静自然凉”所蕴含的理趣了；而从这个意义上说，唯心和唯物难道还是思想形态的两种极端么？</p>","source":"./content/posts/2015-06-13_为什么是阿赫兰.md"},{"id":"2015-05-17_the-breath-of-paris","date":"2015-05-17","title":"The Breath of Paris","titleZh":"巴黎的呼吸","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"Rounding the corner from Dupipeng’s art museum, I came suddenly upon the Hôtel de Ville, ancient and immense. Tourists streamed across the broad square in front of it; someone was blowing soap…","excerptZh":"从杜皮蓬的艺术馆转过街角，突然就看见一大幢雄伟而古老的市政厅。游客熙熙攘攘从建筑前面的大广场走过；有人吹起肥皂泡，引起儿童雀跃和追逐。天气逐渐转晴，云朵…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2015-05-17_巴黎的呼吸.jpg","tags":["travel log","europe"],"tagsZh":["游记","欧洲"],"readingMin":6,"htmlEn":"<p>Rounding the corner from Dupipeng’s art museum, I came suddenly upon the Hôtel de Ville, ancient and immense. Tourists streamed across the broad square in front of it; someone was blowing soap bubbles, and children leapt and scrambled after them. The weather was clearing. Clouds drifted by one after another, and the French flag above the roof snapped in the wind. The mid-May sun was bright, almost hot. Beyond it all lay the Seine, the Île de la Cité, and Notre-Dame…<br><br>This was Paris. I could hardly claim to have yearned for it day and night, yet all the novels I had read had instilled in me a particular expectation of the city. Every district and place name that came into view summoned another story, from another age and another class. Paris bore an immense weight of history without seeming especially burdened by it. In my imagination, the city was a young woman living in another dimension: she had watched whole worlds come and go, yet remained too languid to alter so much as her expression.<br><br>I imagined emerging from a winding lane into the sudden openness of the Champs-Élysées, looking up at sunlight striking the plane trees and listening for the pulse of this vast living city; standing at the Arc de Triomphe in the middle of the Place de l’Étoile, savoring the strange interplay between Chinese script and the district’s radiating streets; following the boulevard toward Montmartre, making my pilgrimage to the hill where Van Gogh once stayed; surveying the Eiffel Tower from afar and, like some old gentleman, condemning a metal structure so offensively at odds with the old buildings around it; drawing close to the lights of the Latin Quarter as they came on at dusk, drawing close to Odette’s secret life and the veil that caused Swann such anguish; wandering the streets around the Moulin Rouge late into the night, searching for traces of Modiano’s Louki and her fugitive youth. And then there were the bells of Notre-Dame, the boats on the Seine… Ah, Paris! Let those enchanted figures and stories arrive one after another; let me kneel at the hem of your dress!<br><br>In reality, all I could do was sling a backpack over my shoulders like any other tourist and thread my way through the city among all the other ordinary tourists, until I too vanished into its noisy backdrop. Again and again I tried to draw closer to that feeling—but what did I actually see? Rubbish outside the Moulin Rouge whipped about by the wind. The Seine running filthy. Sightseers massed in line outside Shakespeare and Company… The sky—low clouds hanging over it, cutting off a soul that should have ranged free! The city I had imagined was made of dreams; the city before me was reality—solid, undeniable, and painfully bright.<br><br>Not long ago, I was still puzzled by this difference—by the immense transformation in the way people travel over the past century. I had encountered that older way of life countless times in books: Russian nobles leaving the Siberian wastes for the social season in Western Europe; aristocratic women descending from inland castles to winter on the Mediterranean; American travelers crossing the ocean for the European continent. Their lives were so migratory that it was hard to say where they had truly settled. There they remained, summering in the country or taking the waters to recover their health. Had someone told you they would stay for a year, a decade, or the rest of their lives, it would hardly have seemed surprising.<br><br>Then came passports, and it was as if we had suddenly been pinned to a single identity, assigned one place as home while every other place was denied the right to shelter us for long. (Passports, of course, are not entirely to blame.) We tour five countries in seven days, see a continent in twenty-five days, and then return to precisely the same track we were on before we left. Has our way of life changed at all? No, no. In every city we are rude guests who barge into someone else’s home and loudly order dinner. How can anyone who stops at a café merely to recover from the journey have the gall to claim an encounter with the real Paris? The travelogues on every travel website are little more than mash-ups of itineraries and to-do lists, judged not by the writer’s sensibility or ideas but by the efficiency of the trip. Beautiful! Beautiful! Beautiful! the comments bellow, as though collecting sights and acquiring experience were the same kind of thing. To my mind, the highest praise such a journey can earn is this: you took a photograph fit for a postcard… Very few people return from a short, punishing trip having seen anything beyond the sights.<br><br>If I could, I would travel with no itinerary at all. I would not race from one attraction to the next by the quickest and cheapest route. I would surrender myself to chance encounters until I discovered some inner necessity in the city—the necessity by which Notre-Dame had to stand in Paris. Without it, Notre-Dame might just as well be an attraction in Rome, where we could pose for photographs with equal abandon. (Unfortunately, this is how nutritionally empty most travel has become.)<br><br>It is estrangement twice over, a double blow. The dreamer sees only flashes of truth crystallized above and apart from ordinary life; the hurried tourist misses the precious sediment left behind by a city’s centuries of silent growth.<br><br>Yes! And there is nothing we can do to change it!<br><br>Farewell, women with tiny irises tilted in the brims of your hats! Farewell, dashing musketeers riding in splendor along the palace avenue!<br><br>Still, if we are thick-skinned enough, we can always say:<br><br>“It isn’t the destination that matters. It’s who you travel with.”<br><br>Which suddenly reminds me: when people in Chengdu go on an outing, all they really do is move their mahjong game somewhere else. A field of rapeseed flowers will do; so will a stream or a hot spring. The scenery is beside the point. What matters is your mahjong partners and your luck at the table… Humanity! What ingenuity you bring to the business of socializing.<br><br>Honestly, I may lose the will to keep traveling—especially to those great metropolises that have already stood for a thousand years and will inevitably stand for a thousand more. Caught between the objective force of modernization and the subjective creed of modern travel—as Han Shaogong observed, all the cities in the world are becoming, more and more, one city—I cannot hope to recover the elegance of carriages and horses, saddles and hairpins. That graceful air survives only in Proust’s childhood now—and the similarly hazy feeling I dimly catch through his words may very well not have been what Proust meant at all.<br><br>Come to think of it, perhaps that hazy feeling alone deserves to be called each city’s unique inheritance.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>从杜皮蓬的艺术馆转过街角，突然就看见一大幢雄伟而古老的市政厅。游客熙熙攘攘从建筑前面的大广场走过；有人吹起肥皂泡，引起儿童雀跃和追逐。天气逐渐转晴，云朵渐次飘过，房子顶层直插的法兰西国旗猎猎翻飞。五月中旬的阳光明媚得甚至有点炎热，而再远处是塞纳河、 湖心岛和圣母院……<br><br>这里是巴黎。虽然说不上魂牵梦萦，但我在无数小说之中，的确对这个城市有着特殊的期待。那些在我眼前延展开的地区和地名总是催生着一个一个的故事——不同的时代、不同的阶级。这个城市的历史如此厚重，然而却并没有显得非常压抑；在我想象中，那仿佛是活在另一个维度里面的少女，虽然见证了无数沧海桑田的变化，却依然慵懒得不肯为之改变一下容貌。<br><br>我幻想着自己踏出曲径，在豁然开朗的香榭丽舍大道上仰望天空，看阳光打在梧桐叶上、聆听这城市巨大的生命的悸动；在星形广场正中央、凯旋门，体味汉字和放射性街区交互的况味；沿着大道走向蒙马特，拜谒梵高呆过的艺术家集聚的高地；从远处观望埃菲尔，像老绅士一样批评这个与老派建筑格格不入的金属塔楼；接近黄昏时分点燃的拉丁区灯光，接近奥黛特的秘密生活和令斯万先生痛彻心扉的面纱；在夜深的红磨坊街区浪游，跟踪露姬小时候叛逃的青春；还有圣母院的钟声、塞纳河的游船……啊！巴黎，那些神奇的人物和故事依次登场，请让我拜服在你的裙裾之下！<br><br>可事实上呢，我只能像一个普通的游客一样背着背包，和另外许多普通的游客一起穿梭和消失在这个城市嘈杂的背景之中。我尝试了无数次，想要接近那种心情——可我看到的又是什么啊？红磨坊的门前的垃圾被大风吹起，塞纳河脏污的流水，莎士比亚书店前成群排队的参观者……天空、低垂的乌云截断了本该自由驰骋的灵魂！我想象中的这座城市充满梦幻，但眼前的风景却是确凿得那么刺眼的真实。<br><br>我不久前还对这样的差异疑惑不解，这百年之间旅游方式的巨大改变。我见过无数次，从西伯利亚荒原到西欧展开社交季的俄国贵族，从内陆的城堡到地中海沿岸越冬的贵妇人，或者从美国到欧洲大陆的旅行家——他们候鸟般的生活方式使你根本不能说哪里才是他们真正的定居点。他们在那里，为了消夏而呆在郊区，为了调养而住进温泉疗养院，即使有人告诉你他们将在那里住上一整年、十年、一辈子，你也几乎不会感到有任何惊奇。<br><br>可是自从有了护照之后、我们仿佛瞬间被强制成为了某种身份，强制在某个地方安家，而否定其他地方可以长期留宿的权利（当然这也不全是护照的错）。我们用七天游玩五个国家，用二十五天看完一个大洲，然后又回到和出发前一模一样的轨迹当中。我们的生活方式发生了任何改变吗？不、不、在每一个城市，我们都像径直走进他人宅院然后大声点菜的不礼貌的客人。因为旅途劳顿而在一家咖啡馆休憩的人，为什么竟会大言不惭地说自己体会到了正宗的巴黎风貌呢？所有旅行网站上的游记，不过都是攻略和待办事项列表的杂合而已；而品评游记高低的，并不是记述人的生活情趣和理念，而是旅行的效率。美！美！美！那些评论大声呼号，仿佛景点和阅历是可以相提并论的东西一样；而我觉得最高的赞美，不过是你照出了明信片一般的照片……能从短期的高强度旅行中看到除景点以外东西的人，少之又少。<br><br>如果可能，我真想做一次毫无攻略的旅行。不是从一个景点用最短的时间最经济的方式奔波到下一个景点，而是采用随机性的邂逅，发现这个城市的某种必然性——那种巴黎圣母院必须伫立在巴黎的必然性。若非如此，那么圣母院作为一个景点出现在罗马，我们也可以放肆地留影了（然而很不幸，现今大多数旅行都是这样地没有营养）。<br><br>这是双重的隔膜，双重的打击！作为一个幻想家只能看到析居于日常之上的瞬间真实；作为一个匆匆的游客却看不到这个城市通过几百年的静默生长而沉淀的那些宝贵之物。<br><br>是的！这些东西我们竟然无法改变！<br><br>再见了！帽檐上斜插着鸢尾小花的女人们！再见了！从王宫大道上骑马而过的器宇轩昂的火枪手！<br><br>可是如果我们脸皮足够厚的话，我们还可以这么说。<br><br>“所谓旅行，重要的并不是目的地，而是和谁一起同行。”<br><br>这么一说我突然想起成都人的郊游也不过是换个地方打麻将。油菜花田里也好，小溪里也好温泉里也好，重要的是不是风景，而是麻友和手气……人类！你们为了社交也是别出心裁。<br><br>说真的，我或将失去继续旅行下去的动力——尤其是，去那些实实在在地伫立了上千年，又必然会继续伫立千年的大都会。由于客观的现代化和主观的现代旅行主义——如韩少功所说，世界上所有的城市，越来越成为一个城市……我是不可能寻着那份车马鞍簪的优雅了。那份绰约的风致，仅仅在普鲁斯特童年里存在着——而我透过文字隐隐约约感觉到的那种类似的朦胧情感，也很可能也并不普鲁斯特的原意。<br><br>说起来，也许只有这份朦胧的情感，才算得上每个城市独特的遗产。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2015-05-17_巴黎的呼吸.md"},{"id":"2015-05-09_go-south-go-south","date":"2015-05-09","title":"Go South, Go South","titleZh":"到南方去 到南方去","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"At Amsterdam’s Van Gogh Museum, I had to revise what I thought I knew about Haizi’s gaunt brother. Van Gogh, it turned out, was more ordinary than I had imagined. The world…","excerptZh":"阿姆斯特丹的梵高博物馆里，我修正了自己对于瘦哥哥的认识。 原来梵高比我想象中更平凡。相较于举世公认的疯癫的称号，和海子眼中痉挛的向日葵；我在博物馆中见到…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2015-05-09_到南方去.jpg","tags":["travel log","europe"],"tagsZh":["游记","欧洲"],"readingMin":6,"htmlEn":"<p>At Amsterdam’s Van Gogh Museum, I had to revise what I thought I knew about Haizi’s gaunt brother.<br><br>Van Gogh, it turned out, was more ordinary than I had imagined. The world remembers him as mad; Haizi saw sunflowers convulsing before him. But the man I encountered in the museum—the man who kept moving south across Western Europe—was no husk seized and driven by inspiration. He was a vulnerable, rough-hewn craftsman who depended on painting to hold his life together, to give it shape and meaning.<br><br>No uncanny mist veiled his eyes. The world inside his mind was neither hallucinatory nor otherwise extraordinary. Those dense strokes of Neo-Impressionist color were simply the channel he had finally cut, after ceaseless groping and searching, so that everything pent up inside him could pour out.<br><br>Pure genius, surely, should be an instrument: a wisp of spirit—or something like that—bound in human form by a god intent on creating beauty in this world. Such people would live only to create; or rather, to relay divine revelation. From the moment of their making, they would stand at the summit, high above everyone else.<br><br>But Van Gogh was not like that.<br><br>My gaunt brother, brush in hand, painted without rest. Somewhere amid that intricate labor, he would recover scattered traces of divine prompting—like radium teased from the waste of pitchblende! Hard work was a passageway. Only by such a terribly inefficient means could a person climb toward the summit and its clear, shining light.<br><br>I felt something similar while studying classical music in a MOOC. Music had already lost its sacred aura for me. Zhou Guoping may have called it “the language of heaven,” but when I followed the lives of Beethoven and Bach, I did not see men frantically transcribing a torrent of inspiration as it flashed across the page. I saw them working from musical theory, testing tonal harmonies again and again, then using repetition, variation, and other techniques to build an entire composition, detail by detail…<br><br>Only then did I understand: music may unfold through time, but it can still obey a kind of spatial symmetry. That formal order is meter in poetry, harmonious color in painting… Beauty has always obeyed something.<br><br>All of this is inheritance. The masters before us left behind frameworks for approaching beauty. Those who came after either reinforced those frameworks or broke them, and in doing so carried humanity’s aesthetic understanding forward. Science proceeds in much the same way. Or perhaps human beings simply cannot live outside the causal sequence of time.<br><br>Humanity, in the face of all that is sublime, is no more than a craftsman.<br><br>And still, we must affirm the spirit of the craftsman. Ignorant and naked though we are, we are not empty-handed.<br><br>Those who know how to gaze, and how to hone, will in the end draw close to truth.<br><br>Let us return to Van Gogh. Let us begin with his life. As a young man, he was “introverted and peculiar.” He worked for an art dealer, then, seized by religious fervor, became a preacher—but only briefly. Later, at his family’s suggestion, he began making his way toward a life as an artist. He was poor, often supported by his younger brother, and moved between the Netherlands and Belgium; all the while, he steadily acquired the fundamentals of painting. He preferred the countryside and its peasants, and portraits of peasants gave him his first foothold. But to encounter the avant-garde of his time, he went to Paris, where he met Paul Gauguin and other painters. There he also encountered Japanese woodblock prints. He fell deeply in love with them, copied them, and began making work in their spirit. His palette shifted from dark to bright. In poor health, he traveled farther south, into the countryside of southern France, where he produced a great body of work. Yet it was also in the warm Yellow House that he and Gauguin fell out. As his mental state grew less stable, he voluntarily entered an asylum. Still painting prolifically, he moved into the final stretch of his career. Unable to bear the loneliness, he eventually chose to shoot himself in a field.<br><br>He was the kind of man who lived inside his own world, inside his own paintings. That was why he produced so much.<br><br>As an ordinary man, he had to do what ordinary people do: hone his technique and his powers of observation, and invent new ways of painting. Only then could he communicate more fully with himself and find forms more closely matched to his own expressive force.<br><br>I looked at his many self-portraits. The guide explained that Van Gogh painted them because he was poor—he could not afford models—and because he wanted to practice, searching for the brushwork that could express him most fully. But I could not help imagining something else. Perhaps, sitting before the mirror every day, or simply very often, he gazed at himself and recorded his face and mood as if keeping a diary. It was like girls taking selfie after selfie in ever-changing makeup; even the form of narcissism was the same. The self Van Gogh saw changed from moment to moment: sometimes weary, sometimes lost; sometimes angular and alert, sometimes dejected and spent.<br><br>Van Gogh kept gazing at himself, catching his inner sensations. It was as though the gaze connected the self to the outside world, as though only this could keep Van Gogh’s body within ordinary bounds, keep it from swelling too far. What was trying to inflate him? I imagine it was something like a gas, continuously produced by a temperament that could never fit itself to life.<br><br>No flood of genius ever surged forth; there lies the limit of human understanding. But if human understanding is a beam of light, then gazing and honing are the instruments that gather it to a focus.<br><br>Keep gazing, keep gazing, and in the end you will become the thing you gaze upon. Down humanity’s long river, generation upon generation, perhaps many have already joined the universe by this means.<br><br>But how ridiculous I was then. The south I kept urging myself toward turned out to be south only in the geographical sense: closer to the equator. Beyond that, it was nothing. Nothing at all.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>阿姆斯特丹的梵高博物馆里，我修正了自己对于瘦哥哥的认识。<br>原来梵高比我想象中更平凡。相较于举世公认的疯癫的称号，和海子眼中痉挛的向日葵；我在博物馆中见到的、那个在西欧大陆上不断向南迁徙的，并不是一个被灵感所俘虏所驱动的躯壳，而是凭借作画这个行为维持人生形态和意义的脆弱而粗犷的工匠。<br>他的眼眸并非染上了异于常人的雾翳，他意识中的世界也并无什么幻觉或特殊之处；而那些新印象派的一笔笔浓重色彩，不过是他为了倾泻心中的积郁，通过不断地摸索和探寻，最终凿通的水渠罢了。<br>是的，纯粹的天才应当是一种工具，一个神为了在人间创造美而用人形束缚的一团灵气或者类似的东西。他们活着仅仅是为了创造，或者换一种说法，为了传达神谕——他们应当自被创造之日起，就站在众人的山巅。<br><br>但梵高不是这样。<br>瘦哥哥、他拿着笔不知疲倦地作画，在繁复的工作中，他可以拾起零星的神灵的启示（犹如从沥青铀矿的废料之中找到的镭！）。勤劳的工作是一条通径，凭借这种效率极低的方式，人们才得以攀上那闪耀着澄澈之光的山巅。<br>我在MOOC上学习古典乐的时候，也有相同的感觉；音乐对于我已经剥离了其神圣性的意味。尽管周国平老师说那是“天国的语言”，但追寻贝多芬和巴赫的人生轨迹，我所看到景象的并不是他们如闪电一般在纸上疯狂写下喷薄的灵感，而是基于乐理基础和对音律和谐的不断推敲，将一种主题通过反复、变奏以及其他的技法，细致地将整个作品充盈起来……<br>那时的我终于看清，尽管音乐是时间性的流动的作品，但并不妨碍它们遵循某种空间意义上的对称性。而这种规章和制度，正是诗歌中的格律和绘画中的和谐色……美一直在遵循某些东西。<br><br>这是一件充满承袭意味的事情。前辈的大师们留下的为了接近美而设立的框架，后辈们或巩固或打破，而得以实现人类审美认知的进化。这和科学是一个道理，或者说，人类本来就这样必然地活在时间序列的因果之中。<br>人类，在所有至高之物面前，不过是工匠般的存在。<br>然而，我们仍然要肯定这样的工匠精神。我们虽然愚昧而赤裸，但我们并非两手空空。<br>因为懂得凝视和磨砺的人，终将接近真理。<br><br>我们继续来谈梵高、让我们从他的生平开始谈起。梵高年轻时候是个“内向而古怪”的人，在艺术品商行工作的他因为狂热的宗教热情而成为了牧师，然而也仅仅是一段时间。后来在家人的提议下他开始向艺术家的方向发展，虽然穷困而时常接受弟弟的接济，而同时也辗转于荷兰和比利时，但他稳步地习得了绘画的基本知识。他偏爱乡村和农民（农民的肖像则是他起家的第一步），然而为了接触同时代的先锋艺术，他来到了巴黎并结识了保罗·高更一类的其他画家。而也是在那个时期，他接触到了日本的装饰性版画的风格（并深深迷恋进而临摹创作），个人的作品色调也由暗转明。因为健康状况不佳，他又向南去了南法的乡村并创作了大量作品；然而也是在那个充满温情的黄房子里，他和高更决裂了。由于精神状态越来越不稳定，他又自愿搬到了精神病院中，同时也是在大量的创作之中进入自己生涯的尾声。因为无法忍受的孤寂，他最终选择在田野之中向自己开枪。<br>他就是那种活在自己的世界里，自己的画中，所以他才如此多产。<br><br>作为常人的他，为了和自己更好地交流，为了更加贴合自己的表现力，他必须像常人一样磨练自己的技法、观察，在绘画的方式上创新。<br>我看着他数量众多的自画像，虽然导览中介绍说梵高这么做是因为穷（没钱聘请模特）和锻炼绘画技法（寻找自己最有表现力的笔触），但我无论如何也不能停止这样的想象，那就是：当他每天，或者频繁地、坐在镜子前凝视自己的时候，他就像是在记日记一样记录自己的面孔和心情。那和频繁变换妆容的女生的自拍一样（连自恋的方式都一样），梵高眼中的自己也在时刻变化着：有时倦怠，有时迷茫，有时充满棱角和机敏，有时却显得颓废。<br><br>梵高一直凝视自己，捕捉内感觉。这仿佛是一种将自我和外界连接的手段，仿佛借助这种形式，梵高的身体才可以保持常态，不至于过度膨胀一样。至于是什么东西要让他膨胀起来，我想那大概是与生活格格不入的性格在一直产生某种气体的类似物吧。<br>天才的洪流从未涌现，这是人类理解力的界限。然而，如果人类的理解力是一束光线的话，凝视和磨砺就是收束这光芒的工具。<br>当你一直、一直凝视，你就终将变做自己的凝视之物。在这漫漫繁衍的人类的长河中，许多人可能已经借此与宇宙融为一体了。<br><br>可是那时的我是多么可笑啊，我催促自己前往的南方，居然仅仅在地理意义上更接近赤道而已。除此之外居然什么也不是。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2015-05-09_到南方去.md"},{"id":"2015-04-12_notes-on-resurrection-time-and-life-in-flow-and-superposition","date":"2015-04-12","title":"Notes on Resurrection: Time and Life in Flow and Superposition","titleZh":"复活随笔：关于流动和叠加的时间和生命","subtitle":"","subtitleZh":"","excerpt":"【Rome · Vatican City】 There were once people who spent their entire lives doing nothing but trying to render the face of Jesus. Watching camera flashes beat relentlessly against the statues, I…","excerptZh":"【罗马·梵蒂冈】 从前有些人，光是为了临摹耶稣的容貌就花上了一辈子。我看着那些被闪光灯不停拍打的雕像，心想也许只有他们，才配被称为真正的野心家。 整个教…","cover":"./images/blog/covers/2015-05-12_复活随笔.jpg","tags":["travel log","europe"],"tagsZh":["游记","欧洲"],"readingMin":5,"htmlEn":"<p>【Rome · Vatican City】<br><br>There were once people who spent their entire lives doing nothing but trying to render the face of Jesus. Watching camera flashes beat relentlessly against the statues, I thought that perhaps they alone deserved to be called truly ambitious.<br><br>The whole church was one vast altar, its elaborate ornament the crystallized wealth of an entire generation—and perhaps, from beginning to end, the whole thing had existed only to serve the desires of a few. Yet its value cannot be denied. Even if only for the sake of those who come to worship, its value cannot be denied. As someone for whom religion alone is not enough, I saw in it the radiance of the human spirit, aesthetically and architecturally. I imagined that every artisan working on the scaffolds, bending beneath the dome to look down at the town spread across the ground, must have had one fleeting moment when they half believed they were building the Tower of Babel. This is a great undertaking, as it has been in every age.<br><br>【Florence】<br><br>Wandering through the Uffizi Galleries, I saw many Jesuses and many Marys. The Jesuses and Marys wore all kinds of faces. That much was obvious: from the Middle Ages through the early Renaissance—the periods to which those painters belonged—religious subjects predominated. But it made me wonder: if every painter carried within them a different Platonic Form for Jesus and for Mary, could it be that neither was a single individual at all, but a set? Yes. In the great current of history, anyone who leads by example and carries humanity’s fate forward may be given the name Jesus; every chaste and noble person is also part of the Virgin. The definitions of Jesus and the Virgin are continually enriched, and that is precisely what allows them to appear with so many different faces. They stand for what humanity pursues, expects, and desires.<br><br>As I climbed the winding, cramped, dark passageways of the Cathedral of Santa Maria del Fiore, I had the strange sense that I was making my way upstream through the cathedral’s esophagus. The figures ahead tilted and swayed until, at moments, I mistook them for afterimages left by visitors who had reached the top long before me. And in truth, why not? The cathedral ingests and disgorges thousands of people every day; who could possibly be remembered by this thing that has stood for centuries?<br><br>From the viewing platform atop the dome, I gazed down almost imperiously over Florence, over its red-and-white houses caught between hills and white clouds. I imagined that this behemoth, from the moment its construction was complete, had somehow begun a new life of its own. It no longer belonged to humanity. It had become something like a spirit medium: a solid fortress reaching flamboyantly into the sky. On the cathedral’s scale of time, the tourists and residents who have crowded this city for centuries are no more than flowing water—if all eras are superimposed upon one another. Under the sun, that water keeps evaporating from the channels divided by the red-and-white houses. Seen from the distant hills, the houses themselves are nothing but the cathedral’s vast root system, spreading in every direction so that it may grow.<br><br>Only the cathedral remains. The cathedral alone is truly alive.<br><br>Humanity! How pale and powerless you look before this thing of your own making! And yet perhaps that is precisely why we had to make the cathedral.<br><br>Let us stay with the metaphor of flowing water. Once the shell of the body slips free of the shackles of its own consciousness, we too can see that the images of an entire life are nothing but water that has washed through it. Yes. As Haruki Murakami wrote, “I was nothing but a pathway for the person known as me.”<br><br>【Cinque Terre】<br><br>Motionless on time’s leeward slope<br>like a general galloping through ranks of crops<br>I rein to a halt and look around, asking the plants:<br>Has everyone been growing well today?<br><br>From the mountaintop the village begins to grow<br>then slides along the ridge toward the plain<br>water dripping through the mountain ravines—<br>this is how the city is fed into being<br><br>【Train Reverie】<br><br>Haruki Murakami’s creative process tells us simply this about the search through life: we seek hope and meaning, only to watch them recede into the intangible. Then we affirm the worth of our own striving and endurance. We affirm our knowledge that, having fallen into the human world, there is something we must bear. At last we begin to believe in the power of love. We become warriors of love and set out along a road governed entirely by causes and conditions. Those who have truly set foot on that road have perhaps come to know that love is the core of the human spirit. With that certainty, they wait in happiness—or, within happiness, they grow. Yes. Love makes the world go round.<br><br>_*Originally written in Chinese. This article is translated by GPT-5.6._</p>","htmlZh":"<p>【罗马·梵蒂冈】<br><br>从前有些人，光是为了临摹耶稣的容貌就花上了一辈子。我看着那些被闪光灯不停拍打的雕像，心想也许只有他们，才配被称为真正的野心家。<br><br>整个教堂就是一个大祭坛，那些繁复的装饰凝结了一整个世代的财富——而也许从头至尾只是为了满足某些人的欲望。可是它的价值无法被否定，即使只是为了那些朝拜的人它的价值也不能被否定。作为一个不单纯满足宗教的人，我从中看到的是人类精神熠熠的闪光，从美学的意义上，从建筑学的意义上。我想每一个在梯架上工作的工匠，当他们在穹顶俯身看向铺在地上的市镇的时候，他们总有一个瞬间恍惚相信自己正在修建巴别塔。这是一项伟大的事业，从古至今都是。<br><br><br>【佛罗伦萨】<br><br>当我漫游在乌菲兹美术馆里的时候，我看到了许多的耶稣和许多的玛丽。耶稣和玛丽们拥有各式的面孔——这是显而易见的事——因为从画家的从属的时代来说，从中世纪到文艺复兴早期的作品大多是宗教题材。可是我就想，既然每个画家心中都拥有不同理型的耶稣和玛利亚的形象，有没有可能他们本身就不是一个个体，而是一个集合呢？是的，在这条历史的洪流中，所有以身作则，背负人类命运前进的人都可以被冠以耶稣的称号；所有贞洁而高尚的人也都是圣母的组成部分。耶稣和圣母的定义是逐渐在丰富的，而因此他们才得以显现不同的面孔。他们代表了人类的追求、期待和愿望。<br><br>攀爬百花圣母大教堂的弯曲、促狭而昏暗的甬道，我恍惚觉得自己是在教堂的食道里逆流而上。我前面的人影歪歪斜斜地摇曳着，以至于有些时刻我竟把它们当做以前登顶的游客留下的鬼影。其实又何尝不是呢？这个大教堂每天吞吐上千的游客，谁又有可能真正被这个矗立了几百年的东西记住呢？我站在穹顶的观景台上，睥睨翡冷翠夹在丘陵和白云之间红红白白的住宅。我想象着，这个庞然大物，自修建竣工之日起，就奇怪地自顾自地开始了新的生命，不再属于人类，而成为了类似灵媒的招展地伸向天空的坚固堡垒。在它的时间刻度里，这个城市几百年来熙熙攘攘的游客和居民不过构成了一种水流般的东西——如果我们将所有的时代重叠起来的话。这些流水在太阳底下不断蒸发着，从那些被红红白白的房子隔开的沟洫里；而从远处的山峦眺望过来，这些住宅也不过是教堂为了生长而延展四方的庞大根系！<br><br>剩下来的唯独教堂，真正活着的只有教堂。<br><br>人类！你们在面对这个自身创造之物时显得多么苍白无力！可是也许正是因为这样，我们才有必要把教堂创造出来吧。<br><br>让我们继续水流的隐喻。当我们的躯壳也脱离了自身意识的枷锁，我们同样可以看见自己一生的影像不过是这副躯壳中冲刷而过的水流。是的，正如村上春树先生所说，“我不过是我这个人的一条通道”。<br><br><br>【五渔村】<br><br>静止在时光的背风坡<br>像将军策马在行列的庄稼中奔驰<br>立马而四顾 向植物问道<br>今天的大家 都有好好地生长吗？<br><br>村庄从山顶长起<br>然后沿着山脊滑向平原<br>那些沿着山涧滴穿的流水<br>城市就这样被喂养起来<br><br><br>【火车狂想】<br><br>村上春树先生的创作过程，无非告诉我们这样一个探寻人生的事实：我们追寻希望和意义，却往往遁于缥缈；而后我们肯定自身奋斗和忍耐的价值，肯定自身因堕入人世而必须背负什么的认识；最后我们开始相信爱的力量，化身爱的战士，踏上一条全然被因缘所左右的道路。真正踏上这条道路的人大概知晓了爱才是人类精神的内核。他们怀着这种确信，幸福中等待，或者、在幸福中生长。是的，世界因爱而转动。</p>","source":"./content/posts/2015-04-12_复活随笔.md"}];
